14.0,Four arrested in Gujarat for using AI deepfakes to commit Aadhaar and banking fraud - madhyamamonline.com,madhyamamonline.com,"Wed, 29 Apr 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://madhyamamonline.com/india/four-arrested-in-gujarat-for-using-ai-deepfakes-to-commit-aadhaar-and-banking-fraud-1515605,success_decoder,success,Four arrested in Gujarat for using AI deepfakes to commit Aadhaar and banking fraud,Web Desk,2026-04-29,"Four arrested in Gujarat for using AI deepfakes to commit Aadhaar and banking fraud
text_fields
Four people have been arrested in Gujarat for using artificial intelligence and deepfake technology to bypass biometric security systems and carry out identity-based financial fraud.
According to investigators, the gang used AI tools, including Gemini and Meta AI, to create deepfake videos with realistic eye-blink animations using victims’ images and personal data.
Police said the accused projected these AI-generated videos during verification procedures to fool liveness detection systems and gain unauthorised access to update mobile numbers linked to victims’ Unique Identification Authority of India Aadhaar profiles through Common Service Center kits.
Once the linked mobile numbers were changed, the gang took control of accounts connected to platforms such as DigiLocker and various banking services.
Investigators said the accused then received one-time passwords directly on their own devices, allowing them to open fraudulent accounts with banks including IDFC FIRST Bank, Kotak Mahindra Bank, and Jio Payments Bank.
The group also secured personal loans through lending platforms, including RKBansal, True Credits, and EarlySalary, leaving victims burdened with debt.
The arrested accused have been identified as Kanubhai Parmar, Ashish Valand, Mohammad Kaif Patel, and Deep Gupta. Police said the suspects come from varied educational backgrounds, including business administration and information technology studies. They are currently in judicial custody while investigations continue to identify other members of the network.
Following the case, Ahmedabad Police launched an Aadhaar security awareness campaign, urging citizens to lock their biometric data using the mAadhaar app or the official UIDAI website.
Authorities also advised people to use masked Aadhaar copies, avoid sharing OTPs, and stay cautious of suspicious links related to KYC updates or Aadhaar verification.",282,,True,False
18.0,"KYC lapses, backlog weaken defences against fraud: RBI - The Times of India",The Times of India,"Tue, 30 Dec 2025 08:00:00 GMT",https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/business/india-business/kyc-lapses-backlog-weaken-defences-against-fraud-rbi/articleshow/126242077.cms,success_decoder,success,"KYC lapses, backlog weaken defences against fraud: RBI - The Times of India",TNN; Updated Dec,2025-12-30,"MUMBAI: In a renewed push to contain fraud and improve customer safeguards, RBI has asked banks to clear the growing backlog in KYC updates and warned against the routine rejection of applications. Lapses in KYC, the central bank said, weaken defences against fraud and exclusion, particularly in rural and semi-urban areas. Alongside fraud, RBI has flagged mis-selling as a persistent conduct risk and has said that it will come out with new guidelines.
In a report, RBI said banks should ""organise camps and launch intensive campaigns"" to deal with delays in periodic KYC updates, with a focus on smaller branches. It also cautioned lenders against mechanical rejections, stressing that onboarding or KYC-updation requests should not be turned down without due consideration and that reasons for rejection must be properly recorded. The regulator noted that it had itself been involved in a nationwide re-KYC drive at the gram panchayat level between July and Oct 2025.
 New age threats
The emphasis on KYC forms part of a broader attempt to curb fraud at a time when financial crime is becoming more sophisticated. 
The report warned that cyber and operational incidents pose a growing threat to financial stability. To counter this, RBI said it is working closely with other authorities, including the ministry of home affairs, to develop and operationalise measures aimed at curbing digital and cyber-enabled fraud while strengthening customer protection.
Technology is central to this effort. Recent initiatives include MuleHunter.ai, a system designed to identify and flag mule accounts through system-wide learning, and a digital payments intelligence platform that uses AI to detect risky transactions and share intelligence across the system. 
Banks have also been advised to use the mobile number revocation list available on the telecommunications department's digital intelligence platform to clean customer databases and prevent fraud carried out through voice calls and SMS. The regulator has asked lenders to tighten internal controls, ensure adequate grievance-redress officers at all levels and invest in digital financial literacy.
The report said mis-selling of financial products by regulated entities has ""significant consequences"" for customers and for the financial system more broadly. In response, RBI plans to issue comprehensive instructions covering advertising, marketing and sales practices across different categories of regulated entities, with the explicit aim of preventing mis-selling.",375,,True,False
20.0,Bank data leak: How insider hands are fueling India’s scam factories - The Times of India,The Times of India,"Mon, 15 Sep 2025 07:00:00 GMT",https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/city/noida/bank-insiders-fueling-data-leaks-enable-rise-in-banking-scams-in-india/articleshow/123895787.cms,success_decoder,success,Bank data leak: How insider hands are fueling Indiaâs scam factories | Noida News - The Times of India,Advitya Bahl,2025-09-15,"- News
- City News
- noida News
- Bank data leak: How insider hands are fueling Indiaâs scam factories
Trending
Cyber fraud is rising in India. Scammers are using leaked customer data from banks. Bank employees are selling private information. This helps criminals commit KYC fraud and other scams. Police have arrested bank staff involved. Experts suggest stricter data access controls. They also suggest better background checks and penalties. Banks should alert customers about data leaks.
NOIDA: When a 32-year-old resident of the Greater Noida area received a call last month from a âbank representativeâ asking her to update her KYC details, she did not suspect foul play. The caller knew her name, her PAN number, her last transaction, and even her motherâs maiden name. Convinced the call was genuine, she shared an OTP that drained Rs 1.4 lakh from her savings account within minutes.Such stories have become common not just in Noida, but across India. Nearly every day, there are news articles of innocent people, in recent days, elderly ones, losing most or all their savings to scammers perpetuating KYC fraud, digital arrest, fake online investments, and other such cyber crimes. The growing list of these crimes has raised two alarming questionsâhow scammers are able to contact people, and how they possess a high level of personal detail of their targets before even making the first call. Increasingly, law enforcement agencies are discovering that the answer to both of these questionsâthe source of peopleâs personal dataâis not just careless online behaviour or phishing traps, but the very institutions that are supposed to safeguard peopleâs money: banks themselves.
You Can Also Check: Noida AQI | Weather in Noida | Gold Rate Today in Noida | Silver Rate Today in Noida
Rogue insiders, rich pickingsIn August, Gurugram police released data stating that 42 bank employees were arrested since the start of 2025 in the city for helping cyber scammers. The arrested employees worked at all levelsâfrom clerks to assistant managersâand took part in the fraud by sharing customersâ private data with scammers, apart from other aid such as approving fraudulent transactions and alerting scammers of police investigations. In several states, including Uttar Pradesh, Maharashtra, Delhi, and West Bengal, such cases have revealed that rogue bank employees are among the primary suppliers of sensitive customer data to fraud networks.âMost major frauds start with some insider tip-off,â Shavya Goyal, Additional Deputy Commissioner of Police (Cyber) told TOI. âThe scamsters often join hands with outsourced staff, temporary employees, or call centre agents handling KYC and loan applications.â The staff may hand over customersâ details in exchange for as little as a few hundred rupees for regular accounts to Rs 20,000-30,000 each for details of âhigh net worth individuals,â Goyal said.Earlier this year in February, officials of cyber crime and intelligence agencies also flagged to the Union Ministry of Home Affairs that bank employees and third-party vendors, who have open access to confidential banking data, fuel online frauds and cause massive financial losses for customers.How the data flowsThe officer revealed that fraudsters rely on a steady pipeline of bank-sourced data to make their scams convincing. Hereâs how the ecosystem works:Lead generation: Scammers directly contact bank employees and offer them money in exchange for customer records.Call centre employees: Staff working in call centres on behalf of banksâ loan, credit card, and customer service divisions, telecoms, insurance firms, or NBFCs can gain access to and sell customer lists.Middlemen brokers: These are insiders who may be former bank employees or people with good connections in the banking industry who can gain access to bulk databases and sell them to cyber criminals.No matter which way the scammers opt to obtain information, convincing a bank employee does not prove difficult. The ADCP explained, âIf a clerk making Rs 20,000 a month is offered Rs 30,000 cash for a few thousand customer leads, they are quite likely to give in.â ""Most private banks and NBFCs now also outsource customer care and KYC operations to third-party call centres. These centres employ hundreds of temporary staff with complete access to their database, often with minimal background checks,"" she said.There are also other ways in which scammers gain access to customer lists:Data shops: In cities like Delhi, Kolkata, Noida, Gurgaon, and Bengaluru, âdata marketsâ openly operate, selling as many as one lakh bank leads for under Rs 50,000.Website hacks: When a bank, telecom provider, e-commerce site, or payment wallet app suffers a breach, millions of phone numbers, email IDs, and sometimes financial details leak online. Poorly secured govt portals (land records, voter IDs, ration cards, etc.) are sometimes hacked.Number-generating algorithms: Scammers can deploy software to randomly generate scores of registered mobile phone numbers and in some cases, also provide basic details like the number-ownerâs name and address.Data gatherers: Real estate agents, SIM card sellers, travel agents, and job consultants often keep copies of documents and later sell them. Hospitals and diagnostic labs also hold sensitive patient details that can be leaked.Digital footprints: People post birthdays, new jobs, travel plans, or even banking/insurance grievances publicly on social media, which fraudsters exploit. People also share numbers and addresses on marketplaces such as OLX or Quikr, job portals, online contests, and other sites.Fake websites and apps: Fraudsters build lookalike bank or UPI apps, tricking users into entering personal information. SMS or WhatsApp links can also capture contacts, location, and sometimes entire phone data if the malware is installed. Even tiny bits of data are enough to build trust. If a scammer knows your name and the last four digits of the account (from a leak), they can impersonate your bank convincingly. If they know your PAN number and date of birth, they can apply for loans or SIM cards in your name.Regulatory gapsThe Reserve Bank of India (RBI) mandates strict confidentiality of customer information, but enforcement is patchy. Most leaks do not get reported formally because victims are told their negligence was the cause. Cyber experts argue that India lacks a strong deterrent. In the US or EU, insider leaks can attract multi-million-dollar penalties under data protection laws.In India, even when caught, rogue employees face minimal consequences. Banks, for their part, continue to insist that their âsystems are secure.â Official statements often blame phishing, customer negligence, or malware for frauds. But law enforcement officials argue that the elephant in the roomâinsider leaksâis rarely acknowledged. The proposed Digital Personal Data Protection Act (DPDPA) could change the landscape by holding both corporations and individuals accountable for leaks. But until its enforcement becomes visible, fraudsters will keep exploiting gaps.What needs to changeExperts and investigators suggest a multi-pronged response:1. Restrict Access:Limit the access to personal data to higher management or authorised employees.2. Background Verification: Ensure deeper vetting of outsourced staff and temporary hires.3. Decoy Data: Plant fake customer records in databases to catch insiders trying to sell them.4. Stronger Penalties: Pursue criminal prosecution of rogue employees, not just termination.5. Customer Awareness: Banks should alert customers about data leaks rather than blaming phishing alone. Consumer activists argue that banks must share liability. If a scammer already has half the data, blaming the customer is unfair. Financial institutions must be held accountable for leaks at their end.âThe battle is no longer just about cyber hygiene,â Goyal stated. âItâs about cleaning up the rot within banks. Until we fix that, scam calls will keep ringing, and accounts will keep emptying.âDos and don'ts- Before any online transaction, put the web address in the URL bar.- Secure your password and PIN.- Check bank statements regularly.- Do not share identification numbers and passwords.- Spot imposters.- Report lost card immediately.- Do not put money into unknown accounts for getting jobs through agents.- Don't share KYC documents with anyone over WhatsApp.- Avoid logging in to third-party websites, clicking on promotional emails, or using a public computer.- Do not share your net banking PIN or details over calls or email.- Refrain from sharing an OTP or installing device-sharing apps.- Do not click on suspicious links in emails.- Report cases as soon as possible.Why senior citizens are most vulnerable- They are usually not very technology-savvy.- They are attractive targets as most tend to have more money at their age.- Seniors often fall for the ruse that the caller is a ""govt official.""
