43.0,Fraud at the Speed of UPI: How AI Is Supercharging India’s Cybercrime Boom - The420.in,The420.in,"Sat, 18 Apr 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://the420.in/ai-fraud-real-time-digital-payments-india-cybercrime/,success_decoder,success,Fraud at the Speed of UPI: How AI Is Supercharging India’s Cybercrime Boom,The,2026-04-18,"New Delhi: India’s fast-growing digital payments ecosystem has made transactions seamless and near-instant, but the same speed is now enabling cybercriminals to execute fraud in “real time.” Latest data and expert insights point to a troubling shift—money is being siphoned off within minutes of a transaction, often routed through multiple accounts before detection systems can respond. The evolving pattern has raised serious concerns about the resilience of Digital India’s security architecture.
UPI Speed, Criminal Greed: Fraudsters Strike Before Systems Can Blink
Over the past decade, India’s digital payments ecosystem has expanded nearly 38-fold. Platforms like UPI have democratised access to payments, making transfers effortless for millions. However, this rapid growth has been accompanied by an explosion in fraud cases. From 2.6 lakh reported incidents in 2021, the number surged to nearly 28 lakh by 2025, involving losses of ₹22,931 crore. The figures underline not just a rise in volume, but a transformation in the scale and sophistication of cyber fraud.
Banking data further highlights the severity of the threat. In FY25, bank fraud losses climbed to ₹36,014 crore—almost three times the previous year. Interestingly, while the number of cases declined, the financial impact per case increased significantly. Experts interpret this as a strategic shift by cybercriminals, who are now focusing on fewer but higher-value, well-orchestrated scams instead of small, repetitive frauds.
FCRF Returns With CDPO, Its Premier Data Protection Certification for Privacy Professionals
Fake Faces, Real Money: Synthetic Identities Fuel the Next Wave of Scams
Investigations suggest that cybercrime is no longer the work of isolated individuals but organised networks operating across platforms. Millions of account takeover attempts have been recorded across banks and fintech systems, with a significant number succeeding. Additionally, over 1.1 million suspected mule accounts—used to channel stolen money—have been identified. Once funds enter these accounts, they are rapidly dispersed across multiple layers, making tracing and recovery extremely difficult within a short time frame.
The methods of cyber fraud have also evolved dramatically. What was once limited to phishing calls, OTP scams, or malicious links has now expanded into a full-fledged “fraud-as-a-service” ecosystem. Ready-made scam tools, stolen personal data, fake KYC documents, and automated scripts are widely available, lowering the barrier to entry. This means even individuals with limited technical expertise can now execute complex financial frauds.
Mule Accounts and AI Tricks Turn Cyber Fraud into an Assembly Line Crime
At the centre of this evolving threat is the growing use of synthetic identities—fabricated profiles created by combining real and fake information to pass verification checks. These identities initially behave like legitimate users, building trust before being used for large-scale fraud. In digital lending, this trend is visible in “loan stacking,” where fraudsters secure multiple loans across platforms within minutes and disappear before detection mechanisms can respond.
Artificial intelligence has further intensified the risk. AI-driven tools can now generate highly convincing voice calls, messages, and documents, making it increasingly difficult for users to distinguish between genuine and fraudulent interactions. In many cases, victims realise they have been scammed only after funds have already been transferred.
Despite the rapid expansion of digital infrastructure, fraud detection systems are struggling to keep pace. Most financial institutions continue to rely on reactive models, identifying suspicious activity only after transactions are completed. With platforms like UPI processing billions of transactions every month, the window for intervention has narrowed significantly.
Banks Are Chasing Shadows as High-Value Scams Get Sharper and Faster
Experts suggest that introducing brief delays in high-value transactions could act as a critical safeguard. Even a short pause could provide systems with the time needed to detect anomalies and prevent fraudulent transfers. Estimates indicate that losses worth up to ₹1,120 crore could have been avoided with more effective use of existing detection technologies.
Commenting on the issue, renowned cybercrime expert and former IPS officer Prof. Triveni Singh said,
“Cyber fraud today has evolved into a networked threat. Criminals are simultaneously exploiting speed, technology, and systemic gaps. Without real-time monitoring and stronger security frameworks, it will be difficult to contain this growing menace.”
Experts also caution that the current figures may significantly understate the true scale of the problem. Many cases go unreported, while some losses are absorbed by platforms. Cross-border cybercrime networks further complicate tracking and enforcement efforts, and fragmented data across institutions hampers a unified response.
As India’s digital economy continues to scale, the challenge is no longer just about enabling faster payments, but ensuring they are secure. The focus must now shift from post-fraud response to proactive prevention—stopping fraud before it happens, rather than reacting after the damage is done.",761,,True,False
44.0,"Fraud turns organised and AI-powered, pushing financial institutions towards converged financial crime contr.. - bfsi.economictimes.indiatimes.com",bfsi.economictimes.indiatimes.com,"Mon, 13 Jul 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://bfsi.economictimes.indiatimes.com/articles/ai-powered-fraud-the-new-face-of-financial-crime-in-india/132360706,success_decoder,success,"Fraud turns organised and AI-powered, pushing financial institutions towards converged financial crime controls",Www Etbfsi Com; Brand Connect Initiative,2026-07-13,"- Articles
- 6 min read
Fraud turns organised and AI-powered, pushing financial institutions towards converged financial crime controls
Financial fraud in India is becoming more organised, technology-driven and interconnected, forcing financial institutions to rethink risk management from the ground up. While digitization has expanded access to financial services and accelerated customer onboarding, it has also widened the attack surface for fraudsters just as quickly, making traditional siloed approaches to fraud, cyber risk and anti-money laundering (AML) less effective.
ETBFSI's roundtable, held in association with Jocata, on the theme ""Siloed Controls to Convergence: AI-led Financial Crime Compliance"", brought together distinguished speakers including Nihal Shah, Director India Country AML Head & South Asia Cluster Head, Citibank; Vinodhkumar Parameswaran, Head Financial Crime Compliance, HSBC; Pankaj Mohta, Head-Fraud Management, SMFG; Ajay Kanth, Head-Fraud Risk Management, Aditya Birla Capital; Sandeep Suresh Vaidya, Head-Fraud Risk Management, Standard Chartered Bank; Abin Mathew, Head-Fraud, SBM Bank; Manish Agrawal, Sr EVP & Head-Fraud & Intelligence Control, HDFC Bank; Prakash Gupta, Chief Compliance Officer, RBL Bank; Ravinder Singh, AGM-Risk & Fraud, Bank of India; Pushkar Deshpande, Head-Compliance & Risk, Easebuzz; and Sougata Pandey, Head-Fraud Risk Management, Edelweiss Life Insurance, along with Jocata’s MD & CEO, Prashant Muddu and Director of Growth, Shailesh Deshpande, for the discussion.
Fraud now operates like a business
The opening consensus was clear: fraud is no longer the work of isolated actors; it has evolved into an organised ecosystem. Fraudsters now operate through networks, share intelligence on digital platforms and even offer ""fraud-as-a-service"" and ""money laundering-as-a-service"" models.
What makes this shift particularly dangerous is that fraudsters study institutional controls, thresholds and blind spots with remarkable precision. When a tactic stops working, they adapt quickly, constantly refining their methods to exploit the next opportunity making yesterday’s controls less effective against today’s threats.
Every digital convenience creates a new vulnerability
The rapid shift towards digital banking, digital lending and remote onboarding has significantly expanded fraud risks. Increased reliance on digital channels has reduced physical verification and introduced new vulnerabilities around identity theft, account takeover and data misuse. Speakers pointed out that while digitization remains essential for financial inclusion and growth, it has also enabled fraudsters to operate remotely and at scale, often using stolen personal information and compromised credentials.
Data protection emerged as a key concern during the discussion. The growing ecosystem of outsourcing, partnerships and third-party integrations has increased the risk of data leakage, and there are emerging cases where identity systems are being manipulated, enabling fraudsters to create synthetic identities, open accounts and execute fraudulent transactions. Stronger data governance and tighter controls over customer information were flagged as urgent priorities.
Cyber, fraud and AML are one continuous crime
Financial crime, the room agreed, can no longer be filed into neat, separate categories. A cyber breach often leads to identity theft, which subsequently facilitates fraud and eventually results in money laundering through multiple layers of transactions. What a criminal executes as a single, seamless operation, most institutions still split across three or four teams and three or four disconnected case files. That gap between how crime flows and how financial institutions are organised is what is now forcing a rethink of structures, and a move towards integrated financial crime frameworks.
Fraud prevention shifting from reactive to proactive models
There was a broad agreement that the industry has historically been more reactive than proactive in its approach to fraud management. While institutions have strengthened controls after incidents occur, pre-emptive risk assessments and predictive intelligence remain areas that need further development with growing appetite for better scenario analysis, early-warning systems and proactive monitoring mechanisms that surface vulnerabilities ahead of exploitation.
Customer profiling and behavioural analytics are becoming central to that pre-emptive posture. Participants suggested that institutions need to move beyond traditional risk categorisation and develop fraud-vulnerability profiles for customers - understanding customer behaviour, transaction patterns and susceptibility to scams can help institutions implement differentiated controls, particularly for exposed groups such as senior citizens. The discussion highlighted that the warning signs often lie not in the transaction amount but in customer behaviour and transaction patterns. Understanding how money moves and identifying customers who are more vulnerable to scams can help institutions intervene earlier and prevent fraud before it occurs.
AI emerging as a necessity rather than a trend
AI is no longer ""nice to have"" or a fear-of-missing-out trend; it has become a baseline requirement for keeping pace with attacks that mutate faster than any rulebook. AI is already being deployed for anomaly detection, transaction monitoring, pattern recognition and identifying emerging fraud typologies that traditional rule-based systems often fail to capture.
The new reality: when both sides are armed with AI
The flipside drew equal attention: the financial sector is entering an ""AI versus AI"" contest, in which institutions strengthen their defenses with the very technology their adversaries are using to sharpen attacks. Deepfakes, advanced social engineering, synthetic identities and AI-generated fraud techniques are already changing the threat landscape. As a result, institutions need to continuously upgrade their capabilities and remain agile in responding to emerging risks.
Why human intelligence still remains indispensable
AI alone is not enough. While it improves speed, efficiency and empowers investigators with contextual insights, financial crime investigations continue to require experienced professionals who understand products, customer behaviour and local nuances. The instinct once embodied by the veteran branch banker - who could simply sense when something was off - is exactly what digitization risks engineering away, and what the room was least willing to give up. The prevailing view was partnership model: artificial intelligence paired with human intelligence, field intelligence and institutional memory with explainability built in, to effectively detect and prevent fraud.
Industry moving towards convergence-led approach for financial crime compliance
All roads led back to the theme. The clear direction of travel is towards integrated financial crime frameworks that unite fraud risk, cyber security and AML functions because siloed teams often struggle to identify interconnected risks leading to delays in response times. Unified intelligence layers, shared data environments and collaborative operating models are increasingly seen as the foundation for effective financial crime management. It is precisely this unified, always-on approach that players like Jocata are building.
Financial crime management is no longer viewed merely as a cost centre. The reputational, financial and regulatory consequences have elevated it to a board-level priority. The closing consensus: the future of financial crime compliance lies in convergence - bringing together technology, intelligence, governance and collaboration to combat increasingly complex and interconnected threats. AI will be a powerful enabler, but success will ultimately depend on the industry's ability to combine it with human expertise and build proactive, integrated defense mechanisms.
