86.0,Pan-India Cyber Fraud Network Dismantled; 52 Arrested - Rediff,Rediff,"Sun, 19 Apr 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://www.rediff.com/news/report/hyderabad-police-bust-pan-india-cyber-fraud-network/20260419.htm,success_decoder,success,Pan-India Cyber Fraud Network Dismantled; 52 Arrested,Rediff News Desk; Text Feed,2026-04-19,"Hyderabad Police's 'Operation Octopus 2.0' has successfully dismantled a vast cyber fraud network, leading to the arrest of 52 individuals, including complicit bank officials, across nine states in India.
Key Points
- Hyderabad Police dismantled a pan-India cyber fraud network in 'Operation Octopus 2.0'.
- 52 individuals were arrested across nine states, including bank officials and mule account holders.
- The network facilitated investment scams, trading frauds, and 'digital arrest' scams.
- The fraudsters used 350 bank accounts to siphon off victims' funds, amounting to nearly Rs 150 crores.
- 'Operation Octopus 2.0' builds on the success of 'Operation Octopus 1', which led to 117 arrests.
Hyderabad Police on Sunday said that they have dismantled a pan-India cyber fraud network involving bank officials by arresting 52 accused across nine states.
Under 'Operation Octopus 2.O', as many as 32 bank officials, 15 mule account holders and five middlemen were apprehended for complicity, Hyderabad Police Commissioner V C Sajjanar said in a release.
Details of Operation Octopus 2.0
The operation launched recently led to the arrest of bank officials who were found to be in connivance in the opening of mule bank accounts, which were used to facilitate cyber frauds, he said.
Police said there has been a significant rise in investment scams, trading frauds, and ""digital arrest"" scams, wherein fraudsters exploit victims through manipulation, intimidation, and emotional coercion, leading to substantial financial losses.
Impact of the Cyber Fraud Network
Recognising the seriousness of the situation, 'Operation OctopusÃ¢Â 1' was conceptualised to deliver a decisive strike against these illegal networks. Through an intensive investigation into the surge of such frauds, the Cyber Crime Police Station (CCPS), uncovered a nexus of 350 bank accounts used to siphon off victims' funds, police said.
These accounts were linked to approximately 850 cases across the country involving transactions amounting to nearly Rs 150 crores, police said.
Previous Operation and Continued Efforts
Accordingly, in February this year, 'Operation OctopusÃ¢Â 1' was executed across 16 states by 32 police teams, resulting in the arrest of 117 accused persons.
Building on the outcomes of Operation OctopusÃ¢Â 1, Operation Octopus 2.O was launched, they said.
The operation Octopus 2.O was executed simultaneously across nine states with 16 special teams deployed to identified bank official targets in Maharashtra, Delhi, Rajasthan, West Bengal, Karnataka, Gujarat, Andhra Pradesh, Telangana and Bihar. The operation went on for seven days, and 52 individuals, including bank officials who played pivotal roles in the execution of the fraud, were apprehended, police said.
The mule account holders knowingly permitted their bank accounts to be used for layering and laundering illicit funds; the five key middlemen were responsible for procuring these accounts and facilitating the movement of funds to the masterminds, police added.
The entire operation was conducted under the direct supervision of DCP (Cybercrimes) V Aravind Babu and ACP (Cyber Crime police station) R G Siva Maruthi, the release added.",482,,True,False
88.0,Bihar Retiree Gets Back Entire ₹35.74 Lakh Lost to Digital Arrest Scam - The420.in,The420.in,"Thu, 20 Aug 2026 13:27:22 GMT",https://the420.in/darbhanga-digital-arrest-scam-full-recovery-cyber-police/,success_decoder,success,Bihar Retiree Gets Back Entire ₹35.74 Lakh Lost to Digital Arrest Scam,The,2026-08-20,"A 73-year-old retired government employee in Bihar has recovered the full ₹35.74 lakh he lost to a digital arrest scam, in what cyber police in Darbhanga describe as the district’s first complete recovery of such a large sum. The funds were traced, frozen and returned within roughly six months of the complaint being filed, a timeline that investigators say reflects the value of acting quickly.
The victim, Ram Bahadur Kamat of Lakshmi Sagar Gangwara, was first contacted by callers claiming to represent Mumbai Cyber Crime, who alleged that a bank account opened using his Aadhaar had been used for illegal transactions. The claim, though false, was enough to set the fraud in motion.
A Familiar Script, Executed With Precision
What followed matches a pattern that has become disturbingly routine across India. Kamat was placed under sustained pressure through video calls, a tactic now widely known as digital arrest, in which fraudsters impersonate law enforcement to create the illusion of an ongoing criminal investigation. No Indian law permits an arrest to be conducted over a video call, yet the theatre of uniforms, official-sounding jargon and constant surveillance has proven effective against victims across age groups and professions.
A second caller, posing as a Canara Bank official, then instructed Kamat to transfer his ₹35.74 lakh to another account for what was described as verification purposes, with a promise that the money would return within a week. Believing the claim, he complied. When the promised repayment never arrived, the deception became clear, and he approached the cyber police on 12 February 2026.
Digital arrest scams have grown into one of the most financially damaging categories of cybercrime in the country. The Indian Cyber Crime Coordination Centre recorded losses running into hundreds of crores from such scams within just a few months in 2024, and the broader toll from these schemes has continued climbing since, with cumulative losses now stretching into thousands of crores over recent years.
How the Money Was Traced and Frozen
Once Kamat’s complaint was registered, investigators moved through the national cybercrime helpline, 1930, to place the transferred funds on hold before they could be withdrawn or moved further. Technical analysis of the transaction trail led them to a Yes Bank account belonging to an organisation based in Bhubaneswar, Odisha, prompting the Darbhanga team to travel first to Mumbai and then to Odisha as the investigation progressed.
This kind of cross-state legwork has become central to Indian cybercrime enforcement, since fraudulently obtained money is typically routed through a chain of mule accounts within hours of being received. Officials across several states have said that when a complaint is filed within six hours of a fraudulent transfer, banks and police working together can often block a significant share of the stolen amount before it disappears further into the network. Nationally, coordinated action between the 1930 helpline and partner banks has helped recover a meaningful, if still partial, share of overall reported losses in recent years.
Reporting Speed as the Deciding Factor
Kamat’s case stands out precisely because full recovery of a sum this large remains uncommon. Cyber police in Darbhanga have pointed to prompt reporting as the single factor that made the outcome possible, since delays typically allow fraudulent funds to be layered across multiple accounts, making them far harder to trace or freeze.
Authorities have reiterated a now-familiar warning that no genuine police officer, cybercrime official, bank representative or government agency will ever demand a money transfer over a phone or video call in the course of an investigation. Anyone contacted in this manner has been advised to end communication immediately and report the matter without delay by calling 1930 or visiting the nearest cyber crime police station, rather than waiting out of confusion, fear or embarrassment.
Investigators in the this case say they continue examining the financial trail to identify those behind the fraud, even as the recovered funds have already been restored to his account.",656,,True,False
89.0,Who Is Rishu Shree? Bihar Contractor at the Centre of Tender Scam That Led to Suspension of Two IAS Officers - NewsGram,NewsGram,"Wed, 03 Jun 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://www.newsgram.com/corruption-files/2026/06/04/who-is-rishi-shree-bihar-contractor-tender-scam,success_decoder,success,Who Is Rishu Shree? Bihar Contractor at the Centre of Tender Scam That Led to Suspension of Two IAS Officers,Varsha Pant,2026-06-03,"Corruption Files Who Is Rishu Shree? Bihar Contractor at the Centre of Tender Scam That Led to Suspension of Two IAS Officers
Rishu Shree, the Bihar contractor accused in a massive tender scam that led to the suspension of two IAS officers. 
