146.0,"AI-Enabled Deep Fake Fraud Hits India: This Kerala Man Loses Rs 40,000 Due To A Fake Call From Fraudsters - Trak.in",Trak.in,"Sat, 22 Jul 2023 07:00:00 GMT",https://trak.in/stories/ai-enabled-deep-fake-fraud-hits-india-this-kerala-man-loses-rs-40000-due-to-a-fake-call-from-fraudsters/,success_decoder,success,"AI-Enabled Deep Fake Fraud Hits India: This Kerala Man Loses Rs 40,000 Due To A Fake Call From Fraudsters - Trak.in - Indian Business of Tech, Mobile & Startups",Rohit Kulkarni,2023-07-22,"At approximately 5:45 am on July 9, PS Radhakrishnan, residing in Kozhikode, Kerala, received an initial call from an unknown number, which he chose to ignore. Shortly after, when he checked his WhatsApp, he discovered a series of messages from the same number, claiming to be his former colleague from Coal India Ltd. Radhakrishnan acknowledged their history of working together for nearly forty years and recognized the displayed photo as his colleague. They engaged in a text conversation, discussing Radhakrishnan’s daughter, her workplace, and sharing family photographs and inquiries about common colleagues.
The Deceptive Call: A Tale of Trust, Scams, and Deep-Fakes
Following that, the person voice-called Radhakrishnan through WhatsApp, asserting that they were presently at Dubai airport, awaiting a flight to India. They explained that they had called early in the morning to request a financial favor. The caller’s sister-in-law was undergoing emergency surgery at a hospital in Mumbai, and an advance payment of ₹40,000 was urgently required. They instructed Radhakrishnan to transfer the money through UPI to a phone belonging to someone accompanying the sister-in-law at the Mumbai hospital.
Despite the caller resembling his former colleague, Radhakrishnan expressed concerns about online scams and the risks associated with transferring a substantial sum to an unknown account. In response, the person promptly suggested a video call to address his doubts.
“A few seconds later, he called, and he looked exactly like my former colleague. Although only his face was visible, it was unmistakable. His lips and eyes moved naturally as we conversed in English. The call lasted only 25 seconds before it abruptly ended. He later called back via voice call, emphasizing the urgency for the money. Without further questioning, I transferred the funds,” recounted Radhakrishnan.
Minutes later, when the same individual contacted him again, requesting additional funds for hospital expenses, Radhakrishnan began to doubt the authenticity of the situation for the first time. He responded by falsely claiming that his account did not have sufficient balance.
Unveiling a Sophisticated Scam Network
After becoming suspicious, Radhakrishnan contacted his saved number for his former colleague, who denied making any calls. It dawned on Radhakrishnan that he had fallen victim to a scammer who, according to the police, likely employed advanced deep-fake technology to mimic the appearance and voice of his former colleague during the video call. Moreover, the scammer possessed personal information about Radhakrishnan’s life.
The septuagenarian promptly filed a complaint with the National Cyber Cell, which was later transferred to the Kerala police. The police acted swiftly and traced the money to an account in RBL Bank in Maharashtra, subsequently freezing the account.
Radhakrishnan noted that his case was not an isolated incident. When he reached out to his former colleagues at Coal India, some of them revealed receiving similar messages and calls, even under Radhakrishnan’s name, soliciting financial aid. However, they did not entertain these requests.
Deputy Commissioner of Police, Kozhikode, KE Byju, informed reporters that this scam was not an isolated occurrence but the work of a group. The traced number led to Gujarat, although it remains uncertain if the perpetrators are based there. In addition to Radhakrishnan’s money, there are additional funds in the account that were presumably involved in the scam.
The police have filed a case and will submit an investigation report to the relevant banks to initiate the process of recovering the lost funds. The police officer stated that this is the first time they have encountered a cyberfraud utilizing deep-fake technology.",578,,True,False
149.0,"Maharashtra Logs 9,922 Cyber Crime Cases in 2024 as Fraud Tops List - The420.in",The420.in,"Thu, 14 May 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://the420.in/maharashtra-cyber-crime-2024-online-fraud-ncrb-data/,success_decoder,success,"Maharashtra Logs 9,922 Cyber Crime Cases in 2024 as Fraud Tops List",The,2026-05-14,"Mumbai: Rapid growth in digital payments, social media usage and online services has pushed cyber crime in Maharashtra to an alarming level. The 2024 statistics released by the National Crime Records Bureau (NCRB) have painted a disturbing picture of rising online fraud, cyber exploitation and digital blackmailing across the state. According to the report, Maharashtra registered a total of 9,922 cyber crime cases in 2024, with online financial fraud accounting for the largest share of offences.
Online Fraud Dominates Cyber Crime Cases
The data showed that out of the total cyber crime cases registered in the state, 6,198 were committed with the intention of fraud. Apart from this, 544 cases were linked to sexual exploitation, while 68 were related to extortion, 64 to prank-related offences and 53 cases were driven by emotional motives such as anger or personal disputes. Investigators said social engineering tactics, fake investment platforms, digital arrest scams, OTP fraud and fake KYC schemes were among the most commonly used methods by cyber criminals.
The NCRB figures also indicated that cyber crime networks are becoming increasingly organised and technologically sophisticated. During 2024, Maharashtra police arrested a total of 2,526 individuals in cyber crime cases, including 51 women. Investigations revealed that several gangs were using fake bank accounts, rented SIM cards, cryptocurrency transactions and social media platforms to conceal money trails and avoid detection.
FCRF Academy Launches Premier Anti-Money Laundering Certification Program
Women and Children Face Rising Digital Risks
Cyber crimes targeting women also witnessed a worrying rise during the year. According to NCRB data, Maharashtra police registered 2,496 cyber crime cases against women in 2024. Among these, 410 cases were related to cyber stalking and cyber bullying. Another 55 cases involved cyber pornography, hosting or publishing obscene sexual content, while 21 cases were linked to online blackmailing and threats.
Cyber security experts say social media platforms and messaging applications are increasingly being used to target women. In several cases, accused individuals allegedly created fake online identities, gained victims’ trust and later used morphed photographs, video call recordings or private chats for extortion and harassment.
The situation involving cyber crimes against children has also emerged as a major concern. NCRB statistics showed that Maharashtra registered 161 cyber crime cases involving children in 2024. Out of these, 49 cases were related to cyber stalking and bullying, while 42 involved online circulation of obscene material depicting children. Five additional cases involved online blackmail, threats and harassment targeting minors.
Renowned cyber crime expert and former IPS officer Prof. Triveni Singh said cyber criminals are now using psychological manipulation and social engineering techniques more aggressively than ever before. According to him, women and children have become the most vulnerable targets on digital platforms, making cyber awareness and online safety education extremely important for families.
Mumbai Among Worst Affected Metro Cities
Among metropolitan cities, Bengaluru recorded the highest number of cyber crime cases in the country with 17,561 cases in 2024. Mumbai ranked second with 4,939 registered cases, while Hyderabad stood third with 4,009 cases.
Online fraud remained the biggest category of cyber crime in Mumbai as well. Out of the city’s 4,939 cases, as many as 3,831 were related to fraud. Additionally, 214 cases involved sexual exploitation, 53 were prank-related, while 34 each were linked to extortion and emotional motives.
Experts believe cyber threats involving AI-generated scams, deepfake videos, fake investment applications and digital identity theft could rise sharply in the coming years. They warn that stronger cyber security infrastructure, faster complaint redressal systems and widespread digital literacy campaigns will be crucial in tackling the rapidly evolving threat landscape.",596,,True,False
150.0,"HDFC And Airtel Payments Bank Reported Most Fraud Cases In FY 2024-25, Govt Reveals - MediaNama",MediaNama,"Wed, 23 Jul 2025 07:00:00 GMT",https://www.medianama.com/2025/07/223-hdfc-airtel-payments-bank-rmost-fraud-cases-fy-2024-25/,success_decoder,success,HDFC And Airtel Payments Bank Reported Most Frauds In 2024-25,Prabhanu Kumar Das,2025-07-23,"Minister of State (MoS) for Finance Pankaj Chaudhary revealed bank and state-wise data on total instances of financial fraud, losses incurred, and the amount recovered, in a written reply to a question in the Lok Sabha on July 21, 2025. Indian National Congress (INC) Member of Parliament (MP) Anto Antony, and Bharatiya Janta Party (BJP) MP Baijayant Panda had raised the query.
Both these lawmakers asked:
(a) whether the Government has any data regarding the number of cyber fraud and
financial fraud cases reported in the country
(b) the details of the total number of such cases and quantum of money lost and
recovered in the country during the last five years, year-wise, bank-wise and State-wise
including Odisha
(c) The number of financial/cyber crime complaints reported and lodged from Odisha in
2024 compared to those in 2023
(d) And the steps taken by the Government to protect account holders from cyber frauds
The Lok Sabha response highlights several initiatives and measures taken by the government to combat cyber fraud in the digital lending sector. Mulehunter AI, which aids in the identification of money mules, as well as awareness campaigns were mentioned, along with the National Payments Corporation of India (NPCI) providing AI fraud monitoring systems for banks, among others.
The answer also pointed out that ‘police’ and ‘public order’ are state subjects. Therefore states and union territories (UTs) are “primarily responsible for the prevention, detection, investigation and prosecution of crimes including cyber crime through their Law Enforcement Agencies (LEAs)”.
Meanwhile, “The Central Government supplements the initiatives of the States/UTs through advisories and financial assistance under various schemes for capacity building of their LEAs”.
MediaNamaâs Take
Indiaâs mounting bank fraud cases reveal a deeper rot in how we define digital trust. Despite rising fintech adoption, fraud reporting remains fragmented, opaque, and customer-unfriendly. Regulatory bodies publish aggregate numbers, but thereâs no granular public data on where fraud hits hardest, how quickly victims are reimbursed, or how effective preventive tech actually is.
This opacity fosters both consumer helplessness and institutional complacency. Fraud isnât just a security issue, itâs a transparency issue. The time is ripe for a central, public-facing fraud registry that discloses resolution timelines, successful redressal rates, and anonymised modus operandi patterns.
Without such infrastructure, innovations like AI-led fraud detection or UPI 2.0 risk become optics, solutions built on shaky public confidence. The next step in digital banking reform isnât just better tech, itâs structural accountability through transparent, accessible fraud governance. Only then can trust be earned, not assumed.
What does the bank-wise data say?
The total loss for FY 2024-25 stood at Rs 101.82 crore. This is less than FY 2023-24, which saw record losses at Rs 189.96 crore. The gap in the amount recovered is a cause of concern here. For context, Rs 48.37 crore was recovered in FY 2024-25, while Rs 151.85 crore was recovered in FY 2023-24.
ICICI Bank, Axis Bank, and Nainital Bank recorded the highest losses in FY 2024-25 at Rs 31.56 crore, Rs 17.37 crore, and Rs 16.02 crore respectively (add figures for these banks). Nainital Bank lost the above amount in a server hacking case between June 16 and June 20 this year.
HDFC Bank faced the highest instances of fraud with 35,074 cases resulting in a Rs. 3.43 crore loss incurred in FY24-25. Airtel Payments Bank recorded 19,697 cases resulting in a Rs 24 lakh loss, followed by Axis Bank with a total of 17,325 cases.
Which states have seen the most loss?
Banks in Maharashtra reported the highest fraud losses, at Rs 27.44 crore. Uttar Pradesh (UP) followed, with banks there losing Rs 19.08 crore, and Haryana saw losses worth Rs 10.26 crore. Maharashtra’s capital Mumbai is the financial nerve centre of India, indicating why it is in the first position for amount lost to cybercrimes. It is followed by the most populous state UP. Haryana has also grown significantly as an economic hub with a high per capita income.
Banks in Maharashtra recovered a substantial Rs 25.12 crore, while those in Haryana and UP only managed to recover Rs 64 lakh and Rs 39 lakh respectively.
Tamil Nadu witnessed the highest quantum of cases in the last financial year, with 37,497 cases leading to a loss of Rs 5.54 crore. Maharashtra and Haryana followed with 31,658 cases and 23,861 cases respectively.
Cases Of Reported Recoveries Being Higher Than Reported Losses
MediaNama noted 27 instances where the reported total amount recovered was higher than the total amount lost in FY 2023-24 and FY 2024-25 for banks. Here are a few examples of the largest deficits:
- HDFC Bank reported a Rs 3.43 crore loss, but recovered Rs 12.08 crore in FY 24-25. They also reported a Rs 6.3 crore loss while recovering Rs 41.58 crore in FY 23-24.
- HSBC Bank reported Rs 16.76 crore recovered as compared to Rs 1.53 crore lost in FY 24-25.
- Union Bank of India reported Rs 4.07 crore lost as compared to a total recovery of Rs 71.90 crore in 2023-24.
- There were also cases where banks with no money loss to frauds in a fiscal year reported recoveries. Some examples of this are the Bank of Maharashtra, DCB Bank Limited, Indusind Bank Limited, among others, in FY 24-25.
Why This Matters
As digital payments continue to grow across India, the latest data from the Ministry of Finance highlights ongoing challenges in preventing financial fraud. In FY 2024â25, banks lost Rs 101.82 crore to fraud, while they recovered only Rs 48.37 crore. ICICI Bank, Axis Bank, and Nainital Bank saw the highest losses, while such incidents were concentrated in states like Maharashtra, UP, and Haryana.
While the RBI and the NPCI have introduced AI-based tools and issued security guidelines, the gap between losses and recoveries points to the need for further improvements in fraud response mechanisms.
The data also shows that some banks reported higher recoveries than losses, suggesting that recoveries from previous years may skew year-wise figures. As digital transactions become more widespread, the ability of banks and regulators to respond to cyber fraud will remain a key area of concern.
Questions for NPCI
MediaNama reached out to NPCI with the following questions:
- How is NPCI measuring the effectiveness of its AI-based fraud detection systems deployed across banks? Are there internal audits or performance benchmarks you can share?
- Given the consistent rise in fraud incidents and low recovery rates, does NPCI have a plan to create a centralised fraud response protocol across all member banks?
- Is there a mandatory compliance timeline for banks to implement NPCIâs latest fraud prevention tools or updates (such as MuleHunter AI)? What happens if a bank delays adoption?
- Has NPCI conducted any independent or third-party evaluation of fraud prevention tools like MuleHunter AI? If so, what were the findings?
- With multiple banks reporting recoveries higher than losses in the same fiscal year, has NPCI issued any clarification or guidance on standardising reporting formats?
Questions For Banks
MediaNama reached out to various banks with the following questions:
- Your bank was among the top three in terms of total financial losses from digital frauds in FY24-25. What were the primary modes of fraud, and how have you addressed vulnerabilities in those areas? (ICICI Bank, Axis Bank, Nainital Bank)
- What proportion of the reported losses in FY24-25 were ultimately borne by the customers versus the bank? (ICICI Bank, Axis Bank, Nainital Bank, State Bank of India, and American Express Bank Corporation)
- How has your fraud detection strategy evolved since the RBIâs 2021 and 2024 directions on digital security? Are you fully compliant with the latest RBI/NPCI mandates? (ICICI Bank, Axis Bank, HDFC Bank, Airtel Payments Bank, State Bank of India, and American Express Bank Corporation)
- What consumer awareness and grievance redressal mechanisms does your bank have in place, especially in high-fraud states like Maharashtra, UP, Haryana, and Tamil Nadu? (ICICI Bank, Axis Bank, HDFC Bank, Airtel Payments Bank, State Bank of India, and American Express Bank Corporation)
- In FY 2024-25, your bank reported recoveries significantly higher than reported losses. Could you explain the methodology used for calculating recoveries? Do they include amounts from previous years? (HDFC Bank, HSBC Bank, Union Bank of India)
- Are your fraud recovery units integrated with law enforcement or specialised cybercrime cells to enable such high recovery rates? If so, how does this differ from practices at other banks? (HDFC Bank, HSBC Bank, Union Bank of India)
- Over Rs 71 crore was recovered in FY23-24 despite only Rs 4.07 crore in reported losses. What accounts for this difference? (Union Bank of India)
