179.0,"Cybercrimes account for 1 in 4r FIRs in Telangana; fraudsters swindle ₹1,524 Cr in 2025 - NewsMeter",NewsMeter,"Fri, 17 Jul 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://newsmeter.in/nation/cybercrimes-account-for-1-in-4r-firs-in-telangana-fraudsters-swindle-1524-cr-in-2025-771859,success_decoder,success,"Cybercrimes account for 1 in 4r FIRs in Telangana; fraudsters swindle ₹1,524 Cr in 2025",Newsmeter Network,2026-07-17,"Cybercrimes account for 1 in 4r FIRs in Telangana; fraudsters swindle ₹1,524 Cr in 2025
According to officials, Telangana registered 21,639 cybercrime FIRs out of 49,620 cases reported nationwide in 2025, accounting for nearly 44% of all cybercrime FIRs in the country
By Newsmeter Network
Representational Image
Hyderabad: Cybercrimes account for one in every four major FIRs registered in Telangana. Last year, cyber fraudsters swindled ₹1,524 crore from people in Telangana
Warning that cybercrime is emerging as the biggest policing challenge, Director General of Police (DGP) C.V. Anand said families are losing their lifetime savings and called for greater use of Artificial Intelligence (AI) and advanced technology to curb the growing menace.
DGP reviews Cyber Security Bureau
The DGP conducted a comprehensive review of the Telangana Cyber Security Bureau (TGCSB), assessing its efforts to curb cybercrime, strengthen technological capabilities and improve victim services.
He visited the Cyber Lab, Security Operations Centre (SOC), Central Monitoring Unit, Social Media Unit, Child Protection Unit and Digital Forensic Unit to review their functioning and investigative capabilities.
TGCSB Director Shikha Goel and senior officials briefed the DGP on emerging cybercrime trends, AI-driven investigations, victim support systems and the bureau's future roadmap through a detailed presentation.
AI-powered call centre earns praise
One of the key highlights of the review was the AI-based cybercrime call centre. During a demonstration, the AI system promptly responded to a mock victim reporting financial fraud, collected essential details, identified the nearest police station and automatically relayed the information to the concerned authorities.
Impressed by the system's efficiency, the DGP directed officials to expand AI-enabled services to ensure faster assistance for cybercrime victims across the state.
Cyber losses decline, recovery improves
Officials informed the DGP that Telangana recorded cyber fraud losses of ₹1,524 crore in 2025. However, financial losses declined by 20% compared to the previous year, while cyber complaints dropped by 3% to 91,369.
The amount placed on hold (POH) in fraudsters' bank accounts increased from ₹255 crore to ₹279 crore, raising the recovery protection rate from 9% to 12%, reflecting improved efforts to safeguard victims' money.
Telangana leads in cybercrime enforcement
According to officials, Telangana registered 21,639 cybercrime FIRs out of 49,620 cases reported nationwide in 2025, accounting for nearly 44% of all cybercrime FIRs in the country. The high number reflects the state's proactive registration and investigation of cybercrime cases.
The state also recorded an FIR conversion rate of 78% in cases involving losses above ₹75 lakh and 56% in cases involving losses exceeding ₹1 lakh, highlighting its strong enforcement and investigation mechanisms.
One in four major FIRs is a cybercrime case
Cybercrime has emerged as one of Telangana's biggest law enforcement challenges. Of the 86,177 major FIRs registered in the state in 2025, 21,639 were related to cyber offences, meaning one in every four major crimes involved cyber fraud.
While all other major crimes together caused financial losses of around ₹207 crore, cybercrimes alone resulted in losses of ₹1,524 crore—nearly seven times higher.
'Cybercrime is the biggest future challenge'
Addressing officials, DGP C.V. Anand said cybercrime is rapidly evolving and warned that failure to tackle investment frauds, online trading scams and digital financial frauds could lead to even greater challenges in the future.
""Many families are being financially devastated by cyber fraud and are losing their lifetime savings in a matter of minutes. Future crime trends will be dominated by cyber offences. Every police officer must strengthen technical expertise and be equipped to effectively investigate cybercrimes,"" he said.
The DGP urged officials to leverage AI, data analytics and other advanced technologies to keep pace with evolving cyber threats and make Telangana a national model in technology-driven policing.
Public advisory
The Telangana Police urged citizens to immediately call the 1930 cybercrime helpline or lodge complaints through the National Cyber Crime Reporting Portal if they become victims of online fraud.
People were also advised never to share OTPs, passwords, bank account details or other financial information with unknown persons.",659,,True,False
180.0,Supreme Court hands CBI full control of nationwide ‘digital arrest’ scam probe - Telangana Today,Telangana Today,"Mon, 01 Dec 2025 08:00:00 GMT",https://telanganatoday.com/supreme-court-hands-cbi-full-control-of-nationwide-digital-arrest-scam-probe,success_decoder,success,Supreme Court hands CBI full control of nationwide ‘digital arrest’ scam probe,Telangana Today; IANS,2025-12-01,"Supreme Court hands CBI full control of nationwide ‘digital arrest’ scam probe
                The Supreme Court has transferred the nationwide ‘digital arrest’ scam investigation to the CBI, directing it to probe all related cases. The court sought assistance from RBI and DoT on fraud detection, SIM regulation and AI-based monitoring while urging swift cybercrime infrastructure upgrades
                
                                        
                    Published Date - 1 December 2025, 03:11 PM 
                 
                                    
                                                
                
                            
New Delhi: The Supreme Court on Monday handed over the investigation into the nationwide ‘digital arrest’ scam to the Central Bureau of Investigation (CBI), granting the agency sweeping powers to probe what it described as a crime of alarming scale and sophistication.
A Bench led by Chief Justice of India (CJI) Surya Kant said the CBI would be the first and primary agency to investigate all cases related to the scam, which involves fraudsters impersonating officials, coercing victims — often senior citizens — into digitally staged “arrests,” and extorting money.
                            
                                            
                
                
    
In a significant move, the court empowered the CBI under the Prevention of Corruption Act (PCA) to examine the complicity of bankers who facilitated the opening of accounts used in these frauds.
It also issued a notice to the Reserve Bank of India (RBI), making it a party to the suo motu proceedings. The Bench asked the RBI to assist in determining timelines for deploying Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning tools to detect suspicious bank accounts and automatically freeze proceeds of crime.
The court further directed that all authorities must comply with CBI requests under the IT Intermediary Rules, 2021. It said the States that have not granted general consent to the CBI must permit the agency to investigate IT Act cases in their jurisdiction so that nationwide operations are not hindered.
The CBI may seek assistance from Interpol wherever required, it further said. The top court directed the Department of Telecommunications (DoT) to submit a proposal on regulating the issuance of SIM cards and multiple SIMs under a single identity to prevent misuse.
The apex court said the State governments must expedite the setting up of cybercrime centres and report any bottlenecks to the court. “Authorities must preserve mobile phone data from all devices seized in cybercrime-related FIRs. All States and Union Territories must hand over FIRs registered under the IT Act, 2021, related to the digital arrest scam, to the CBI,” the court said.
The Bench noted that after the court took suo motu cognisance of the matter, several victims came forward seeking action, and numerous FIRs were filed across states, underscoring the severity of the scam.
CJI Surya Kant observed, “The gravity and scale of the crime can be gauged from the fact that in most states, senior citizens have been targeted by fraudsters using various methods. Every cybercrime case, especially those involving senior citizens, needs a thorough investigation. But the digital arrest scam demands immediate attention from the country’s premier investigating agency.”
The matter will be heard next in two weeks.",488,,True,False
181.0,"Multi-State Cyber Fraud Network Busted In Hyderabad, 19 Arrested In A Week - NDTV",NDTV,"Thu, 02 Apr 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://www.ndtv.com/india-news/multi-state-cyber-fraud-network-busted-in-hyderabad-19-arrested-in-a-week-11302518,success_decoder,success,"Multi-State Cyber Fraud Network Busted In Hyderabad, 19 Arrested In A Week",Ashish Kumar Pandey,2026-04-02,"In one of the biggest crackdowns this year, Cyberabad Cyber Crime Police in Hyderabad detected 17 cybercrime cases and arrested 19 people from different states between March 25 and March 31, uncovering an organised multi-state network behind online trading, digital arrest and fake job scams.
11 of the 17 cases cracked in the week were trading frauds, while two cases each related to digital arrest scams, part-time job fraud and job fraud.
""12 of the 19 accused arrested in the week were involved in trading frauds alone, making it the biggest category of cybercrime detected during the period. The remaining arrests were linked to digital arrest fraud, fake part-time job scams and bogus employment offers,"" said T Sai Manohar, Deputy Commissioner of Police, Cyber Crimes, Cyberabad.
Investigators said the gang members operated from multiple states using WhatsApp groups, fake websites, mule bank accounts and mobile numbers obtained with forged documents.
""Most of them were part of organised networks targeting people through fake trading platforms and social media groups,"" said the Deputy Commissioner of Police.
The most significant breakthrough came in a Rs 2.93-crore trading fraud case in which a Hyderabad resident was cheated through a fake WhatsApp investment group. Victim was added to a WhatsApp group where fraudsters posed as market experts and financial advisers. To gain his trust fraudsters initially allowed the victim to withdraw a small amount and once he started investing bigger amounts, the cyber criminals trapped him.
'""Believing the investment was genuine, the victim transferred Rs 2.93 crore in phases to several bank accounts linked to the gang. When he later tried to withdraw the money, the fraudsters blocked his access and demanded additional payments,"" investigators said.
Cybercrime police tracked the money trail and arrested two men from Telangana - Shekar and Tinesh Kumar Parjapathi - for suppllying bank accounts to the gang and received commissions for routing the money.
""These accused were not innocent account holders. They knowingly provided mule bank accounts to move the fraud amount and helped conceal the identity of the main masterminds,"" DCP Sai Manohar said.
Police said the investigation revealed a wider network spread across several states, with cybercriminals using multiple accounts and digital wallets to make tracing difficult.
Apart from the arrests, Cyberabad Cyber Crime Police also secured 884 refund orders from courts in 125 cases during the week. A total of Rs 4.35 crore will now be returned to the victims.
