283.0,"CBI develops ABHAY -AI chatbot that counters fake notices scam, digital arrests - BW Police World",BW Police World,"Tue, 21 Apr 2026 07:43:23 GMT",https://www.bwpoliceworld.com/article/cbi-develops-abhay-app-that-counters-fake-notices-scam-digital-arrests-603294,success_decoder,success,"CBI develops ABHAY -AI chatbot that counters fake notices scam, digital arrests - BW Police World",BW Reporters,2026-08-30,"ABHAY, an AI based chat bot is the first technology developed in India that will verify the notices given by CBI (expected for other agencies too) to help citizens prvent themselves from digital arrest and fake notices
                        
                    New Delhi, April 21
Impersonation, fraudulent notices in the name of central agencies, digital arrests have scarred the citizens and damaged the economy in the country at a larger scale. ABHAY- an AI chat bot developed by Central Bureau of Investigation (CBI) will be a strong solution to fake notices and digital arrests using those fake notices. 
ABHAY is a the unique chatbot developed by CBI that will verify the authenticity of the notices given in the name of CBI. Accesible only through the CBI's official website and verify notices issued by CBI to prevent persons from falling in trap of scamsters sending fake notices. As it is available only on the official website of CBI, the scamsters will also not find a loophole to develop fake platforms in the name of ABHAY and scam victims in any form.
Director CBI Praveen Sood said, “ABHAY is a powerful tool developed by CBI to tackle fake notices and digital arrests. We are going to start issuing all notices of CBI whether to the victims or accused with QR codes starting May 1. These QR codes could be scanned on the ABHAY app to verify its authenticity. The app will be the biggest tool to prevent citizens from being trapped by scamsters”. 
The app that is an AI powered is available on the website of CBI. The user has to click on the app and verify the summons/notice given by the agency. If the notice is genuine, the helpbot will show the authenticity or flag it if it’s fake. 
To understand the functionality of ABHAY, BW POLICEWORLD checked the functioning of the chatbot. 
To begin with, when a user clicks on the app, they are directed to the ABHAY page for the process. The app says, 
Welcome to ABHAY, the notice verification chatbot of CBI. Be aware that there is no such legal provision as 'digital arrest'. If anybody is claiming that you are under 'digital arrest', it is a scam. Report immediately to www.cybercrime.gov.in or call 1930. Do not transfer any money to any accounts under the guise of bail or verification of funds. 
To proceed, please enter your name and phone number. 
After the name and number was given, an OTP was generated which takes the user to next level where ABHAY asks to upload the notice. 
It says, “Please upload the document you wish to verify. Keep the following guidelines in mind when uploading: 1. Scan the notice in good light and in portrait orientation. 2. Ensure that no part of the notice is obscured or covered. 3. Ensure the notice is kept on a surface with a plain background and not on other printed material like newspaper, etc. 4. File formats allowed: .jpg/.jpeg/.pdf 5. If the notice contains multiple pages, scan them as a PDF & upload”. 
The app then verifies the notices and shows it’s authenticity. 
In the scam of fake notices, the fraudsters use the names, designations, and even forged signatures of senior CBI officers, including the Director. The notices are created like an original format that they carry the official logo or stamps of other agencies like CBI, ED, Intelligence Bureau (IB) and more. 
These notices are generally issued for crimes of serious nature like child pornography, narcotics, or illegal foreign fund transfers. These fake summons or warrants are primarily circulated via WhatsApp, emails, or instant messaging platforms. The scammers then demand money for settling these fake cases. 
Under digital arrest, the fraudsters pose as officials of agencies like CBI, ED and others are trap citizens stating that they are under “digital” arrest. They show notices with logos and stamps and accuse of them of committing serious criminal offences. They also pose as lawyers later to win over the victims and prove that the arrest is real. 
The scamsters then start extracting money from the victims in the name of settlements and processes. By the time the victim understands that the scamsters looted him or her, they would have already lost lakhs to them. 
In India, between 2022 and early 2026, over 2.41 lakh complaints were registered regarding digital arrest scams, with losses estimated at Rs. 30,000 crores. Many scams are orchestrated by transnational syndicates operating from Cambodia, Laos, and Myanmar. 
The Supreme Court of India has described these frauds as ""absolute robbery"" and a crime against human dignity. 
ABHAY app which appears to be promising solution for such frauds, is expected to be replicated across the country in various agencies and departments of the government to debunk the fake notices and digital arrest scams.",797,,True,False
284.0,Digital arrest scams: CBI to launch chatbot to help people verify notices issued to them - The Economic Times,The Economic Times,"Sun, 19 Apr 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://m.economictimes.com/news/india/digital-arrest-scams-cbi-to-launch-chatbot-to-help-people-verify-notices-issued-to-them/articleshow/130368182.cms,success_decoder,success,Digital arrest scams: CBI to launch chatbot to help people verify notices issued to them,SECTIONS Digital arrest scams CBI,2026-04-19,"New Delhi: To tackle the menace of digital arrest frauds, the Central Bureau of Investigation (CBI) will on Monday unveil an artificial intelligence-powered chatbot that will help people verify the authenticity of notices issued to them.
The chatbot, 'Abhay', will be launched by Chief Justice of India (CJI) Surya Kant on the occasion of the 22nd D P Kohli Memorial Lecture organised by the CBI in honour of its founding director, officials said.
The CJI will deliver the lecture on 'Challenges of Cyber Crime - Role for Police and Judiciary'. He will also confer 24 Medals for Meritorious Service.
""The AI-powered notice verification chatbot, ABHAY, will allow the public to verify the genuineness of a notice purportedly issued by the CBI. In the context of digital arrest frauds, this provides a much-required tool for the citizens,"" a CBI spokesperson said.
In a number of cases, it has emerged that scammers posing as CBI officials have coerced the public by showing fraudulent notices purportedly issued by the agency in non-existing cases, holding the victim hostage in the name of digital arrest and extorting crores of rupees in the process, the spokesperson said.
The Supreme Court had recently flagged that Rs 54,000 crore was siphoned off by cyber criminals in various scams and termed the menace ""robbery or dacoity"".
Organised since 2000, the D P Kohli Memorial Lecture has seen addresses by many eminent personalities.
""Born in 1907 in Uttar Pradesh, Kohli joined the police service in 1931 and went on to serve with distinction in multiple regions and the government of India. He led the Delhi Special Police Establishment (DSPE) from 1955 and became the founding director of CBI upon its establishment on 1st April 1963, serving until his retirement in 1968,"" the CBI said in a statement.
The lecture brings together eminent speakers from diverse fields to share insights on law enforcement, criminal justice and investigative challenges. It serves as a platform for dialogue and knowledge-sharing and advancing the understanding of modern policing and accountability, it said.
