321.0,When scammers dialled Haryana DGP and his wife hoping to trap them in digital arrest bids - The Tribune,The Tribune,"Sat, 21 Mar 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://www.tribuneindia.com/news/top-headlines/when-scammers-dialled-haryana-dgp-and-his-wife-hoping-to-trap-them-in-digital-arrest-bids/,success_decoder,success,When scammers dialled Haryana DGP and his wife hoping to trap them in digital arrest bids - The Tribune,Bhartesh Singh Thakur,2026-03-21,"When scammers dialled Haryana DGP and his wife hoping to trap them in digital arrest bids
From personal scare to public safety tool, DGP unveils anti-scam app ‘Abhedya’
Haryana Police DGP Ajay Singhal on Saturday launched ‘Abhedya’ app, the first of its kind in India, to block suspicious threat calls, extortion messages, stalking, and harmful digital content before they reach the user.
Singhal narrowly avoided becoming a victim of a digital arrest scam. Recently, he received a call displaying the picture of a police inspector. When he answered, the caller told him that there was an arrest warrant against him. However, he soon realised that the call was originating from Pakistan, as the number began with the +92 country code.
“When I pointed out that the call was from Pakistan, the caller immediately disconnected, but not before hurling expletives that I cannot repeat,” said Singhal during a press briefing.
He also shared that his wife received a similar call. “She brought the phone to me, saying that a ‘police officer is calling repeatedly and it must be for you’. I told her not to answer the call, as it was from Pakistan.”
The app launch comes at a time when the state recorded 178 extortion calls in 2024 and 107 in 2025.
Also Read: Haryana Police launch 'Abhedya' app to block extortion calls, threat messages
The app monitors incoming WhatsApp calls and messages from unknown numbers. It checks whether the number is an unsaved international number (genuine or virtual) or a virtual Indian number. If found suspicious, the system immediately rejects the call, blocks the number, and removes the notification, voice note or chat from the user’s phone.",278,,True,False
324.0,"Fraudsters use AI to create fake websites of 126 religious sites, dupe 21,985 people - India Today",India Today,"Thu, 13 Aug 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://www.indiatoday.in/india/story/gujarat-cops-bust-interstate-cyber-fraud-gang-behind-126-fake-religious-websites-over-21000-people-duped-2970818-2026-08-13,success_decoder,success,"Fraudsters use AI to create fake websites of 126 religious sites, dupe 21,985 people",Brijesh Doshi,2026-08-13,"Fraudsters use AI to create fake websites of 126 religious sites, dupe 21,985 people
Police said an interstate gang created fake booking websites for religious destinations and cheated devotees through advance payments. Investigators say the probe has widened across states as they trace domains, funds and other possible links.
Fraudsters allegedly used Artificial Intelligence to create 126 fake websites of religious sites, hotels, dharamshalas and ashrams across 11 states, duping nearly 22,000 people across the country, the Gujarat Cyber Center of Excellence said. The interstate racket, which allegedly targeted devotees looking for online bookings, puja and VIP darshan services, has led to the arrest of two people.
The accused allegedly created 126 fake websites across 11 states, including around 40-41 portals targeting religious destinations in Gujarat alone. Police said the gang used these websites to offer online bookings for rooms, puja, VIP darshan and other services, collecting advance payments from unsuspecting devotees.
An investigation by the Gujarat Cyber Center of Excellence found that around 21,985 people across the country were allegedly cheated by the accused, with the fraud running into crores of rupees.
The arrested accused have been identified as Vicky Gupta, a B Tech engineer from Delhi who is alleged to be the mastermind, and Shailesh Saini, a resident of Bharatpur, Rajasthan. Police said Gupta operated the network from his home, while Saini allegedly assisted with financial transactions and other technical aspects of the operation.
AI USED TO MAKE FAKE WEBSITES LOOK GENUINE
According to investigators, the accused used their technical expertise and Artificial Intelligence (AI)-generated images to make the fake portals resemble genuine websites of hotels, dharamshalas and ashrams.
The websites appeared when devotees searched online for services such as room bookings, online puja, VIP darshan and accommodation near famous religious sites. Once a victim contacted the operators, they were asked to make advance payments. The fraud came to light when devotees reached the destination and found that their bookings did not exist.
Police said the gang had been operating the racket for around eight months and frequently changed the domain names of the websites to evade detection by cyber agencies.
GUJARAT RELIGIOUS SITES AMONG BIGGEST TARGETS
Investigators found that devotees visiting religious destinations in Gujarat were among the major targets. Around 40 fake portals were allegedly created in the name of prominent religious sites in the state.
The fake websites included multiple portals targeting Salangpur Kashtabhanjan Dev, along with websites created in the names of Somnath at Prabhas Patan, Dwarka Jagat Mandir, Palitana Jain shrine, Girnar/Ambaji in Junagadh and Akshardham in Gandhinagar.
The gang also allegedly created fake websites targeting devotees visiting major religious destinations such as Tirupati Devasthanam, Tirumala, Katra, Shirdi, Jagannath Puri, Vrindavan, Rishikesh and Haridwar.
OVER 70 CYBER FRAUD COMPLAINTS FILED
Police said around 70 cyber fraud complaints linked to the racket have been registered across the country so far. Dr Rajdeepsinh Jhala, Superintendent of Police, Cyber Center of Excellence, said the investigation has now expanded beyond Gujarat. Two special police teams have been deployed outside the state to trace the people who purchased the 126 domains and created the websites.
Investigators are also probing the source of funds used to operate the network and whether more people were involved in the interstate racket.
Police are now working to identify additional victims and determine the total amount siphoned off through the fake booking portals.",560,,True,False
326.0,"Dhubri’s ₹200 Cr crypto scam traced to Dubai; two held, more arrests likely - The Assam Tribune",The Assam Tribune,"Wed, 05 Mar 2025 08:00:00 GMT",https://assamtribune.com/assam/dhubris-200-cr-crypto-scam-traced-to-dubai-two-held-more-arrests-likely-1570175,success_decoder,success,"Dhubri’s ₹200 Cr crypto scam traced to Dubai; two held, more arrests likely",The Assam Tribune,2025-03-05,"Dhubri’s ₹200 Cr crypto scam traced to Dubai; two held, more arrests likely
Police uncover ties between arrested suspects and Dubai-based Lavish Chaudhury, accused in a ₹500-crore Himachal crypto scam
Dhubri, March 5: In a major crackdown, the South Salmara-Mankachar Police have uncovered international links to a massive cryptocurrency-based, multi-level marketing (MLM) scam, which defrauded thousands of investors across Assam.
The investigation led to the arrest of Akheruzzaman Ahmed (26) and Nur Alom Ahmed (27) from Tumni Simlakandi village in the early hours of March 4, following a General Diary Entry (GDE No. 21 dated 04.03.2025).
The duo allegedly lured investors with false promises of 5-6% monthly returns through AI-powered forex trading platforms under names like Bot-Bro, Algo-Bot, and Bot-Alpha.
During the operation, police discovered links between the arrested individuals and Lavish Chaudhury, a Dubai-based scamster who is already accused in a ₹500-crore cryptocurrency scam in Himachal Pradesh.
Evidence of their connection was found in a pass recovered from a crypto promotional event at Greenwood Resort, Guwahati, where Chaudhury allegedly hosted a meeting.
Initial investigations suggest the scam operated as a pyramid scheme, where investors were encouraged to onboard more people, creating an extensive network of over 1 lakh downline members.
An image of the seized items from the duo. (AT Photo)
The police also seized five mobile phones, three laptops, an ATM swipe machine, 11 ATM cards, five bank passbooks, and seven cheque books linked to multiple bank accounts, from their possession.
Police estimate the total fraud amount to be over ₹200 crore, with funds siphoned to foreign countries, including Dubai, via cryptocurrency transactions.
The accused allegedly operated multiple fraudulent websites hosted in the USA and Germany, which are now under investigation.
The scam came to light after a flood of complaints from investors who had stopped receiving promised returns in recent months.
This is the second major crypto-related scam busted by South Salmara Mankachar Police in just one week.
A similar MLM fraud was exposed two days ago, highlighting how such deceptive networks are rapidly spreading across lower Assam districts like Dhubri and South Salmara Mankachar.
Police believe that low awareness about cryptocurrency and the pull of quick profits are making economically weaker regions easy targets for such scams.
The Indian Cyber Crime Coordination Centre (I4C) under the Ministry of Home Affairs is assisting in the probe. Authorities are working to identify other key players, and further arrests are expected.
An FIR is being registered, and both arrested individuals will be produced before the court. The police have urged the public to stay alert and avoid falling prey to investment schemes that promise unrealistic returns.",437,,True,False
329.0,"7 AI-powered scams Indians are falling for in 2026: Deepfakes, fake HR calls and more - Livemint",Livemint,"Wed, 20 May 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://www.livemint.com/technology/7-ai-powered-scams-indians-are-falling-for-in-2026-deepfakes-fake-hr-calls-and-more-11779242136292.html,success_decoder,success,"7 AI-powered scams Indians are falling for in 2026: Deepfakes, fake HR calls and more | Mint",Tarunya Sanjay,2026-05-20,"Tarunya Sanjay
Updated20 May 2026, 08:27 AM IST
Artificial intelligence is rapidly making online fraud more sophisticated in India, with scammers now using cloned voices, deepfake videos, AI-generated emails and realistic chatbots to target users across job searches, banking, investing and social media. Unlike older scams that were easier to identify because of poor grammar or suspicious messages, AI-powered scams often sound polished, professional and emotionally convincing.
Here are seven AI-powered scams Indians are increasingly falling for right now.
Scammers are increasingly posing as recruiters from companies, and victims receive professional-looking interview calls, often followed by fake offer letters and onboarding emails. After multiple “screening rounds”, candidates are asked to pay registration, training, or laptop-delivery fees. In some cases, fraudsters also collect Aadhaar, PAN and bank details, which can later be misused for identity theft or financial fraud.
Fraudsters are cloning relatives' voices using short audio clips from platforms like Instagram, WhatsApp,and Facebook. Victims receive panic-inducing calls from what sounds like a son, sibling or parent claiming they are injured, stranded or in police custody and urgently need money.
Cybercriminals impersonating police officers, CBI officials or telecom regulators are threatening users over video calls and falsely accusing them of involvement in crimes such as money laundering or parcel fraud. Victims are told they are under “digital arrest” and must stay on continuous video surveillance while transferring money for “verification”. WhatsApp recently told the Supreme Court it banned more than 9,400 accounts linked to such scams, according to Moneycontrol.
AI-generated videos featuring personalities such as Elon Musk and Mukesh Ambani are being used to promote fake crypto platforms and trading schemes. The deepfake videos are edited to appear as if the celebrities are endorsing guaranteed-return investments. Users are often redirected from ads on YouTube or Facebook to fraudulent apps and websites.
Unlike older phishing emails filled with spelling mistakes, AI-generated scam emails now mimic corporate communication styles, HR departments and banking alerts. Some even use Indian workplace language and realistic formatting, making them harder to detect. Victims are tricked into clicking malicious links, downloading malware or sharing OTPs and login credentials.
Apps claiming to use “AI-powered investing” or “machine-learning stock prediction” are attracting users with promises of unusually high returns in stocks, forex and crypto. Many display fake dashboards, manipulated testimonials and fabricated profit screenshots before disappearing with user deposits or blocking withdrawals entirely.
Scammers are increasingly using AI chatbots, cloned voices, and spoofed caller IDs to impersonate customer support executives from companies such as Amazon, Flipkart, and State Bank of India. Victims searching online for customer care numbers often end up contacting fake helplines and are persuaded to install remote-access apps or reveal sensitive banking details.
