A shocking online fraud racket involving fake escort services, AI-generated photos, and duping high-profile customers has been busted in Jaipur. The gang, led by a 25-year-old graduate, ran the operation from a small rented room in Chandwaji and spread its network across five states.
Trap through WhatsApp messages
The scam came to light after an engineer from Uttar Pradesh reported the incident. Victims were lured with WhatsApp messages that read: “Congratulations! You have become our valuable customer after depositing ₹10,000. Photos of girls and rate cards have been sent. Let us know your preference – type and age of girl.”
To make the offer look genuine, the gang even assured customers that there would be no police trouble if they availed themselves of services at “known hotels.” Hundreds of people across India fell prey to this elaborate fraud.
AI-generated photos and fake ads
The mastermind, Vikas Yadav (25), and his team created photos of glamorous women using AI tools. They modified images sourced from Google and social media, tailoring them to the cultural preferences of different states.
These pictures were then used in advertisements on escort websites, with targets chosen from cities like Delhi, Lucknow, Agra, and Gwalior. Victims who responded were directed to WhatsApp numbers operated from Jaipur, where men and women posed as escorts to build trust.
Security deposit scam
To execute the fraud, customers were asked to pay a hefty “security deposit” of ₹15,000–20,000. The gang convinced them that the money would be refunded if they treated the girl well, but forfeited if they misbehaved.
After selecting a girl’s photo, victims were charged additional amounts ranging from ₹5,000 to ₹20,000 for services, hotels, or “safety kits.” QR codes were sent for payments. Once the fraudsters felt the customer had paid enough, they blocked their numbers.
Girls used to gain victims’ trust
To make the setup look authentic, Vikas employed young men and women, including one minor, to chat with customers. Girls in the gang would talk directly to clients over voice and video calls, coaxing them into paying more.
Police later found thousands of photos of young women saved in seized mobile phones.
Call centre model of fraud
The gang operated like a call centre. Six members were found working at the rented house during the raid — four men and two women. They were hired by Vikas Yadav on a salary of ₹10,000 per month. Many were unemployed youths preparing for competitive exams, lured into the scam as a side job.
Audio recordings revealed Vikas instructing his recruits to keep conversations sweet and engaging, urging them to involve “madam” to extract more money from clients.
Police raid and recovery
Acting on cyber cell inputs, Jaipur Rural Police raided Suresh Gurjar’s house near an SBI branch in Chandwaji on August 27. They arrested Vikas and five associates, recovering 13 mobile phones, 10 ATM cards, Aadhaar and PAN cards, and bank passbooks.
Police also found two diaries, one pink and one orange, detailing transactions worth ₹40 lakh from the racket.
Network across five states
Jaipur Police confirmed the gang’s operations were not limited to Rajasthan. Complaints against their numbers had already been registered on the cybercrime portal (1930) in Mumbai (Maharashtra), Ghazipur and Gautam Buddha Nagar (Uttar Pradesh), Bilaspur (Chhattisgarh), and Cyberabad (Telangana).
Case registered under IT Act
A case has been registered at Chandwaji police station under multiple sections, including 295/25, 319(2), 318(4), 336(3), 340(2), 61(2) BNS, and Section 66D of the Information Technology (Amendment) Act, 2008.
Cyber Cell Deputy SP Khalil Ahmad is investigating the case. Police say Vikas Yadav turned to online fraud after failing to secure a job, but quickly expanded operations as demand grew.
Rising threat of AI-driven cybercrime
This case highlights how fraudsters are increasingly misusing artificial intelligence to generate fake photos and lure victims. Officials fear the use of AI tools will make such scams harder to detect, urging people to avoid online escort services and report suspicious activity on the cybercrime helpline.
