399.0,"Four Men Arrested in Uttar Pradesh for Aadhaar Fraud, 1,500 Records Manipulated - MediaNama",MediaNama,"Thu, 10 Apr 2025 07:00:00 GMT",https://www.medianama.com/2025/04/223-aadhaar-fraud-uttar-pradesh-1500-cards-compromised/,success_decoder,success,Four Arrested in Major Aadhaar Fraud Case in Uttar Pradesh,Azdhan,2025-04-10,"In yet another Aadhaar fraud case, four young men in their 20s were arrested in Uttar Pradesh for tampering with Aadhaarâs systems. They had manipulated the biometric data of over 1,500 Aadhaar cardholders, said Sambhal SP Krishna Kumar Bishnoi.
The criminal group was headed by the âmastermindâ Ashish Kumar, a B.Tech dropout. Their network allegedly had 200â300 âretailersâ spread across over 12 states in the country, including Uttar Pradesh, Madhya Pradesh, Delhi, Tamil Nadu, Andhra Pradesh, and West Bengal, as reported by the media.
These so-called retailers allegedly acted as agents or intermediaries for the group of hackers. They collected documents from individuals seeking to alter their Aadhaar details illegally. The group charged between Rs 2,000 and Rs 5,000 per alteration, according to the police.
F3: Fake, Fake, Fake.
Ashish Kumar developed free fake websites designed to mimic the official (Unique Identification Authority of India) UIDAI and Passport Seva portals. These pseudo-government websites were then used to collect fake document data from retailers and agents.
These fake platforms were also used to generate fraudulent supporting documents to alter Aadhaar data. These documents included fake passports, said Bishnoi. Fake ration cards and fake birth certificates were also created and submitted as proof to generate fake Aadhaar IDs. “Over 20 fake passports, several of which were successfully uploaded to UIDAI for validation,” said the police.
The group managed to manipulate Aadhaar systems by impersonating authorized Aadhaar operators. They were able to bypass sensitive biometric security measures such as iris scans, fingerprints, and even geo-fencing restrictions, altering the Aadhaar database using fraudulent documents.
Fake thumb imprints were taken from unsuspecting real operators from economically weaker sections. Kasim Hussain, a co-accused, allegedly played a key role in modifying fingerprint scanners. “This allowed them to bypass Aadhaar’s real-time biometric locks,” said ASP Sharma.
Why Does This Matters?
This crimeâcreating fake Aadhaar identities using forged documentsâis yet another damning indictment of the security vulnerabilities in the Aadhaar system. Their âsecuredâ systems seem to have always broken. There were multiple instances of such serious sneak-ins by hackers.Â
The World Economic Forum’s Global Risks Report 2019, said that Aadhaarâs data breach was the biggest data breach in the world. âAadhaar reportedly suffered multiple breaches that potentially compromised the records of all 1.1 billion registered citizens. In January 2018, it was reported that criminals were selling access to the database at a rate of Rs 500 for 10 minutes, while in March, a leak at a state-owned utility company allowed anyone to download names and ID numbers,â the report said.
In 2023, Gujaratâs cyber police stated that creating fake Aadhaar cards had become a matter of just a few clicks. They referred to a fraudulent website that had more than 10 million downloads of fake Aadhaar IDs.
