Delhi Police apprehended two individuals involved in a scam that deceived people through fake AI-based trading advertisements. The culprits used bogus business tycoon endorsements and shell company accounts to swindle Rs. 9.53 lakh from a victim. The investigation uncovered 40 shell companies and 250 mule bank accounts.
NEW DELHI: The Delhi Police arrested two men who, along with their associates, were duping people on the pretext of AI-based trading. An inquiry revealed that they used fake pictures or videos of business tycoons to lure people into believing the investment was genuine.
Police said that on March 22 last year, they received a complaint from a resident of Vasant Kunj. The complainant told the police that he saw an advertisement on social media featuring a video of a business tycoon promoting AI-based trading. Intrigued, he contacted a marketing person via email. Subsequently, a person using a WhatsApp number sent him a link to a website along with login credentials.
The complainant was added to a WhatsApp group and contacted via WhatsApp. Later, he was informed about an opportunity to purchase Bitcoin, which was presented as highly profitable.
Convinced by the offer, the complainant agreed and made the payment as per the provided payment details. Through this scheme, the complainant was induced to invest in the aforementioned fake platform and was persuaded to transfer a total of Rs. 9.53 lakh in multiple transactions, the police said.
The alleged firm was registered with proxy directors, namely Sonu Kamat and Rajeev Kumar. Further, based on technical surveillance, a raid was conducted in Sector 91, Faridabad, and two accused persons, Babloo (37) and Anand Kumar Jha (26), were nabbed. A further raid was conducted at their office in Faridabad, and bank and other documents were recovered.
They used to arrange proxy directors and proprietorships for shell companies and facilitated the opening of bank accounts in the name of those shell companies. So far, 40 such shell companies and 250 mule bank accounts have been traced to them.
According to the police, a new type of cybercrime has recently emerged in which fraudsters utilise AI to falsely attribute schemes to prominent figures and business tycoons. They lure ordinary people into these schemes by presenting investment plans in highly lucrative and deceptive ways.
