A 57-year-old Bengaluru woman lost Rs 3.75 crore after being duped by fraudsters using an AI-generated deepfake of Sadhguru. She invested in an online trading platform, realising the scam only when withdrawals failed.
A 57-year-old resident of CV Raman Nagar in Bengaluru was allegedly defrauded of Rs 3.75 crore after being tricked by fraudsters using an AI-generated deepfake video of spiritual leader Sadhguru.
According to the complaint filed at a cyber police station in Bengaluru, the victim, unaware of deepfake technology, was convinced to invest in an online trading platform.
In the video, a Sadhguru lookalike claimed that he had been trading with a particular online firm and encouraged viewers to click a link and pay USD 250 to improve their finances. The link also requested the victim’s name, email, and phone number.
Following this, she was contacted by a company representative and began investing in the platform. Between February and April, the woman transferred a total of Rs 3,75,72,121 from her bank accounts into the trading application.
She realised she had been duped only when she could not withdraw any profits. Police have registered a case and are investigating the matter under cybercrime provisions.
In another incident from August, former Karnataka BJP MLA from Aurad, Gundappa Vakil, lost nearly Rs 30.99 lakh to a sophisticated “digital arrest” scam.
Scammers impersonated officials from the Central Bureau of Investigation (CBI), the Enforcement Directorate (ED), and even a judge, accusing Vakil of being linked to a money laundering case involving Jet Airways founder Naresh Goyal.
The fraudsters staged a fake online court hearing and warned Vakil not to disconnect the call. Believing the claims, he transferred Rs 10 lakh initially, and later Rs 20 lakh more, allegedly needed for a property-related investigation. He was assured the money would be returned, but it never was. Following his complaint, the Central Crime Branch (CCB) Cyber Crime Police in Bengaluru have launched an investigation.",333,,True,False
613.0,Scammers are using video deepfakes of journalists to peddle products online - Nieman Lab,Nieman Lab,"Wed, 01 Oct 2025 07:00:00 GMT",https://www.niemanlab.org/2025/10/scammers-are-using-video-deepfakes-of-journalists-to-peddle-products-online/,success_decoder,success,Scammers are using video deepfakes of journalists to peddle products online,Andrew Deck,2025-10-01,"Nieman Foundation at Harvard
		
		
	
Oct.  1, 2025, 3:27 p.m.
Broadcast journalists around the world are being targeted amid an explosion in deepfake scams. Are social media platforms doing enough to counter them?
 
In May 2024, Pooja Shali, an anchor for India Today, received a concerned message from a friend. The friend had been scrolling Instagram Reels and came across what appeared to be a video of Shali announcing the news on her morning show, First Up.
Audiences across India are used to seeing Shali behind the anchor desk, but in this clip, something felt off. Shali’s lips matched the words being spoken, but after the first sentence, her intonation changed and her cadence became stilted. She also appeared to be promoting a mobile trading app, one she said had been vetted by the India Today newsroom and received a $3 billion investment from India’s richest man.
Shali had never heard of the app, let alone promoted it to her viewers on air.
“I was completely shocked,” Shali said. She immediately recognized the clip as a deepfake. While the first seconds of audio were pulled from a real First Up segment, everything spliced in after was an AI-generated fabrication. “We report on digital financial scams consistently and we are the ones alerting people not to fall for digital scams, but you wouldn’t expect your face or your voice to be misused.”
Fake Alert!
A scam video *featuring me* on INSTA is DEEP-FAKE. Scary!
Obvious but for clarity- My news bulletin, edited by A.I to attract Farzi investments.
I endorse only real news :) Not such criminal schemes. DON’T SHARE/PAY. BEWARE.
Pls note @dcp_ifso @sumannalwa pic.twitter.com/LHVRDMllvg
— Pooja Shali (@PoojaShali) May 6, 2024
Soon, Shali received more messages from other friends who’d seen the video circulating on their feeds. She’s thankful those closest to her weren’t duped, but also concerned that the average viewer on Instagram wouldn’t be able to tell the difference. “It wasn’t just about AI, it was asking people to invest their money,” she said.
For years, scammers have been doctoring clips of public figures to promote products on social media. But with the rise of AI-generated video, these schemes are increasingly using the likenesses of journalists — particularly broadcast journalists — to peddle their products. TV broadcasters like Shali are familiar, trusted faces who speak with authority from their anchor chairs. Crucially, the journalists’ faces are also featured in countless hours of easily accessible videos, looking straight into the camera. That footage is primed to be fed into tools that generate deepfakes.
“With celebrities or other public figures, you may believe them for any number of reasons, but a journalist would base what they say on truth and facts,” said Shali, considering why journalists are so often used for deepfakes that push commercial products. “That connection a viewer has with the journalist, especially TV news journalists, is what is being misused.”
Shali is far from the first journalist to have her credibility exploited by deepfake scammers. Broadcast journalists have been dragged into similar frauds around the world, including in the U.K., Canada, Denmark, Russia, Peru, South Africa, and the Philippines. In the U.S., scams have targeted national news anchors including Al Roker and Gayle King. These videos promote a wide array of products, but frequently link to cryptocurrency cons, paywalled financial investment groups, gambling apps, and snake oil medicines.
“Broadcast journalism is one of the most accessible forms of media. People don’t read as much as they watch, whether it’s news on television or on their phones,” said Kiran Nazish, the founder and director of The Coalition for Women in Journalism (CFWIJ). “Because of the reach of broadcast journalists, there is a vulnerability there — they are targeted more often.”
Nazish has been following the rise of deepfakes through the CFWIJ initiative Women Press Freedom. When the project first began recording incidents of online harassment against women journalists back in 2019, the use of still images manipulated through basic software editing tools, like Photoshop, was still the norm. At the time, creating a scam video like the one featuring Shali would have required knowledge of complex deep learning techniques and significant computing resources.
Now, with the emergence of generative AI and cheap off-the-shelf tools, some scammers are able to easily create voice clones of journalists. Then they may generate lip-syncs, modifying the mouth movements in an existing video clip to match a new synthetic audio track. “Using popular journalists and their names and their faces to sell products online has become ubiquitous because of the tools being available to everybody,” said Nazish. “The increase has been exponential.”
Beyond social media scams, women journalists have also increasingly been the target of pornographic deepfakes used to harass and blackmail them, and deepfakes created to fuel disinformation campaigns and push political narratives. “The misogyny directed towards women is now being funneled in our current political environment,” said Nazish. “A lot of the targeting of journalists is habitual.”
A year ago, CFWIJ stopped publicizing information about specific deepfake cases, in part because its limited resources have been redirected toward crisis response, including supporting individual journalists as they navigate the fallout from these campaigns. With social media scams, it often falls on the targeted journalists to identify, document, and report videos, and also publicly defend their reputations.
“Journalists are expected to keep themselves safe, but gathering all the information about these deepfakes is laborious and it’s traumatic,” said Nazish.
Freelancers, independent journalists, and journalists working for smaller, underresourced outlets are often the most in need of support. But larger news organizations don’t always have their employees’ backs either. Declining to name names, Nazish said Women Press Freedom worked with a journalist in the U.K. whose employer put her on leave after a deepfake scam video went viral on social media. She ultimately quit due to a lack of support.
Shali, thankfully, has a different story. Within days of reporting the deepfake to her superiors, India Today produced and published a video debunking the mobile trading app advertisements and pushed it out across the network’s social media channels.
View this post on Instagram
Some deepfakes that include disinformation and abusive or degrading imagery may not benefit from direct acknowledgment — especially since many of these operations are aiming for visibility. In the case of deepfake scams, though, there are far fewer downsides to publicly calling out the fraud.
“This is one of the places where it’s a lot lower risk to talk about how AI is being used,” said Sam Gregory, the executive director of Witness, an international nonprofit focused on using video and technology to defend human rights. “[If you do] people will think, ‘I shouldn’t click through when that video comes to me.’ It also has a halo effect on similar scams, where people see a prominent figure advertising something and ask, ‘Were they paid for it or have they been faked into it?’”
Without intervention from major social media platforms — either by preemptively monitoring ads or moderating those that have been reported — there’s only so much individual journalists and newsrooms can do to counter these scams.
In early March, Martin Wolf, the chief economics commentator for the Financial Times, received an email from a former colleague. They had seen a vertical video on Instagram that seemed to show Wolf talking to camera and directing users to a private WhatsApp channel, where he was allegedly sharing exclusive investment and stock trading advice. Over the next few days, more emails came in from coworkers and friends, linking to similar deepfake advertisements on Facebook.
Wolf contacted the cybersecurity and social media teams at the FT, expecting Meta would pull the fraudulent videos immediately. The ads were in clear violation of the company’s community standards, which prohibit impersonating public figures. Days after connecting with the company, the videos continued to rack up views. Here’s one of the videos, courtesy of the FT and Meta’s ad library:
Increasingly frustrated, the FT reached out directly to Nick Clegg, then the president of global affairs at Meta, who Wolf knew in his past life as a British politician and member of the U.K. parliament. Within hours of connecting with Clegg, all the videos and associated accounts were pulled from Meta-owned platforms. In the weeks that followed though, new accounts and ads emerged to replace them.
“We were playing what we call whack-a-mole for quite a long time,” according to Wolf, who said the FT hounded Meta to pull the new ads for almost six weeks. Using Meta’s ad library for markets in the European Union, the FT documented over 1,700 ads placed on Facebook and Instagram using Wolf’s likeness. Together, the deepfakes reached 970,000 users across Europe. Wolf estimates the actual number would likely be millions if the U.K. and U.S. were taken into account.
Eventually, Wolf decided to write about his experience in a column for the FT. It was only after his column ran, he said, that the ads were scrubbed entirely from Meta’s platforms.
For Wolf, the delays were unacceptable. “This didn’t really make me anxious; it made me angry. I was angry that I was being dragged into something from which I don’t know how many people, but probably a lot of people, have been harmed,” he said. “I felt this was outrageous, that anyone would do this, and that any major company would profit from it.”
Meta is not only earning money off deepfakes videos of journalists. A new report from the Tech Transparency Project found 63 advertisers had run over 150,000 deceptive political ads on Meta platforms this year, including deepfakes of political figures. Collectively, those advertisers had spent $49 million and were among Meta’s top ad spenders in the category.
“Scammers are relentless and continuously evolve their tactics to try to evade detection, which is why we’re constantly developing new ways to make it harder for scammers to deceive others — including using facial recognition technology,” a Meta spokesperson told me in a statement. “We have removed and disabled the ads, accounts, and pages that were shared with us.”
As part of Meta’s response, Wolf was invited to enroll in the company’s facial recognition program aimed at countering what it calls “celebrity-bait ad scams.” The company relaunched the program last fall after shutting it down nearly three years earlier. Using an automated system, Meta compares uploaded ads to photos or video selfies of enrolled public figures. If a match is confirmed, then the ad is reviewed and taken down.
While Wolf was ultimately able to fight off his scammers, other journalists may not be able to lean on personal connections or institutional support to get the attention of social media companies. Many journalists don’t even live in countries where Meta’s facial recognition program is operational.
“Trying to go through the official channels is frustrating, it’s time-consuming, and it’s largely ineffective,” said Matthew Garrahan, the head of digital platforms at the FT. “It was only [after] Martin’s column that Meta began to really move. We had a former deputy prime minister of the U.K. intervening to help us get it taken down, and then they were still back.”
Meta did not respond to questions about Clegg’s involvement or whether Wolf’s column influenced the company’s response.
“On the platform side, you need to have this likeness protection really broadly available to a much wider range of public figures,” Witness’s Gregory said of facial recognition programs like Meta’s.
He points to YouTube as a platform that is beginning to take stronger proactive measures. In mid-September, the social media giant announced it was rolling out facial recognition-based moderation to all members of its YouTube Partner Program, which includes more than 3 million creators around the world. (Gregory said that even with increased reach, he’s found YouTube’s moderation enforcement to be “patchy.”)
Women Press Freedom, meanwhile, has scaled back the resources it puts into corresponding with social media companies. Nazish said the decision was prompted in part by the industry’s continued disinvestment in global moderation infrastructure.
In 2022, X laid off the majority of their global trust and safety teams. Earlier this year, Meta ended its third-party fact-checking program. Women Press Freedom used to be able to collect documentation of deepfakes and send them directly to company contacts who they relied on to advocate for post removals. Those channels had all but disappeared by 2022, even before this most recent boom in deepfake scams.
In the vacuum that’s left, targeted journalists are running out of ways to defend their credibility.
“Now when I speak I’m a little bit more cautious,” said Shali, of her appearances at the anchor desk since last year. Eight months after the first scam ads using her image appeared, a new deepfake cropped up on social media promoting a similar trading app. This time, the deepfake video featured both Shali and Google CEO Sundar Pichai. “It’s heavy on my mind,” she told me. “When I’m talking I don’t know how they will be able to manipulate it.”
Photo of newscaster behind an anchor desk by fedorovekb used under an Adobe Stock license.
Andrew Deck is a staff writer covering AI at Nieman Lab. Have tips about how AI is being used in your newsroom? You can reach Andrew via email, Bluesky, or Signal (+1 203-841-6241).
