651.0,"In a first, Hyderabad Police name Google India head as co-accused in investment app fraud cases - NewsMeter",NewsMeter,"Tue, 21 Jul 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://newsmeter.in/business/in-a-first-hyderabad-police-name-google-india-head-as-co-accused-in-investment-app-fraud-cases-772120,success_decoder,success,"In a first, Hyderabad Police name Google India head as co-accused in investment app fraud cases",Newsmeter Network,2026-07-21,"In a first, Hyderabad Police name Google India head as co-accused in investment app fraud cases
Google India head named co-accused in Hyderabad cyber fraud cases over fake investment apps
By Newsmeter Network
Representation Image
Hyderabad: In a first-of-its-kind move, the Hyderabad Cyber Crime Police have named the head of Google India as a co-accused in three cyber fraud cases after victims alleged they lost up to Rs 24 lakh through fake stock market investment apps downloaded from the Google Play Store.
The case has raised serious questions over intermediary liability and app-store due diligence.
Victims lost lakhs
The three complainants, aged between 40 and 71 years, claimed they invested in fraudulent trading platforms after downloading the applications from Google’s official app marketplace, believing them to be genuine.
The victims allegedly lost amounts ranging from Rs 7 lakh to Rs 24.37 lakh.
Police have registered cases against the alleged fraudsters as well as the Google India head under provisions of the Information Technology (IT) Act and the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita (BNS).
Google booked under intermediary liability provisions
Hyderabad Cyber Crime officials said the Google India head was arrayed as a co-accused based on the allegations made by the complainants.
The police have also issued notices to Google directing it to remove the fraudulent applications from the Play Store.
Officials said intermediaries are protected under Section 79 of the IT Act only if they exercise due diligence and promptly remove unlawful content after receiving notice. Failure to do so without valid reason could attract liability under Section 79(3) of the Act.
A verification of the three application links cited in the complaints found that only one of the apps remained accessible on the Google Play Store.
Google India had not responded to media queries till late Monday.
Here’s a detailed account of how each victim was targeted and cheated of lakhs:
1. Facebook ad leads to Rs 24 lakh fraud
In one of the cases, registered on July 15, a 69-year-old resident of Santosh Nagar alleged that he lost Rs 24.37 lakh after falling victim to a fake stock market investment scheme.
According to the complaint, he came across a Facebook advertisement featuring Union Finance Minister Nirmala Sitharaman purportedly endorsing an investment opportunity promising returns of Rs 22 lakh per week.
After clicking the advertisement, he was contacted on WhatsApp by a woman identifying herself as Dia Zian.
She allegedly persuaded him to download an investment application from the Google Play Store, claiming it was licensed and regulated by South Africa’s Financial Sector Conduct Authority. Acting on her instructions, the complainant transferred Rs 24.37 lakh to multiple bank accounts during May and June.
When he later attempted to withdraw his investment, the requests were allegedly denied. The victim told police he trusted the application because it was available on the Play Store and sought legal action against Google for allegedly failing to verify the application’s authenticity.
Police registered a case against ‘Dia Zian’, Aneesh Mathew and the Google India head under Sections 66-C and 66-D of the IT Act and Sections 318(4), 319(2), 336(3) and 340(2) of the BNS.
2. Govt employee from Adibatla loses over Rs 17 lakh
In another case registered on July 14, a 40-year-old government employee from Adibatla alleged that he lost more than Rs 17 lakh after investing through another fake trading platform downloaded from the Google Play Store.
The complainant told police that he received a WhatsApp message from a woman identifying herself as Ananya Kulkarni, who claimed to represent a company offering stock market investments, IPO opportunities and AI-based trading strategies.
Believing the claims, he downloaded the application and transferred more than Rs 17 lakh, including funds borrowed through personal loans, to multiple bank accounts between May and June.
Although the application displayed profits of Rs 41.7 lakh, the accused allegedly demanded additional payments before allowing withdrawals. After realising he had been cheated, the victim lodged a complaint, alleging that Google had failed to exercise due diligence before hosting the application.
Police booked ‘Ananya Kulkarni’, Gul Tekchandani and the Google India head under the relevant provisions of the IT Act and the BNS.
3. Retired employee from Erragadda cheated of Rs 7 lakh
In the third case, registered on June 30, a 71-year-old retired government employee from Erragadda alleged that he lost Rs 7 lakh after investing through another fraudulent stock market application downloaded from the Google Play Store.
According to the complaint, two persons identifying themselves as Swarnali and Avishek contacted him through WhatsApp and persuaded him to invest through the application.
After he allegedly lost the money and was unable to recover it, he approached the Hyderabad Cyber Crime Police.
A case has been registered against the two alleged fraudsters as well as the Google India head. Police said further investigation is underway to trace the accused and examine the role of the intermediary under the provisions of the IT Act.",819,,True,False
653.0,Hyderabad: Retired IAS officer loses Rs 3.37 Cr after falling prey to online investment scam - NewsMeter,NewsMeter,"Sun, 22 Jun 2025 07:00:00 GMT",https://newsmeter.in/crime/hyderabad-retired-ias-officer-loses-rs-337-cr-after-falling-prey-to-online-investment-scam-750508,success_decoder,success,Hyderabad: Retired IAS officer loses Rs 3.37 Cr after falling prey to online investment scam,Newsmeter Network,2025-06-23,"Hyderabad: Retired IAS officer loses Rs 3.37 Cr after falling prey to online investment scam
72-year-old Kaza Ratna Kishore, a former bureaucrat says that he was lured into fake scheme by fraudsters using the brand name and logo of Dhani Securities
By - Newsmeter Network |Representational Image
Hyderabad: A retired IAS officer from Hyderabad has fallen victim to an elaborate online investment scam, losing over Rs 3.37 crore to cyber fraudsters who impersonated officials of a reputed trading firm and promised high returns through AI-based trading platforms.
In his complaint to the Cyber Crime Police Station, TGCSB, Hyderabad, 72-year-old Kaza Ratna Kishore, a former senior bureaucrat, alleged that he was lured into the fake scheme by fraudsters using the brand name and logo of “Dhani Securities,” falsely citing SEBI registration and a depository account number.
Modus Operandi
According to the complaint, the fraud began in early May 2025, when the complainant came across a trading platform link shared by a woman identifying herself as Arjun Mehta. She initiated contact via WhatsApp and introduced Kishore to an investment model promising lucrative returns through mutual funds, IPOs, and options trading.
Soon after, a man named Arjun Ramesh Mehta, posing as the platform’s Chief Investment Officer, began sending detailed trading strategies and market predictions, claiming to use AI-powered tools with 90 percent accuracy. He also offered access to “exclusive IPO listings” with 120–160 percent profit margins, further enticing the victim.
The scammers created the illusion of legitimacy through fake login portals and daily WhatsApp broadcasts. They maintained pressure by emotionally manipulating the victim, praising his persistence and encouraging him to “complete the journey” by paying a “platform fee.”
False assurances and heavy losses
Kishore was repeatedly told that his investment profits, amounting to Rs 25.91 crore, would be released only after paying a 10 percent platform fee of Rs 2.23 crore. Despite transferring funds in multiple transactions between March 30 and May 15, totaling Rs 3.37 crore, he was unable to access any returns or withdraw funds.
The funds were routed through 33 transactions across ICICI Bank, SBI, and Union Bank of India. The fake platform, despite showing a running balance, never reflected the platform fee payments.
Police case registered
Based on the complaint, the Cyber Crime Police have registered a case under Sections 318(4), 319(2), and 338 of the Bharatiya Nyay Sanhita (BNS), Section 66-D of the Information Technology Act, 2000.
The case is being investigated by Inspector D Ashish Reddy under the supervision of KVM Prasad, Deputy Superintendent of Police, who confirmed that a team has been assigned to trace the accused and identify the accounts used in the fraud.
Advisory to the Public
Officials have warned the public to exercise caution when approached with high-return investment schemes online. They have advised verifying SEBI registration details on official portals and refraining from transferring funds without due diligence.",476,,True,False
654.0,"Hyderabad firm's AI, blockchain platform promises to end menace of phishing messages - Moneycontrol.com",Moneycontrol.com,"Tue, 28 Feb 2023 08:00:00 GMT",https://www.moneycontrol.com/news/business/hyderabad-firms-ai-blockchain-platform-promises-to-end-menace-of-phishing-messages-10172821.html,success_decoder,success,"Hyderabad firm's AI, blockchain platform promises to end menace of phishing messages- Moneycontrol.com",Aihik Sur,2023-02-28,"HDFC Bank has partnered with Tanla Platforms to curb phishing, even as regulators across the globe are starting to realise the impact of phishing and are seeking solutions 
  February 28, 2023 / 12:18 IST  
         Hyderabad-headquartered cloud communications company Tanla Platforms has come up with an end-to-end phishing protection platform called Wisely ATP that leverages artificial intelligence (AI) to prevent spam messages from reaching a phone.
The messages it will ward off are the kind we get almost every day—promising a job by clicking on a link or a warning that our bank account will be suspended if we don't update your KYC.
These messages are used by scamsters to steal money and personal information. These online scams amount to a loss of nearly $55 billion globally, Global Anti Scam Alliance has said.
Tanla Platforms while blocking these also bring them to the notice of Google, WhatsApp and law enforcement agencies by giving them real-time alerts on the spam messages getting blocked by the platform.
The company has leveraged a blockchain platform to create an audit trail of these messages and sends them to authorities, so that, if necessary, they can even be used as evidence in a court of law.
3-way check
Last week in a live demonstration of Wisely ATP to select mediapersons, Tanla showed that the platform uses three AI engines to block spam messages.
The first algorithm, reputation engine, detects the trustworthiness of the number that is sending the messages. The semantic engine verifies the language of the message and lastly, the evaluation engine decides whether the message should be allowed or blocked.
But how accurate is it? Is there a chance of legitimate messages getting blocked?
Tanla's chief executive officer Uday Reddy claimed that Wisely ATP processes over 1 trillion transactions annually in real-time with an accuracy of over 99 percent.
The company also said it recently conducted a Telecom Regulatory Authority of India (TRAI)-mandated regulatory sandbox to validate the workings of Wisely.
“TRAI was the first regulator to introduce blockchain-based DLT solution to curb the menace of spam. Tanla has been a key player in providing the solution,” said TRAI chairman PD Vaghela has said.
How is it going to be deployed?
During the demonstration at Tanla's headquarters, Reddy said the platform can be deployed at the network level, banks can also be its customers, it can also be used with caller-ID applications such as Truecaller and so on.
Ravi Santhanam, Chief Marketing Officer at HDFC Bank, said, “At HDFC Bank, we obsess over customer safety and security. With the rising threat of phishing in India, the bank has been raising customer awareness through two large public campaigns. Taking another step in this direction, we are delighted to partner with Tanla to curb phishing at the source and offer end-to-end protection to our customers.""
