748.0,Scammers are using AI to lure shoppers to fake businesses - BBC,BBC,"Wed, 08 Oct 2025 07:00:00 GMT",https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/ced5wvn48q5o,success_decoder,success,Scammers are using AI to lure shoppers to fake businesses,Alice Cullinane,2025-10-08,"Scammers using AI to lure shoppers to fake businesses
Unscrupulous foreign firms are using AI-generated images and false back stories to pose as family-run UK businesses to lure in shoppers.
Customers say they feel ""completely ripped off"" after believing they were buying from independent boutiques in England but were delivered cheap clothes and jewellery, mass-shipped from warehouses in east Asia.
Among the websites is C'est La Vie, a shop purporting to be run by couple Eileen and Patrick for 29 years and based in Birmingham's historic Jewellery Quarter - but with a returns address in China.
Consumer guide Which? said the growing use of AI tools was making it possible for fraudsters to mislead the public on an ""unprecedented"" scale.
Another website appearing to use AI-generated images is Mabel & Daisy, a seemingly quintessential, mother and daughter-owned clothing firm, which claims to be based in Bristol but has an address in Hong Kong.
More than 500 one-star reviews for the companies appear on review platform Trustpilot, with customers complaining of paying high prices for cheaply made goods and being charged extortionate return fees.
Shoppers told the BBC they were targeted by ads as they scrolled on Facebook.
The Advertising Standards Agency (ASA), which recently banned ads by Marble Muse, a Chinese clothing company pretending to be based in London, said social media platforms also had their part to play in tackling the issue.
The BBC has contacted Facebook owner Meta for comment.
A grieving widow and 80%-off sale
With their warm smiles, branded shirts and cabinets full of jewellery behind them, Eileen and Patrick are the perfect image of a successful husband and wife team.
Except, they are literally too perfect, according to a professor of Artificial Intelligence at the University of Birmingham, Mark Lee.
""Previously there were obvious clues, for instance AI had difficulty generating realistic hands with plausible finger placement,"" he said after viewing the website.
""This seems well done but the images look a little bit too perfect and staged to be real.""
'Lumps of resin'
Recent adverts for C'est La Vie, which claims to be based in Colmore Row, Birmingham, stated that the couple had befallen a tragedy - Eileen's ""beloved husband"" Patrick had died and she was shutting the business down with an 80%-off clearance sale.
""I've battled to keep our handmade jewellery boutique alive but grief has drained my strength and I must close our doors,"" it said.
But customers lured in by the sob story posted furious reviews on Trustpilot about receiving ""lumps of resin"", ""plastic junk"" and ""cheap metal rubbish"".
""If I could give 0 stars, I would - complete scam, no such company in Birmingham, can't believe I fell for it,"" one said.
Sunny Pal, who runs Astella Jewellery in Birmingham, said that the scams damaged the reputation of the city's Jewellery Quarter and tarnished the hard work of local businesses.
""The lying ruins the distinguishing factor between different businesses. It takes the credibility away from family-run businesses that have been here for so long,"" he said.
Within hours of the BBC's first approach to C'est La Vie, its website stated all its products had sold out, the business was closed with a message from Eileen that said: ""We're carefully packing and shipping the final orders that were placed.""
The name of the business on its website then temporarily changed to ""Alice and Fred"" before returning to its C'est La Vie branding.
The company did not reply to a request for comment.
Justyne Gough spent £40 on a pink floral dress from Mabel & Daisy, a business that claims to have created ""timeless clothing"" in Bristol since 2022.
She realised it was a ""complete scam"" when the garment arrived weeks later looking nothing like the elegant dress on the website and made of an ""awful material"".
""The website looked lovely, professional and authentic but I had to email a few times to say the items hadn't arrived even though the money had been taken straight away,"" she said.
When she tried to return it, Ms Gough was told it would cost another £20. She eventually managed to get a refund of £20.
Emma, from Birmingham, said she tried to send a £50 jacket back to Mabel & Daisy which was far too big - but was told to keep it and, for an extra £10, she could be sent a smaller size.
""There was no way I wanted to give them any more money - I realised it wasn't based in Bristol when a load of Chinese symbols popped up when my initial transaction went through,"" she said.
Mabel & Daisy did not respond to our questions.
'Poor-quality tat'
Last month, in response to a complaint from BBC's Watchdog, the ASA banned ads for a so-called ""British"" clothing firm that used images of roses, cobbled streets and the union jack when it was shipping goods from a warehouse in Asia.
The regulator said it was continuing to take action on misleading ads.
But their spokesperson added: ""Other regulators and the platforms on which these ads appear all have a part to play in tackling this issue.""
Sue Davies, from Which?, said trading standards teams were ""severely under-resourced and not well-equipped"" to deal with the websites, meaning many could be going uninvestigated.
""Although the onus should not fall on consumers, there are steps they can take to limit the risk of ending up with poor-quality tat,"" she said.
Shoppers should check reviews online to see how other people have found the company's products and customer service.
It is also worth checking if the company is based in the UK - terms and conditions pages can reveal the location of businesses.
Identifiable locations
Prof Mark Lee said some things to look for were ""images of the person in different settings, with different backgrounds and the inclusion of real, identifiable locations"".
But he warned: ""AI is becoming better every day and soon the challenge might not be to prove whether the site is AI-generated but whether there's actually a real human involved at all.""
Follow BBC Birmingham on BBC Sounds, Facebook, X and Instagram.",1020,,True,False
749.0,"Scammers threaten West Bengal man with morphed image, dupe him of Rs 6.5 lakh in new cyber scam - India Today",India Today,"Tue, 25 Feb 2025 08:00:00 GMT",https://www.indiatoday.in/technology/news/story/scammers-threaten-west-bengal-man-with-morphed-image-dupe-him-of-rs-65-lakh-in-new-cyber-scam-2685131-2025-02-25,success_decoder,success,"Scammers threaten West Bengal man with morphed image, dupe him of Rs 6.5 lakh in new cyber scam",Divya Bhati,2025-02-25,"Scammers threaten West Bengal man with morphed image, dupe him of Rs 6.5 lakh in new cyber scam
The scammers duped a 70-year-old man from West Bengal out of Rs 6.5 lakh by using his morphed images. They even threatned him posing as fake police officials.
In the past few years, we have seen a stark rise in cases of online scams. Over these years, the tricks used by scammers have also evolved, becoming more dangerous for victims and impacting them heavily, both financially and emotionally. One such scam has been recently reported from West Bengal, where a 70-year-old retired government employee lost Rs 6.6 lakh after being threatened with morphed images by scammers.
The case was reported by The Telegraph Online. According to the report, the victim is from the South Dinajpur district, West Bengal. The scam began about a month ago when the victim received a video call from an unknown number. Upon answering, he saw a young woman on the screen. Though the call seemed innocuous at first, it turned out to be the beginning of a well-planned scam. The report does not mention whether the victim communicated with the woman on the call. However, a few days later, the man reportedly received another video call—this time, the callers showed him a digitally morphed photo of himself alongside the woman.
Seeing his morphed photo, the victim was shocked and panicked. To further escalate the fear and dupe him out of money, the woman then demanded payment, threatening to circulate the morphed images on social media if he refused to comply. Fearing damage to his reputation, the elderly man initially considered paying the demanded amount but later hesitated. Sensing his reluctance, the fraudsters then started threatening him more.
After his initial refusal to pay, the scammers called him again—this time, impersonating police officers. They falsely claimed that the woman had attempted suicide and was undergoing treatment at a hospital in Delhi. The fake officers even warned the man that he would be arrested unless he paid for her medical expenses. Intimidated by the threats, the victim transferred approximately Rs 6.5 lakh in panic to the scammers.
However, the scammers did not stop there. They continued demanding more money. This ongoing pressure raised the victim's suspicion, and he finally shared his ordeal with his wife. His wife realized that this was a scam and urged him to report it to the police as soon as possible.
How to stay safe from such scams
This case is not an isolated one. Many users on social media have shared their experiences of receiving similar fake calls. These scams generally start with a call from someone of the opposite gender, sometimes texting first. The caller tries to coax the victim romantically or capture screenshots of video calls. The scammers then morph these images and attempt to blackmail the victim for money. In the above case, the scammers escalated their threats, even impersonating officials and fabricating a suicide case to extort money.
To stay safe:Â
It is highly advisable not to entertain any suspicious calls. If someone tries to blackmail you, end the call immediately and report it. Scammers often use such tactics to gather information or manipulate victims.
Additionally, if someone claims to be a police officer or government official, verify their identity by contacting the relevant department directly. Do not rely on contact details provided by the caller.
Most importantly, never share personal details, photos, or financial information with strangers, especially over the phone or online.
Remember, scammers often use fear tactics to pressure victims into complying with their demands. If you receive such calls, stay calm and seek advice from family or law enforcement before taking any action.",618,,True,False
750.0,Guernsey AI scam targets islanders with fake Chief Minister posts - BBC,BBC,"Fri, 01 Aug 2025 07:00:00 GMT",https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c07pg73vmr1o,success_decoder,success,Guernsey AI scam targets islanders with fake Chief Minister posts,Jack Silver,2025-08-01,"AI scam targets islanders with fake minister posts
AI-generated content of Guernsey's top politician has been used by fraudsters in a scam to try and persuade islanders into making investments, police say.
The scam, known as a deepfake, shows fake video and images of Guernsey's chief minister, Deputy Lindsay de Sausmarez, appearing to recommend an investment scheme, as well as fake local newspaper articles.
De Sausmarez said she hoped ""most people would quickly realise it's fake"", but advised islanders to warn vulnerable people the posts were fraudulent.
A Guernsey Police spokesperson said: ""Please be aware that this story, along with any others that may surface featuring Deputies requesting payment or investment, are not real and are a scam.""
Police added people should be wary of ""articles that promote too-good-to-be-true investments or prizes"".
Deepfake scams have become more common in recent years as AI technology improves, with faked images of high-profile figures such as financial journalist Martin Lewis and London mayor Sadiq Khan used by people online to trick people.
Guernsey Police warned people to think twice before clicking links, entering personal details or providing bank details.
Anyone who suspects they may have been the victim of online fraud should contact their bank immediately, a police spokesperson added.
