According to investigators, the gang allegedly targeted financially stable people by collecting GST numbers, PAN card details and mobile-linked Aadhaar information from online platforms.
Police said the accused used photos taken from apps and social media platforms like PhonePe, Google Pay, WhatsApp, Facebook and Instagram. These pictures were then used to create AI-generated deepfake videos that looked real enough to fool Aadhaar verification systems.
“The accused used highly advanced techniques to bypass digital verification systems. They created AI-generated eye-blinking videos from Aadhaar photographs to mimic live biometric authentication,” a Cyber Crime Branch officer said.
Investigators believe the gang changed victims’ Aadhaar-linked mobile numbers through CSC centres. Once the number was replaced with a SIM card controlled by the accused, they allegedly gained access to DigiLocker accounts, OTPs, Aadhaar services and banking platforms.
Police said the fraudsters then opened bank accounts and took instant personal loans in the victims’ names. The money was later transferred through multiple accounts to avoid suspicion.
Cyber Crime DCP Dr Lavina Sinha said the gang earned around Rs 10 lakh to Rs 15 lakh every year through these fake loan activities.
Police alleged that Rabbul Hussain handled the AI-based identity theft work along with absconding accused Oli Ullah. Kazimuddin is accused of allowing fake loan money to pass through his bank accounts in return for commission.
The case was uncovered after a businessman from Ahmedabad noticed he had stopped receiving OTPs linked to his Aadhaar-enabled payment system. Later, he found out that his Aadhaar-linked mobile number and biometric details had allegedly been changed without his permission, and loans had been taken in his name.
