Gorakhpur police arrest MSc teacher impersonating as IAS:Gaurav Kumar extorted crores using AI documents social media & fake tenders
- Copy link
Gorakhpur police arrested Gaurav Kumar Singh, also known as Lalit Kishore, for impersonating an IAS officer and running an elaborate fraud network across UP, Bihar, Jharkhand, and Madhya Pradesh.
Gaurav maintained an extravagant lifestyle, spending Rs 5 lakh monthly to uphold IAS protocol, often moving with 10-15 accomplices to create an air of authority.
Gaurav would tour villages in a white Innova with red-blue lights. During a visit to Bhagalpur village in Bihar, he encountered the real SDM.
Gaurav slapped officer twice leaving SDM shocked
When questioned about his rank and batch, Gaurav slapped the officer twice, leaving the SDM too shocked to file a complaint.
Digital trail reveals personal deception
Police seized two mobile phones from Gaurav, revealing chats with four girlfriends, three of whom are currently pregnant. All were unaware of his real marital status and believed he was an IAS officer. Gaurav is married to a woman from Bihar, whom he eloped with during his underground stay a few years ago.
Fraud network
With assistance from his brother-in-law, Abhishek Kumar, Gaurav created fake IAS credentials and a nameplate. Abhishek, a software professional with strong local connections, initially arranged forged documents through other fraudsters. After the launch of AI, the duo began generating fake newspaper clippings, inspection reports, and government documents online, making detection difficult.
To expand the network in UP, Gaurav recruited Parmanand Gupta, a friend of Abhishek, presenting Gaurav as an IAS officer on social media and community groups. This promotion helped lure builders and businessmen, who were offered government contracts and AI-generated tender papers.
Beginning: Aditya Super-50 coaching scam
Gaurav, originally from Mehsaul village in Sitamarhi, Bihar, completed his MSc in Mathematics in 2019 and aspired to become a DIOS. After three years of civil service preparation, he opened Aditya Super-50 coaching in 2022. He committed his first fraud here by collecting Rs 2 lakh from a student promising a government job, which he failed to deliver. An FIR was registered against him, and Gaurav went underground for a year before reemerging as a self-proclaimed IAS officer.
Manipulating social relationships for gain
During his underground period, Gaurav eloped and married a girl from Bihar. Needing money, he began extorting people by promising jobs and contracts. Through social media, he befriended women, eventually creating four girlfriends, three of whom are now pregnant. They were unaware of his real identity and marital status.
High-profile fraud operations
Gaurav’s misdeeds include visiting villages and government offices to pose as an IAS officer. In Bhathhat, Gorakhpur, a teacher grew suspicious during a school inspection. Gaurav claimed he was sent by the central government to investigate 18 districts.
He extorted Rs 5 crore and two luxury Innova cars from a Bihar contractor named Madhav by promising a Rs 450 crore tender. Gaurav flaunted dominance with cars, gunmen, and fake government documents.
AI-generated news clippings showed Gaurav in DM chairs and inspection settings, convincing victims of his authenticity. He often traveled in convoys with horns blaring to create an authoritative presence.
Hiding and eventual arrest
After trouble with the Bihar contractor, Gaurav moved to Gulriha, Gorakhpur, living with his wife and children. A large board outside his house read IAS Gaurav Kumar, intimidating locals. Intelligence and local police tracked him for three months, culminating in his arrest.
During the Bihar Assembly elections, GRP recovered Rs 99.90 lakh in cash from Gaurav’s associate, intended as bribes to secure jobs via the fake IAS. Investigation revealed over 40 victims across four states, cheated under the guise of government contracts, jobs, and marriage arrangements.
Authorities on the case
SP City Abhinav Tyagi confirmed, “Gaurav alias Lalit was working in a large network. Many new facts are expected, and we are coordinating with Bihar, Jharkhand, and Madhya Pradesh police to uncover the entire network.”",681,,True,False
779.0,AI Finds What Manual Audits Missed: Police Salary Scam Busted - The420.in,The420.in,"Thu, 02 Jul 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://the420.in/ai-audit-police-salary-scam-chhattisgarh-three-constables-arrested/,success_decoder,success,AI Finds What Manual Audits Missed: Police Salary Scam Busted,The,2026-07-02,"Three police constables have been arrested after an alleged police salary scam worth nearly ₹2 crore was uncovered in Chhattisgarh’s Bastar district. Investigators claim the fraud remained undetected for almost three years and came to light only after the police department, for the first time, used Artificial Intelligence (AI) to analyse a large volume of salary records.
According to the preliminary investigation, digital salary records were allegedly manipulated between October 2023 and May 2026 to misappropriate government funds. Authorities allege that the three accused gradually increased their salaries over the period, illegally receiving between ₹1.5 crore and ₹2 crore.
The arrested accused have been identified as Girish Rai, Rajkumar Katlam and Hemant Mathew. Investigators said Girish Rai was working as an assistant in the salary section of the Superintendent of Police’s office and was responsible for preparing salary bills. He is accused of abusing his position by altering electronic salary records and allegedly increasing the salaries of himself and the two other constables.
Investigators believe the alleged manipulation was carried out in small monthly increments, allowing the irregularities to go unnoticed for a prolonged period. Officials said frequent transfers, fresh postings, retirements and routine fluctuations in police staff strength naturally caused changes in salary expenditure, helping the alleged fraud remain hidden within normal financial transactions.
The suspected scam was detected during a government audit in which AI tools were used to analyse the salary data of nearly 2,000 police personnel. While examining the massive dataset, the AI system reportedly identified unusual salary patterns and suspicious payments, prompting a detailed investigation that ultimately led to action against the three accused.
Officials associated with the investigation said identifying such subtle irregularities through conventional manual scrutiny would have been extremely difficult given the volume of data involved. They said AI-based analysis successfully isolated abnormal payment patterns and directed investigators towards suspicious transactions.
The accused have been booked under various provisions of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita (BNS), including charges related to cheating, forgery, criminal breach of trust and alleged misappropriation of government funds. They were produced before a court and remanded to 14 days of judicial custody. Investigators are also examining financial transactions, bank accounts and digital records to determine whether any other individuals were involved in the alleged fraud.
The case has renewed discussions on strengthening financial oversight and digital auditing within government departments. Experts believe regular AI-driven analysis of large financial databases can help detect concealed fraudulent payments, salary irregularities and other financial anomalies much earlier.
According to a researcher at Algoritha Security, AI-powered data analytics is not limited to cybersecurity but is also highly effective in identifying unusual patterns, suspicious transactions and record manipulation in large government financial databases. The researcher said regular digital audits supported by automated analytics could play a significant role in preventing similar financial irregularities in the future.",471,,True,False
785.0,Cyber shield op busts massive online matrimony scam: 11 held in Jharkhand and Chhattisgarh - The Times of India,The Times of India,"Sat, 11 Oct 2025 07:00:00 GMT",https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/city/raipur/cyber-shield-op-busts-massive-online-matrimony-scam-11-held-in-jharkhand-and-chhattisgarh/articleshow/124481969.cms,success_decoder,success,Cyber shield op busts massive online matrimony scam: 11 held in Jharkhand and Chhattisgarh | Raipur News - The Times of India,Rashmi Drolia,2025-10-11,"RAIPUR:
 Chhattisgarh cyber police exposed an inter-state large-scale cyber fraud racket under operation cyber shield, that used fake social media accounts to dupe thousands of people across India through âmatrimony scams.â 
Raipur range inspector general of police Amresh Mishra on Saturday said that police arrested 11 accused from Jamshedpur (Jharkhand) and Bilaspur (Chhattisgarh) and seized 36 mobile phones and several other digital devices.
Investigations revealed that the gang was running fake call centres for the past two years, operating primarily from Jamshedpur and Bilaspurâs Sarkanda area. They created 262 fake IDs across Instagram, Facebook, and YouTube, amassing over five million followers. Using womenâs photos and fake phone numbers, they would lure people â especially those seeking matrimonial alliances â and then extort money under false pretences.
How the Fraud Worked
According to police, the fraudsters used stolen or AI-generated photos of women to create fake social media profiles and âmatrimonialâ IDs. They posted these along with fabricated contact numbers, prompting unsuspecting men to reach out. 
Once in touch, the victims were promised details such as the womanâs biodata, family background, and address â for a price. Payments were made through mule bank accounts, after which the scammers disappeared.
A case was registered at DD Nagar police station in connection with 79 mule bank accounts opened in HDFC Bank. The investigation, led by the Raipur range cyber police station, was based on digital footprints, transaction records, and data extracted from seized devices. Earlier, four suspects had been arrested in the same case, whose interrogation led to the latest arrests.
IG Amresh Mishra said the police are identifying more suspects involved in creating fake social media IDs and using mule accounts for fraud. âThis is one of the largest inter-state cyber fraud operations uncovered in recent times. The investigation is expanding to trace others who facilitated banking channels and online identity manipulation,â he said.
Police confirmed that all 11 accused have been remanded to judicial custody, while technical teams continue to analyse their seized devices for additional leads.
The crackdown highlights how social media platforms are increasingly being misused for financial scams, with authorities intensifying efforts to monitor cyber activity and dismantle digital fraud networks across India.
11 Arrested Across Two States
Police teams conducted simultaneous raids in Jamshedpur and Bilaspur, arresting 11 accused, including the masterminds who handled the digital operations and money transfers. The arrested individuals include:
- Shubham Das, Laxman Gop, and Asit Patar â residents of Jamshedpur, Jharkhand
- Suraj Patel, Sukhsagar Kevat, Manasu Dahire, Aniket Kuldeep, Karan Pushpakar, Ramakant Gandharv, Siraj Khan, and Triyambak Bhaskar â residents of Bilaspur and nearby districts in Chhattisgarh",437,,True,False
795.0,"Delhi police bust AI-based QR code fraud, accused arrested from Rajasthan - ANI News",ANI News,"Tue, 23 Dec 2025 08:00:00 GMT",https://www.aninews.in/news/national/general-news/delhi-police-bust-ai-based-qr-code-fraud-accused-arrested-from-rajasthan20251223183748,success_decoder,success,"Delhi police bust AI-based QR code fraud, accused arrested from Rajasthan",ANI,2026-08-30,"New Delhi [India], December 23 (ANI): Delhi police have arrested a man from Rajasthan for carrying out a sophisticated cyber fraud involving tampered QR codes, using artificial intelligence tools to divert digital payments. The arrest was made following a complaint in North Delhi, where two digital transactions amounting to Rs 1.40 lakh were deducted from a customer's bank account but never reached the intended shopkeeper. 
Speaking on the case, North Delhi DCP Raja Banthia said the cyber police registered an FIR after the discrepancy came to light and launched a detailed investigation. ""Two transactions totalling Rs 140,000 occurred, but the payment didn't reach the shopkeeper despite being deducted from the customer's account,"" DCP Banthia said. He added that investigations traced the suspicious transactions to a bank account linked to the accused, who was later identified, located and arrested from Chaksu in Jaipur district, Rajasthan. 
During a search of the accused's residence, police recovered more than 100 tampered QR codes, pointing to a larger and well-planned fraud operation. Explaining the modus operandi, DCP Banthia said the accused would approach vendors online and ask them to share their QR codes for payment. ""He would then use AI software to alter the information on the QR codes and send the modified versions back to the vendors via WhatsApp,"" the DCP Banthia stated. 
In several cases, the edited QR codes were automatically saved in the vendors' phone galleries, with the merchant's name changed while the QR code structure remained intact. When customers later made payments using these saved QR codes, the money was diverted to the accused's account instead of the rightful merchant. 
""This method formed the core of his fraud scheme, and we suspect there could be more victims involved,"" DCP Banthia added. He further said police suspect more victims may be involved and that the investigation will continue based on the seized devices and analysis of the accused's mobile phone and digital data. (ANI)
