842.0,"Haryana strengthens cyber policing, posts 31% fraud fund restoration rate - The Times of India",The Times of India,"Mon, 06 Jul 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/city/chandigarh/haryana-strengthens-cyber-policing-posts-31-fraud-fund-restoration-rate/articleshow/132223083.cms,success_decoder,success,"Haryana strengthens cyber policing, posts 31% fraud fund restoration rate",Jul,2026-07-06,"Chandigarh: Haryana has achieved a 31% recovery rate in restoring money lost to cyber fraud, while removing over 14,000 unlawful online contents in the first six months of 2026, officials said on Monday.
Officials said Haryana secured 2,241 restoration orders in 7,316 cyber fraud cases through the Money Restoration Module (MRM), achieving a recovery rate over eight times the national average of 3.85%. The Money Restoration Module (MRM) is an online govt portal that allows cyber crime victims to file claims for the restoration and refund of money that was fraudulently transferred from their bank accounts.
The higher recovery rate was attributed to dedicated monitoring by the State Cyber Crime Coordination Centre (S4C), prompt follow-up with victims and faster processing of indemnity bonds for releasing frozen funds.
Chief secretary Anurag Rastogi reviewed the performance during a meeting on the state's PRAGATI cyber policing action plan. He directed departments to strengthen AI-based surveillance, technology-driven policing and cyber investigation to counter evolving digital threats.
Crackdown on organised cybercrime
The review also highlighted Haryana Police's crackdown on organised cybercrime, particularly in Nuh, identified as a cybercrime hotspot. 
Between Jan 2025 and June 2026, police registered 473 suo motu FIRs, arrested 927 cybercriminals, and seized 751 mobile phones and 1,442 SIM cards. Across the state, 3,947 cybercriminals were arrested during the first half of 2026.
Police also blocked 43,354 mobile numbers and 5,007 linked IMEIs using intelligence-led analysis to disrupt organised cyber fraud networks.
The S4C, established in Panchkula in 2023, has expanded its operations with dedicated AI integration, social media monitoring and other specialised units. Haryana also operationalised the e-Zero FIR system on June 25, enabling cyber fraud complaints involving over Rs 1 lakh received through the national 1930 helpline to be automatically converted into Zero FIRs for immediate investigation.
Through the Sahyog Portal, authorities removed 14,139 unlawful online contents, including phishing websites, fake advertisements and fraudulent social media posts, between January and June.
The state has also stepped up capacity building, training more than 9,100 police personnel in cyber investigation since Jan 2025. Haryana responded to over 97% of inter-state requests through the Samanvaya Portal and organised 1,322 public awareness programmes reaching nearly 3.14 lakh people.
Rastogi directed officials to expedite manpower approvals, strengthen AI-enabled cyber intelligence and ensure faster investigation, prosecution and victim assistance to further improve cyber policing. 
