891.0,AI tackles learner licence fraud - Bangalore Mirror,Bangalore Mirror,"Sun, 14 Dec 2025 08:00:00 GMT",https://bangaloremirror.indiatimes.com/bangalore/others/ai-tackles-learner-licence-fraud/articleshow/125953797.cms,success_decoder,success,AI tackles learner licence fraud,Sridhar Vivan,2026-08-30,"AI tackles learner licence fraud
.
Highlights
The Transport Department’s AI monitors online LLR tests to detect fraud and link records to the Sarathi Parivahan Portal
Karnataka deploys Smart Lock system to curb proxy exam fraud 
The Karnataka Transport Department has rolled out a cutting-edge ‘Smart Lock’ system across the state to prevent malpractice in the Learner’s Licence (LLR) exam. The initiative leverages artificial intelligence to ensure transparency and curb rampant complaints of candidates obtaining LLRs without appearing for the online exam by paying middlemen, cyber centres, or driving schools.
Rampant LLR fraud
Investigations revealed a widespread scheme where proxy candidates appear for online LLR exams, allowing applicants to secure learner licences and later permanent driving licences (DLs) without attending. Thousands reportedly exploit this loophole daily, often lacking basic knowledge of driving and traffic rules, leading to a surge in road accidents. The Transport Department, recognising the threat, has deployed the Smart Lock system to close these loopholes and ensure real-time monitoring.
After submitting an application, candidates must appear in front of a computer, laptop, or mobile screen. The AI-based software monitors behaviour during the exam, tracking head movement, hand gestures, and eye focus. Each candidate is photographed and recorded, while timed questions on traffic rules, symbols, and road signs appear on the screen. Any attempt by a proxy or receiving remote help is detected, and the system automatically invalidates the exam, rejecting the application.
A transport department official said, “The Smart Lock system is integrated with the Sarathi Parivahan Service Portal, making all LLR and DL processes—from document submission to fee payment—fully online. Only two applications are permitted per IP address, preventing cyber centres, driving schools, or middlemen from submitting multiple applications for several candidates. Attempts to bypass this limit result in automatic rejection.”
Technology-driven oversight
According to Transport Commissioner AM Yogish, this AI-enabled system functions as a real-time digital invigilator approved by the central government. It ensures that LLR exams are taken legitimately, maintaining the integrity of the licensing process while reducing fraud and enhancing road safety. Bengaluru takes the lead in using AI to make driving licence issuance more secure and efficient, combining convenience with stronger anti-fraud measures.
