- From Constable Scam To High tech Verification: MP To Roll Out Facial Recognition In All Exams From July
Monday Special22 FIRs in 9 districts over Aadhaar photo tampering:After biometric scam in police exam, MP Board to switch to face recognition for recruitment tests
- Copy link
MP Employee Selection Board will now use face recognition technology instead of biometric (fingerprints) to identify candidates in recruitment examinations. As a pilot project, candidate verification will be done using the new technology in the Pre-Nursing Selection Test on June 24.
Employee Selection Board Director Saket Malviya says that this technology will be implemented in all examinations from July after this.
It should be noted that the Employee Selection Board is using biometric technology to identify candidates. However, fraudsters breached the biometric technology in the Police Constable Recruitment Examination held in 2023. Investigation revealed that selected candidates had changed their photos and biometric data in Aadhar cards to allow solvers to take the exam in their place.
So far, 22 FIRs have been filed in 9 districts of the state regarding this fraud. Read in the Monday story how biometric technology was breached? How will candidates be verified with the new technology? How foolproof is the new technology compared to biometric technology?
Understand from this case how biometric technology was breached? Atendra Singh Rana, a resident of Sabalgarh in Murena district, was selected as a police constable. He arrived to join the 13th battalion of SSF. His fingerprints taken during appointment did not match with the fingerprints taken during the examination. Then his Aadhar card update history was checked.
It was found that he had updated his Aadhar card several times before and after the exam. Before the exam, Atendra updated the solver's photo in his Aadhar card. After this, the solver took the exam in his place. After the exam was over, Atendra updated his Aadhar again. In this, he changed the solver's photo and put his own picture.
During document verification, when biometric check was done, thumb impressions did not match, which raised suspicion of irregularity. After confirmation of fraud, a case was registered at Madhoganj police station on the complaint of Sub-Inspector Hariom of 13th Battalion. On June 19, police arrested Atendra Singh Rana.
Face recognition technology will now be used After the fraud cases that emerged in Police Constable Recruitment Exam 2023, the Employee Selection Board is now going to use face recognition technology for candidate verification. The Board's Director Saket Malviya told Bhaskar that candidate verification in the Pre-Nursing Selection Test to be held on June 24 will be done using this new technology.
This is a pilot project. The same technology will be used for other exams to be held this year as well. Face recognition technology was launched by UIDAI in 2021. It uses artificial intelligence and machine learning to perform face verification.
This is how Face Recognition System will be implemented
- The Staff Selection Board will request data from UIDAI. UIDAI provides this verification data free of charge to central and state government undertakings.
- Software will be needed to match Aadhaar data with the facial print data of the candidate. This will verify whether the person taking the exam is genuine or not.
- Thumb impression machines are used for biometric verification. Cameras will be needed for this. Camera-equipped tablets or mobile phones will be used.
How will verification be done with new technology in exam hall Employee Selection Board Director Saket Malviya explains that verification will be done exactly the same way as biometric verification was done until now. Upon entering the exam hall, candidates will need to show their admit card and a valid ID proof (Aadhar, PAN or driving license) to prove their identity.
These documents will be checked to ensure that the candidate's name and photo on the admit card matches with the name and photo on the valid ID card. Previously, fingerprints were taken at the registration desk, but now verification will be done through facial recognition. The face will be matched with the photo registered in Aadhaar card or other valid documents, only then will the candidate be allowed entry into the hall.
1 Candidate will be verified in one minute According to Staff Selection Board Director Saket Malviya, biometric verification of 3 candidates happens in 1 minute. Now with face recognition technology, verification of one candidate will take 1 minute. The board will have to set exam schedules keeping this new technology in mind.
Problems may arise if photo is not updated in Aadhar Cyber expert Yasdeep Chaturvedi says that problems may arise during verification in some cases. Sometimes it happens that the Aadhar card does not have a photo with glasses, but that person wears glasses. Then verification can be done by removing the glasses.
Sometimes problems can arise due to facial injury or major changes. For this, it is better that the photo in Aadhar should not be very old but updated and it should be taken properly in good lighting.
Being used for elderly life certificates and NEET exam Since the implementation of face authentication by UIDAI in 2021, its biggest use has been for issuing life certificates to the elderly. Previously, where seniors had to stand in line every year to get life certificates for pension, now proof of being alive can be given through ""AadhaarFaceRd"" and ""Jeevan Pramaan Face App"".
Additionally, it is being used as a pilot project in the NEET exam. At Madhya Pradesh's Vallabh Bhavan (Ministry), attendance of officers and employees is being recorded using the Aadhaar-based face attendance app.
