952.0,"Is cyber fraud a private dispute? Rajasthan High Court draws line, denies anticipatory bail in Rs 80 lakh ‘digital arrest’ case - The Indian Express",The Indian Express,"Thu, 02 Apr 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://indianexpress.com/article/legal-news/rajasthan-high-court-digital-arrest-scam-bail-denied-elderly-woman-10615636/,success_decoder,success,"Is cyber fraud a private dispute? Rajasthan High Court draws line, denies anticipatory bail in Rs 80 lakh ‘digital arrest’ case",Vineet Upadhyay; Annu,2026-04-02,"5 min readNew DelhiApr 2, 2026 07:00 PM IST 					
						First published on: Apr 2, 2026 at 07:00 PM IST 					
				
							 
			
				
Rajasthan High Court news: The Rajasthan High Court has refused anticipatory bail to an accused in an alleged Rs 80 lakh “digital arrest” scam, holding that such serious economic offences cannot be brushed aside on the basis of private settlements.
Justice Sameer Jain was hearing an anticipatory bail application filed by accused Naveen Temani in connection with the “digital arrest” of an 83-year-old woman.
“In cases involving serious economic offences and organised cyber frauds, the element of societal impact assumes significance, and such matters cannot be lightly compounded on the basis of a purported settlement,” the Rajasthan High Court said on April 1.
Compromise rejected
The case involves allegations of a sophisticated cyber fraud racket in which an 83-year-old woman was allegedly duped into transferring Rs 80 lakh under the pretext of a “digital arrest.” The Rajasthan High Court noted that the matter reflects a grave instance of organised cybercrime with international dimensions.
The accused had sought bail on the ground that a compromise had been reached with the complainant in what was described as a commercial dispute. However, the court found this contention unconvincing. It noted that the alleged compromise was not even signed by the accused himself but by his father, who was also an accused and reportedly absconding.
More importantly, the court held that the nature of the offence, being an organised cyber fraud, transcends private disputes and impacts society at large.
 Justice Sameer Jain was hearing the anticipatory bail plea filed by the accused Naveen Temani.
‘Digital arrest’, financial exploitation
The elderly complainant was allegedly subjected to psychological coercion and manipulation, forcing her to transfer her life’s savings from her bank account. The Rajasthan High Court recorded that the victim suffered severe mental trauma, depression, and required hospitalisation following the incident.
It also took note of her fragile condition, observing that she appeared before the court in a wheelchair and was struggling to meet medical and daily expenses.
Complex modus operandi, international links
The investigation revealed a layered and organised criminal operation involving multiple actors and financial channels. The defrauded amount was routed through 34 mule accounts across different layers and subsequently transferred through hawala channels to foreign jurisdictions, including Dubai.
Authorities further alleged that the proceeds of crime were used to purchase cryptocurrencies such as Bitcoin and USDT, indicating the use of digital assets to obscure financial trails.
The Rajasthan High Court noted that 187 complaints had been registered on the relevant cybercrime portal, pointing to the scale and pattern of the alleged racket.
State opposes bail
Opposing the bail plea, the state and cybercrime authorities argued that the accused was a key conspirator in an international fraud network. Senior police officers, including the director general of police (cyber crime) and the superintendent of police, informed the court that the case involves multiple accused and transnational operations.
The funds were routed through hawala channels and digital platforms, and electronic devices and SIM cards are yet to be recovered, the officers said, while informing the Rajasthan High Court that custodial interrogation was necessary to uncover the larger conspiracy.
The prosecution stressed that granting anticipatory bail at this stage would seriously hamper the investigation.
Victim’s distress highlights human cost
The complainant told the court that she was left with no money and was dependent on a monthly pension of Rs 35,000 for survival.
She stated that she had agreed to the compromise under compulsion due to her deteriorating health and financial distress, even expressing willingness to accept a fraction of the defrauded amount merely to sustain herself.
The court acknowledged her precarious condition, noting that she remained in financial and emotional distress at an advanced age.
The Rajasthan High Court underscored that the investigation was at a crucial stage, with 18 people already arrested, including the accused’s father. It held that custodial interrogation was necessary to recover digital evidence and trace the larger network.
Granting anticipatory bail in such circumstances, the court observed, would impede the investigation and undermine efforts to dismantle organised cybercrime networks.
Relief for victim
While denying bail, the court took a humanitarian view regarding the complainant’s condition. Invoking its inherent powers, it directed the trial court to expeditiously consider releasing Rs 13.40 lakh, part of the recovered amount, to the victim.
The court instructed that such an application be decided within seven days, with due sensitivity to the complainant’s financial and medical needs.
Strong message against cybercrime
In conclusion, the Rajasthan High Court held that the gravity of the offence, the organised manner in which it was carried out, and its broader societal impact outweighed the grounds for granting anticipatory bail.
The ruling reinforces a growing judicial stance that cyber fraud, especially targeting vulnerable individuals, cannot be treated as a private dispute capable of settlement, but must be addressed as a serious threat to public trust and digital security.",825,,True,False
954.0,"Rajasthan Police Strike Cyber Fraud Financial Chain, 20 Arrested in Bundi - The420.in",The420.in,"Mon, 20 Jul 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://the420.in/rajasthan-police-cyber-shield-bundi-mule-account-arrests/,success_decoder,success,"Rajasthan Police Strike Cyber Fraud Financial Chain, 20 Arrested in Bundi",The,2026-07-20,"In a targeted strike against the financial shadow infrastructure powering digital fraud across Northern India, law enforcement authorities in Rajasthan have arrested 20 individuals for allegedly renting their personal bank accounts to organized cybercrime syndicates. The synchronised arrests, executed in the Nainwa region of Bundi district, form part of a broader state-wide initiative known as Operation Cyber Shield. By targeting domestic account holders who facilitate the movement of stolen capital, investigators are shifting their strategy from chasing anonymous online scammers to systematically dismantling the underlying banking pipelines that keep cyber fraud alive.
The operation exposes the pivotal role played by “mule accounts” in contemporary digital financial crimes. Cybercriminals routinely avoid conducting illicit transactions using bank accounts registered under their own names or immediate networks. Instead, syndicates actively recruit vulnerable individuals, unemployed youth, and rural residents with promises of easy commissions, part-time work, or small cash payouts in exchange for their passbooks, net banking credentials, and linked mobile SIM cards.
Once acquired, these rented accounts serve as immediate landing zones for proceeds siphoned from victims across the country. Illicit funds are routed through multiple layers of secondary and tertiary bank accounts within minutes, baffling automated banking security triggers and allowing syndicates to cash out via automated teller machines or peer-to-peer cryptocurrency networks. The speed of these multi-tiered transfers severely restricts the intervention window available to financial security teams.
Disrupting the Financial Pipeline of Digital Syndicates
The Nainwa crackdown specifically targeted a network of local account holders whose banking facilities were repeatedly flagged in nationwide cybercrime complaints. According to Bundi district police, the individuals arrested—including Chetram, Khushiram, Anjani Kumar, and 17 others—acted as crucial financial enablers by providing active bank accounts to route fraud proceeds. Official records indicate that the accused received financial compensation or percentage-based commissions for every transaction routed through their accounts.
Investigating officers conducted extensive forensic analysis of financial logs, transaction velocity, and communication trails before moving in to make the arrests. Preliminary findings indicate that several of the seized accounts experienced high-volume, short-duration fund flows that bore no relationship to the account holders’ documented income profiles or local commercial activity. In many instances, large sums deposited from victim accounts were withdrawn at local ATMs within hours.
Rajasthan Police have intensified hotspot mapping across districts like Bundi, Jaipur, and Jhalawar under the Cyber Shield campaign. Across the State, law enforcement teams working alongside digital forensic experts have frozen hundreds of suspect bank accounts and blocked thousands of fraudulent Unified Payments Interface handles to cut off criminal cash flows. These proactive measures have disrupted millions of rupees in fraudulent transfers before they could be integrated into the shadow economy.
High-Tech Intelligence Meets Ground Enforcement
The success of the Nainwa operation relies heavily on inter-agency data sharing anchored by the Indian Cyber Crime Coordination Centre, an attached office of the Union Ministry of Home Affairs. Through the National Cybercrime Reporting Portal and the SAMANVAYA management platform, law enforcement agencies aggregate real-time transaction data and cross-reference complaint histories across state borders. This national repository gives district investigators visibility into interstate cybercrime patterns that were previously hidden.
This centralized intelligence allows local police stations to identify bank accounts that appear as recurring nodes in financial fraud networks spanning multiple jurisdictions. When a victim in any part of the country reports a fraudulent transfer via the national 1930 helpline, the transaction metadata is automatically cross-matched against national registries. This automated linkage exposes the underlying web of mule accounts operating quietly in rural hubs like Bundi.
The integration of centralized digital registries has fundamentally altered local law enforcement operations. By marrying national intelligence feeds with swift ground-level execution, district police can act on actionable leads before illicit capital can be fully withdrawn or converted into untraceable assets. The model demonstrates how regional police forces can leverage central digital infrastructure to neutralize complex financial crimes.
Regulatory Tightening and Legal Reclassification
The enforcement drive in Rajasthan reflects a wider national pivot towards holding financial enablers legally accountable. Police departments across several states are increasingly invoking organized crime provisions under the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita against individuals who knowingly rent or sell their financial credentials. State authorities emphasize that claiming ignorance of how an account was utilized provides no legal protection once financial benefit or negligence is established.
At the policy level, the Union Government has accelerated measures to curb the proliferation of mule networks across the banking sector. The Indian Cyber Crime Coordination Centre recently partnered with the Reserve Bank Innovation Hub to integrate artificial intelligence tools, such as MuleHunter.ai, directly into commercial banking systems. By feeding data from the national Suspect Registry into bank risk management algorithms, regulators aim to automatically flag and freeze suspicious accounts in real time.
While state campaigns like Operation Cyber Shield demonstrate the effectiveness of targeted enforcement, authorities acknowledge that public awareness remains the primary line of defense. Police agencies continue to issue advisories urging citizens to safeguard their banking details and report suspicious financial solicitations immediately. Dismantling the domestic financial infrastructure powering cybercrime remains essential to securing India’s rapidly expanding digital economy and protecting vulnerable citizens from exploitation.",847,,True,False
959.0,Doctor-pharmacy nexus busted in RGHS scam uncovered by AI audit: Rajasthan govt suspends Dr. Manoj Jain ove... - Bhaskar English,Bhaskar English,"Mon, 28 Apr 2025 07:00:00 GMT",https://www.bhaskarenglish.in/local/rajasthan/jaipur/news/sms-hospital-doctor-suspended-in-multi-crore-rghs-scam-uncovered-by-ai-audit-134930704.html,success_decoder,success,"Doctor-pharmacy nexus busted in RGHS scam uncovered by AI audit: Rajasthan govt suspends Dr. Manoj Jain over fake OPD slips, crores billed illegally - Jaipur News",,2025-04-29,"- English News
