Doctor-pharmacy nexus busted in RGHS scam uncovered by AI audit:Rajasthan govt suspends Dr. Manoj Jain over fake OPD slips, crores billed illegally
- Copy link
The doctor Manoj Kumar Jain of Sawai Man Singh (SMS) Hospital, who was involved in a scam worth crores of rupees in the Rajasthan government's free treatment scheme (RGHS), has been suspended. During the suspension period, Dr. Jain will be attached to the headquarters of the Medical and Health Department.
After the matter came to light, the Deputy Secretary of the Government, Syed Siraj Ali Zaidi, issued orders for the immediate suspension of Dr. Manoj Jain.
How the Fraud Happened Under the RGHS scheme, government employees and pensioners receive free treatment and medicines. The medicine prescribed by the doctor is available for free from an authorized medical store. Later, the medical store prepares a bill and receives payment from the finance department.
Taking wrongful advantage of this system, Dr. Manoj Jain fraudulently prepared more than 500 OPD slips from his private clinic. Expensive medicines were prescribed on these slips without any mention of tests or illnesses.
Based on these slips, Arpita Medical Store and Laxmi Medical Store in Jaipur made bills worth crores of rupees and claimed them from the finance department.
Finance Department Detected Fraud with the Help of AI The Rajasthan government issues a card under the Rajasthan Government Health Scheme (RGHS) for free treatment to government employees and pensioners in 14 categories. Through this card, a government employee can avail cashless treatment for any illness. The medicines prescribed by the doctor are available at an RGHS authorized medical store without any payment.
The medical store later prepares a bill for the medicine and sends it to the finance department for payment. When claims worth crores of rupees suddenly increased from January to March, the finance department became suspicious. An audit conducted with the help of Artificial Intelligence (AI) revealed the entire fraud.
Case Study: Two medical stores sent bills worth crores, prescriptions from the same doctor found From two pharmacies (medicine shops) in Jaipur, bills worth crores were sent to the finance department for approval through prescriptions written by the same doctor. When the audit team examined those prescriptions, many surprising facts came to light.
In fact, medicines were prescribed on the doctor's OPD slips without any medical history, symptoms of the disease, or any type of examination written. Two pharmacies, Arpita Medical Store and Lakshmi Medical Store, billed for those medicines. Then those bills were sent to the RGHS portal for claims.
Editing on the slips Dr. Manoj Kumar Jain was a General Physician at SMS Hospital. The OPD was issued on the slip of his private clinic 'Aarav Diabetes and Multi-Specialty Unit'. However, the seal (stamp) of SMS Hospital was used on the OPD.
According to the rules, a government doctor cannot conduct OPD on a private clinic's slip. In such a case, a method of editing was devised. By hiding the name of the private clinic written at the top of the OPD slip, the upper part of Dr. Manoj Kumar Jain's SMS OPD slip was placed over it and a photo was taken.
Then that OPD slip was sent to the RGHS portal for claiming. Tampering has been revealed in about 500 such OPD slips. In the investigation by the finance department, one slip came to light in which the name of an RGHS cardholder Mohanlal Sharma's wife was written as Sushil Sharma instead of Sushila, and the OPD slip was made twice. The third time, the bill was cut with the real name Sushila Sharma, so that the fraud would not be caught.
Such discrepancies are coming to light in the finance department's audit
- Medical stores and doctors are colluding to prepare fake prescriptions in the name of RGHS cardholders.
- The finance department believes that if it were written in the presence of the patient, this would not have been possible.
- The prescriptions mention the results of the patient's general blood test, but no related test report has been attached.
- No medical history of the patient has been written on the doctor's OPD prescriptions.
- The presence of the doctor's private clinic name on the prescription and hiding it on the portal is evidence of a major fraud.
In this case, the RGHS licenses of both pharmacies have been revoked.
How is a claim raised? To raise a claim, medical stores log in to the RGHS website. During this process, it is necessary to scan and upload the pharmacy bill, the doctor's valid prescription (OPD slip), and the patient's RGHS card.
After uploading all the documents, you need to click on the ‘Submit’ button, immediately after which a unique TID (Transaction ID) number is generated. This TID number can be used to track the status of the claim online later.
It is important to note that bills can only be claimed if the medicines are purchased from a pharmacy included in the RGHS panel. The validity of the doctor's prescription should not exceed seven days. Additionally, bills for a maximum of one month's medicines can be claimed at a time. If the prescription is from a private doctor, it is mandatory to upload it on the portal for the claim to be approved.
