970.0,Punjab CM fake video suspect Jagman Samra hit with cryptocurrency scam charge. - The Times of India,The Times of India,"Fri, 07 Nov 2025 08:00:00 GMT",https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/city/ludhiana/punjab-cm-fake-video-suspect-jagman-samra-hit-with-cryptocurrency-scam-charge-/articleshow/125168756.cms,success_decoder,success,Punjab CM fake video suspect Jagman Samra hit with cryptocurrency scam charge. | Ludhiana News - The Times of India,Payal Dhawan,2025-11-07,"Ludhiana: Jagman Samra, a Canadian resident from Sangrur, whose name first surfaced in a controversy over circulating alleged ""AI-generated"" videos of Punjab chief minister Bhagwant Singh Mann, has seen his legal troubles expand significantly.
The Ludhiana police have now charged Samra and his son, Harkirat Singh, with cheating, criminal conspiracy, and forgery related to an elaborate cryptocurrency investment scam. Police have also issued a lookout circular (LOC) against the father and son.
The Cryptocurrency Fraud
The case originated from an FIR lodged by the Cyber Police Station on October 24, based on ""information"" received by Sub Inspector Harinderpal Singh. Police stated they learned the accused were defrauding people by luring them with promises of high benefits through investments in a nonexistent cryptocurrency called ""5k coin.""
Four co-accused â Parmel Singh, Gurpreet Singh, Bachittar Singh, and chartered accountant Kartik Mittal â have already been arrested by city police. Additional deputy commissioner of police (ADCP-headquarters) Vaibhav Sehgal confirmed the charges. 
""The FIR was lodged on the basis of the information that all these accused have been defrauding people in the name of investment in cryptocurrency and immigration work,"" Sehgal said.
Unraveling the Conspiracy
During their interrogation, the arrested accused identified Samra and his son as the main conspirators running the fraud network. The police probe revealed that the local accused first connected with Jagman Samra through social media advertisements for crypto investment. Parmel Singh initially invested about â¹50,000, Gurpreet Singh about â¹20,000, and Bachittar Singh about â¹40,000.
Sehgal explained: ""Jagman would send them some screenshots showing that they were gaining profit. However, gradually they realised that it was fraud."" Instead of taking action, the three men allegedly joined Samra's operation, lured by the promise of high profits. They began persuading others to invest in the fraudulent ""5k coin"" (cryptocurrency) and were paid a commission of 2-to-3% on invested money, plus a monthly salary of â¹20,000 each.
Hawala Transfers and New Plans
The investigation further uncovered sophisticated mechanisms used to handle the illicit funds. ""The probe also revealed that the accused would transfer money through hawala,"" ADCP Sehgal said, referring to an unofficial method of transferring money without physical movement. He added that the accused were reportedly planning to establish a biotech company to facilitate future transactions, with the arrested chartered accountant, Kartik Mittal, assisting them.
Police have, so far, frozen about five bank accounts related to the case. The exact total amount of money duped from people has yet to be ascertained. However, the arrested individuals revealed they came into contact with Samra in March or April and managed to open at least three offices in Sangrur, Moga, and Chandigarh within a few months, indicating a quick expansion of the fraud network.
