985.0,"AI ‘double-edged sword’, becomes new tool for fraudsters, say experts - The Times of India",The Times of India,"Sun, 31 Aug 2025 07:00:00 GMT",https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/city/hyderabad/ai-double-edged-sword-becomes-new-tool-for-fraudsters-say-experts/articleshow/123606410.cms,success_decoder,success,"AI âdouble-edged swordâ, becomes new tool for fraudsters, say experts | Hyderabad News - The Times of India",Yashaswini Sri,2025-08-31,"Hyderabad: Every day, the Telangana Cyber Security Bureau fields nearly 250 calls on cybercrime, with financial losses averaging Rs 5 crore daily. Against this backdrop, experts warned that Artificial Intelligence (AI), while transformative, is also being rapidly weaponised by fraudsters.
At TRUST AICS â 2025: Where AI Integrates Governance, Cybersecurity, and Privacy, organised by ISACA Hyderabad Chapter, Shikha Goel, director of the Telangana Cyber Security Bureau, cautioned that AI is a ""double-edged sword."" 
""While fraudsters are using it to devise innovative scams, banks and security agencies are also deploying AI to strengthen algorithms and safeguard public money. Without trust, technology collapses into fear, governance loses legitimacy, and privacy becomes a delusion,"" she said. Goel emphasised the importance of accountability, collaboration, and capacity-building, stressing that AI must be harnessed as a shield for society, rather than a threat.
While criminals exploit AI, businesses are also beginning to use it as a safeguard to protect themselves. 
Vasanth Kumar, director of compliance at Darwinbox, explained how AI is reshaping corporate governance. ""AI can now review compliance evidence itself, instead of waiting for an auditor. 
Systems can flag violations in real time, and in some cases, even prevent them before they occur. That's the roadmap most companies are working on,"" he said.
Large Language Models (LLMs) are central to current AI advancements, with a focus on data consumption, regional operation, data localisation, and privacy assurances. But beyond efficiency, speakers highlighted the issue of liability in AI. 
Anuraag Gorty, security lead at Glean, pointed to well-known failures, such as facial recognition systems that misidentify darker skin tones and a 2018 hiring tool by a company that favoured male applicants. ""AI liability cannot rest on a single actor. Developers must ensure data diversity, deploying organisations must run fairness and bias checks, and regulators must both guide and enforce. Responsibility is therefore shared across all three,"" he noted.
Adding a legal perspective, Sameer Avasarala, principal associate at Lakshmikumaran & Sridharan Associates, said accountability must extend into the legal domain. ""AI raises not just technical but legal questions: who is liable if something goes wrong, the developer, the deployer, or the user? The legal framework demands clarity, with regulators expected not only to issue guidelines but also to enforce them,"" he said.",374,,True,False
