1016.0,"'Why not use AI to freeze bank accounts of cyber criminals', SC asks RBI on digital arrest scams; orders Pan-India CBI probe - Details - ET Now",ET Now,"Mon, 01 Dec 2025 08:00:00 GMT",https://www.etnownews.com/news/why-not-use-ai-to-freeze-bank-accounts-of-cyber-criminals-sc-asks-rbi-on-digital-arrest-scams-orders-pan-india-cbi-probe-details-article-153230766,success_decoder,success,"'Why not use AI to freeze bank accounts of cyber criminals', SC asks RBI on digital arrest scams; orders Pan-India CBI probe - Details",Shriti Aniraj,2025-12-01,"TRENDING :
Highlights
- SC orders unified pan-India CBI investigation into digital arrest scams
- RBI asked to explain why AI is not used to detect and freeze fraud-linked accounts
- States, telecom operators and IT intermediaries told to tighten coordination and curb cybercrime loopholes
The Supreme Court on Monday directed the Central Bureau of Investigation to lead a nationwide investigation into the rising number of digital arrest scams, marking the strongest judicial intervention yet against a crime that has spread rapidly across the country.
A Bench led by Chief Justice of India Surya Kant and Justice Joymalya Bagchi asked all States, including West Bengal, Tamil Nadu, Karnataka and Telangana, to grant consent to the CBI so that investigations are not fragmented across jurisdictions. The order stems from a suo motu case triggered by a complaint from an elderly couple in Haryana.
The Department of Telecom was told to ensure telecom operators stop issuing multiple SIM cards to single users or entities, a loophole often exploited by scammers. States and Union Territories must also establish regional cybercrime coordination centres to strengthen cooperation with central agencies.
The court further instructed the CBI to investigate bank officials suspected of enabling mule accounts used to launder fraudulent payments. It reiterated that all police agencies and the CBI are empowered to freeze accounts involved in cyber fraud, PTI reported.
