In Myanmar's Myawaddy, scam centers run by Chinese gangs use AI-generated models and stolen TikTok data to deceive millions. Captives, including Vidhan Gautam from India, were forced to trap targets into investing in fake websites. These hi-tech scams target individuals from countries like Turkey and Russia, pressuring them into huge financial losses.
Vidhan Gautam, who was held captive in one such centre in Myanmar's border town of Myawaddy, returned home to Dehradun after two months of what he termed as ""an experience straight out of hell"". He went to Thailand after an alleged 'lucrative job offer' in May.
Sharing a first-hand insight into the world of the hi-tech scamsters, the 24-year-old told
TOI
, ""The modus operandi is to use AI-generated female models with popular names for video calls. The 'targets' are contacted after stealing their personal information from TikTok accounts. To deceive, they hire actual models whose faces are replaced with an AI-generated face of a popular woman on TikTok.""
He added: ""The scamsters are using an in-house-developed mobile app, enabling centre operatives to run 30 different WhatsApp accounts simultaneously on a single phone. Apart from this, they run a fake investment website on which they exhort their targets, especially millionaires from Turkey and Russia, to invest money by promising them lucrative returns.""
Bulk of the operations are being conducted by people from other countries who are held captive by them, he pointed out, adding, ""Each captive is given a Chinese name by the abductors. For instance, I was given the name 'Qian Liu' (âmoney axeâ in English) which the Chinese handlers used to call me and also while communicating among themselves.""
He further said that they had given all the abductees a ""target of duping people to the amount of $2 mn in one-and-half-years or face torture"".
âIt is like a sophisticated industry. Every new captive would be given a four-day tutorial on how to contact a fresh target. Most of the targets were from countries like Turkey, Russia, Kazakhstan and Pakistan where the TikTok app is still allowed.""
As per their ""tutorial"", on day 1, the operatives were supposed to share greetings with a general introduction. On day 2, after taking the targeted men into confidence, they were to dig out their personal information like marital status and income. Subsequently, the operative while chatting as a ""model"", would send some emotional messages, for instance, she was in dire financial need and he could help her by investing in an online trading website -- which was a completely fake one developed by the scamsters.
The 'targets' would be initially given returns to gain their confidence, and lure them to invest more. Once they invested a hefty amount, they were blocked from withdrawing it.
""I've seen people being duped of $80,000 to $1 million in a single stroke,"" he added.
Among the abductees, most were Ethiopians followed by Moroccans, Sri Lankans, Indians, Pakistanis, Bangladeshis, Malaysians and Chinese, Gautam said.
âI also want to caution all that if they are getting any âlucrative job offer' in Mae Sot district or any other area in Thailand other than those of a cook in a restaurant or cab driver, they should know that it is a scam,â he said.
The Doon youngster was released from the scam centre on July 20 before spending a month in a Myanmar jail for entering the country illegally. From there, he reached Thailand on Aug 19 and finally reached back to India early this week.
