An Ahmedabad resident living under the name Abdul Rehman Khan has come under police scrutiny after passport verification allegedly revealed that he had assumed a false identity and obtained several government documents without formally converting his religion.
The Special Operations Group (SOG) has launched an investigation after discrepancies emerged when the accused applied for a fresh passport.
Passport verification links Danilimda resident to Ayodhya
Abdul Rehman Khan, who lives in Janta Row House near Barrel Market in Ahmedabad’s Danilimda area, had applied for a new passport after his old one expired.
During the verification process, officials examined his previous passport and found that it had been issued from Lucknow in the name of Yogendra Kumar Maurya, a resident of Ayodhya, formerly known as Faizabad.
Following the discovery, the matter was referred to the SOG for further investigation.
Man allegedly adopted Muslim identity after marriage
During questioning, the accused allegedly told investigators that his real name is Yogendra Maurya and that he had eloped with Khushnumabanu in 2011 after the two fell in love.
According to police, the couple first travelled to Delhi, where they got married under Hindu customs through the Arya Samaj. They later obtained a nikahnama, or Muslim marriage certificate, in which Yogendra allegedly used the name Abdul Rehman Khan.
Police said the accused did not formally convert to Islam before assuming the new identity.
Fake PAN card allegedly used to obtain government documents
Investigators alleged that after moving to Ahmedabad, Yogendra paid ₹400 to a person near Jamalpur Bridge to procure a PAN card in the name of Abdul Rehman Khan.
Using the PAN card, he allegedly obtained several government documents, including an Aadhaar card, voter ID card and ration card. The accused currently lives in Ahmedabad with his wife and three children.
Overseas job plan exposes alleged fraud
Police said Yogendra recently applied for a passport as he wanted to travel abroad for work. The alleged discrepancies surfaced during the verification process, exposing the use of multiple identities.
According to officials, the accused allegedly adopted a Muslim name and procured government documents without completing the legal process required for religious conversion.
The SOG has registered a case and initiated further investigation into the alleged forgery and the procurement of official documents.",398,,True,False
