‘I’m Govindbhai Dholakia’:New scam uses deepfake video to dupe people, claims company near Rajkot airport, promises registration for ₹1,500; retired DySP exposes fraud
- Copy link
A social media fraud involving the identity and reputation of Surat industrialist and Rajya Sabha MP Govindbhai Dholakia has come to light. Fraudsters allegedly created fake social media profiles and used his old photographs and videos to produce deepfake clips.
In the videos, the scammers allegedly impersonated Dholakia by saying, ""I am Govindbhai Dholakia speaking,"" and promoted misleading advertisements claiming that old currency coins were being purchased at high prices on behalf of the Reserve Bank of India (RBI).
Fraudsters sought Rs 1,500 as UPI processing fee
According to the complaint, Bhavesh Valjibhai Lathiya, personal secretary to Govindbhai Dholakia and director of SRK Company in Katargam, approached the Cyber Crime police over the alleged fraud.
The scammers reportedly created multiple social media accounts using names such as 'Old Coin Company'. They allegedly approached people interested in selling old coins and promised them high prices.
Victims were then asked to transfer Rs 1,500 through UPI on the pretext of processing fees or other charges. The complaint alleges that the payment request was part of the fraudulent scheme.
Retired DySP uncovers scam while verifying suspicious numbers
The alleged fraud came to light when retired DySP R.G. Jadeja, associated with the Security and Surveillance Department of SRK Company, cross-checked the contact numbers displayed in the misleading videos.
Jadeja reportedly called the numbers and was answered by an unidentified man speaking in Hindi. The caller allegedly claimed to be Govindbhai Dholakia and said he was calling from Rajkot. He then sought an online payment towards charges.
When Jadeja asked further questions about the payment and the transaction, the caller disconnected the phone, raising further suspicion.
Cyber Crime registers case under BNS and IT Act
Govindbhai Dholakia and his SRK organisation have no involvement in the alleged old-coin buying scheme. The complaint states that the fake profiles and deepfake videos were created to misuse Dholakia's identity, damage his public image and allegedly cheat people financially.
Following the complaint, Surat Cyber Crime police registered a case under relevant provisions of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita (BNS) and the Information Technology Act. The police are investigating the fake social media accounts, phone numbers and financial transactions linked to the alleged network.
What is deepfake technology and how can you stay safe?
Deepfake is an AI- and machine learning-based technology that can digitally manipulate a person's face, voice or manner of speaking and make it appear as though they are saying or doing something they never actually did. With increasingly sophisticated tools, such videos can look highly realistic and be difficult to identify as fake.
Cyber police have advised people to remain cautious if someone claiming to be a known person approaches them on social media and asks for money, processing fees or other financial transactions. People should independently verify the person's identity before making any payment.
Police have also urged citizens not to respond to suspicious payment requests and to report such attempts through the cyber helpline or appropriate cybercrime reporting channels.",539,,True,False
