1080.0,"AI-generated Trump video used to dupe people in Karnataka, 200 people lose over Rs 2 crore - The Indian Express",The Indian Express,"Mon, 26 May 2025 07:00:00 GMT",https://indianexpress.com/article/cities/bangalore/ai-generated-trump-video-dupe-people-karnataka-10029617/,success_decoder,success,"AI-generated Trump video used to dupe people in Karnataka, 200 people lose over Rs 2 crore",Express Web Desk,2025-05-26,"Stay updated with the latest - Click here to follow us on Instagram
			This is an archive article published on May 26, 2025
		
	
														
An AI-generated video of US President Donald Trump has been used to dupe more than 200 people in the last few months in several cities of Karnataka as cybercriminals asked them to invest in Trump Hotel rentals, promising higher returns.
According to the police, these cases of cybercrime have been largely reported in Bengaluru, Tumakuru, Mangaluru, and Haveri. In Haveri alone, more than 15 people have reportedly lost money in the scam.
The police said the victims were offered lucrative returns and work-from-home, and investment opportunities. The police said the fraudsters initially asked users to pay Rs 1,500 to set up their accounts, and then assigned them tasks such as writing company profiles. Each completed task appeared to increase their “earnings” on the app dashboard, but the money was never real.
While speaking to indianexpress.com, a 38-year-old advocate claimed he deposited Rs 5,93,240 between January 25 and April 4 this year. “In January this year, I came across shorts on YouTube that offered to invest in the Donald Trump Hotel. Upon clicking the link, it directed me to download a mobile application. I was asked to fill out a form, which I did. It also asked me to add bank account details and IFSC code,” said the advocate.
The advocate said he was then asked to pay a deposit of Rs 1,500, and in return, he got Rs 30 in his profile. “I was paid Rs 30 every day, and I could only withdraw it after it crossed Rs 300. As the money was being paid on time, and I was able to withdraw it, they started asking me to invest more. It started with Rs 5,000 and ended at Rs 1,00,000. Finally, they asked me to pay taxes to withdraw the money, but later they did not return the money,” he added.
“There were days when they lured me with Rs 1,00,000 investment and Rs 1,00,000 returns in 24 hours. I know many people who are in the police, government departments, and businessmen who have lost their money,” he added.
The Haveri Cybercrime Economics and Narcotics (CEN) police have registered a case based on his complaint and are probing.
