Cyber gang busted in Shivpuri for ‘digital arrest’ blackmail:₹2.5 cr fraud uncovered, obscene calls made via 10 apps
- Copy link
A major cyber fraud gang that blackmailed people across India through fake “digital arrest” threats has been exposed in Madhya Pradesh’s Shivpuri district. Police said the gang trapped victims through obscene video calls and extorted money by posing as police officers. The accused are suspected to have cheated people of more than ₹2.5 crore.
The gang operated through mobile apps such as HIIU, TOKKI, MIKA, ELOELO, GAGA, HANI, SUGO, COMMECTO, HITSU, HONEY and COMET, which were downloaded using APK files. People interested in online chatting installed these apps, after which the accused targeted them.
Property worth ₹1 crore seized, including cars and mobile phones
Police have arrested 20 of the 32 named accused so far. During the operation, they seized property worth about ₹1.07 crore. The seized items include seven cars, one motorcycle, 29 mobile phones, 16 ATM cards, seven bank passbooks, a house registry, and ₹1.20 lakh in cash.
Police said one of the accused had constructed a luxury house using money obtained through fraud. The property documents have also been seized.
Gang posed as women, recorded video calls to blackmail victims
Superintendent of Police Aman Singh Rathore said the accused created fake WhatsApp accounts using women’s names and photographs. They sent friend requests to people interested in online chats and later engaged them in obscene conversations and video calls.
The gang secretly recorded these calls and chats. They then contacted victims posing as police officers and threatened to implicate them in serious offences such as rape or child pornography. Victims were told they were under “digital arrest” and were forced to transfer money to settle the case.
Many victims transferred large amounts out of fear of legal action and social embarrassment, police said.
Action taken under Operation Matrix, special teams formed
The crackdown was carried out under “Operation Matrix”, an anti-cybercrime initiative running in the Gwalior zone. Nine special teams were formed in Shivpuri district to trace and arrest the accused.
Police teams from Karera, Bhaunti, Amola, Surwaya, Satanbada, Kotwali, Pichhore and Kolaras police stations conducted joint raids under the supervision of SDOP Karera and SDOP Pichhore.
12 accused absconding, two women among those involved
Police had identified 32 accused in the case. Of them, 20 have been arrested so far, while 12 remain absconding. Two women are also part of the gang. Police said efforts are ongoing to arrest the remaining accused.
Police advise public to stay alert against ‘digital arrest’ fraud
Police have urged people not to accept video calls or chat requests from unknown persons. They said citizens should not panic if someone threatens them with a “digital arrest”.
Victims have been advised to immediately report such incidents at the nearest police station or through the cybercrime helpline. Police also urged people to use social media and chatting apps cautiously to avoid falling prey to such fraud.",511,,True,False
1096.0,'Click the link': A mistake on an answer sheet exposed an alleged AI cheating scandal at MP Central Unive - The Times of India,The Times of India,"Fri, 31 Jul 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/education/news/click-the-link-a-mistake-on-an-answer-sheet-exposed-an-alleged-ai-cheating-scandal-at-mp-central-university/articleshow/132755720.cms,success_decoder,success,'Click the link': A mistake on an answer sheet exposed an alleged AI cheating scandal at MP Central University,TOI Education; Jul,2026-07-31,"What appeared to be an ordinary university answer sheet turned into the starting point of a major investigation after a single sentence raised serious questions about the use of artificial intelligence during examinations. A line that read, ""Click the link to watch the full video answer,"" alerted an examiner to what is now being treated as one of the most unusual alleged cheating cases involving AI at a central university.
The incident has prompted Dr. Harisingh Gour Central University in Madhya Pradesh to withhold the examination results of an entire affiliated college while an inquiry is underway.
A single sentence that changed everything
The controversy began when an evaluator checking answer sheets at Dr. Harisingh Gour Central University noticed an unusual sentence written by a student: ""Click the link to watch the full video answer.""
The line made little sense in a handwritten university examination, but it instantly stood out. Instead of ignoring it as a careless mistake, the evaluator reported the matter to the Controller of Examinations.
That one observation triggered a wider review of answer sheets submitted by students from the same college. 
As more papers were examined, university officials reportedly found a recurring pattern that suggested the issue was far bigger than a single student's error.
Investigation points to alleged AI-assisted cheating
According to the university's preliminary findings, several students from Satpura College of Information and Biotechnology in Sausar, Chhindwara, allegedly copied responses generated by ChatGPT during their examinations.
Officials suspect students used mobile phones inside the examination hall to generate answers and then reproduced them almost word for word in their answer booklets.
The accidental inclusion of the phrase about a ""video answer"" is believed to have been copied directly from AI-generated content without being edited, effectively exposing the alleged method used during the examination.
Similar answers found across multiple subjects
The inquiry reportedly found that the suspected malpractice was not limited to one examination or one student. University officials examined answer sheets across three different subjects and discovered that numerous students had written nearly identical responses. Such similarities, investigators believe, are consistent with AI-generated answers being copied verbatim rather than independently written.
The examinations were conducted in May for students of the second, fourth and sixth semesters under the university's centralised evaluation system. Introduced from the previous semester, the system requires answer sheets from all 17 affiliated colleges to be sent to the university in Sagar for assessment, making it easier to identify unusual patterns across institutions.
Entire college's results put on hold
While results for students of Dr Harisingh Gour Central University and its other affiliated colleges have already been declared, the university has withheld the results of all students from Satpura College of Information and Biotechnology pending the outcome of the investigation.
The move reflects the seriousness with which the university is treating the allegations, as authorities continue to verify the extent of the suspected malpractice.
University plans further action
University Public Relations Officer Vivek Jaiswal said notices will first be issued to the college management seeking an explanation.
Depending on the findings of the inquiry, the university may take additional measures, including conducting re-examinations or shifting the examination centre for future tests. The final decision will be based on the outcome of the ongoing investigation.
AI brings new challenges to examination integrity
This is an example of the problems that educational institutions will be facing increasingly more frequently as AI technologies become easier to access for students. Even though tools like ChatGPT can serve as great learning tools in the hands of students who use them ethically, their unethical application in the context of examinations poses a new problem.
Indian universities are being compelled to reconsider the examination system, tighten invigilation procedures, and find innovative ways to spot instances of cheating with the help of technology.
In this particular case, the cheat was spotted not by any advanced technology, but by a simple mistake of including one line of text in an answer paper.",662,,True,False
1097.0,Elderly Indore woman duped in digital arrest scam: Fraudster posing as J&K police officer cited money laund... - Bhaskar English,Bhaskar English,"Fri, 12 Dec 2025 11:47:11 GMT",https://www.bhaskarenglish.in/local/mp/news/elderly-indore-woman-duped-in-digital-arrest-scam-jk-police-impersonator-claimed-laundering-case-stole-29-lakh-136649904.html,success_decoder,success,"Elderly Indore woman duped in digital arrest scam: Fraudster posing as J&K police officer cited money laundering case, siphoned Rs29 lakh via multiple accounts - Madhya Pradesh News",Tarun Tiwari,2025-12-12,"- English News
