The ex-CM had contested Lok Sabha election from Rajgarh constituency and Sarang was among the BJP leaders who had campaigned there. In his statement, Ravi said, “Sarang had conducted padyatra in Rajgarh constituency against the ex-CM. And everyone knows that Raja Sahab (Digvijaya Singh) does not spare his enemy”.
State BJP media incharge Ashish Usha Agarwal attacked Congress and alleged that its leaders fabricated facts. He stated that Congress leader Ravi was accepted that people approached him with the AI generated audio and video including the documents against the minister. He said Congress leaders Jitu Patwari, Umang Singhar, Digvijaya Singh should apologies for their misconduct.
Digvijaya Singh meets CM
Former chief minister Digvijaya Singh and NSUI leader Ravi Parmar met chief minister Mohan Yadav and asked to resolve the nursing college entrance, study and exam issue, which are causing problems to lakhs of students in the state. The Congress leaders met the CM here on Friday.
The Pre Nursing Selection Test -2022 was conducted by Professional Examination Board for admission in government nursing colleges, in which 66,000 girl students appeared in the examination but the results have not been released till date.
Ravi explains
“When pressure, influence and temptation did not work, I am being cyber attacked. Using AI, a fake video has been released and an unsuccessful attempt is being made to divert the whole matter,” Ravi said.
He added, “I am fighting for the nursing students. Attempts were made to bring me down earlier as well. I was sent to jail, beaten with sticks, paraded around in handcuffs. Attempts were made to suppress my voice by making me fill a bond of Rs 50,000. When they did not succeed, they stooped to the lowest level.”
“I will follow legal process and take legal action against anyone who tries to tarnish my fight,” he said.",430,,True,False
1104.0,Reels for robbery: 2 held in fake notes scam - Mumbai Mirror,Mumbai Mirror,"Wed, 08 Apr 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://mumbaimirror.indiatimes.com/mumbai/reels-for-robbery-2-held-in-fake-notes-scam/articleshow/130095011.html,success_decoder,success,Reels for robbery: 2 held in fake notes scam,Anish Patil anish patil com; Apr,2026-04-08,"Advertisement
Reels for robbery: 2 held in fake notes scam
 | Anish Patil anish.patil@timesofindia.com | Apr 08, 2026, 04:00 IST
Reels for robbery: 2 held in fake notes scam
Gang offered bundles of fake currency at throwaway rates on Instagram to bait victims, then robbed them
The Cyber Police have uncovered a racket where scammers used AI-generated Instagram reels to lure victims — only to rob them during physical meetings.
Investigations into the ongoing Fake Indian Currency Notes (FICN) crackdown revealed that the accused had shifted from traditional robbery to digital crime, using social media as the bait. They created reels showing bundles of fake currency being sold at throwaway rates, often adding dramatic background scores to make the videos look authentic and convincing.
  
However, police said the entire setup was a well-planned deception — with no counterfeit currency actually involved. “The accused used AI tools and edited videos to project that they had access to large quantities of fake notes. In reality, they did not possess any such currency,” an official said.
The gang’s modus operandi involved posting reels offering “duplicate notes” at highly discounted rates and sharing WhatsApp numbers for contact. Interested individuals were then persuaded to meet at pre-decided locations under the pretext of delivery.
“Once the victims arrived, they were robbed. In some cases, they were threatened or intimidated, with the accused projecting links to criminal networks to scare them,” the officer said.
Cyber Police registered four FIRs after identifying multiple Instagram accounts promoting such content. Acting on inputs and local intelligence, police arrested Rahul Anil Pawar and Yogita Bhosale in connection with the case.
Officials said the trend had gained traction between late 2024 and early 2025, particularly in Dhule, Jalgaon and Buldhana, where several such accounts were actively posting reels to attract potential victims.
The racket had also drawn attention earlier after social media users flagged suspicious posts showing fake currency being sold online. Cyber police subsequently issued notices under relevant provisions of the IT Act and BNSS to take down the content and obtain details of account holders.
Investigators believe the case highlights a growing shift in criminal tactics, where conventional offenders are leveraging social media platforms and emerging technologies like artificial intelligence to expand their reach and target victims more effectively. “What appears to be a fake currency sale is actually a trap designed to commit robbery. People should not engage with such content under any circumstances,” police said.
Sources said the accused may be linked to similar incidents, and efforts are underway to trace more victims — many of whom may not have approached police fearing legal repercussions for attempting to procure counterfeit notes.
Additional Director General of Police, Maharashtra Cyber, Yashasvi Yadav said that two complainants have approached us in this matter. Based on the information provided by them, further verification and investigation are currently underway.”
