1145.0,Gautam Gambhir’s ₹2.5 crore AI impersonation case targets major platforms - PitchOnnet,PitchOnnet,"Fri, 20 Mar 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://www.pitchonnet.com/pitch-feature/gautam-gambhirs-25-crore-ai-impersonation-case-targets-major-platforms-39388.html,success_decoder,success,Gautam Gambhir’s ₹2.5 crore AI impersonation case targets major platforms,Author Name,2026-03-20,"Two-time World Cup winner and current India head coach Gautam Gambhir has filed a petition in the Delhi High Court, alleging extensive misuse of his identity across digital and e-commerce platforms, describing it as a coordinated case of AI-driven impersonation. Highlighting the gravity of the matter, Gambhir stated that his ""identity has been weaponised,"" pointing to the escalating risks posed by deepfake content in the online ecosystem.
The complaint centres on the alleged use of artificial intelligence tools to produce fabricated videos of Gambhir on platforms like Instagram, X, and YouTube. These clips falsely attributed statements to him, including one resembling a resignation announcement that garnered over 2.9 million views. Another video, showing him commenting on senior cricketers’ World Cup participation, surpassed 1.7 million views, demonstrating how quickly such content can spread and mislead viewers.
The issue extends beyond misinformation to commercial exploitation. The suit claims that online marketplaces, including Amazon and Flipkart, hosted unauthorised merchandise featuring Gambhir’s name and likeness. According to his legal team, this indicates a broader problem where manipulated identities are monetised, merging content misuse with commerce.
The case names a total of 16 defendants, including individual accounts, technology platforms such as Meta, X Corp., and Google, as well as e-commerce intermediaries. Additionally, the Ministry of Electronics and Information Technology and the Department of Telecommunications have been listed as proforma parties to facilitate implementation of any court-mandated measures.
Legally, the petition relies on provisions under India’s Copyright Act, Trade Marks Act, and Commercial Courts Act. It also cites previous Delhi High Court rulings recognising personality rights, emphasising that an individual’s identity cannot be commercially exploited without consent, even when AI-generated content is involved.
From a marketing perspective, the case underscores the importance of brand safety and authenticity. Celebrity campaigns and influencer collaborations depend on trust and credibility, yet AI-generated impersonations threaten that foundation. As synthetic content production becomes easier, the risk of unauthorised endorsements and misleading brand associations is poised to rise.
Gambhir has demanded damages of ₹22.5 crore, a permanent injunction to prevent further misuse of his identity, takedown of all infringing content, and disclosure of any revenue generated from such material.
This development contributes to the global discussion on AI accountability. For marketers and digital platforms, it signals a critical shift from experimenting with AI to urgently establishing governance frameworks that protect identity, consent, and trust in the digital economy.",396,,True,False
