Bengaluru: A software engineer lost nearly Rs 91 lakh to fraudsters who offered him online training in the stock market and assured him of huge profits.
Ranjan (name changed), a resident of Horamavu, trusted them as all they wanted of him was to vote for them in the âGlobal AI Smart Trading Competition'. They got him to make investments over 45 days and he realised he had been cheated only when he could not withdraw his money.
In his complaint to East CEN Crime police, Ranjan, 39, stated that he received a message on WhatsApp on July 29 offering him training in the stock market in exchange for voting in the competition.
Ranjan started voting every day. After a few days of online classes, he was added to a Telegram group with 80 participants. A person named Chandra Takal, who claimed to be a trading leader, provided training on how and when to trade, and which broker to use. He was made to download two apps â one that showed his investment and profit details and the other, Dwaai, for making investments.
The fraudsters also got Ranjan to invest in IPOs under the Qualified Institutional Buyers (QIB) category, promising him at least 50% profits on the listing day. When Ranjan's investment touched Rs 28 lakh, they said he could invest more by borrowing from their banking app. When the apps showed his investments touched Rs 91 lakh, he was asked to repay the bank the remaining Rs 63 lakh so that he could sell the stocks allotted to him in the IPOs. His âdemat account' showed that the market value of his stocks was Rs 1.5 crore, but the fraudsters locked his investment app as he didn't repay his borrowings for a week.
As Ranjan paid Rs 63 lakh in installments, the app was unblocked. However, when he tried to withdraw the money, he was asked to pay an 8% fee for foreign transactions. The app was blocked again, citing Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) regulations. A person claiming to be a customer care manager told him he had to pay a 10% fee again to unblock the app. Ranjan didn't pay and when his queries were not answered, he contacted police on Nov 25.
A case was registered under the Information Technology Act and the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita (BNS) section 318 (cheating).
