Investment scam
At Kamla Nagar police station, Shivam Katiyar was cheated of Rs23 lakh after a woman contacted him on social media and persuaded him to invest in foreign trading schemes through Telegram.",403,,True,False
1338.0,"Mumbai loses Rs 155cr to digital arrest scams, 33% rise in 1 year - The Times of India",The Times of India,"Sat, 14 Feb 2026 08:00:00 GMT",https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/city/mumbai/mumbai-loses-155cr-to-digitalarrest-scams-33-rise-in-1-yr/articleshow/128332521.cms,success_decoder,success,"Mumbai loses Rs 155cr to digital arrest scams, 33% rise in 1 year | Mumbai News - The Times of India",Mateen Hafeez; Nitasha Natu,2026-02-14,"MUMBAI: People in the city lost Rs 155 crore to digital arrest scams alone in 2025, a 33% surge compared to the previous year. The spike occurred despite a marginal dip in the number of registered cases, from 195 digital scams in 2024 to 191 in 2025. Investment scams, job frauds, and digital arrest scams collectively accounted for 28% of all cyber offenses in the city last year. 
Cybersecurity experts warn that access to AI tools has altered the criminal landscape and scams are likely to increase manifold.
  Watch
 Pune: NCP Picks, Online Scam Loss, Illegal Sub-Tenancy and More
 ""AI just turned cybercrime into something anyone can do. We are not talking about sophisticated crime syndicates anymore. Today, someone with a smartphone and access to plug-and-play AI models can run scams that used to require entire BPO-style operations. 
The barrier to entry has collapsed,"" said Sundareshwar Krishnamurthy, partner & India cyber leader, PwC India. 
While law enforcement has intensified its crackdown, the battle remains uphill. Mumbai Police data indicates that the detection rate for investment, job, and digital arrest scams combined rose from 23% in 2024 to 29% in 2025.
 However, arrests are frequently limited to the ""beneficiaries"" operating mule accounts, while the masterminds continue to operate with impunity from overseas. 
""With AI-based calls, scam attempts are going to increase exponentially. We need specific legislation to address AI-driven crimes,"" said cybersecurity expert Nikhil Mahadeshwar.
Research conducted by PwC in 2023 in collaboration with Stop Scams UK highlights that AI tools can be utilised to sift through large volumes of data to identify potential victims and tailor fraudulent content to an individual's specific vulnerabilities. For instance, AI tools can identify an individual's employment details or family circumstances or where they have been on holiday recently to craft highly convincing personalised scam messages.
The human cost of this technological shift is being felt most acutely by the elderly. Mahadeshwar pointed out that while govt awareness campaigns are ongoing, senior citizens remain uniquely vulnerable due to challenges in maintaining cyber hygiene. ""Many struggle to comprehend what a malicious APK file is, or how to enable two-factor authentication, or verify if an email password has been compromised. This vulnerability is being exploited by scammers who are constantly evolving,"" he said.
In a recent case, fraudsters swindled a senior citizen of Rs 16 lakh by convincing him that he was the subject of an ""NIA investigation into the Delhi blasts"" and coercing him to download an encrypted communication platform which could conceal their digital trail.
Despite a 5% dip in overall registration of cybercrime in Mumbai -- from 5,087 cases in 2024 to 4,825 cases in 2025 -- the financial impact is undeniable. Police data shows that Mumbaikars lost Rs 1,031 crore to all cybercrimes in 2025. DCP (cyber) Purushottam Karad has a simple piece of advice for the elderly: ""There is no provision for digital arrest in Indian law. Don't trust what a stranger online says."" To their families, he advises, ""If a senior citizen behaves differently, talk to them and find out what is wrong.""",512,,True,False
1361.0,"IPS officer poses as farmer, exposes cyber scam in Jaipur: Scammer woman promised huge profits on live call... - Bhaskar English",Bhaskar English,"Sun, 16 Feb 2025 08:00:00 GMT",https://www.bhaskarenglish.in/local/rajasthan/news/ips-officer-poses-as-farmer-exposes-cyber-scam-on-live-call-in-jaipur-134483953.html,success_decoder,success,"IPS officer poses as farmer, exposes cyber scam in Jaipur: Scammer woman promised huge profits on live call; fraudsters’ tactics revealed - Rajasthan News",,2025-02-16,"- English News
- Local
- Rajasthan
- IPS Officer Poses As Farmer, Exposes Cyber Scam In Jaipur: Scammer Woman Promised Huge Profits On Live Call; Fraudsters’ Tactics Revealed
IPS officer poses as farmer, exposes cyber scam in Jaipur:Scammer woman promised huge profits on live call; fraudsters’ tactics revealed
- Copy link
A massive Rs 10,000 crore cyber fraud was recently uncovered in Rajasthan. The masterminds, caught 18 days ago in Sri Ganganagar, ran a fake investment scheme targeting high-profile individuals. To understand how these scams operate, Bhaskar team visited Jaipur’s Police Headquarters.
During the visit, Cyber SP Shantanu Singh received a call from a suspected scammer. He put the call on speaker at the team's request and pretended to be Vikas Sharma, an orange farmer. The scammer, a woman, tried to lure him into a fake investment scheme. The entire conversation was recorded on camera, exposing the fraud in real-time.
Scammer calls IPS officer, tries to sell fake investment scheme
On Saturday at 1:30 PM, SP Shantanu Singh received a call from a cyber fraudster. The woman introduced herself as a representative of a financial company. She claimed they offered fixed returns on investments in cryptocurrency and foreign markets.
She said that investments start at Rs 50,000, and returns would be credited the next day. The company's ""technical team"" supposedly invested in stocks, IPOs, and other assets to generate profits.
SP pretends to be a farmer
SP Shantanu Singh, acting as ‘Vikas Sharma’, questioned the woman about how the investments worked. Some key points from their conversation:
- How will I track my investment? The scammer claimed the money would be in an ""investment account"" in their app. She promised a legal agreement for transparency.
- What happens if I invest Rs 10 lakh? The woman said there was a six-month lock-in period. To withdraw money early, an investor had to fill out a form. After that, it would take one month to process the withdrawal.
- Where does my profit go? She assured that profits would be sent to the investor's bank account. The company only ""worked on the capital"".
Fake references and suspicious data collection
When SP Shantanu Singh asked for references, the woman said she would check her records. Later, she offered contacts from Gujarat or Mumbai, even mentioning Jain clients. This suggested the scammers had access to personal data.
Fraud confirmed after background check
After the call, the scammer sent company documents and details via WhatsApp. Upon verification, investigators found:
- No GST number
- No SEBI-registered portfolio managers
- No valid investment license
- Fake website with celebrity photos but no real information
SP Shantanu Singh confirmed the company was fraudulent, and an investigation was launched.
How scammers target high-profile individuals
Cyber SP Shantanu Singh explained that fraudsters use stolen data from the dark web to target government officers, doctors, and engineers. Many scams involve fake stock market investments.
How they operate
- Creating Fake Groups & Websites Scammers promise high returns and create fake trading platforms.Victims see profits in their accounts, but it's all fake.
- Six-Month Lock-In Period Victims cannot withdraw money for six months.When they try, their accounts are blocked.
- AI-Powered Data Collection Fraudsters track Google & YouTube searches related to investing.Fake ads and websites steal personal data when clicked.
- Fake Investment Companies Fraudsters register fake financial firms.Victims receive calls, emails, and messages from so-called ""investment experts.""
Doctor in Jaipur loses Rs 1.5 cr in similar scam
A Jaipur doctor became a victim of the same scam.
- Joined WhatsApp group In August 2024, he clicked a YouTube link that led to an investment group.Group members claimed they had secret investment tips.
- Gained trust The doctor earned ""points"" for daily messages.After 10 points, he received a gift via Amazon (September 12, 2024).After 30 points, he got Rs 3,000 in his bank account (October 5, 2024).
- Invested in fake trading app Fraudsters sent him an app called Anisha.apk for stock trading.The doctor invested Rs 50-60 lakh, seeing a Rs 15-16 lakh profit in the app.
- Fraud exposed When he tried to withdraw, fraudsters demanded 30% more (Rs 45 lakh).Realising the scam, he approached the Cyber Crime Police.But the money was already transferred to multiple bank accounts and lost.
- Fake App investigation The app was banned on Google Play Store.It was a fake forex trading platform designed to scam investors.
Money laundered to Gulf countries
Cyber SP Shantanu Singh revealed that scammers transfer funds abroad, mainly to Gulf nations. This makes tracing the money extremely difficult.
Joint Task Force formed to fight cyber crime
A Joint Task Force has been created to tackle cyber fraud. It includes:
- Rajasthan Police Cyber Cell
- Enforcement Directorate (ED)
- Income Tax Department
- CBI
This team will focus on:
- Investment frauds
- Property scams
- Money laundering cases",810,,True,False
1363.0,Faridabad Cyber Police bust Rs.93.51 lakh share market investment scam; two Rajasthan men arrested - The Impressive Times,The Impressive Times,"Sat, 28 Jun 2025 07:00:00 GMT",https://impressivetimes.com/latest/faridabad-cyber-police/,success_decoder,success,Faridabad Cyber Police bust Rs.93.51 lakh share market investment scam; two Rajasthan men arrested,The Impressive Times,2025-06-28,"Victim duped through WhatsApp investment group; accused provided bank accounts and SIM cards to facilitate fraud
                    Faridabad: In a significant crackdown on cybercrime, the Faridabad Cyber Police, under the leadership of Deputy Commissioner of Police (DCP), Usha Devi, HPS, have apprehended two individuals involved in a large-scale share market investment fraud amounting to Rs. 93,51,000. The case was registered following a complaint from a resident of Ozone Park Apartments, Sector 86, Faridabad, who fell victim to an elaborate scam operated through a WhatsApp group.
According to the complaint filed at Cyber Police Station Central, the victim was added to a WhatsApp group on July 1, 2024, where fraudsters shared seemingly genuine stock market investment tips. The accused persuaded him to open a trading account on the platform named DAIWA SECURITIES AI Smart Demat for pre-market and American market investments.
Initially, the victim invested Rs.10,000 to test the platform and successfully withdrew Rs.1,000, which gave him confidence to proceed. Encouraged by this, he invested a total sum of Rs.93,51,000. However, soon after, all communication with the fraudsters ceased, and the victim realized he had been defrauded. Promptly, he approached the Cyber Police Station Central to report the crime, leading to the registration of a case under relevant cybercrime laws.
The cyber police team acted swiftly and arrested two suspects: Shubham Sharma (27) and Bhag Chand Upadhyay (33), both residents of Beawar district, Rajasthan, currently staying at Balaji Heights, Gandhi Path West, Lalarpur, Vaishali Nagar, Jaipur. Investigations revealed that the accused played a crucial role by providing bank accounts and associated SIM cards to the scammers, facilitating the illegal transactions.
Further interrogation disclosed that the accused identified and procured bank accounts from unsuspecting holders, kept control over the SIM cards linked to those accounts, and forwarded the account details to the main fraud operators. Educational records show Shubham holds a B.Tech degree, while Bhag Chand is a B.A. graduate.
Both accused have been remanded to police custody for 10 days for further investigation. The Faridabad Cyber Police continue to pursue leads to dismantle the entire fraudulent network and safeguard investors from such cyber scams.
This case highlights the growing menace of cyber frauds exploiting digital platforms and the importance of vigilance and timely action by law enforcement agencies. Authorities have advised the public to be cautious of unsolicited investment tips on social media and messaging apps and to verify the authenticity of trading platforms before making any financial commitments.
