1532.0,Op Mule Hunt: How Gujarat is hitting at heart of cyber-fraud - India Today,India Today,"Wed, 17 Dec 2025 08:00:00 GMT",https://www.indiatoday.in/india-today-insight/story/op-mule-hunt-how-gujarat-is-hitting-at-heart-of-cyber-fraud-2837454-2025-12-17,success_decoder,success,Op Mule Hunt: How Gujarat is hitting at heart of cyber-fraud,Jumana Shah,2025-12-17,"Op Mule Hunt: How Gujarat is hitting at heart of cyber-fraud
A Gujarat-wide crackdown on mule bank accounts used to launder cyber-fraud proceeds signals a strategic shift in the fight against digital scams
The long-running battle between cyber-fraud networks and Indian law enforcement reached a turning point in Gujarat this month when police across the state intensified a coordinated crackdown on mule accounts—bank accounts used to launder proceeds of digital scams.
The campaign, Operation Mule Hunt, followed months of rising fraud complaints and the discovery of increasingly sophisticated financial channels connecting ordinary bank accounts in small towns to handlers based in Dubai, China and several West Asian countries.
The immediate trigger, according to officials, was the exposure of a Rs 719 crore laundering syndicate traced to Bhavnagar in Gujarat. Investigators found that dozens of accounts—mostly created with forged documents—had been funnelling money from investment scams, digital arrest schemes and online job frauds into overseas-controlled pipelines.
What unsettled investigators was the ease with which criminal networks had succeeded in recruiting these “account mules” that invariably belong to daily wagers, small traders and even students persuaded to hand over bank credentials for a few thousand rupees.
“Looking to arrest fraudsters from their international dens, such as Myanmar and Cambodia, requires the intervention of the ministry of external affairs and is a long-winding process involving multiple agencies. However, we cannot lose precious time while victims continue to get fleeced every day. So, we have decided to crack down on the digital money trail,” said an investigating officer, one of the first in the country last year to crack the modus operandi of online transactions of digital arrest.
Besides intensive nationwide campaigns, spreading awareness is the only way to counter the spreading scourge of digital arrest scams. How does the scam work? As soon as a victim transfers money, it first gets deposited in a ‘mule account’, which has to be an Indian bank account to follow through with the scamster’s story of representing an Indian investigative agency.
The account, through a complex web of offline and online manoeuvres, is managed by operators sitting in Dubai or other locations. The money deposited by victims is moved within minutes to other offshore accounts or to cryptocurrencies.
To break the nexus, the cyber units, district police and financial intelligence wings have started coordinating more actively. Their priority is to crack down on the mule account network that operates with local offline support in India—Gujarat being one of the biggest recruitment centres for ‘mules’.
In Ahmedabad, police crime branch teams placed 34 suspicious accounts under scrutiny and began filing FIRs. In Morbi, six individuals were arrested for renting out accounts and SIM cards. In a separate case, a Bhavnagar bank manager was detained after investigators linked customer accounts he had overseen to foreign-based operators. Officers described the ecosystem surrounding mule accounts as the “financial backbone” of cybercrime.
Telangana, Maharashtra, Delhi and Karnataka have announced parallel drives while the Indian Cybercrime Coordination Centre (I4C), set up by the Union home ministry, has warned of a steep rise in foreign-controlled scam operations using Indian financial infrastructure. Internationally, law enforcement agencies in Southeast Asia, the Gulf and Europe are pursuing similar laundering networks tied to call-centre scams and crypto-based frauds.
Gujarat’s campaign is not only punitive but attempts to be preventive, aimed at stopping young people from inadvertently orbiting into cybercrime networks by renting out their accounts. Officials have emphasised community outreach, verification drives and coordination with banks to flag unusual activity more quickly.
As global cyber-fraud becomes more decentralised, mule accounts remain the weak link. Gujarat’s sweep, though still unfolding, signals an acknowledgment that chasing perpetrators is not the singular solution. Destroying the infrastructure that allows them to move money must be dismantled first.
Subscribe to India Today Magazine",630,,True,False
