Rejecting allegations of a leaked question paper, Bains said the accused had planned to use advanced electronic devices and technology to facilitate cheating during the examination. He also said the Punjab government had nothing to hide and was prepared for any investigation, including a CBI probe, if required.
How The Haryana Network Expanded Into Punjab
According to Bains, the alleged syndicate initially operated in Bhiwani, Haryana, where it helped candidates fill examination forms and arranged methods to facilitate cheating. The network later expanded into Punjab by setting up a local branch.
The minister said the Punjab link emerged after members of the group came in contact with a cafe in Ferozepur associated with the pharmacy recruitment examination. That connection eventually helped investigators trace the network and uncover the alleged racket.
How Police Foiled The Cheating Operation
Deputy Inspector General Neelambari Jagdale said the investigation began after police received confidential information about the network.
During the probe, investigators found that the residence of accused Gurmeet Singh in Faridkot was being used as the operation’s control room. Several handlers were allegedly coordinating the cheating operation from the location, and six of them were arrested during the raid.
Police said one candidate had scanned the question paper and attempted to send it outside the examination centre, while accused Deepak and Manjit were allegedly preparing to circulate the answers. However, officials intervened before the plan could be executed.
Based on roll number lists, nearly 200 police personnel carried out checks at examination centres across the state. During the searches, officials recovered mobile phones, a headphone, an RF antenna and other electronic devices allegedly used to facilitate cheating.
The investigation also revealed that members of the syndicate had learned several of their high-tech cheating techniques through online platforms, including YouTube, highlighting the increasingly sophisticated methods being used in recruitment exam fraud. The investigation is continuing.
Quick Answers
The syndicate involved in the Punjab Pharmacy recruitment examination is described as inter-state, with members originating from Haryana, Punjab, and Rajasthan. The network allegedly operated from Bhiwani, Haryana, and expanded into Punjab.",521,,True,False
