This story is from April 8, 2024
Cyber-criminals employ AI to extort money through digital arrest, targeting victims with fake arrests and money laundering accusations. Victims coerced into transferring funds to foreign accounts. Authorities stress awareness and caution against phishing and fraudulent calls in the digital age.
BHOPAL: Cyber-criminals are upping their game, and getting more creative and technically-advanced in their approach of duping people. In their latest modus operandi, they are extorting money using coercive techniques like âdigital arrestâ or issuing threats of kidnapping and/or arresting their victimâs children, especially those studying abroad. These criminals have taken their game up a notch and started using AI software to imitate the voice of their victimâs children in the background during calls or send morphed picture showing their children tied up to convince them that they have their kin is in fact under their captivity.
Recently, a retired senior Navy officer was kept under digital arrest at his home by the cyber-criminals posing as senior officers of Mumbai police and he was duped of around Rs 68.49 lakhs. Later, police found that there was involvement of Dubai-based cyber-criminals in the case.
The cyber-crime branch officials maintained that the online fraudsters call using VoIP (Voice over Internet Protocol) calls and the numbers displayed are of foreign countries. They have advised people not to trust calls received from unknown numbers and avoid receiving them. The State Cyber-crime headquarters have released an advisory on the issue.
Citing the Navy officerâs case, the senior cyber-crime branch officer said that despite being a retired Commodore-level officer, the victim was kept under digital arrest at his home by the fraudsters for more than two days. They instructed him to post âpresent sirâ on their skype account every half-an hour and he could not sleep for the next 48 hours. They showed him his bank account details citing that he was involved in money laundering and made him transfer over Rs 68 lakh into their bank accounts. The accused sent a fake RBI letter asking him to transfer the money into the bank account stating that his money will be kept as security deposit during investigations. The retired officer withdrew money from his fixed deposits and transferred it into the bank accounts of the fraudsters. Police found that the money was transferred into bank accounts taken on ârentâ by the cyber-criminals in Kerala and Rajasthan. Money was withdrawn from these accounts and transferred into other bank accounts of Dubai-based criminals. Now police are contacting the Interpol through CBI to nab the Dubai-based accused.
The officer said that cyber-criminals take the details of bank accounts of their potential victims online. They target people like pensioners, who have received hefty amounts as retirement funds.
The officer said that another modus operandi is that the cyber-criminals make fake kidnapping calls to the victims saying that their child was under their âcustodyâ. They specifically target people, whose children study abroad. Sometimes, they call posing as cops saying that their child was arrested for a crime and use AI tools to emulate their child's voice in the background to convince the victims. They demand money from the victims either for release of their child or to settle the case registered against them. The officer said that before making the calls to the victims, the accused use AI bots to make the mobile numbers of their children unreachable so that victims cannot confirm by calling their child. He said that recently, a senior politician approached the cyber-crime branch and said that he received calls from an unknown number saying that his son, who was studying abroad, had booked a courier carrying drugs. The politician tried to call his son but could not contact him. The crime-branch officials advised him to block the number and later it was found to be a hoax call.
A senior cyber-crime branch officer said that the rate at which the cyber-crimes are rising, spreading awareness seems to be the only way to avoid falling prey to them. People fall in the trap either due to greed or fear. If they exercise caution before every transaction online or while communicating with strangers, they can avoid the risk to a large extent.
Additional DCP Shailendra Singh Chouhan said that 'digital arrestâ and fake child-kidnapping/arrests calls are latest modus operandi of cyber-criminals. They use phishing techniques to siphon personal data and use it to make such calls. On receiving calls from strangers where they demand financial transactions, people should think twice. They should avoid immediate action and never transfer money to stranger. In this era of AI voice replication people should not trust anything blindly, he said.
End of Article
