1882.0,AI in Indian banking cybersecurity drives powerful fraud crackdown - Pune Mirror,Pune Mirror,"Wed, 13 May 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://punemirror.com/technology/ai-in-indian-banking-cybersecurity-push/,success_decoder,success,AI in Indian banking cybersecurity drives powerful fraud crackdown,Shalabh Singh,2026-05-13,"AI in Indian banking cybersecurity is at the centre of a fresh government push to tackle fast‑rising online fraud and protect digital payments.
AI in Indian banking cybersecurity: New AI shield against mule accounts
India’s cybercrime coordination centre (I4C) has signed a memorandum of understanding with the Reserve Bank Innovation Hub (RBIH) to use artificial intelligence to spot and disable mule bank accounts used by fraudsters.
Data from I4C’s Suspect Registry will feed into AI‑driven systems such as MuleHunter.ai, allowing banks to flag suspicious accounts and transactions in near real time across the banking and digital payments network.
Union Home Minister Amit Shah said the move “unleashes the power of Artificial Intelligence to combat cyber fraud”, adding that it is designed to serve as a “next‑gen shield against cyber crime” by swiftly detecting hidden mule accounts.
Officials expect faster fraud‑risk intelligence sharing and closer coordination between banks, regulators and cyber agencies to cut delays in freezing funds and to strengthen public confidence in digital banking.
Also Read: AI cybersecurity risks: Dire warning as India moves to shield banks
AI in Indian banking cybersecurity: AI risks put banks on alert
Global bodies such as the International Monetary Fund have warned that while AI can improve fraud detection, it can also be weaponised by criminals to probe software weaknesses.
In April, Finance Minister Nirmala Sitharaman and IT Minister Ashwini Vaishnaw held a high‑level meeting with bank chiefs on AI‑linked risks, calling for “very high” vigilance, better cybersecurity staffing and real‑time threat‑intelligence sharing.
Cybersecurity specialists say AI‑enabled scams, deepfake identities and automated probing of bank systems are evolving faster than traditional controls, forcing lenders to redesign their defences around continuous monitoring and anomaly detection.
Experts argue that AI in Indian banking cybersecurity must be embedded into core risk frameworks rather than treated as a standalone tool if India is to stay ahead of organised cybercriminal networks.
AI in Indian banking cybersecurity: A cautious but necessary leap
For now, AI in Indian banking cybersecurity offers regulators and banks a more proactive way to chase suspicious money flows instead of reacting after victims report losses.
However, the same technology that powers these tools is also arming fraudsters, meaning India’s new AI systems will need constant upgrades, rigorous oversight and strong collaboration if they are to deliver lasting protection for customers.
