""Karan Chauhan is not connected with the company with which ILBS signed the MoU, as he is neither shareholder nor Director nor partner nor employee nor functionary in the said company,"" said the hospital in a statement.Â
In connection with the alleged hospital scam, Delhi Vigilance Minister Atishi has called for the termination of the agreement between MetaMix and ILBS and for the matter to be referred to the Central Bureau of Investigation (CBI) for a thorough probe under the Prevention of Corruption Act.",325,,True,False
1902.0,"Sandalwood scam! Kannada actor couple Upendra, Priyanka’s phones hacked; contacts duped via WhatsApp mess - The Times of India",The Times of India,"Tue, 16 Sep 2025 07:00:00 GMT",https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/city/bengaluru/digital-drama-fraudsters-hack-phones-of-actors-upendra-priyanka-pocket-money-from-family-friends/articleshow/123906848.cms,success_decoder,success,"Upendra Rao Wife Phone Hacked: Kannada actor couple Upendra, Priyankaâs phones hacked; contacts duped via WhatsApp messages | Bengaluru News - The Times of India",TNN; Updated Sep,2025-09-16,"BENGALURU: In a shocking turn of events, Sandalwood couple 
Upendra Rao
 and 
Priyanka Upendra
 became victims of a ruthless digital scam. What began as a routine day quickly spiralled into a nightmare when fraudsters hacked their phones, impersonated trusted couriers, and unleashed a deceptive campaign to swindle money from their contacts. 
Some of their family and friends transferred money to the fraudsters, forcing the couple to fight back and raise alarm before more damage could be done.
 Fraudsters hacked the phones of the couple and their manager by impersonating a courier and sent messages to their contacts seeking financial help. Messages were sent from actress Priyanka Upendra's WhatsApp account, and at least four people, including her son and two of his friends, transferred money through a digital payment application to a number provided by the fraudsters.
Have you ever been a victim of a digital scam?
Realising the fraud, the couple reported the issue to the cyber helpline 1930, spread awareness through their social media accounts, and alerted the media. The couple also filed a complaint at Sadashivanagar police station. 
""We have taken up the case under the Information Technology Act and have taken measures to freeze money from the fraudster's account,"" a senior police officer said. 
How their phones were hacked
Priyanka had placed an order through a Dubai-based online platform and was expecting the delivery. 
Around 10am, she received a call from an unknown number. The caller introduced himself as an executive of the online platform and told her that their delivery partners were unable to deliver her order as they could not trace her address. 
""The caller said he would send me a code to type and dial. The delivery boy would contact me, and the item would be delivered at the earliest. I initially suspected, but in chaos, I followed the instructions of the fraudster. I put the code *21*9279295167# and dialled. My iPhone got hacked. I thought it was hanging and immediately took Uppi's phone and input the same code and dialled. His phone also got hacked. I didn't have any idea that our phones were being hacked,"" Priyanka said. 
""Our staff member, Mahadeva, came to our house in a few minutes. I borrowed his phone and dialled the code in a bid not to miss the item delivery. His phone also got hacked. All three phone calls were forwarded to the fraudster's number. They also installed WhatsApp on our numbers and started sending messages to our contacts,"" she added.
 What Was in the Message?
âHi, needed some help pls.' Once the respective contact replied, the fraudster sent, âThere will be 55k in your account now? I will return in just 2 hrs. My UPI is not working properly. It's very urgent now.' 
If someone tried to reach Priyanka or Upendra, their numbers were not reachable. ""My son, his friends, and others, at least four people, transferred the money after they tried reaching us. My son thought his mother was in some need and transferred the money immediately through UPI,"" Upendra Rao said. 
""The fraudsters are asking for Rs 55,000 from all our contacts, promising to return it in two hours. We have lodged a complaint with the police. They assured us they would catch the fraudsters. We request people to be aware of such frauds and recover the money. Do not send money to anyone without verifying,"" Priyanka said. ""Yesterday, I saw someone dubbed my voice using AI and discussed it with Uppi. The fraudsters may send phishing links and create fake social media accounts. It was my mistake to dial the code.""",599,,True,False
1905.0,India retains position as APJ’s top phishing target despite falling attack volumes - ET CISO,ET CISO,"Wed, 12 Aug 2026 10:30:00 GMT",https://ciso.economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/cybercrime-fraud/india-retains-position-as-apjs-top-phishing-target-despite-falling-attack-volumes/133179050,success_decoder,success,<b>India retains position as APJ’s top phishing target despite falling attack volumes</b>,Www Etciso In; ETCISO Desk,2026-08-12,"- Cybercrime & Fraud
- 2 min read
India retains position as APJ’s top phishing target despite falling attack volumes
India recorded 52.8 million phishing attempts during the year, accounting for 36.6% of phishing activity across APJ. More than 2.65 billion encrypted attack hits were routed through India, representing 10.4% of encrypted attack traffic globally. More than 762 million encrypted attacks targeted Indian organisations.
The report said attackers are increasingly shifting from high-volume phishing campaigns to more targeted attacks using artificial intelligence (AI), encrypted communications and real-time session hijacking techniques.
Threat actors are using AI-powered “text-to-site” tools and session hijacking kits to create phishing websites and bypass multi-factor authentication (MFA). The report found that 95.2% of phishing attempts were concealed within encrypted traffic. Deception telemetry recorded nearly 90 million hostile interactions, indicating scanning and probing of enterprise identities and collaboration platforms before initial compromise.
ThreatLabz identified 413,524 AI-generated site instances, with nearly 10% flagged as malicious. The report said tools including Manus AI, Blackbox AI and Lovable AI are being used to create phishing portals designed to resemble legitimate websites.
The Services sector recorded a 65.5% year-over-year increase in phishing hits, with attackers targeting workflows such as billing, onboarding and support renewals.
Other findings from the report include:
•The U.S. remained a leading target for email phishing attacks, while Brazil recorded a 2,522% increase in phishing hosting and became one of the top five global origins.
•Manufacturing and Government were among the primary targets for email phishing attacks. Government-related hits increased by 50%.
•Microsoft and Google were the most frequently imitated brands in phishing attacks.
•About 87% of malicious activity was delivered through HTTPS.
•Attackers used more than 121,000 unique public cloud-hosted IP addresses for reconnaissance.
•Global phishing volume declined by 20% for the second consecutive year.
•The report identified more than 413,000 AI-generated phishing instances.
•Session hijacking kits, including BlackForce, were used to bypass MFA and hijack active sessions.
•Deception telemetry recorded 89.9 million hostile interactions from 1.37 million unique attacker IP addresses over six months.
“We are witnessing a strategic recalibration in the way adversaries approach initial access,” said Deepen Desai, Chief Security Officer, Zscaler. “The decline in raw phishing volume isn’t a sign of retreat; it’s a sign of evolution. Attackers are trading quantity for quality, using GenAI to eliminate traditional ‘tells’ like poor grammar and generic lures. With 95% of phishing now hiding in encrypted traffic, organizations can no longer afford to leave their TLS traffic uninspected. A Zero Trust architecture is the only way to break the attack chain, from discovery to data exfiltration.”
The report is based on data collected from January to December 2025, supplemented by deception telemetry collected between October 2025 and March 2026.
ThreatLabz said it analysed more than 500 trillion daily signals from the Zscaler Zero Trust Exchange and reported blocking more than 9 billion threats daily.",477,,True,False
1912.0,"Trai directs Google, WhatsApp, Meta to take measures to avoid pesky calls, phishing, spam - ET Government",ET Government,"Wed, 05 Jul 2023 07:00:00 GMT",https://government.economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/digital-india/trai-directs-google-whatsapp-meta-to-take-measures-to-avoid-pesky-calls-phishing-spam/101509687,success_decoder,success,"Trai directs Google, WhatsApp, Meta to take measures to avoid pesky calls, phishing, spam",Www Etgovernment Com; Online Desk,2023-07-05,"- Digital India
- 2 min read
Trai directs Google, WhatsApp, Meta to take measures to avoid pesky calls, phishing, spam
While a few models to check spam and phishing are being worked on, the regulator does not want to disclose the details so as not to alert fraudsters.
“We will have a collaborative effort with the OTT players. They will do the due diligence on the issues pointed out by us. Broadly, the platforms agree on the pain points,” a senior Trai official told ET on condition of anonymity.
Currently, there are no plans to regulate apps or take punitive measures, the official said, adding, “The platforms have informed us that going forward, they will increase consumer awareness, as it can be a barrier in checking phishing attempts.”
The regulator will regularly meet the OTT players to finalise a workable solution, the official said.
Another official said Trai is trying to see how the OTT platforms can integrate themselves in the system proposed by the regulator to check spam and phishing.
The meeting comes in the backdrop of a surge in phishing attempts and spam calls and messages on OTT platforms, primarily after users getting missed calls from international numbers.
WhatsApp, a major player in OTT communication, told ET that safety of users is its top priority, and it has devoted efforts to empower users with resources and tools that equip them to safeguard themselves from instances of spam and scams.
“Product features like silence unknown callers helps to automatically screen out spam, scams and calls from unknown people for increased protection and the ability to block and report provides a convenient way for users to report suspicious accounts or problematic messages from unknown numbers,” a WhatsApp spokesperson said.
Additionally, the Meta-owned platform said it has applied spam detection technology to spot and take action on accounts engaging in abnormal behaviour to stop spam and has consistently invested in artificial intelligence (AI) and other state of the art technology, data scientists and experts to keep users safe.
Trai has already directed telecom operators to put in place an AI-based solution to check spam. But since OTTs are not regulated, there is a need to either bring the platforms on the same system or set up another mechanism.
Trai has issued various directions to telcos recently to curb spam calls and messages and phishing – fraud, mainly financial, through messaging or calls. In its latest directive on June 13, the watchdog proposed the use of AI and machine learning-based technologies to check the nuisance. On June 2, the regulator had directed telcos to set up a digital consent acquisition process for commercial communications.
However, the solutions developed by telcos work on calls and SMS traffic, but not on apps.",459,,True,False
