2027.0,"Digital arrest cases rise in Himachal, scammers dupe people posing as RBI, police officers - The Tribune",The Tribune,"Thu, 12 Mar 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://www.tribuneindia.com/news/himachal/digital-arrest-cases-rise-in-state-scammers-dupe-people-posing-as-rbi-police-officers/,success_decoder,success,"Digital arrest cases rise in Himachal, scammers dupe people posing as RBI, police officers - The Tribune",Navneet Rathore,2026-03-12,"Digital arrest cases rise in Himachal, scammers dupe people posing as RBI, police officers
Cybercriminals coerce victim into transferring money in the form of a fine to their bank accounts so as to evade serious consequences and legal action
The cases of digital arrest, which has become a major cybercrime, continues to rise in Himachal as scammers are duping people by posing as officials of the police, RBI and various other government agencies.
According to the police, scammers first contact a person through an audio or video call and in some cases via text messages. They accuse the person of being involved in illegal activities by showing him fake documents and warrants and warn him that he has been digitally arrested. The scammers then coerce him into transferring money in the form of a fine to their bank accounts so as to evade serious consequences and legal action.
The police have alerted people to be aware of such scams and advised them to not believe any such calls or messages. They say that there is no such legal procedure known as digital arrest, so people must not panic if they receive any such calls and should immediately alert the police by dialing toll-free cybercrime helpline number 1930.
The police say that there is no provision to arrest someone through video or audio calls and text messages. They have advised people to never share their personal or financial details with anyone as this could lead to identity theft or financial losses.
Over the past few years, Himachal Pradesh has witnessed a surge in cybercrime cases — 18,706 complaints of cybercrime were received in 2025. To tackle cybercrime in the state, the police have established dedicated stations and integrated cyber cells with Indian Cyber Crime Coordination (I4C). Besides, awareness campaigns are being held regularly in educational institutions and rural areas to increase awareness about cybercrimes among people. The police are also working in coordination with banks to block fraudulent transactions.",328,,True,False
2041.0,‘Great scam’: US after India named among 40 nations aiding China evade tariffs - The Indian Express,The Indian Express,"Fri, 14 Aug 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://indianexpress.com/article/world/us-india-40-nations-china-tariff-evasion-ai-crackdown-10832295/,success_decoder,success,‘Great scam’: US after India named among 40 nations aiding China evade tariffs,Express Global Desk,2026-08-14,"The United States has accused more than 40 countries, including India, of taking part in what it called a shadow network that helps China get around steep American tariffs. Officials also outlined plans to use artificial intelligence to spot and act on such shipments, PTI reported. The Indian government has yet to respond to this accusation.
A report titled ‘The Great Transshipment Scam’ was released by Peter Navarro, the top trade adviser to President Donald Trump. Navarro said the practice grew more common after 2018, when the Trump administration first placed Section 301 tariffs on China over trade practices it considered unfair.
The report put the value of goods rerouted through third countries each year at somewhere between USD 40 billion and USD 303 billion, depending on how the figure is worked out.
The list of nations said to make up what Navarro called China’s Shadow Transshipment Network includes several of America’s largest trading partners. These range from Mexico and Canada to the European Union, India, Japan and South Korea.
Navarro was quoted by PTI as saying the arrangement had let “Communist China launder its exports through more than 40 countries.”
Story continues below this ad
Chinese goods were passed through other countries for light processing, relabelling, repackaging, reinvoicing or a change of shipping route. This created the appearance of a different country of origin while the goods remained largely Chinese in content.
Chinese manufacturers and trading firms move goods through places with low labour costs, limited customs oversight, relaxed free-trade zones or favourable access to the US market.
The report singled out India’s Pune-Gujarat-Chennai manufacturing belt, saying it absorbs pumps and compressors that would otherwise affect supply chains in Cincinnati, Dayton and Columbus in the US.
Washington plans to bring in an artificial intelligence tool named “Detective Border” to help identify goods that reach the US after passing through third countries.
The system is expected to draw on shipment records, routing history, product classification, ownership links, production capacity, anomaly detection and computer vision, according to the report.
The report as stating that the aim was to help US Customs and Border Protection tell legitimate nearshoring and foreign investment apart from illegal pass-through trade, flag high-risk shipments, and turn findings into enforcement steps such as duty collection, penalties and exclusion orders.
