- Fake 'Supreme Court' Scam: Gandhinagar Ex geologist Loses ₹40 Lakh In Digital Arrest Fraud
Fake 'Supreme Court' scam drains Gandhinagar ex-geologist’s retirement savings:Fraudsters threaten with TRAI case, extort ₹40 lakh; digital arrest scam exposed
- Copy link
Cyber fraudsters have trapped yet another retired senior government official, swindling nearly ₹40 lakh by staging a fake “digital arrest” and a bogus Supreme Court hearing over a video call.
The victim, a former geologist from the Office of the Commissioner of Geology and Mining, Gandhinagar, lives in Sector-3 of the city. A complaint has been registered at the Gandhinagar Range Cyber Crime Police Station.
According to the complaint, the fraud began on November 10, 2025, when the retired official received a call from a man identifying himself as Arun Yadav, claiming to be an officer from the Telecom Regulatory Authority of India (TRAI). The caller alleged that the victim’s Aadhaar card had been misused and that a case had been registered against him on October 27.
The call was then transferred to another fraudster posing as Inspector Sandeep Rao of the Mumbai Crime Branch. The fake officer warned that threatening calls were being made using a mobile number linked to the victim’s Aadhaar, causing panic and fear.
During a subsequent video call, the fraudsters appeared in police uniform with a Mumbai Police logo visible in the background. Under the guise of interrogation, they extracted personal and family details. The retired geologist was later told that his bank account had been used in the Naresh Goyal money laundering case, further intensifying the pressure.
Fearing arrest, the victim disclosed details of his retirement savings, including an ICICI Direct demat account holding around ₹40 lakh. Another fraudster, posing as Karan Sharma, Additional Director, questioned him and collected copies of his Aadhaar card, PAN card, photograph and signature for a fake court affidavit.
The victim was then placed under constant surveillance and instructed not to inform anyone “in the interest of national security”. He was forced to send WhatsApp messages every two hours during the night, stating: “I am safe. I am here. Nation secret is safe.”
On November 11, the fraudsters instructed him to appear before the Supreme Court via video call. A fake virtual courtroom was staged, complete with a judge, lawyers, police officers and an accused. He was told that, as per court orders, all funds from his demat account must be deposited with the Supreme Court for verification.
Using screen sharing, the cyber criminals obtained his demat login credentials and OTPs, sold all his shares and mutual funds, and transferred ₹39.99 lakh—₹28 lakh from shares and ₹11 lakh from mutual funds—into multiple bank accounts via RTGS and UPI.
After the transfer, the fraudsters deleted all chats during a screen-sharing session and disappeared. Realising he had been cheated, the retired geologist approached the cyber crime police.
Police have registered a case and launched an investigation, warning citizens—especially senior citizens—to remain alert against “digital arrest” scams involving fake law enforcement agencies and courts.",507,,True,False
2253.0,Retired Court Registrar in Umargam Duped of ₹30.76 Lakh in 'Digital Arrest' Cyber Fraud - GujaratSamachar English,GujaratSamachar English,"Tue, 04 Aug 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://english.gujaratsamachar.com/news/valsad/retired-court-registrar-in-umargam-duped-of-indian-rupee3076-lakh-in-digital-arrest-cyber-fraud-47112864506,success_decoder,success,Retired Court Registrar in Umargam Duped of ₹30.76 Lakh in…,Siddhi Dahanukar,2026-08-05,"Retired Court Registrar in Umargam Duped of ₹30.76 Lakh in 'Digital Arrest' Cyber Fraud
Summarized by AI; it may make mistakes. Check important info
A retired court registrar from Gujarat's Umargam was allegedly duped of ₹30.76 lakh in a 'digital arrest' cyber fraud after scammers posing as officials from the Mumbai Crime Branch and Delhi Telecom Department threatened to implicate him in a money laundering case.
The victim, Ishwarbhai Bhagwanji Patel (62), a resident of Saronda village in Umargam and a retired registrar of the Valsad court, has filed a complaint with the Valsad Cyber Crime Police.
Threatened With Fake Money Laundering Case
According to the complaint, Patel received a call on July 9, 2026, from an unknown number. The caller claimed to be an official from the Delhi Telecom Department and alleged that another SIM card issued in his name was being used for illegal activities.
Soon after, another fraudster introduced himself as an officer from the Mumbai Crime Branch and accused Patel of being linked to a ₹5 lakh money laundering case involving Naresh Goyal.
To make the claims appear genuine, the fraudsters allegedly shared forged documents carrying the logos and seals of the Supreme Court, CBI and the Enforcement Directorate (ED).
Kept Under 'Digital Arrest'
The scammers allegedly instructed Patel to remain inside his house, avoid informing anyone about the matter and stay connected on a continuous video call, claiming he was under ""digital arrest.""
Using the pretext of verifying his bank transactions, they convinced him to liquidate investments from his SBI account and mutual funds.
The victim then transferred ₹30.76 lakh in multiple transactions to bank accounts in DCB Bank and ICICI Bank, believing he was cooperating with an official investigation.
Family's Suspicion Exposes Fraud
The fraud came to light on July 31, when the scammers allegedly asked Patel to purchase gold.
When he visited a bank for the transaction, his wife and children grew suspicious and began questioning him. After verifying the facts, they realised he had fallen victim to a cyber fraud and immediately approached the Valsad Cyber Crime Police.
Police have registered an FIR against unidentified accused under relevant sections related to cheating and cybercrime and have launched an investigation.",367,,True,False
2255.0,"CBI, RBI and Police Impersonation Used in ‘Digital Arrest’ Scam Targeting BHEL Employee - The420.in",The420.in,"Tue, 12 May 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://the420.in/15-lakh-digital-arrest-scam-bhel-employee-cbi-rbi-fake-calls-cyberabad-2026/,success_decoder,success,"CBI, RBI and Police Impersonation Used in ‘Digital Arrest’ Scam Targeting BHEL Employee",The,2026-05-12,"A major “digital arrest” cyber fraud case has surfaced in Hyderabad, where a 56-year-old employee of Bharat Heavy Electricals Limited (BHEL) was allegedly cheated of ₹15 lakh by cybercriminals posing as officials from the CBI, Reserve Bank of India (RBI), police, and the Telecom Regulatory Authority of India (TRAI). The accused allegedly used fear, intimidation and fake legal notices to convince the victim that he was linked to money laundering and human trafficking activities before forcing him to transfer money in the name of “verification.”
Fraud Unfolds: From SIM Alert to Threats
According to investigators, the fraud began in March 2026 when the victim received an automated phone call warning that his mobile SIM card could soon be deactivated. During the call, the fraudsters claimed that multiple SIM cards had been illegally issued in Bengaluru using the victim’s Aadhaar credentials. Initially, the victim reportedly believed it was a routine security verification process, but the calls soon became more threatening and aggressive.
Police said one of the accused later contacted the victim through a WhatsApp video call while posing as a police officer. The caller allegedly informed the victim that his Aadhaar details had been linked to illegal activities, including human trafficking. Soon after, another fraudster introduced himself as a CBI officer and allegedly warned the victim that failure to cooperate could result in arrest, passport blockage and freezing of his bank accounts.
FCRF Academy Launches Premier Anti-Money Laundering Certification Program
Forged Notices and Forced Transfers
To make the operation appear genuine, the accused allegedly sent forged notices carrying the names and formats of agencies such as the Enforcement Directorate (ED) and RBI. Investigators said the documents were designed to resemble official government communications and were used to psychologically pressure the victim into believing that he was under investigation in a serious financial crime case.
The fraudsters allegedly told the victim that his bank accounts needed to undergo a “clean verification” process and instructed him to temporarily transfer funds to a designated account for scrutiny. Under sustained mental pressure and fear of legal action, the victim reportedly transferred ₹15 lakh to the bank account provided by the accused.
Investigation and Expert Warnings
The victim later became suspicious and contacted the concerned authorities, after which he realised he had been cheated. He subsequently approached the Cyber Crime Unit of Cyberabad Police and lodged a formal complaint.
Investigating agencies are now examining bank accounts, mobile numbers, WhatsApp call records, IP logs and digital transaction trails linked to the fraud. Preliminary findings suggest the operation may involve an interstate cybercrime network using fake KYC documents, internet-based calling systems and mule bank accounts to route money and conceal identities.
Renowned cybercrime expert and former IPS officer Prof. Triveni Singh said, “Digital arrest fraud has emerged as one of the fastest-growing cybercrime patterns in India. Fraudsters impersonate officials from agencies such as the CBI, ED, RBI, police or TRAI and create fear of arrest or legal action. They use video calls, forged notices and official-looking documents to mentally manipulate victims before forcing them to transfer money.”
Cyber experts have advised citizens not to panic in response to unknown calls, video calls or legal notices received digitally in the name of government agencies. No government authority conducts financial verification through personal bank transfers over phone or video calls. Experts also stressed that people should independently verify such claims through official helplines or local police before taking any action.
Cyberabad Police has urged the public to immediately report cases involving digital arrest threats, fake KYC verification or agency impersonation through the national cybercrime helpline 1930 or the official cybercrime reporting portal. Officials said prompt reporting significantly improves the chances of freezing suspicious transactions and tracing the accused before the money is moved through multiple accounts.",627,,True,False
2258.0,"Indore Couple Receive Video Call Showing Son Soaked In Blood, Lose Rs 1 Lakh In Deepfake Scam - News18",News18,"Tue, 03 Mar 2026 08:00:00 GMT",https://www.news18.com/cities/indore/indore-couple-receive-video-call-showing-son-soaked-in-blood-lose-rs-1-lakh-in-deepfake-scam-ws-el-9940171.html,success_decoder,success,"Indore Couple Receive Video Call Showing Son Soaked In Blood, Lose Rs 1 Lakh In Deepfake Scam",Parishmita Saikia,2026-03-03,"Indore Couple Receive Video Call Showing Son Soaked In Blood, Lose Rs 1 Lakh In Deepfake Scam
