AI And Deepfake Technology Used
Additional DCP Rajesh Dandotiya stated that early findings indicate the misuse of artificial intelligence. The scammers likely employed deepfake technology, digitally altering a person’s face and expressions to make the video appear real.
Deepfakes make it extremely difficult for ordinary people to detect deception, adding a dangerous dimension to the scam. Police are currently investigating this angle with technical support.
The Family’s Ordeal
The victim, son of Govind Prajapat and Pooja Prajapat, lives in the MIG Police Station area. On February 2, he went to a coaching class but did not return home until late, worrying his family.
On February 3, the parents received a video call from an unknown number. Their son appeared on screen, seemingly being stabbed by the caller, who threatened to kill him unless Rs 1,02,000 was transferred. Acting out of fear, the parents immediately sent the money.
The next day, their son returned home safely, explaining that he had visited a temple without informing his parents. That is when the family realised they had fallen victim to a cyber scam.
Police Investigation Underway
Authorities are investigating the case as part of a larger cybercrime operation. The use of multiple states in the transaction, Haryana, Uttar Pradesh, and Bihar, suggests an organised gang working to make detection difficult.
The police are combining digital forensics, bank records, and CCTV footage to track the criminals. The misuse of AI and deepfake technology has made the scam more sophisticated and dangerous, prompting technical teams to assist in the investigation.",510,,True,False
2260.0,"Fraudsters pose as J&K ATS, dupe retd bank official of Rs 28L - The Times of India",The Times of India,"Sun, 27 Jul 2025 07:00:00 GMT",https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/city/lucknow/fraudsters-pose-as-jk-ats-dupe-retd-bank-official-of-rs-28l/articleshow/122927648.cms,success_decoder,success,"Fraudsters pose as J&K ATS, dupe retd bank official of Rs 28L | Lucknow News - Times of India",TNN; Jul,2025-07-27,"Lucknow: In a new variation of âdigital arrest' scam, cyber fraudsters posing as officers of the Jammu & Kashmir Anti-Terrorism Squad (ATS) duped a 63-year-old retired bank cashier in Lucknow of over Rs 28 lakh after confining him to psychological captivity through threats of espionage and sedition charges.
