2267.0,"3 days on video call: Fraudsters digitally arrest Mumbai couple, swindle Rs 50 lakh - India Today",India Today,"Thu, 30 Oct 2025 07:00:00 GMT",https://www.indiatoday.in/india/story/mumbai-retired-banker-loses-rs-505-lakh-in-cyber-scam-as-fraudsters-impersonate-police-and-force-three-day-video-call-2810507-2025-10-30,success_decoder,success,"3 days on video call: Fraudsters digitally arrest Mumbai couple, swindle Rs 50 lakh",India Today News Desk,2025-10-30,"3 days on video call: Fraudsters digitally arrest Mumbai couple, swindle Rs 50 lakh
A retired banker and his wife in Mumbai were trapped in a cyber scam where fraudsters impersonated police officials and forced them to stay on a video call for three days, leading to a loss of Rs 50.5 lakh. This alarming case highlights the increasing sophistication of digital frauds targeting vulnerable citizens in India.
In a chilling case of “digital arrest,” a retired banker and his wife in Mumbai were forced to stay on a video call for three days by cybercriminals posing as police officials, and were tricked into transferring Rs 50.5 lakh of their life savings.
According to police, the shocking incident came to light on October 10, when the senior citizen from north Mumbai filed a complaint after realising he had fallen prey to an elaborate cyber scam.
FAKE FIR, FALSE CHARGES, AND FEAR
Between September 11 and 24, the victim received WhatsApp calls from a number claiming to belong to the Nashik police. The caller, posing as a police officer, alleged that the man’s name had surfaced in a money-laundering investigation. To make the threat convincing, the fraudsters even showed a fake FIR bearing his name.
The caller then introduced himself as an IPS officer from the National Investigation Agency (NIA) and told the couple they were under surveillance. Under the pretext of an ongoing probe, the couple was instructed to remain on a continuous video call for three days, a tactic designed to isolate and control them psychologically.
During the so-called “interrogation,” the criminals extracted details of the couple’s bank accounts and fixed deposits. Claiming that the money needed to be “verified,” they coerced the victim into transferring Rs 50.5 lakh to an account provided by them. Once the funds were sent, the calls abruptly stopped.
TWO ARRESTED FOR AIDING CYBERCROOKS
The North Region Cyber Police launched an investigation and traced Rs 29.5 lakh of the defrauded money to a “first-layer” mule account operated by Ravi Ananda Ambore (35) of Ulhasnagar, Thane district.
Ambore was arrested on October 25 and reportedly confessed to renting out his bank account to cybercriminals in exchange for a commission. His information led to the arrest of Vishwapal Chandrakant Jadhav (37), whose account had allegedly been used in at least seven similar cyber frauds across multiple states.
A GROWING MENACE
Police said both Ambore and Jadhav acted as facilitators, allowing scammers to route stolen money through their accounts to obscure the trail. Officials warned that such “digital arrest” scams, where victims are made to believe they are being monitored by law enforcement, are on the rise across India.
“The criminals use fear and authority to trap vulnerable people,” a senior police officer said. “No police or NIA officer will ever demand money transfers or keep citizens under video surveillance.”
Authorities have urged the public to verify any such calls directly with the police and to immediately report suspicious activity to the national cybercrime helpline 1930.",499,,True,False
