2307.0,"Tamil Nadu Cyber Crime Wing Busts International SIM Box Racket In Delhi, Arrests Three - News18",News18,"Mon, 06 Oct 2025 07:00:00 GMT",https://www.news18.com/india/tamil-nadu-cyber-crime-wing-busts-international-sim-box-racket-in-delhi-arrests-three-ws-kl-9616769.html,success_decoder,success,"Tamil Nadu Cyber Crime Wing Busts International SIM Box Racket In Delhi, Arrests Three",Apoorva Misra; News Desk,2025-10-06,"Tamil Nadu Cyber Crime Wing Busts International SIM Box Racket In Delhi, Arrests Three
Police teams simultaneously conducted raids in Delhi, Bihar, and Maharashtra, leading to the identification of SIM boxes at two locations in Delhi’s Narela and one in Nilothi
Got Question about the News?
In a technologically complex operation against transnational organised cybercrime and digital arrest, the Tamil Nadu Cyber Crime Wing Headquarters achieved a major success by busting 24 automated fraud calling machines—also known as signal boxes—in New Delhi that were operated by foreign nationals remotely.
Last month, following intelligence-led operations, multiple synchronised raids were conducted across Tamil Nadu, resulting in the seizure of 14 high-capacity Quectel SIM boxes in Chennai, and the arrest of six suspects.
Recommended Stories
- 'Can't Be Threatened For Asking Questions': SC Judge Defends Students’ Right To Dissent Amid NLSIU Row
- PM Modi’s Uzbekistan Visit: 11 MoUs And Agreements, 5 Announcements & State’s Highest Honour
- AI Video Showing Bhagwant Mann Being Slapped Sparks Row, AAP Reacts: 'Anti-Punjab, Hateful Mindset'
- 'System Crashed 20 Times': NEET-PG Candidates In Jaipur Stage Protest Over Technical Disruptions
Subsequent investigations led to the recovery of one additional SIM box, while another five SIM boxes were found to have been destroyed by the accused in Ramanathapuram district, fearing arrest by the police. On further investigation, it was noted that the key suspect had handed over eight SIM boxes in Delhi. It was also found that the same network has operations extending to Delhi, Mumbai and Bihar.
To pursue the leads, Dr Sandeep Mittal, IPS, Additional Director General of Police, Cyber Crime Wing, directed the formation of multiple investigation teams to operate under the supervision of I Shahnaz, IPS, Superintendent of Police, State Cyber Crime Investigation Centre, and headed by Deputy Superintendent of Police and Inspectors of Police. These teams simultaneously conducted raids in Delhi, Bihar, and Maharashtra, leading to the identification of SIM boxes at two locations in New Delhi’s Narela and one location in Nilothi.
The special team arrested Tarikh Alam (M/19), a resident of Nilothi and two of his associates—Lokesh Kumar (M/33) and Ashok Kumar (M/40). The raids resulted in the seizure of 16 SIM boxes from Narela and eight SIM boxes from Nilothi. On analysis of the tower dump and CDR of these spots, it was revealed that the SIM boxes were actively used in digital arrest scams operated by international cyber scam compounds.
In a consorted fight against cybercrime, the Tamil Nadu Cyber Crime Wing has successfully dismantled 44 SIM boxes belonging to the international network in the last two months. Dismantling these SIM boxes has served a major blow to the international cyber scam syndicate that used them to operate fraudulent VoIP call routing hubs. The extensive involvement of these SIM boxes is currently being analysed to identify the interstate and intrastate linkages.
Asking citizens to remain vigilant, the police issued the following advisory:
• Do not be carried away by the promise of commission for opening bank accounts, operating SIM boxes, transporting SIM box or any electronic gadgets in disguise, etc.
• Do not panic if you receive a call claiming you’re involved in a crime. Scammers often use fear tactics to pressure victims into compliance. There is no concept called Digital Arrest or arresting online.
• Government agencies will never ask for money for any grievance reported or verification.
• Never share personal or banking details over phone or video calls, including Aadhaar, PAN, bank credentials, or OTPs.
• Avoid accepting video calls from unknown persons claiming to be officials. Scammers may impersonate officers using AI or pre-recorded videos.
• Do not download remote access apps like AnyDesk or TeamViewer when asked by unknown callers. This could give scammers control over your device.
•If you have fallen victim to such scams, please dial 1930 for the toll-free assistance provided by Cyber Crime wing or register your complaint at www.cybercrime.gov.in",650,,True,False
2310.0,Youtuber Ankush Bahuguna's harrowing tale of a 40-hour digital arrest scam: Why his story is a lesson for - The Times of India,The Times of India,"Sun, 05 Jan 2025 08:00:00 GMT",https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/etimes/trending/youtuber-ankush-bahugunas-harrowing-tale-of-a-40-hour-digital-arrest-scam-why-his-story-is-a-lesson-for-all/articleshow/116970346.cms,success_decoder,success,Youtuber Ankush Bahuguna's harrowing tale of a 40-hour digital arrest scam: Why his story is a lesson for all - The Times of India,TOI Trending Desk,2025-01-05,"Trending
Ankush Bahuguna, a well-known YouTuber, shared his alarming experience of being trapped by scammers for 40 hours, during which he lost money and peace of mind. The scammers convinced him of his involvement in illegal activities through a fake digital arrest scenario. He revealed the ordeal to raise awareness and caution others.
Ankush Bahuguna, a popular YouTuber and social media influencer, recently recounted a harrowing 40-hour ordeal where he fell victim to a sophisticated digital arrest scam. In an emotional Instagram post, Bahuguna detailed how scammers exploited his trust, leaving him financially drained and mentally shaken.
In his Instagram post, Bahuguna talked about his chilling experience. He wrote, âI have been missing from social media and everywhere for the last three days, I was held hostage by some scammers for 40 hoursâ¦ I have lost money, I have lost my mental health to this, I canât believe this has happened to me.â He expressed how he was shocked and confused during the whole ordeal as the scammers manipulated him into believing that he was involved in some criminal activities.
Explaining the ordeal, Ankush said, âIt seemed like an international number. I picked it up without thinking much. It was an automated call that said your courier delivery had been canceled. Press zero for support.â Ankush didnât doubt the anonymous call and pressed zero which redirected the call to customer support. The person on the other side of the call informed Ankush that his name was associated with a package that contained illegal items, which had been seized by customs. The caller accused Bahuguna of attempting to send the package to China.
Credit: Instagram/@ankushbahuguna
Ankush refuted the claim, and said, âI got scared, I said I hadnât sent anything.â However, the callers continued to scare Ankush. They told Ankush that his Aadhar card and other personal details were connected to the package, and he was now under digital arrest. âThis is a very serious crime, and now you will be under digital arrest,â the scammers allegedly told him.
After accusing Ankush of crimes that ranged from money laundering and drug trafficking, Ankush was told that he needed to talk to the police. They pressured him, and he had no time to go to an actual Police station. They then connected Ankush with a âpolice officerâ. The callers switched the call to video call, where Bahuguna could see a police officer in a uniform. Ankush was then asked to cut off all contact with the outside world. Then he was forced to tell all his details to the caller.
The scammers put him in, what they called, a âself custodyâ. âI was under self-custody... I was on a video call with them for 40 hours,â he said in the video. To ensure their scam, the people on the other side of the call prohibited Ankush from taking any calls. At one point, the scammers instructed him to visit a bank and carry out suspicious transactions. Fortunately, the transactions failed as the branch was closed. It was only when a friend reached out, cautioning him about the likelihood of a scam, that Bahuguna realized he had been duped.
Ankush then asked people to not judge as different people react differently to panic. âNot everybody reacts the same way to panic. Instead of calling this stupid, make people around you aware of it,â he said in a follow-up comment. He also encouraged people to stay vigilant against any such fraudulent calls.
