Fraudsters target Ahmedabad plane crash victim’s family in Udaipur:Posing as legal aid, fraudsters lure kin with fake ₹10 crore crash compensation offer
- Copy link
In a disturbing development following the tragic Ahmedabad plane crash, fraudsters are now targeting the families of the deceased by luring them with fake promises of compensation worth up to Rs 10 crore. One such case has come to light in Rundeda village of Vallabhnagar Tehsil in Udaipur district, where unidentified individuals attempted to dupe and threaten a grieving family.
Fraudsters arrive at victim’s home in Gujarat-registered car
According to the police complaint filed by Deepak Menaria, son of the deceased Vardichand Menaria, a man and a woman arrived at their home 13 days ago in a car bearing a Gujarat registration number. Posing as representatives from the Supreme Court, the duo promised financial assistance and demanded that the deceased’s wife sign and provide a thumb impression on blank stamp papers.
Family receives threats after refusing to cooperate
Upon refusal, the family began receiving threatening WhatsApp messages and voice calls. One of the messages, sent around 10 p.m., warned the family that the fraudsters would return. In subsequent messages, the accused claimed to be an advocate from Delhi and offered help in filing a case to secure compensation allegedly released by Boeing.
Fraudsters claim link to international legal action
In reality, the accused tried to pressure the family into signing documents, sending threatening audio clips and legal-looking paperwork. Despite their tactics, the family did not comply and immediately sought protection and action from the district administration.
Family seeks police protection, FIR registered
The victim’s family met with the Udaipur Collector and Superintendent of Police, following which an FIR was registered at Vallabhnagar police station on July 19. Police have lodged a case against unidentified persons under relevant sections.
As per the complaint, the fraudsters visited the house on July 18 at around 7:45 p.m. and attempted to mislead the widow of the deceased, claiming they were empowered to handle legal matters related to the crash. When questioned, the woman claimed she wanted to file a case against Boeing in the U.S. The complainant, however, expressed his intention to pursue legal action on his own against Boeing, Air India, the Government of India, and the Airport Authority.
When he refused to sign the documents, the accused left the premises. The police are currently investigating the case.
Victim was a cook in London, recently returned
Vardi Chand Menaria, 49, was a resident of Rundeda village under Vallabhnagar police station in Udaipur district. He had returned from London a month before the accident, where he worked as a cook. He died in the Air India Boeing Dreamliner crash on June 12, 2025, in Ahmedabad. His wife and son had dropped him at the airport just before the tragic incident.
Plane crashed on June 12
Air India's flight number AI-171 was flying from Ahmedabad to London on June 12. It was carrying a total of 230 passengers, including 169 Indians, 53 British, 7 Portuguese, and one Canadian national. Among them were 103 men, 114 women, 11 children, and 2 infants. The remaining 12 were crew members. Former Gujarat Chief Minister Vijay Rupani also died in the crash.
