In the video she said that the airport authorities have reached out to me minutes after she posted the first video and that the authorities are actively assisting with the situation.
Common Phone Scams to Avoid
- TRAI Scam: You receive a call claiming your number is linked to illegal activities. The call is forwarded to a fraudster impersonating a cybercrime official.
Note: TRAI does not disconnect services; telecom companies do.
- Digital Arrest Threat: A fraudster poses as a police officer, claiming the victim is under investigation and threatens digital arrest through fake video calls.
Note: Police do not conduct digital arrests. Disconnect and report.
- Family Member Arrest Scam: A caller claims a relative will be arrested unless charges are paid off. Some scammers may use AI to imitate the family member’s voice.
Note: Confirm with the family member before taking action.
- Stock Trading Scam: Fake ads promise high stock returns. Victims are led to bogus portfolios and encouraged to invest more until the scammers vanish.
Note: High-return schemes are often scams.
- Easy Task Offers: Scammers promise money for simple tasks, then request investments in cryptos for returns. Victims lose larger sums after initial small payments.
Note: Easy money schemes are typically scams.
- Customs Interception: Victims are told a parcel with contraband is stuck at customs and must pay to resolve it.
Note: Clicking links can compromise your device. Disconnect and report.
- Bogus Credit Card Calls: A fake executive claims a large transaction occurred and requests card details.
Note: You will receive SMS alerts for legitimate transactions.
- False Money Transfer Claims: A scam text claims funds were credited to your account, and the scammer asks for repayment of a 'mistaken' transfer.
Note: Verify with your bank before acting.
- KYC Expiry Scam: Victims receive requests to update KYC via links, leading to personal data theft.
Note: Banks do not request KYC updates via links.
- Tax Refund Scam: Fraudsters posing as tax officials ask for bank details to expedite refunds, then steal funds.",478,,True,False
