Tirupati: Tirupati police arrested seven members of a notorious gang of cyber criminals involved in âdigital arrest' modus operandi and recovered cash and gold ornaments worth approximately Rs 95 lakh from the possession of the accused.
Addressing a press conference at the district police office on Tuesday, Tirupati SP V Harshavardhan Raju said this gang recently duped a 65-year-old woman in Tirupati by posing as CBI officers investigating a Rs 200 crore money laundering case.
""The gang resorted to digital arrest and siphoned off Rs 2.5 crore from the elderly woman. After she realised that she was conned by the gang, the victim lodged a formal police complaint at the Tirupati West police station on Jan 13,"" he said.
Special police teams were formed to investigate the case and they zeroed-in on the gang at Visakhapatnam, and took seven accused into custody. They were identified as Palakollu Arun Vinay Kumar (Rajahmundry), Singampalli Ganesh (34), Palakollu Ravi Kumar (28), U Jagadish (37), P Anand Santosh Kumar (39), U Amar Anand (33) and Karri Vasudev, all residents of different localities at Visakhapatnam.
Police recovered Rs 57 lakh in cash and gold ornaments worth approximately Rs 38 lakh from the possession of the accused. Police also froze a total sum of Rs 36 lakh parked in numerous bank accounts of the accused. Various equipment such as sim modules, routers and mobile phones belonging to the accused were also seized.
According to the Tirupati SP, the gang members had undergone training to carry out various cyber crimes at Cambodia and set up a hi-fi cyber crime office at Visakhapatnam, from where they were resorting to various cyber crime activities including digital arrests.
Harshavardhan Raju cautioned people to take note that there has been a sudden spike in digital arrests trend across the country in the recent times. ""People should know that no Central govt agency or state police departments would place anyone under arrest digitally. People should also be extremely careful when they receive anonymous calls as it is clear that cyber criminals are not only targeting high-profile people but have also been targeting the Aam janta off late,"" the SP cautioned.
