Chandigarh: In another case of cyber fraud, a 73-year-old retired Haryana govt officer was duped of Rs 85 lakh after falling prey to a sophisticated ""digital arrest"" scam that spanned over a month.The victim, a Sector 13 resident, told police that on Oct 9, he received a call from a man claiming to be Vijay Pandit from the Telephone Authority of India. The caller alleged that the victim's Aadhaar had been misused to open a bank account in Delhi for money laundering. Soon, the complainant was connected to three others posing as senior vigilance officers â Rajesh Pardhan, Neeraj Thakur, and Pardeep Singh âwho threatened arrest of him and his wife unless he complied.The fraudsters convinced him that a âPriority Innocence Certificate' was needed to clear his name and demanded multiple deposits into different accounts for ""RBI verification."" Over several weeks, the victim transferred Rs 85 lakh while receiving fake RBI notices and court summons on WhatsApp. The scammers even assured him that the money would be refunded after verification.The ordeal ended when the victim approached Manimajra police and learned he had been conned. A case has been registered at the Sector 17 cyber police station, and investigators are tracking the money trail. No arrests have been made yet.
