Gujarat High Court has granted regular bail to Banwarilal Yadav, a garage mechanic accused in a ₹3,33,500 cyber fraud case, observing prima facie that he appears to have been implicated merely because a co-accused used his mobile hotspot for the fraudulent activity.
A single-judge bench noted that co-accused Rahul Meena allegedly perpetrated the fraud by using Yadav's mobile hotspot. Beyond this connection, the court observed that there was no evidence suggesting Yadav played any active role in the offence.
""Prima facie, it also does not appear that the dummy message had been sent with the consent of the applicant - accused or that the applicant had any knowledge or had received any benefit out of the entire fraudulent transaction,"" the court remarked while exercising its discretion for bail.
Considering the limited role attributed to the applicant - accused and the investigation had reached the charge-sheet stage, and his clean criminal record with no prior antecedents, acted as key factors favouring bail.
The court clarified that these observations are preliminary for the purpose of bail and should not influence the final trial.
Yadav, who had been in judicial custody since December 22, 2025, was arrested after investigative trails linked the complainant's ""dummy message"" to his IP address.
The FIR was registered at the Morbi City 'A' Division Police Station following a complaint that the victim was defrauded of ₹3,33,500 after accessing a suspicious message on his mobile phone.",263,,True,False
3105.0,"From Digital Arrest to Online Fraud, FIR Can Now Be Filed from Home - The420.in",The420.in,"Thu, 30 Jul 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://the420.in/gujarat-launches-e-zero-fir-cyber-fraud-system/,success_decoder,success,"From Digital Arrest to Online Fraud, FIR Can Now Be Filed from Home",The,2026-07-30,"In a major step to strengthen the response to cyber financial fraud, the Gujarat government has launched the Cyber Financial Fraud e-Zero FIR system, enabling victims to initiate legal proceedings without visiting a police station. Under the new mechanism, complaints related to cyber financial fraud registered through the national cybercrime helpline 1930 will automatically generate an e-Zero FIR, which will then be electronically forwarded to the jurisdictional police station. The initiative is expected to eliminate delays in the initial stage of investigations and improve the chances of freezing or recovering defrauded money.
The new system was formally launched by Gujarat Deputy Chief Minister Harsh Sanghavi in Gandhinagar. Developed in coordination with the Indian Cyber Crime Coordination Centre (I4C) under the Union Ministry of Home Affairs, the initiative is designed to enable faster police intervention, preserve digital evidence at an early stage, and enhance the effectiveness of cybercrime investigations.
According to the government, victims of digital arrest scams, investment fraud, fake trading schemes, UPI fraud, banking fraud, credit card fraud, and other forms of cyber financial crime can now report incidents through the 1930 helpline, following which a Zero FIR will be generated automatically and transmitted to the appropriate police station. The concerned police station will then complete the legal formalities, register a regular FIR, and immediately initiate the investigation.
Deputy Chief Minister Harsh Sanghavi said the Gujarat government remains committed to making policing faster, more transparent, and citizen-friendly through technology. He said the e-Zero FIR system would allow cyber fraud victims to lodge complaints from the comfort of their homes while significantly improving the chances of recovering stolen funds through prompt police action.
Gujarat Director General of Police (DGP) Gyanendra Singh Malik has directed all field units across the state to handle cyber financial fraud complaints with seriousness and sensitivity. He instructed police officers to ensure the effective implementation of the new system and provide timely justice to complainants through swift action.
Inspector General of Police (IGP) Bipin Ahire, who heads the state’s Cyber Centre of Excellence, said the e-Zero FIR mechanism had been developed in collaboration with I4C. He explained that every cyber financial fraud complaint received through the 1930 helpline would automatically generate a Zero FIR, which would be digitally transmitted to the concerned police station. Officers would then contact the complainant, obtain the required information, complete legal formalities, and immediately begin the investigation.
Officials also highlighted the first case registered under the new system. An Ahmedabad resident allegedly targeted in a digital arrest scam became the first beneficiary of the e-Zero FIR mechanism. Acting swiftly after receiving the complaint, the Cyber Crime Department successfully prevented a financial loss of more than ₹15.76 lakh. Officials said prompt action during the initial hours is often critical in cyber financial fraud cases and can substantially improve the chances of recovering stolen funds.
Renowned cybercrime expert and former IPS officer Prof. Triveni Singh said time is the most critical factor in cyber financial fraud investigations. According to him, if victims report incidents immediately through the 1930 helpline and law enforcement agencies initiate legal action without delay, the likelihood of freezing suspicious bank accounts and recovering stolen money increases significantly. He added that the e-Zero FIR system reduces the time gap between complaint registration and the start of the investigation, making it a valuable tool against digital arrest scams, investment fraud, UPI fraud, and other forms of online financial crime.
Cybersecurity experts believe fraudsters often exploit delays in complaint registration by rapidly moving stolen funds through multiple bank accounts and digital wallets. Reducing the response time between reporting and investigation is therefore considered one of the most effective ways to combat cyber-enabled financial crime. The e-Zero FIR initiative is expected to significantly strengthen this early-response mechanism.
The Gujarat government said additional technological enhancements would be introduced in the future to further streamline digital complaint processing, banking coordination, and investigative procedures. Authorities have urged citizens to report any cyber financial fraud immediately through the 1930 national cybercrime helpline or the National Cyber Crime Reporting Portal, emphasizing that timely reporting remains the most effective way to improve fund recovery and bring cybercriminals to justice.",690,,True,False
3106.0,"Ahmedabad reports 694 cyber fraud cases in 2 years, 664 acc… - GujaratSamachar English",GujaratSamachar English,"Sun, 28 Jun 2026 02:19:04 GMT",https://english.gujaratsamachar.com/news/gujarat/ahmedabad-reports-694-cyber-fraud-cases-in-2-years-664-accused-arrested-27458290619,success_decoder,success,"Ahmedabad reports 694 cyber fraud cases in 2 years, 664 acc…",Siddhi Dahanukar,2026-03-27,"Ahmedabad reports 694 cyber fraud cases in 2 years, 664 accused arrested
Summarized by AI; it may make mistakes. Check important info
A total 694 offences related to cyber fraud have been registered across police stations in Ahmedabad city in a span of two years between February 1, 2024 and January 31, 2026.
According to data presented in the Gujarat Legislative Assembly in Gandhinagar, the Ahmedabad Cyber Crime Police Station recorded the highest number of cases at 238. In contrast, Krishnagar Police Station registered 22 cases, while Naroda Police Station reported 21 offences. Sardarnagar, Danilimada, and Isanpur police stations each recorded 19 cases during the two-year period.
Notably, River East and River Front West police stations reported zero cyber fraud cases. Vatva and Chandkheda police stations registered only one case each, and Elisbridge Police Station saw two offences.
The total amount involved in these cyber frauds in Ahmedabad stood at ₹134.45 crore. Out of this, police have successfully recovered ₹49.01 crore. Detection was achieved in 315 cases, leading to the arrest of 664 accused persons across the two years.
Police officials investigate these cases thoroughly by examining bank KYC details, bank statements, transaction records, Call Detail Records (CDR), and IP addresses. For accused persons operating from other states, technical analysis of bank accounts and mobile numbers is conducted. Teams of police officials are dispatched to coordinate with local police in other states to trace and apprehend the culprits.
To facilitate the recovery of fraud amounts, banks are promptly notified through nodal officers. Details of the concerned accounts and transactions are obtained, and with court orders, the frozen amounts are refunded to the victim-complainants.
The government has emphasised that coordinated efforts between police, banks, and cyber cells are ongoing to strengthen investigations and minimise losses due to rising digital frauds. Authorities have urged citizens to remain vigilant against suspicious calls, links, and investment offers that often lead to such scams.",319,,True,False
3111.0,"3 held from Gujarat, Raj, MP in separate cyber fraud cases | Hindustan Times - Hindustan Times",Hindustan Times,"Sun, 10 May 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://www.hindustantimes.com/cities/others/3-held-from-gujarat-raj-mp-in-separate-cyber-fraud-cases-101778350099191.html,success_decoder,success,"3 held from Gujarat, Raj, MP in separate cyber fraud cases",HT Correspondent,2026-05-10,"3 held from Gujarat, Raj, MP in separate cyber fraud cases
