3259.0,"'Not a traitor': Cyber fraud ties lawyer to Pahalgam attack, he dies by suicide - India Today",India Today,"Thu, 27 Nov 2025 08:00:00 GMT",https://www.indiatoday.in/cities/bhopal/story/senior-advocate-suicide-cyber-fraud-terror-funding-madhya-pradesh-2826654-2025-11-27,success_decoder,success,"'Not a traitor': Cyber fraud ties lawyer to Pahalgam attack, he dies by suicide",Ravish Pal Singh,2025-11-27,"'Not a traitor': Cyber fraud ties lawyer to Pahalgam attack, he dies by suicide
A senior advocate in Madhya Pradesh died by suicide after receiving threats linked to a fake terror funding case. Police said it appeared to be a case of extortion through digital arrest.
A 68-year-old senior advocate in Madhya Pradesh died by suicide after cyber fraudsters allegedly threatened to implicate him in a fake terror funding case, police said.
Shivkumar Verma, a long-time resident of Bhopal’s Jahangirabad area, was found hanging in his room on Monday night. Police said a note recovered from the spot indicated he died by suicide out of fear after receiving threats linked to a bank account opened in his name allegedly linking him with the Pahalgam terror attack.
""I am committing suicide of my own free will. Because someone opened a fake HDFC Bank account using my name to fund Pahalgam terrorist Asif. I will not tolerate being branded a traitor,"" the note read.
Verma’s wife and daughter were in Delhi at the time of the incident, while his son works in Pune. Police said his wife became worried after he did not answer her calls and asked their tenant to check on him. The tenant looked through a window and saw Verma hanging, officers said. He was taken to a nearby hospital, where doctors declared him dead.
The advocate also wrote in his note that he had done public service during the 1984 Bhopal Gas Tragedy, performing last rites for “hundreds of victims.” He said the then chief minister provided him with an ambulance in recognition of his services. Verma also stated he had donated blood nearly 50 times and “saved many lives.”
Police said the case appears to be linked to a form of cyber extortion known as “digital arrest,” in which victims are coerced into believing they face immediate legal action.
Bhopal Police Commissioner Harinarayanchari Mishra told India Today’s sister channel Aaj Tak that a case has been registered and investigation is on. Efforts are on to identify the fraudsters behind the threats.
""The suicide note mentions that his bank account and mobile number were used in an incident,” the official said. “The police have gathered all the facts and the accused will be arrested soon.""",377,,True,False
3261.0,"Laos based cybercrime gang exposed in Indore digital arrest case: 350 SIM cards routed abroad, 19 arrested ... - Bhaskar English",Bhaskar English,"Thu, 27 Nov 2025 14:44:03 GMT",https://www.bhaskarenglish.in/local/mp/news/laos-cybercrime-gang-exposed-in-indore-case-350-sim-cards-sent-abroad-19-held-victim-kept-4-days-136526814.html,success_decoder,success,"Laos based cybercrime gang exposed in Indore digital arrest case: 350 SIM cards routed abroad, 19 arrested so far; victim was 'digitally arrested' for 4 days - Madhya Pradesh News",Tarun Tiwari,2025-11-27,"- English News
