According to official sources, CBI constituted nearly 60 specialised teams to conduct searches at over 80 locations spread across 16 states.
The Central Bureau of Investigation (CBI) has launched a major nationwide crackdown on “digital arrest” scams and organised cyber fraud syndicates, carrying out coordinated searches across several states, including Assam and Manipur.
The operation, named Operation Chakra-VI, aims to dismantle cybercrime networks that have been exploiting citizens through impersonation, fake legal threats, and sophisticated online deception.
According to official sources, the CBI constituted nearly 60 specialised teams to conduct searches at over 80 locations spread across 16 states. The raids targeted key regions such as Punjab, Delhi, Gujarat, Assam, and others as part of an extensive effort to disrupt cybercrime infrastructure operating nationwide.
In Assam, the agency carried out searches in Sivasagar and Charaideo districts, focusing on individuals and networks suspected of being linked to large-scale cyber fraud operations. Investigators revealed that the accused groups had developed fake websites designed to resemble official government portals, including a counterfeit version of the Supreme Court of India’s website.
These fraudulent platforms were allegedly used to intimidate victims by displaying fabricated court orders, arrest warrants, and legal notices. Victims were made to believe they were under a so-called “digital arrest” and were pressured into transferring large amounts of money to avoid legal action.
During the searches, officials seized several incriminating materials, including mobile phones, electronic devices, and financial records believed to be connected to the fraud network. The evidence is expected to help investigators trace the broader ecosystem supporting such cybercrime activities.
The latest operation follows earlier investigations in Assam linked to digital arrest scams. In a previous case, the CBI busted a SIM card racket in Silchar, Assam’s Cachar district, arresting a telecom company’s regional sales manager and two middlemen. The accused were allegedly involved in activating thousands of unauthorised “ghost SIM cards,” which were later supplied to cybercriminals for fraudulent activities.
Meanwhile, authorities have issued a strong public advisory, emphasising that “digital arrest” has no legal validity in India. Law enforcement agencies, including the CBI and police, cannot arrest or detain individuals through phone or video calls. Citizens have been urged to remain vigilant and immediately report any such fraudulent attempts to the authorities.
