3412.0,Cyber cell cracks down on multiple cyber fraud syndicates operating across India - ANI News,ANI News,"Mon, 10 Nov 2025 08:00:00 GMT",https://www.aninews.in/news/national/general-news/cyber-cell-cracks-down-on-multiple-cyber-fraud-syndicates-operating-across-india20251110150118,success_decoder,success,Cyber cell cracks down on multiple cyber fraud syndicates operating across India,ANI,2026-08-30,"New Delhi [India], November 10 (ANI): In a major breakthrough, the cyber cell of the Crime Branch has conducted multi-state raids in Delhi, Haryana, Uttarakhand, and Punjab and cracked down on several cyber fraud syndicates.
Several key masterminds of Digital Arrest and Investment Fraud rackets have been arrested, and at least Rs. 5 crore of cryptocurrency trail linked to Dubai-handlers was recovered. 
Along with this, fake firms, mule accounts, and e-commerce front operations were also uncovered during the operation. 
Among the arrested individuals are, Sumit Kumar, Atul Sharma of Kurukshetra, Rahul Manda of Hisar, Varun Anchal alias Lucky of Jalandhar, and Amit Kumar Singh alias Kartik of Saran have been have been arrested in different cases. 
Key syndicate operator working under the Dubai-based handler Sumit Garg was arrested in the Investment Scam.
Rahul Manda was arrested in a Digital Arrest fraud case, where a victim lost Rs. 30 lakh after falling prey to his scam. 
Varun Anchal was apprehended for managing several mule accounts and fund transfers, while Amit Kumar Singh, who is an ex-bank employee, helped facilitate the transactions. 
The Supreme Court has also cancelled the bail of another accused, Lakshay Nanda of Ludhiana, involved in a Rs. 48.35 lakh investment fraud case. 
The Cyber Cell of the Crime Branch recovered mobile phones, SIM cards, laptops, cheque books, and three cryptocurrency wallets holding approximately 552,944 USDT (approx Rs. 5 crore) in a raid in Gurugram.
The scammers used fake police and agency identities to intimidate victims into surrendering to a Digital Arrest. They also created fake investment platforms promising high returns to fish victims for investment fraud. 
They recruited financially weaker students, PG residents, and unemployed youngsters to open mule current accounts. They used fake e-commerce companies to mask transactions and cash flow. (ANI)
Cyber cell cracks down on multiple cyber fraud syndicates operating across India
ANI | Updated: Nov 10, 2025 15:01 IST",315,,True,False
3413.0,From Jamtara to Dubai — the web of cyber fraudsters spreads fast - The Indian Express,The Indian Express,"Sun, 28 Sep 2025 07:00:00 GMT",https://indianexpress.com/article/cities/chandigarh/from-jamtara-to-dubai-the-web-of-cyber-fraudsters-spreads-fast-10275559/,success_decoder,success,From Jamtara to Dubai — the web of cyber fraudsters spreads fast,Jagpreet Singh Sandhu; Gouravmishra,2025-09-28,"The Cyber Crime Unit of Chandigarh Police has learnt that the web of cyber criminals which initially started in Jamtara, Jharkhand, has now reached Dubai and Cambodia, along with several states across India.
DSP (Cyber Crime), Chandigarh Police, A Venkatesh said that during investigations of multiple cases, it has been learnt that earlier around five years ago, most of the cybercrime cases of “phishing” originated from Jamtara, but now the web of the cyber criminals has spread to South East Asia, including countries like Cambodia and Dubai.
There have been several withdrawals of money involved in cyber fraud in Dubai, Cambodia, said the DSP.
As per the Chandigarh Police Cyber Crime unit, recently a transnational cybercrime syndicate networking from Southeast Asia was traced.
DSP Venkatesh said that the syndicate was operating “digital arrest” scams across India with roots traced to Southeast Asian countries, including Cambodia, Laos, Myanmar, Hong Kong, and financial links stretching to hubs like Dubai.
Police investigations during a cyber fraud case involving a loss of Rs 1.01 crore had revealed that the syndicate operates using SIM box technology, primarily devices manufactured by Dinstar. These allow fraudsters to route internet-based international calls into India, disguising them as local numbers. Each SIM box can generate over 200,000 IVR (interactive voice response) calls daily, enabling mass-scale fraud attempts.
Many of these SIM box operations were remotely managed from Southeast Asia, with handlers located in Cambodia, Malaysia, Taiwan, and Hong Kong. Financial flows were also routed through intermediaries in Dubai, a common transit point for laundering illicit money, said the DSP.
Rajasthan – emerging as new hub of cyber criminals
In its 56 raids across 10 states in India till August 2025, the Cyber Crime Police of Chandigarh has arrested 109 people, and among which 26 accused landed into police net from Rajasthan.
As per the Cyber Crime Police, the raids spanned cybercrime hotspots across Gujarat, Rajasthan, Uttar Pradesh, Maharashtra, Punjab, West Bengal, Delhi NCR, Haryana, Jharkhand, and Bihar. These states have emerged as hubs for organised cybercriminal gangs who exploit digital platforms for financial frauds, phishing, identity theft, and elaborate scams.
Police data reveals that apart from Rajasthan, 23 have been arrested from Uttar Pradesh, 12 from Haryana, 11 from Punjab, eight from Delhi-NCR.
Sector 46 and Manimajra hotspots
The City’s Cybercrime Police has also identified hotspots: Sector 46 and Manimajra in Chandigarh for cheque withdrawal frauds. These hotspots were flagged on the Pratibhimb Portal, and further prompting targeted policing in these areas.
‘If SIM cards, bank accounts not misused, fraud can be prevented’
As per DSP Venkatesh, the cyber criminals work through SIM cards obtained through fake IDs and bank accounts obtained from gullible people.
While the SIM cards are further used by the cyber criminals to dupe the victims by calling them, the bank accounts are used further to receive the money from victims.
From “digital arrest” to other banking frauds, it has been learnt that the criminals have been receiving money involved in fraud, in multiple bank accounts, and have been buying the accounts from gullible people by paying them some money. The cheated money is transferred from one bank account to another to deceive the police, and then the money is withdrawn. In multiple cases, the cyber police has arrested the persons holding the bank accounts, but during the investigation the police learnt that the bank account holders had sold their bank account details to the actual accused involved in the crime.
“We have ben registering FIRs against the persons whose bank accounts and SIM cards have been used in the crime,” said the DSP.
Verify your transaction even before using apps
DSP Venkatesh has cautioned people on using apps like Google Pay, Paytm and others.
“One must verify the transaction before doing it,” said the DSP.",631,,True,False
3424.0,Retired Bank Employee Duped Of Rs 55 Lakh In Digital Arrest Fraud In Rajasthan - ETV Bharat,ETV Bharat,"Mon, 06 Jul 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://www.etvbharat.com/en/state/rajasthan-retired-bank-employee-duped-of-55-lakh-in-digital-arrest-cyber-fraud-enn26070602551,success_decoder,success,Retired Bank Employee Duped Of Rs 55 Lakh In Digital Arrest Fraud In Rajasthan,ETV Bharat,2026-07-06,"Retired Bank Employee Duped Of Rs 55 Lakh In Digital Arrest Fraud In Rajasthan
The fraudsters allegedly kept the victim engaged on video calls for two days before duping him, reports Rahul Manohar.
Published : July 6, 2026 at 3:47 PM IST
|Updated : July 6, 2026 at 3:54 PM IST
Sikar: A 65-year-old retired bank employee was allegedly duped of Rs 55 lakh by cyber fraudsters in Rajasthan’s Sikar district after they threatened to implicate him in a fake money laundering case and subjected him to a so-called digital arrest, police said.
According to Cyber police station in-charge Mukesh Kumar, the victim, Mahavir Prasad Sharma, received a WhatsApp call from an unidentified person claiming to be a Delhi Police officer. The caller alleged that a SIM card issued in Sharma’s name had been used to send objectionable messages and that multiple criminal cases had been registered against him.
When Sharma denied the allegations, the fraudsters allegedly threatened to implicate him in a money laundering case and claimed to represent agencies including the Enforcement Directorate. They also posed as police officials and even a Supreme Court judge during video calls, displaying fake court proceedings to intimidate him, police said.
The accused allegedly kept the victim engaged on WhatsApp video calls for two days and instructed him not to contact his family or anyone else, citing national security concerns. Under fear and mental pressure, Sharma transferred Rs 55 lakh in two instalments to bank accounts specified by fraudsters. The fraud came to light after his son grew suspicious of his behaviour and spoke to him.
A case has been registered at the cyber police station, and officials are examining the WhatsApp call records and mobile numbers used in the fraud. Police said the accused switched off all the numbers after the crime. Cyber police officials said 19 cyber fraud cases have been registered in Sikar district between January and June this year, with total losses exceeding Rs 1.5 crore.
Cyber police station in-charge Mukesh Kumar advised the public that there is no legal provision for digital arrest and urged people not to transfer money or share banking credentials, OTPs or passwords with anyone claiming to be from law enforcement agencies. They also asked citizens to report suspected cyber fraud immediately by calling the national cyber helpline 1930.
