The Rajasthan High Court in Jaipur has taken serious note of a case involving an 83-year-old woman who was allegedly duped through a “digital arrest” cyber fraud. The court has summoned the Director General of Police (DGP) and the Superintendent of Police (Cyber Crime) in connection with the matter.
The order was passed by Justice Sameer Jain while hearing the bail plea of accused Naveen Temani.
Court questions ‘forced settlement’ claim
During the hearing, the accused argued that he had reached a settlement of ₹10 lakh with the elderly woman and sought bail on that basis.
However, when the court interacted with the victim, she stated that she was being forced into the compromise due to lack of justice from the investigating agency. She said she was pressured to settle her legitimate claim of ₹80 lakh for just ₹10 lakh.
‘Case has shaken court’s conscience’
Observing the seriousness of the matter, the court remarked that the case was “unique and disturbing” and had deeply shaken its conscience.
Considering the condition of the victim and the gravity of the alleged cyber crime, the court deemed it necessary to summon senior police officials.
DGP, Cyber SP summoned; officer to appear in person
The court directed that the DGP and SP (Cyber Crime) appear via video conferencing on March 27. It also ordered the investigating officer to remain present in court in person.
The court noted that the elderly woman is a victim of a serious economic cyber offence.
Government asked to arrange stay for victim
The High Court also directed the state government to make proper arrangements if the woman wishes to stay in the city for the case proceedings.
It ordered that high-quality accommodation and food be provided at the state’s expense, warning that any negligence in compliance would be taken seriously.
₹80 lakh transferred to Dubai-based accounts
According to government counsel Vivek Sharma, the victim, a resident of Ajmer, was duped of ₹80 lakh through a “digital arrest” scam. The money was routed through layered accounts and withdrawn in different bank accounts in Dubai.
Although a chargesheet has been filed, the accused are reportedly based in Dubai and have not been arrested yet.
Court seeks complete case documents
The court has directed the government counsel to compile and present all relevant documents related to the case before the next hearing.",430,,True,False
3436.0,"Rajasthan HC Summons DGP, Cyber Crime SP In ₹80 Lakh Digital Arrest Fraud Case - The420.in",The420.in,"Thu, 26 Mar 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://the420.in/rajasthan-hc-summons-dgp-cyber-fraud-80-lakh/,success_decoder,success,"Rajasthan HC Summons DGP, Cyber Crime SP In ₹80 Lakh Digital Arrest Fraud Case",The,2026-03-26,"The Rajasthan High Court has summoned the Director General of Police (DGP) and the Superintendent of Police (Cyber Crime) in connection with a cyber fraud case involving ₹80 lakh, highlighting judicial concern over the handling of digital arrest scams.
The court directed both senior police officials to appear via video conferencing on March 27, while the investigating officer has been asked to appear physically along with the case diary.
Elderly Victim Duped In Digital Arrest Scam
The case pertains to an 83-year-old woman who was allegedly defrauded of ₹80 lakh through a “digital arrest” scam—an increasingly common cybercrime tactic where fraudsters impersonate law enforcement officials to extort money.
In similar cases reported in Rajasthan, victims are contacted via video calls by scammers posing as police or central agency officers and are coerced into transferring money under the threat of arrest or legal action.
Such scams typically involve psychological pressure, isolation of victims, and rapid transfer of funds through multiple bank accounts or cryptocurrency channels.
FCRF Launches Premier CISO Certification Amid Rising Demand for Cybersecurity Leadership
Court Flags Victim Vulnerability
During the hearing, the court took note of the victim’s age and vulnerable condition. The woman reportedly informed the court that she lives alone and has not been able to recover from the trauma of the fraud.
The bench emphasised the need for a sensitive and victim-centric approach in such cases, especially where elderly individuals are targeted.
Directions For Victim Support
In a significant direction, Justice Sameer Jain instructed the state government to ensure proper accommodation and food arrangements for the victim, with all expenses to be borne by the State.
The court’s intervention reflects a broader judicial trend of recognising the psychological and financial impact of cyber fraud on victims, particularly senior citizens.
The matter forms part of a growing number of cyber fraud cases in Rajasthan involving “digital arrest” tactics. Courts have previously observed that such scams have no legal basis and are being used by organised networks to exploit fear and misinformation.
Investigations in similar cases have revealed that fraudsters often route money through multiple accounts and convert it into cryptocurrency to evade detection.
Judicial Oversight On Cybercrime Enforcement
By summoning top police officials, the High Court has signalled the need for greater accountability and effective investigation in cyber fraud cases.
The case will be closely watched as it progresses, particularly in terms of enforcement response, victim compensation, and broader measures to tackle digital arrest scams.
