Sunday Big StoryRajasthan youths trafficked to Cambodia for cyber fraud:Chinese gangs train fake officers using Indian SIM cards to defraud ₹1,100 crore
- Copy link
Unemployed youths from Rajasthan are being trafficked to Cambodia and trained to carry out large-scale cyber fraud. Chinese cybercriminals allegedly train these youths to impersonate senior Indian officials, including CID, IPS, CBI and Enforcement Directorate officers, to defraud businessmen and wealthy individuals in India.
The fraud operations rely heavily on bank accounts and mobile SIM cards sourced from Rajasthan itself. These revelations emerged following three major cybercrime operations conducted over three consecutive days on 18, 19 and 20 January. During these operations, police exposed an extensive network involving human trafficking, fake SIM cards and mule bank accounts, all linked to Cambodia-based cyber fraud syndicates.
Broker arrested for sending youths to Cambodia
On 19 January, Kotputli–Behror police arrested Suresh Sen, a resident of Ramnagar in Bansur. Suresh acted as a broker, sending youths from Rajasthan and other states to Cambodia to work in cyber fraud centres.
According to police, Suresh sent at least 50 youths from the Bansur area alone to Cambodia. They were promised salaries ranging from ₹1 lakh to ₹1.5 lakh per month and were lured with job offers such as data entry, customer support and digital marketing. However, upon arrival, they were forced into cyber fraud operations.
Training in ‘Digital arrest’ and investment scams
During interrogation, Suresh revealed that the youths were trained in a so-called 'digital arrest' scam. They were provided scripts and trained to impersonate IPS officers, CBI inspectors and ED officials.
They were taught how different officials speak, how to threaten victims, and the use official terminology and methods to build trust. If victims did not fall for digital arrest scams, the youths were trained to defraud them through fake stock market and cryptocurrency investment schemes.
Advanced technical training was also provided, including the use of VPNs and foreign servers to evade law enforcement, creating fake apps and websites, and tracking and managing fraudulent transactions.
20 more youths counselled
Kotputli–Behror Superintendent of Police Devendra Bishnoi stated that families of youths already sent to Cambodia were counselled and informed about their sons’ involvement in illegal activities. Around 20 other youths who were in contact with Suresh were also counselled and released. A case has been registered against Suresh, and the investigation is ongoing.
Fake SIM cards sent from Jodhpur to Cambodia
A separate cyber fraud case registered at Basni Police Station in Jodhpur revealed that active Indian SIM cards were being used in Cambodia. The SIM used in one fraud case was registered at a Jodhpur address but was operating in Cambodia.
Police analysed data of over 2.3 lakh mobile numbers active in Cambodia. This analysis, conducted by the Indian Cybercrime Coordination Centre (I4C), revealed that 36,000 Indian SIM cards are currently active in Cambodia. Further investigation showed that fraud worth ₹1,100 crore had been committed using 5,300 of these numbers, with cybercrime continuing through them.
Rahul Jha identified as SIM supplier
One SIM card, registered in the name of Murad Khan from Salawas Road, Jodhpur, had been roaming in Cambodia since activation. Using this SIM, fraud worth ₹90 lakh was committed in Telangana. When contacted by Telangana Police, Murad Khan filed a complaint in Jodhpur, claiming misuse of a fake SIM issued in his name.
This led police to Rahul Jha, a resident of Sangaria in Jodhpur, who allegedly procured fake SIM cards from Jodhpur, Nagaur and other districts and supplied them to Cambodian fraud gangs. Police are currently searching for Rahul Jha.
On 20 January, police arrested Mohammad Sharif and Prakash Bhil from Jodhpur, Ramavatar Rathi and Hemant Panwar from Nagaur, Harish Malakar from Ajmer, and Sandeep Bhatt from Punjab for selling fake SIM cards.
Foreign Nationals collected SIMs in Jaipur
Malaysian nationals Li Jian Hui, Lo Di Khen, Chen Yu Ming and Leong Ken Neth reportedly stayed at a hotel on Tonk Road in Jaipur. Rahul Jha would meet them there and hand over consignments of SIM cards. These SIMs were used to make roaming calls for fraud and were also linked to mule bank accounts, giving fraudsters complete control over banking transactions.
Bank accounts supplied from Kota to Cambodia
In another breakthrough, Delhi Police arrested Aditya Pratap Singh from Kota on 18 January in connection with a ₹15 lakh cyber fraud involving a Delhi woman. Investigations revealed Aditya as the mastermind supplying mule bank accounts to Cambodian handlers.
Sunil from Hyderabad opened fake companies and rented bank accounts, which passed through a chain involving Shankar (Hyderabad), Manoj (Lucknow) and Sandeep (Varanasi). Eventually, access to these accounts reached Aditya in Kota, who forwarded them to fraud handlers in Cambodia.",802,,True,False
