3495.0,Ludhiana: GADVASU professor duped of Rs 20 lakh in cyber fraud; probe on - The Indian Express,The Indian Express,"Sat, 19 Jul 2025 07:00:00 GMT",https://indianexpress.com/article/cities/chandigarh/ludhiana-gadvasu-professor-duped-rs-20-lakh-cyber-fraud-10135963/,success_decoder,success,Ludhiana: GADVASU professor duped of Rs 20 lakh in cyber fraud; probe on,Express News Service,2025-07-19,"Stay updated with the latest - Click here to follow us on Instagram
			This is an archive article published on July 19, 2025
		
	
														
A senior professor at the Guru Angad Dev Veterinary and Animal Sciences University (GADVASU), Ludhiana, was allegedly duped of Rs 20 lakh by a cyber fraudster posing as a Central Bureau of Investigation (CBI) officer, police said on Friday.
The victim — Dalpat Singh Malik, professor of Department of Livestock Production Management — was targeted by the cyber criminal, who staged a “digital arrest”, claiming his name had surfaced in a human trafficking investigation.
On July 2, Malik received a video call from the accused — dressed in office uniform, with a CBI logo in the background — who claimed to be a Bengaluru-based officer at the investigating agency. He told Malik that two men, arrested by the CBI, had identified him as an “accomplice”, following which, an “arrest warrant” was issued against him.
Fearing the “digital arrest”, Malik on July 3 transferred Rs 10 lakh to a bank account as mentioned by the accused.
The accused later demanded Rs 10 lakh more from the professor if he wanted to avoid the “digital arrest”, which he on July 15 transferred from a joint account he shares with his wife. Noticing the transaction, Malik’s wife — who is an advocate — questioned him. After Malik shared the ordeal with his wife, she found the matter suspicious and immediately consulted a police officer known to her. The police officer confirmed there was “no such legal concept as a digital arrest”, and that Malik was duped in a cyber fraud.
An FIR was then registered against the accused under sections 318(4) (cheating), and 319(2) (cheating by personation) of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita (BNS) at the Cyber Crime police station, officers said.
Inspector Satvir Singh, Cyber Crime police station, said: “The professor was threatened with arrest, and made to believe he could avoid it by staying under a ‘digital arrest’ as well as paying Rs 20 lakh. The accused manipulated him, convinced him through fake official visuals.”
Story continues below this ad
An investigation is underway to trace the accused at the earliest, police said.
“We are obtaining the bank details where the professor made the payments. We suspect multiple mule accounts may have been used in this case,” the inspector said.
