3507.0,Hyderabad Police arrest 56 persons from multiple States in cyber fraud cases - The Hindu,The Hindu,"Tue, 05 May 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://www.thehindu.com/news/cities/Hyderabad/hyderabad-police-arrest-56-persons-from-multiple-states-in-cyber-fraud-cases/article70943498.ece,success_decoder,success,Hyderabad Police arrest 56 persons from multiple States in cyber fraud cases,The Hindu Bureau,2026-05-05,"The cyber crimes wing of Hyderabad Police arrested 56 individuals from different states in April as part of a crackdown on organised online fraud networks. This has led to the recovery and refund of nearly ₹70 lakh to victims.
The arrests were made under a special drive, “Operation Octopus 2.0”, which focused on dismantling cyber fraud networks involving bank insiders and mule accounts. Among those held were 32 bank officials, including managers, KYC approvers and relationship managers, along with 15 mule account holders and nine account operators linked to 101 cases registered in the city.
Police said the cases largely involved investment scams and trading frauds, which accounted for the majority of offences, followed by social media frauds, job scams and instances of digital arrest scams, where victims are coerced through intimidation.
The accused were traced across several states, including 17 from Telangana, 14 from Maharashtra, nine from Delhi, four from Karnataka, three from Rajasthan, two from Andhra Pradesh and one each from Tamil Nadu, Bihar and Gujarat. Investigators seized 34 mobile phones, cheque books, shell company stamps and other material used in executing frauds.
Officials said that during April alone, victims reported losses exceeding ₹9.13 crore across 33 major cases, of which ₹69.99 lakh was successfully refunded through coordinated intervention.
In parallel enforcement efforts, cyber patrol teams identified 184 social media profiles running over 800 paid advertisements promoting illegal betting apps and fake investment platforms. Cumulatively, 427 social media profiles and 1,903 paid promotional advertisements have been taken down.
Police also highlighted the role of their C-Mitra initiative, through which over 1,200 victims were contacted for assistance, and nearly 200 Zero FIRs were registered to ensure prompt action.
Authorities have urged the public to remain cautious of unsolicited investment offers, suspicious links and impersonation scams, stressing that early reporting through the cybercrime helpline can significantly improve chances of recovering lost money.
Published - May 05, 2026 08:11 pm IST",320,,True,False
3508.0,Malkajgiri police arrest five persons in multiple cyber fraud cases - The Hindu,The Hindu,"Sat, 30 May 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://www.thehindu.com/news/cities/Hyderabad/malkajgiri-police-arrest-five-persons-in-multiple-cyber-fraud-cases/article71041668.ece,success_decoder,success,Malkajgiri police arrest five persons in multiple cyber fraud cases,The Hindu Bureau,2026-05-30,"Five individuals were arrested by the cybercrime wing of the Malkajgiri police in connection with separate cyber fraud cases during the week ending May 30, uncovering links to cybercriminal networks operating across multiple states.
The arrested individuals were identified as Yakub Ibrahim Khan of Mumbai, Maharashtra; Orsu Sreenivasu of Suryapet district, Telangana; Gunda Nagaraju of Bachupally, Hyderabad; Pilli Aditya of Mettuguda, Hyderabad; and Pagadala Krishna Chaitanya of NTR district, Andhra Pradesh.
According to the police, the five were acting as mule account holders, providing bank accounts to cyber fraudsters involved in investment scams, job frauds, customer care fraud and digital arrest scams.
In one of the investment fraud cases, a victim was lured into investing through a fraudulent trading application after being added to a WhatsApp stock market group. The victim allegedly lost more than ₹1.54 crore after being shown fake profits and persuaded to make additional payments.
In another investment scam, a victim was induced to join a WhatsApp trading group and invest through a fake trading platform, resulting in a loss of over ₹80 lakh.
Police also arrested a suspect in a job fraud case in which a Hyderabad resident was cheated of ₹2.67 lakh through a fake overseas employment offer. The fraudsters posed as recruiters and collected money under the pretext of processing fees and other charges.
In a separate customer care fraud case, the accused impersonated representatives of online service platforms and extracted money from a victim by claiming that refunds were stuck in a digital wallet. The victim reportedly lost nearly ₹2 lakh.
Another accused was arrested in connection with a ‘digital arrest’ scam in which fraudsters posed as law enforcement officials and threatened a victim with a fabricated criminal case. The victim was allegedly coerced into transferring ₹71.25 lakh to accounts controlled by the fraudsters.
Police said all five accused had been arrested and remanded to judicial custody. Investigators are continuing efforts to identify and apprehend other members of the fraud networks.
The police also processed court-approved refunds amounting to ₹50.31 lakh during the week and facilitated the return of the money to victims of cybercrime.
Published - May 30, 2026 07:58 pm IST",360,,True,False
3510.0,Hyderabad cyber cops book Google India head in stock app fraud cases - The Times of India,The Times of India,"Wed, 22 Jul 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/city/hyderabad/google-india-head-named-co-accused-in-3-cyber-fraud-cases/articleshow/132523353.cms,success_decoder,success,Hyderabad cyber cops book Google India head in stock app fraud cases,Mahesh Buddi,2026-07-21,"HYDERABAD: In an unprecedented move, Hyderabad Cyber Crime Police have named the head of Google India as a co-accused in three cyber fraud cases after victims alleged they were duped through fraudulent stock market investment apps downloaded from the Google 
Play Store
.
The three complainants, who claimed to have lost between Rs 7 lakh and Rs 24 lakh, alleged that they trusted the apps because they were available on Googleâs official app marketplace. Based on their complaints, police have invoked provisions of the Information Technology (IT) Act and the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita (BNS) against the alleged fraudsters as well as the Google India head.
When TOI checked the three app links cited in the complaints, only one remained accessible.
âWe have booked the Google India head as a co-accused based on the specific complaints of the victims. Notices have been issued to Google directing it to remove the fraudulent applications. If the app is removed, the intermediary is exempt from liability and will not be charged. 
However, if it is not removed without a valid reason, the company also becomes an accused under Section 79(3) of the IT Act,â a Hyderabad Cyber Crime officer said.
On July 15, a 69-year-old resident of Santoshnagar filed a complaint alleging that he lost Rs 24 lakh in a fake stock market investment scheme.
According to the complaint, he came across a Facebook advertisement on May 20 featuring Union finance minister 
Nirmala Sitharaman
 purportedly endorsing an investment opportunity that promised returns of Rs 22 lakh a week. After clicking the advertisement, he was contacted on WhatsApp by a woman identifying herself as Dia Zian.
âShe made me install the One***ma***ts app from the Google Play Store, claiming it was licensed and regulated by South Africaâs Financial Sector Conduct Authority. Acting on her instructions, the complainant transferred Rs 24.37 lakh to multiple bank accounts during May and June 2026.
When he later sought to withdraw his investment, the money was awithheld. In his complaint, the victim alleged that the fraudulent appâs availability on the Play Store contributed to his loss and sought legal action against Google for allegedly failing to exercise due diligence before hosting the application.
Police registered a case against Dia Zian and Aneesh Mathew, who allegedly posed as representatives of the investment platform, along with the Google India head. The FIR invokes Sections 66-C and 66-D of the IT Act and Sections 318(4) (Cheating), 319(2) (Cheating by personation), 336(3) (Forgery), and 340(2) (Using a forged document or electronic record as genuine) of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita on July 15.
In another case registered on July 14, a 40-year-old govt employee from Adibatla alleged that he was cheated of more than Rs 17 lakh through a fake Fi****e Securities Platform.
The complainant told police that he received a WhatsApp message on May 12 from a woman identifying herself as Ananya Kulkarni, who claimed to represent a company offering stock market investment services, IPO strategies and AI-based investment opportunities.
He was allegedly instructed to download the F**L Smart app from the Google Play Store through a link shared by the accused. Between May 12 and June 16, he transferred more than Rs 17 lakh, including money borrowed through personal loans, to multiple bank accounts.
âI took personal loans and transferred over Rs 17 lakh to different bank accounts provided through the app between May 12 and June 16,â the victim said. Although the app reflected profits of Rs 41.7 lakh, the accused allegedly demanded additional payments before permitting withdrawals. The complainant said he realised he had been defrauded only after repeated demands for more money.
He also alleged that Google failed to exercise due diligence in allowing the fake application on the Play Store. Based on his complaint, police booked Ananya Kulkarni, Gul Tekchandani and the Google India head under provisions of the IT Act and the BNS.
In the third case, a 71-year-old retired govt employee from Erragadda alleged that he lost Rs 7 lakh after investing through a fraudulent stock market application, DS**, which he downloaded from the Google Play Store.
According to the complaint, two individuals identifying themselves as Swarnali and Avishek contacted him on WhatsApp and persuaded him to invest through the application.
Police have registered a case on June 30 against the two alleged fraudsters as well as the Google India head.
âGoogle Play strictly prohibits all forms of deception. We investigated this issue as soon as we were alerted, and confirmed that only one app was available on Play Store. Our reviews reveal that the app complies with Play's financial services policy. We review valid legal removal requests when notified through the correct legal processes and, where appropriate, we restrict or remove apps in keeping with local laws,â said a Google Spokesperson.",795,,True,False
3513.0,Doctors In Telangana Lost Rs 30 Crores To Cyber Fraud - Deccan Chronicle,Deccan Chronicle,"Wed, 17 Jun 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://www.deccanchronicle.com/southern-states/telangana/doctors-in-telangana-lost-rs-30-crores-to-cyber-fraud-1964091,success_decoder,success,Doctors In Telangana Lost Rs 30 Crores To Cyber Fraud,DC Correspondent,2026-06-17,"Doctors In Telangana Lost Rs 30 Crores To Cyber Fraud
“Cybercriminals today are targeting everyone, including healthcare professionals. Being highly educated or professionally accomplished does not make anyone immune to cyber fraud.
Hyderabad: With 735 doctors having been scammed of Rs 30 crore by cybercriminals since September 2024, the Telangana Cyber Security Bureau (TGCSB) held a high-level cyber awareness meeting with the medical fraternity here on Tuesday.
Speaking at the meeting, TGCSB Director Shikha Goel highlighted the rapidly evolving cyber threat landscape and emphasised the need for greater vigilance among healthcare professionals.
“Cybercriminals today are targeting everyone, including healthcare professionals. Being highly educated or professionally accomplished does not make anyone immune to cyber fraud. Awareness, caution, and timely reporting remain our strongest defenses,” she said.
The interaction was aimed at strengthening collaboration with doctors, improve cyber awareness, and formulate preventive strategies against emerging digital threats. Around 72 representatives, including presidents, secretaries, treasurers, and senior office bearers of major medical associations across Telangana, participated in the session.
Officials revealed that since September 2024, at least 735 doctors in Telangana have fallen victim to cyber frauds, suffering cumulative losses of `29.88 crores. Among the fraud categories, business and investment scams caused the biggest financial damage, with 127 victims losing `22.39 crore.
Cases of digital arrest fraud, identity theft, impersonation fraud, and advertisement scams also accounted for substantial losses. Other crimes reported among doctors included job frauds, sextortion, insurance frauds, UPI scams, matrimonial frauds, and cryptocurrency-related cheating.
Officials stressed that educational qualifications or professional success do not guarantee immunity from cybercrime, warning that even highly qualified healthcare professionals are increasingly becoming soft targets for sophisticated scams. The bureau highlighted the importance of the 'golden hour' in cyber fraud cases, stating that immediate reporting through the 1930 cybercrime helpline and the National Cyber Crime Reporting Portal significantly improves the chances of freezing fraudulent transactions and recovering stolen funds.
Representatives from leading medical bodies, including associations of paediatricians, cardiologists, dentists, orthopaedic surgeons, gynaecologists, and hospitals, participated in the discussion and agreed to jointly spread cyber awareness through hospitals, clinics, conferences, and medical education programmes to strengthen digital safety among doctors.",355,,True,False
3514.0,Mule account supplier arrested by Cyberabad Police in cyber fraud case - The Hindu,The Hindu,"Thu, 21 May 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/telangana/mule-account-supplier-arrested-by-cyberabad-police-in-cyber-fraud-case/article71006207.ece,success_decoder,success,Mule account supplier arrested by Cyberabad Police in cyber fraud case,The Hindu Bureau,2026-05-21,"A man was arrested for allegedly supplying mule bank accounts used in cyber fraud activities by the Cyber Crime Police and Special Operations Team (SOT), Cyberabad Police, during a joint operation on Wednesday, May 21.
The accused was identified as 27-year-old Dilip Singh, working as a delivery agent and residing at Apurupa Colony in IDA Jeedimetla under Quthbullapur mandal of Medchal-Malkajgiri district. Police said he is originally from Nagaur district in Rajasthan.
During the operation, police seized ₹1.02 lakh in cash, three mobile phones, 12 debit cards, one credit card, three bank passbooks, seven cheque books and 33 bank passbooks that had allegedly been couriered to different states.
According to Cyber Crimes DCP T. Saimanohar, the accused was involved in arranging and supplying mule bank accounts allegedly used in cyber frauds including trading frauds, digital arrest scams, online gaming frauds, stalking cases and part-time job scams.
The officer said that the accused came into contact with unidentified persons named “Jordan” and “Pintu Bhaiya” in 2024 through an Instagram advertisement seeking bank accounts.
After clicking the advertisement link, the accused allegedly communicated with the fraudsters through international WhatsApp numbers and was offered commissions of ₹25,000 for every savings account and ₹50,000 for every current account supplied.
Investigators stated that the accused was allegedly informed that the bank accounts would be used for cyber fraud activities but still knowingly participated for financial gain.
Police alleged that he procured and supplied bank accounts belonging to himself and others and forwarded ATM cards, SIM cards, passbooks, cheque books and related banking material to cyber fraudsters.
Officials said the investigation also revealed alleged involvement of certain bank employees in facilitating the opening and supply of accounts later used in cyber fraud transactions. Further investigation is underway to identify other persons linked to the network. Police warned the public against renting or sharing bank accounts, ATM cards, SIM cards, cheque books or internet banking credentials in exchange for commission or easy money.
