- Lucknow Cyber Fraud Tops 76 Crore; Cyber Commandos Deployed
Cyber fraud in Lucknow increased 5 times in 4 years:Fraud of over 76 crores in a year; Cyber commandos deployed, working like SPG-NSG
- Copy link
Cases of cyber fraud in Lucknow have increased 5-fold in 4 years. In 2022, only 42 cases were registered at the cyber police station, which increased to 215 in 2025.
In 2025, cyber fraudsters cheated people of Rs 76 crore 50 lakh. However, the police have also managed to recover Rs 54 crore 20 lakh through their actions.
Cyber commandos have been prepared to protect people from cyber fraud and help victims recover their money. Cyber commandos have been deployed in all three zones of the Lucknow Police Commissionerate. These cyber commandos are equipped with modern equipment and advanced cyber technology.
Training received from IIT experts
DCP Crime Kamlesh Dixit said that the 15-member cyber commando team has been trained by experts from prestigious institutions like IIT Kanpur, Raipur, Madras and National Defence University, along with cyber experts from Gujarat.
During the training, they were taught how to technically prevent cybercrime. How to trace cyber criminals when crimes occur, and how to recover fraudulent amounts. The team has also been provided with several modern tools for this purpose.
Cyber sector commandos will be like SPG-NSG
Kamlesh Dixit said that just as SPG and NSG commandos are proficient in their respective fields, cyber commandos are also fully capable in the cyber sector.
This trained team is providing training to police personnel at the zone level, so that cyber commandos can be deployed at every level in the coming time. This will make the investigation of cybercrime cases faster and more effective.
Kamlesh Dixit said that cyber fraud, online cyber attacks, social media crimes, data theft, phishing, digital blackmailing and digital arrests are increasing rapidly.
The role of expert cyber commandos is considered very important in these cases, who will take solid action with technical expertise.
Cyber commandos are police personnel who are fully trained to deal with cyber challenges. These are police personnel above the rank of head constable.
The government is very sensitive considering the large-scale cyber fraud, and for this reason, cyber commandos have been appointed and trained in the state.
Fraud in the name of investment has increased
DCP Kamlesh Dixit said that new methods of cyber fraud are emerging at the current time. Fraudsters extort money by scaring, threatening or luring people.
Especially, fraud in the name of investment has increased rapidly. In which fraudsters pose as girls on social media and lure people with promises of high profits.
To deal with these challenges, the state government and Uttar Pradesh Police are appointing and continuously training cyber commandos, so that strict action can be ensured against cyber criminals.
Now read some major fraud cases that happened in 2025...
Vikasnagar resident Anil Kumar Dwivedi said that on June 5, 2025, he received a message on Telegram from ID JUCVJRG. It talked about earning huge profits through crypto trading. After the conversation, the victim was added to a Telegram group called Google eecutes917 group.
People in the group started instigating to increase investment. Falling for their words, he deposited a total of 60 lakh rupees through the links provided by the fraudsters, which were not returned. He complained about this to the police.
80-year-old elderly person cheated by showing fear of money laundering
Singar Nagar Alambagh resident 80-year-old Om Prakash Narayan Mishra is a retired Deputy General Manager (DGM) of UPPCL. He said that on November 11, around 12 noon, he received a WhatsApp audio call from an unknown number.
The caller identified himself as a CBI officer. He said - A bank account has been opened with your Aadhar number, in which money laundering has taken place.
Within a short time, a copy of the arrest warrant was also sent to their WhatsApp. Upon seeing the warrant copy, they became worried. Meanwhile, the caller pressured them to join a video call.
On the video call, the fraudster wearing a uniform said the case would be heard in the Mumbai High Court. After this, they were cheated out of 47 lakh rupees in two days in the name of an investigation.
Cheated of 19.83 lakhs in the name of opening a petrol pump
Sector-K Aliganj resident Ambika Pratap Singh said that he registered on the website jiobpromlbpdealership.in, found on Google on October 26. After this, on November 4, he received an email from jiobp@rbmlbpdealership.in on his email ID to confirm the dealership.
In which some documents were requested. After submitting the documents, they asked for money in the name of a processing fee.
The victim first sent five thousand rupees online. After that, 19.83 lakh rupees were deposited in Axis Bank account through RTGS. Contact with the fraudsters continued through WhatsApp and calls.
A few days later when he inquired from the bank, he found out that the given account belongs to Jalaun district. After this, when he called the fraudster's mobile number, the phone was switched off.
Kept under digital arrest for 6 days claiming Pakistan connection
Janakipuram resident Gaya Prasad is a retired teacher. He said that he received a call on his mobile on November 7. The caller identified himself as Inspector Ranjit Kumar from ATS Department, Lucknow Police Headquarters.
The caller said that your number was found in a money laundering case connected to Pakistan. Mentioning the name of Afzal Khan from Delhi, he informed that your number is with Pakistani handlers and your name is included in case number JK19282015.
The caller threatened that you could be immediately arrested and sent to jail, but I can save you. For this, an NOC needs to be obtained. After that, he asked the victim to provide information about all bank accounts and transfer money to different accounts and UPI IDs.
He also instructed not to tell anything to family members and the bank. Under pressure from the fraudsters, the victim transferred a total of 12 lakh 57 thousand rupees from three different accounts.",1021,,True,False
