3698.0,ED uncovers shell firms in Goa ‘digital arrest’ scam funds transfer - The News Mill,The News Mill,"Tue, 25 Aug 2026 13:47:14 GMT",https://thenewsmill.com/2026/08/ed-uncovers-shell-firms-in-goa-digital-arrest-scam-funds-transfer/,success_decoder,success,ED uncovers shell firms in Goa ‘digital arrest’ scam funds trail,TNM,2026-08-25,"The Enforcement Directorate has uncovered a network of shell companies registered in the names of low-income individuals, including employees, drivers and tenants, in its money laundering investigation linked to a Goa resident defrauded in a 2025 “digital arrest” scam.
The probe centres on the routing of illicit proceeds through firms directed by people of modest means, serving as fronts to launder the money. On August 23, the ED’s Panaji Zonal Office arrested Fahim Moin Hussain Sayed and Naim Mueen Sayyed under Section 19 of the Prevention of Money Laundering Act (PMLA), 2002. They were remanded in ED custody for five days by a special PMLA court in Goa on August 24.
The case originated from a First Information Report filed by the Cyber Crime Police Station in North Goa after a resident was deceived into believing she was under investigation. She was continuously monitored via video call and coerced into transferring Rs 2.60 crore between May 21 and June 2, 2025, into accounts misrepresented as “Secret Supervision Accounts.”
Investigations revealed the funds were swiftly moved through a complex organised system designed to convert cyber-fraud proceeds first from banking credits into cash, then into foreign currency via companies licensed as Full Fledged Money Changers by the Reserve Bank of India.
Within hours, the victim’s money was segmented across more than 400 beneficiary accounts using various methods such as transfers, cash withdrawals, self-cheques and payment gateways. Further inquiry linked the transactions to a group of commodity, trading, travel and foreign exchange businesses.
These entities collectively conducted banking transactions exceeding Rs 27,850 crore and deposited roughly Rs 2,904 crore in cash. Notably, Rs 584.70 crore was deposited in 61,448 transactions through Bulk Note Acceptance Machines at multiple locations, an unusual pattern inconsistent with standard business operations.
The ED stated the involved bank accounts are connected to 330 victim complaints and 163 FIRs across 20 states and union territories, reporting a combined loss of Rs 417.49 crore. In 101 complaints, funds from a single victim were routed to multiple entities within the group during the same fraud, indicating these accounts functioned as a common pool rather than separate businesses.
The investigation confirmed the companies used for laundering were incorporated by economically disadvantaged individuals, listed as directors while operational control rested with others. Searches under Section 17 of the PMLA were conducted at 20 locations in Mumbai and Goa on July 17 and additional premises on August 21, resulting in the seizure of Rs 3.25 crore in cash and freezing of syndicate accounts holding over Rs 30 crore. Digital devices, financial records and statutory registers are under expert examination.
The ED warned the public that no Indian investigative or law enforcement agency imposes “digital arrest,” conducts investigations over video calls, or mandates money transfers for “verification” or “supervision.” It advised anyone receiving such calls to disconnect and report immediately to the national cybercrime helpline 1930 or via www.cybercrime.gov.in.",483,,True,False
