3716.0,Gujarat Police Bust Pan-India Cyber Fraud Network Operating from Ahmedabad - GujaratSamachar English,GujaratSamachar English,"Fri, 17 Jul 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://english.gujaratsamachar.com/news/gujarat/gujarat-police-bust-pan-india-cyber-fraud-network-operating-from-ahmedabad-41842427910,success_decoder,success,Gujarat Police Bust Pan-India Cyber Fraud Network Operating…,Siddhi Dahanukar,2026-07-18,"Gujarat Police Bust Pan-India Cyber Fraud Network Operating from Ahmedabad
Summarized by AI; it may make mistakes. Check important info
The Gujarat Cyber Centre of Excellence (CCoE) has dismantled a massive financial scam network running out of the city, arresting three local men who allegedly helped launder nearly £290,000 (₹2.9 crore) stolen from victims across 18 states.
The suspects—Nikunj Patel, Dikshit Patel, and Sumit Soni—were nicked by investigators following raids on their offices. Far from operating in secret hideouts, the trio ran their network right out of busy commercial premises on Ashram Road and near CTM.
The Fake Corporate Front
What sets this lot apart from typical cyber criminals is how professional they made the operation look. Instead of using individual, personal bank accounts, the trio set up two completely fake companies. They used these sham businesses to open high-limit corporate bank accounts. By using business accounts, they gained access to ""bulk mode"" transaction features, allowing them to rapidly move massive sums of stolen money into different accounts before banks or victims could clock the transactions and freeze the funds.
In total, police found that the group had set up 72 of these ""mule accounts."" While 22 accounts were already being used to funnel the cash, detectives managed to seize the remaining 50 banking kits before they could be sorted and activated.
Over 50 Victims Across the Country
The true scale of the racket came to light when investigators checked the group's bank details against the National Cyber Crime Reporting Portal. The trio's accounts were directly linked to 54 different fraud cases across India, including three victims right here in Gujarat.
Some of the biggest hits funneled through their network include:
• A ₹93 lakh online banking fraud targeting a victim in Bihar.
• A ₹70 lakh fake investment scam in Karnataka.
• A ₹38 lakh ""digital arrest"" scam in Maharashtra, where fraudsters posed as police officers to extort a resident.
How the Racket Worked
According to the CCoE, the three men had very specific roles. Nikunj Patel was the paperwork chap, handling the registration of the fake companies and opening the bank accounts. Dikshit Patel managed the actual bank kits and logistics, while Sumit Soni acted as the broker, passing the ready-to-use bank accounts along to higher-level fraudsters in exchange for a hefty cut of the spoils.
To keep the money moving smoothly without getting blocked, the group used a sneaky piece of software called DOTP.APK. Once installed on a target's phone, it allowed the scammers to secretly read and bypass OTP text messages, letting them transfer funds without the account holder ever knowing. They also used merchant payment gateways to scrub the money trail, making it incredibly difficult for police to track where the cash actually ended up.
During the raids, officers seized a laptop, six mobile phones, eight chequebooks, and 31 debit and credit cards. While Nikunj, Dikshit, and Sumit are now behind bars, Gujarat police say they are still hunting for two more suspects linked to the ring.",502,,True,False
3717.0,"₹200 cr cyber fraud network exposed in Gujarat, over 500 fa… - GujaratSamachar English",GujaratSamachar English,"Mon, 29 Jun 2026 21:31:43 GMT",https://english.gujaratsamachar.com/news/gujarat/rs-200-cr-cyber-fraud-network-exposed-in-gujarat-over-500-fake-firms-accounts-busted-in-2-years-23077926614,success_decoder,success,"₹200 cr cyber fraud network exposed in Gujarat, over 500 fa…",Siddhi Dahanukar,2026-04-09,"₹200 cr cyber fraud network exposed in Gujarat, over 500 fake firms’ accounts busted in 2 years
Summarized by AI; it may make mistakes. Check important info
A large network of shell companies used to launder cybercrime proceeds has been uncovered in Gujarat, with investigators tracing fraud worth around ₹200 crore over the past two years, officials said. The operation involved creating fake firms and routing illicit funds through multiple bank accounts before moving the money overseas.
Over 500 fake company accounts detected
Cybercrime police said they have identified bank accounts linked to more than 500 such fake companies in the past two years. These accounts were allegedly used to transfer large sums abroad, often converting the money into cryptocurrency to obscure the trail and enable international laundering.
Investigators described the network as an organised, multi-layered operation spanning Gujarat and other parts of the country.
Shell firms used to route illicit funds
According to officials, cyber fraud syndicates set up shell companies with the help of accomplices to facilitate the movement of illegal funds. While companies are required to register on the Ministry of Corporate Affairs (MCA) portal, fraudsters allegedly use forged bank statements to show shareholding and complete registration formalities.
The proceeds of cyber fraud are then routed through a chain of accounts before being sent abroad and converted into cryptocurrency, making recovery and tracing difficult.
Police step up crackdown across cities
Law enforcement agencies have intensified action to curb such operations. The Ahmedabad Crime Branch has identified around 200 suspicious shell companies as part of its ongoing investigations.
In a separate case, Surat police recently uncovered 44 shell companies and mule accounts linked to a fraud estimated at ₹2,000 crore, highlighting the scale and spread of such networks.
Mule accounts and testing patterns
Officials said the syndicates rely heavily on mule bank accounts, often procured or rented through online platforms, including Telegram channels. These accounts are used to layer transactions and disguise the origin of funds.
Typically, smaller amounts ranging from ₹2 lakh to ₹5 lakh are first transferred to test the system before larger sums are routed through the fake companies. In many cases, the companies are registered in the names of economically vulnerable individuals who are unaware of the scale or nature of the operations.
Masterminds remain elusive
While Gujarat cyber police have identified and acted against several individuals involved in setting up shell companies, tracing the key operators behind these networks remains a challenge. Officials said the cross-border nature of transactions and the use of cryptocurrency make it difficult to identify the masterminds or the final recipients of the funds.
Investigations are ongoing, with agencies focusing on dismantling the broader network and tightening checks on company registrations and financial transactions.",455,,True,False
3720.0,"Cyber Fraud Hub Worth ₹20 Crores Run from Lodge Room, 3 Arrested with Equipments - The420.in",The420.in,"Sat, 15 Aug 2026 07:58:25 GMT",https://the420.in/vasai-cyber-fraud-hub-lodge-room-three-arrested/,success_decoder,success,"Cyber Fraud Hub Worth ₹20 Crores Run from Lodge Room, 3 Arrested with Equipments",The,2026-08-15,"Mumbai: A lodge room in Vasai East, Maharashtra, was allegedly being used as an operating base for a cyber fraud network. Police have arrested three Gujarat-based men following a raid and alleged that the gang targeted victims across nine states, causing losses of more than ₹20 crore. Officers recovered 20 mobile phones, five laptops and fake Aadhaar and PAN cards from the accused. The investigation has also found that around ₹1.5 crore of the suspected fraud proceeds was deposited into two bank accounts linked to the arrested men.
According to police, the activities of the three men staying at the lodge had raised suspicion among an employee. The trio had allegedly been staying there for about a month. They reportedly remained inside their room during the day and made frequent phone calls, while spending their nights inside the room drinking alcohol. The employee eventually alerted the Waliv police after becoming suspicious of their activities.
Acting on the information, Waliv police raided the lodge room on Wednesday and found all three accused inside. During questioning, the police allegedly learned that the room was being used to operate cyber fraud activities. A search of the premises led to the recovery of several electronic devices and fake identity documents. The three men were subsequently arrested and the investigation was expanded to identify other members of the alleged network.
The arrested accused have been identified as Sheikh Aftab Ahmed, 28, Sheikh Mohammad Saif, 21, and Shahrukh Pathan, 30, all residents of Vadodara, Gujarat. Police said Aftab works as a driver, while Pathan is a delivery agent. The three have allegedly been involved in sextortion, digital arrest scams and other forms of cyber fraud.
Investigators found that the gang allegedly targeted people in different states using multiple methods. According to police, the accused sent malicious APK files to potential victims and used digital arrest calls to intimidate them. Victims were allegedly threatened with legal action and other consequences and pressured into transferring money.
Police also found links between the accused and alleged mule bank account holders operating in different parts of the country. These accounts were reportedly used to receive and move lakhs of rupees every day. Investigators are now attempting to identify the actual operators of these accounts and determine whether they knowingly participated in the alleged fraud or had allowed their accounts to be misused.
Two bank accounts linked to the arrested men have emerged as a key part of the investigation. Police said around ₹1.5 crore in suspected proceeds was deposited into these accounts. As many as 25 complaints have reportedly been registered at different locations in connection with the accounts. Investigators suspect that the network was therefore not limited to Vasai or Gujarat but had links across multiple states.
Police are examining the complete transaction trail of the bank accounts, mobile numbers, electronic devices and communication records associated with the accused. The five laptops and 20 mobile phones recovered during the raid are expected to provide crucial digital evidence about the alleged operation, including details of potential victims, payment records, contacts and other members of the network.
The recovery of fake Aadhaar and PAN cards has also become an important part of the investigation. Police are examining whether the documents were allegedly used to open bank accounts, obtain mobile connections or access other financial and digital services. Investigators are also trying to establish how the identities of different individuals were allegedly used in the operation.
Police have registered a case against the three accused under relevant provisions of the Information Technology Act. Investigators are now searching for four other alleged members of the gang. At the same time, efforts are underway to identify additional mule account holders and victims who may have been linked to the network.
The immediate focus of the investigation is to determine the complete structure of the alleged cyber fraud operation and establish the actual amount involved. Investigators are tracing digital devices, bank transactions and communication networks to determine the role played by each accused. The probe will also seek to establish how the suspected proceeds were collected, transferred through different accounts and moved across the network. The findings could help investigators identify the wider network allegedly operating across nine states.",704,,True,False
3724.0,Haryana Cyber Policing Campaign Secures Record 31% Fraud Fund Recovery Rate - The420.in,The420.in,"Tue, 07 Jul 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://the420.in/haryana-cyber-policing-fraud-fund-restoration-rate-success/,success_decoder,success,Haryana Cyber Policing Campaign Secures Record 31% Fraud Fund Recovery Rate,The,2026-07-06,"Haryana has emerged as a national leader in digital law enforcement, recording an unprecedented 31 percent recovery rate in restoring funds stolen through online scams during the first six months of 2026. This remarkable milestone highlights the state’s aggressive shift toward technology-driven policing strategies designed to counter the rising tide of sophisticated virtual crimes. By deploying localized software solutions and streamlining administrative coordination, Haryana’s specialized cyber units have successfully dismantled the procedural bottlenecks that traditionally allow cybercriminals to siphon off frozen funds indefinitely.
The state’s breakthrough performance was officially reviewed during a high-level meeting of the PRAGATI cyber policing action plan, presided over by Chief Secretary Anurag Rastogi. During the administrative session, officials revealed that Haryana’s fund recovery rate has skyrocketed to more than eight times the national baseline of 3.85 percent. This massive gap underscores the operational effectiveness of Haryana’s centralized monitoring networks, which prioritize rapid financial freezing and swift administrative relief for defrauded citizens.
A Record Setting Surge in Recovering Fraudulent Wealth
The backbone of Haryana’s financial recovery success is the innovative Money Restoration Module, a dedicated government portal built to bridge the gap between cyber victims, law enforcement, and banking institutions. Between January and June of 2026, the state successfully secured 2,241 formal restoration orders across 7,316 active cyber fraud cases handled through the portal. This automated process ensures that once stolen capital is intercepted and frozen within the banking ecosystem, it can be systematically returned to the rightful account holders without dragging families through exhausting, multi-year court battles.
According to senior administrative officials, maintaining this high rate of return requires relentless oversight from the State Cyber Crime Coordination Centre based in Panchkula. The coordination center acts as a round-the-clock command unit, managing real-time follow-ups with affected victims and accelerating the legal processing of indemnity bonds required to release locked funds. Chief Secretary Rastogi commended these fast-tracked workflows but urged departments to expand their efforts by embedding advanced artificial intelligence applications into state-level predictive policing and data analytics pipelines.
Automated Actions and Digital Takedowns Through Unified Portals
To permanently alter its enforcement landscape, Haryana officially rolled out the automated e-Zero FIR system. This digital infrastructure completely changes how serious financial scams are processed by state police forces. Under this newly established framework, any cyber fraud complaint involving financial damages of ₹1 lakh or more received via the national 1930 helpline is automatically converted into an official Zero FIR. This systemic automation bypasses traditional jurisdictional disputes, allowing specialized investigating officers to instantly preserve digital footprints, track illicit wire transfers, and identify fraudulent bank accounts before evidence is erased.
Simultaneously, the state has weaponized its localized Sahyog Portal to execute sweeping preemptive strikes against malicious web infrastructure. Within the same six-month window, digital enforcement teams successfully identified and removed 14,139 items of unlawful online content. This large-scale digital cleanup resulted in the immediate elimination of thousands of malicious phishing websites, deceptive advertisements, and fraudulent social media profiles engineered to target unsuspecting consumers. Furthermore, Haryana has prioritized interstate operational harmony, addressing more than 97 percent of incoming security requests routed through the federal Samanvaya Portal to ensure cross-border syndicates find no safe havens.
Dismantling Organized Syndicates Across Regional Hotspots
Beyond digital asset seizures, field-level enforcement operations have delivered devastating blows to organized cyber syndicates on the ground. Law enforcement operations heavily targeted notorious regional cybercrime hubs, most notably across the sensitive Nuh district. A sustained law enforcement campaign spanning from January 2025 through June 2026 yielded the registration of 473 separate proactive FIRs and the direct arrest of 927 active cybercriminals within the hotspot alone. During these targeted raids, tactical teams confiscated 751 mobile devices and 1,442 illegally obtained SIM cards used to run industrial-scale phishing operations.
This local crackdown forms part of a massive statewide sweep that resulted in the arrest of 3,947 cybercriminals during the first half of 2026. To support this high-velocity operational push, Haryana has invested heavily in institutional capacity building, successfully training more than 9,100 police personnel in advanced digital forensics and cyber investigation methodologies since early 2025. By combining advanced tech infrastructure with localized public awareness campaigns that reached over three lakh citizens, Haryana is actively pioneering a resilient national model for digital public safety.",701,,True,False
3725.0,Two Arrested From Haryana In Digital Arrest Scam - The Times of India,The Times of India,"Sun, 26 Jul 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/city/kolkata/two-arrested-from-haryana-in-digital-arrest-scam/articleshow/132632578.cms,success_decoder,success,Two Arrested From Haryana In Digital Arrest Scam | Kolkata News - The Times of India,Tamaghna Banerjee,2026-07-26,"Kolkata: Two men from 
