Dewas: Police in Madhya Pradesh's Dewas have busted an interstate cyber fraud network and arrested 12 people following an investigation into a 'digital arrest' scam in which a retired factory employee was allegedly duped of around Rs 63 lakh.
The accused allegedly posed as officials from the Department of Telecommunications, Mumbai Crime Branch and the Central Bureau of Investigation (CBI), threatening the victim with arrest in a money laundering case. Police said the suspects used the threat of 'digital arrest' to intimidate the victim and extract money from him.
The government's NITI Aayog defines 'digital arrest' as a scam designed to extort money from victims using fear, deceit, and intimidation. Fraudsters impersonate law enforcement officials, using threats of arrest, frozen bank accounts, and passport cancellations to coerce victims into paying a ""fine"" or ""security deposit"" to avoid legal action.
In one such case, retired employee Suresh Ingle approached the Madhya Pradesh cybercrime helpline 1930 after allegedly losing around Rs 63 lakh to the fraudsters.
In his complaint, Ingle said that in March 2026, he received phone calls and video calls from people claiming to be officials of the Department of Telecommunications, Mumbai Crime Branch and the CBI. The callers allegedly threatened to implicate him in a money laundering case and arrest him.
The accused kept him under fear for nearly eight days and obtained details of his bank accounts and investments. They then allegedly directed him to transfer money into different bank accounts under the pretext of an investigation.
The victim filed a complaint on April 3, following which police registered an FIR and began investigating the case from April 4. Dewas Police investigated for 127 days, involving 70 personnel divided into 19 teams.
Superintendent of Police Puneet Gehlot said the investigation found some leads from several bank accounts, mobile numbers and financial transactions linked to the accused. Technical surveillance helped police trace the interstate network.
The police teams travelled approximately 58,315 kilometres and conducted raids across 33 districts in 17 states before arresting 12 alleged cyber fraudsters. The operation covered states including Rajasthan, Gujarat, Maharashtra, Karnataka, Delhi and Bihar.
Police said 11 of the arrested accused have been sent to jail, while a resident of West Bengal is being questioned. Cash amounting to Rs 54.84 lakh, allegedly linked to the fraud, has also been recovered.
""There is no such thing as a digital arrest,"" Gehlot said, advising people to immediately report such incidents to their nearest police station or the cybercrime helpline 1930.
Police also said that people should not share their bank account details, investment information, OTPs or transfer money to accounts at the direction of unknown callers.
