Talking about the modus operandi, police said the cyber criminals first gathered comprehensive information and profile photos of the company's chairman/director. They then set one of these photos as the display picture (DP) on a new WhatsApp number and messaged the company's accountant. Upon seeing his boss's name and DP on WhatsApp, the employee transferred Rs 5.3 crore to the provided bank accounts without any suspicion.
After registering the complaint, a special team was constituted at the State Cyber Crime Police Station, Jaipur, on the directions of Deputy Inspector General of Police Shantanu Kumar Singh and under the supervision of Superintendent of Police Sumit Mehrada.
The team conducted a technical analysis of the bank accounts, mobile numbers, and digital transactions used in the fraud.
Following the investigation, the accused, Rahul Ashok Sopan (32)—a resident of Pune—was identified.
With the assistance of the Maharashtra Police, the Rajasthan Police nabbed the accused from Pune, obtained a transit remand from a local court, and brought him to Jaipur.
During interrogation, the accused confessed to facilitating the transactions of illegal money and providing fake bank accounts to cybercriminals in exchange for hefty commissions.
The accused, a daily-wage labourer, also registered a fake firm using forged documents with the help of Amit Singh.
The credit limit of the current bank account associated with this fake firm had been raised to Rs 50 crore through fraud.
The accused used this specific account to commit fraud amounting to Rs 5.3 crore. He had helped open three other bank accounts for the gang.
The police are now investigating the potential involvement of the bank employees who opened these fraudulent accounts.
The police are currently scrutinizing digital evidence related to other bank accounts and mobile numbers used in the case to nab other key figures of this gang, including Amit Singh.
The investigation team is led by Gajendra Sharma—Station House Officer (SHO) and Deputy Superintendent of Police at the State Cyber Crime Police Station, Jaipur and also comprising inspector Mukesh and constables Amit Kumar and Jaysingh.
