3850.0,"36,000 Indian SIM cards active in Cambodia: ED says 5,300 linked to cyber fraud; Malaysian couriers under s... - Bhaskar English",Bhaskar English,"Tue, 09 Jun 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://www.bhaskarenglish.in/national/news/ed-raids-cyber-fraud-network-cambodia-rajasthan-punjab-138154634.html,success_decoder,success,"36,000 Indian SIM cards active in Cambodia: ED says 5,300 linked to cyber fraud; Malaysian couriers under scanner",,2026-06-10,"- English News
- National
- ED Raids Cyber Fraud Network | Cambodia SIMs Linked To Scams
36,000 Indian SIM cards active in Cambodia:ED says 5,300 linked to cyber fraud; Malaysian couriers under scanner
- Copy link
Enforcement Directorate (ED) has carried out a major operation against an international cyber fraud network allegedly run from Cambodia, conducting large-scale raids in Rajasthan and Punjab. Investigators found that thousands of Indian SIM cards were fraudulently activated and supplied to foreign networks, where they were used in large-scale cyber scams targeting people in India.
During the probe, the agency analysed around 2,30,000 suspicious mobile numbers. It found that nearly 36,000 Indian SIM cards were active in Cambodia. Of these, about 5,300 SIM cards were directly linked to cyber fraud cases in India. Investigators said these numbers were used to facilitate scams worth hundreds of crores of rupees across the country.
The ED launched the investigation under the Prevention of Money Laundering Act (PMLA) based on an FIR registered at the Jodhpur Cyber Police Station. As part of the operation that began on June 5, searches were conducted at seven locations in Kishangarh, Nagaur and Jodhpur in Rajasthan, as well as Ludhiana in Punjab. During the raids, officials seized a large number of documents, digital evidence, electronic devices and 14 mobile phones.
SIM cards routed through Malaysian nationals
According to the ED, the network operated in a highly organised manner. SIM vendors allegedly used telecom companies’ Point of Sale (POS) IDs to lure people with offers of new SIM cards or mobile number porting. Using documents obtained from less educated and less aware individuals, additional SIM cards were activated in their names without their knowledge. These SIM cards were later sent to Cambodia through Malaysian nationals.
How the racket operated
- People were approached with offers of free SIM porting services.
- Additional SIM cards were activated using their documents.
- The fraudulently activated SIM cards were passed on to Malaysian nationals.
- The SIM cards were then transported to Cambodia.
- Cyber criminals based in Cambodia used these Indian mobile numbers to carry out scams, including “digital arrest” frauds and other forms of online cheating.",362,,True,False
