Youth sent abroad for cyber fraud, passports and IDs seized:Accused arrested before fleeing India, linked to scam networks in Laos, Cambodia, Vietnam, Myanmar
- Copy link
Five members of a gang accused of luring Indian youths abroad with promises of lucrative jobs and forcing them into cyber fraud have been arrested.
According to Rajasthan Police, the gang confiscated the victims' passports and identity documents after they reached foreign countries and forced them to work at cyber scam centres linked to networks in Laos, Cambodia, Vietnam and Myanmar. Investigators say the syndicate effectively turned Indians into cyber slaves.
The international cyber slavery and human trafficking racket was busted by Rajasthan Police on Monday night. Police arrested Rajkaran alias Ronnie and Sagar Singh from Beawar, while Jeeturaj Phulwari alias James, Ravi Kumar alias Mahi and Anil Kumar alias Devil were arrested in Kolkata.
According to investigators, Jeeturaj had supplied the passports and personal documents of 10 Indian youths to Chinese handlers operating scam compounds.
More than 500 Rajasthan residents identified
ADG (Cyber Crime) Vijay Kumar Singh said data analysis by the Indian Cyber Crime Coordination Centre (I4C) identified more than 500 people from Rajasthan who had been sent to Cambodia, Vietnam, Myanmar and Laos after being promised overseas jobs.
Many of them have still not returned to India. Victims who managed to return told investigators they were assaulted, mentally tortured and forced to participate in cyber fraud operations.
Multi-crore fraud through fake social media profiles
ADG Vijay Kumar Singh said raids in Beawar led to the arrest of Rajkaran alias Ronnie (ROXX) and Sagar Singh.
Police recovered mobile phones, passports and digital evidence that allegedly contained crucial information about foreign handlers, VPN usage, fake GPS tools and international cyber networks.
Based on their interrogation, Jeeturaj alias James, Ravi Kumar alias Mahi and Anil Kumar alias Devil were arrested in Kolkata. Police said all three were preparing to travel to Myanmar on tourist visas.
During questioning, the accused allegedly revealed that recruits first received training at cyber scam compounds before being instructed to create fake social media profiles to befriend Indian citizens.
Victims were then persuaded to invest large sums in fake cryptocurrency and investment schemes. The money was routed through mule bank accounts, converted into cryptocurrency and transferred to handlers based in China and Cambodia.
Passports seized, victims held captive
Investigators said the gang lured young Indians with promises of monthly salaries of 800 to 1,000 US dollars before sending them to Cambodia, Vietnam, Myanmar and Laos. Once they arrived, their passports and identity cards were confiscated.
Victims were allegedly forced to carry out cyber crimes, including digital arrest scams, fake investment fraud and cryptocurrency scams, while working from 9 am to 11.30 pm every day.
Anyone attempting to leave was allegedly threatened with heavy financial penalties worth thousands of dollars and subjected to physical violence.
Role of the five accused
1. Rajkaran alias Ronnie (ROXX)
Police said Rajkaran worked as an HR coordinator at a scam compound in Poipet, Cambodia, where numerous Indian nationals were allegedly forced to conduct online fraud from 9 am to 11.30 pm daily.
After returning to India four months ago, he allegedly recruited young people from Beawar by offering salaries of 800 US dollars per month, receiving substantial commissions for each recruit sent abroad.
2. Sagar Singh
Investigators said Sagar created fake Facebook profiles posing as Indian women while operating from Poipet.
After building trust over five to seven days of conversation, he allegedly collected confidential information about victims, including family details, bank accounts, businesses and financial status.
The information was then passed to team leaders, who persuaded victims to invest in fake cryptocurrency schemes and transferred the money into mule accounts.
3. Jeeturaj Phulwari alias James
Police said a Binance cryptocurrency account was operated in Jeeturaj's name. He allegedly transferred funds to network members in Cambodia using USDT (Tether) cryptocurrency.
Investigators also accused him of supplying fake mule bank accounts, Indian SIM cards, and the passports and personal documents of around 10 Indian youths to Chinese handlers so they could be recruited into scam compounds in Cambodia.
4. Ravi Kumar alias Mahi
According to police, Ravi had been operating from scam compounds in Cambodia since 2023. He allegedly trained recruits to target Indian citizens through fake Facebook profiles.
After spending an extended period working in cyber fraud operations in Cambodia, he was preparing to shift to a new scam centre in Myanmar after establishing contact with Chinese handlers.
5. Anil Kumar alias Devil
Anil, the brother of Jeeturaj alias James, travelled to Cambodia in 2024, police said.
He allegedly spent around a year working in a cyber scam compound and was due to be sent to another scam centre in Yanchia Township, Myanmar, under the cover of working as a construction supervisor for a Chinese company.
Police arrested him in Kolkata shortly before he was due to board a flight to Myanmar.
Investigation continues
Police have seized passports, foreign visa documents, mobile phones, digital evidence and other records from the accused. Investigators are now working to identify the wider international network and trace other members of the gang.",873,,True,False
