3967.0,"Pune nurse falls victim to deepfake Ratan Tata investment scam, loses Rs 4 lakh - India Today",India Today,"Mon, 10 Aug 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://www.indiatoday.in/cities/pune/story/deepfake-ratan-tata-video-scam-pune-nurse-loses-rs-4-lakh-in-fake-investment-scheme-2967713-2026-08-10,success_decoder,success,"Pune nurse falls victim to deepfake Ratan Tata investment scam, loses Rs 4 lakh",Omkar Wable,2026-08-10,"Pune nurse falls victim to deepfake Ratan Tata investment scam, loses Rs 4 lakh
A Pune nurse was allegedly lured into a fake investment scheme through a deepfake video of Ratan Tata. The case has prompted a cyber police probe and fresh caution over social media frauds.
A 38-year-old nurse from Maharashtra's Pune was allegedly duped of over Rs 4 lakh after cyber fraudsters used a deepfake video of late industrialist Ratan Tata to lure her into a fake online investment scheme.
The Pune Cyber Police have registered an FIR against unidentified persons and launched an investigation into the alleged fraud.
According to the police, the woman, a resident of Ambegaon, came across a video on Instagram in October 2024 that purportedly featured Ratan Tata speaking about lucrative investment opportunities in the stock market.
Investigators suspect the video was created using deepfake technology to impersonate the late industrialist and gain the trust of potential victims.
Believing the video to be genuine, the woman clicked on an advertisement linked to it and submitted her personal details.
She was subsequently contacted by the alleged fraudsters, who directed her to register on what appeared to be an online investment platform.
A TOTAL OF RS 4 LAKH TRANSFERRED
Police said that between October 9, 2024, and June 13, 2025, the woman transferred a total of Rs 4,09,100 through 11 online transactions to multiple bank accounts provided by the accused.
To make the scheme appear legitimate, the fraudsters allegedly gave her access to an online portal where her investments appeared to generate significant returns.
However, when she attempted to withdraw the money, she was allegedly asked to deposit additional funds to process the withdrawal.
The repeated demands for more payments raised the woman's suspicion, following which she realised she had been cheated.
She approached the Pune Cyber Police and lodged a complaint on March 4, 2026.
POLICE LAUNCH PROBE
After verifying the allegations and examining the available digital evidence, police registered an FIR on Saturday against unidentified persons.
The case has been registered under Sections 318(4) and 319(2) of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita (BNS), along with Section 66D of the Information Technology Act, which deals with cheating by personation using computer resources.
Police are now tracing the bank accounts, digital payment trails and online platforms allegedly used in the fraud to identify those involved.
Authorities have also urged people to verify investment opportunities through official sources and remain cautious of deepfake videos and unsolicited online financial schemes circulating on social media.",416,,True,False
