3971.0,Deepfakes in advertising: When fiction becomes fraud - ET BrandEquity,ET BrandEquity,"Wed, 16 Jul 2025 07:00:00 GMT",https://brandequity.economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/research/advertising/deepfakes-in-advertising-when-fiction-becomes-fraud/122551625,success_decoder,success,Deepfakes in advertising: When fiction becomes fraud,Www Etbrandequity Com; Manisha Kapoor,2025-07-16,"- Exclusive
- Advertising
- 4 min read
Deepfakes in advertising: When fiction becomes fraud
Highlights
- The Advertising Standards Council of India has identified a troubling trend of deepfake advertisements featuring prominent individuals endorsing products without their consent or knowledge.
- Deepfake technology, which creates hyper-realistic digital content, poses serious ethical and regulatory challenges in advertising, especially when misused to fabricate endorsements.
- The rise of deepfakes is attributed to low barriers for creation, algorithmic reach for amplification, and delayed detection, leading to potential reputational harm for impersonated individuals.
- The article emphasizes the importance of obtaining consent and maintaining transparency in advertising, urging marketers to use artificial intelligence ethically to enhance consumer trust.
This is the world of deepfakes in advertising.
Deepfakes refer to hyper-realistic content generated using artificial intelligence (AI), where faces, voices or personas of real individuals are digitally manipulated or recreated. Initially seen in entertainment and satire, deepfake technology has now entered advertising – with serious ethical and regulatory consequences when done with malintent.
The ads identified by ASCI through its proactive monitoring feature AI-generated avatars or manipulated videos of influential personalities, speaking directly to the camera in convincingly real voices, recommending investment apps, miracle health cures or money-making schemes. These are not parodies. These are fraudulent.
For example, there is one of a prominent news anchor interviewing a key Central minister. Both endorse a trading platform in this deepfake. The voices are close to their own and, unless close attention is paid, the ad would seem to be genuine. Similarly, there are deepfakes of one of India’s most prominent industrialists endorsing the platform. In another example, some of India’s most prominent film stars and cricketers seem to endorse an illegal betting website.
What makes these ads so dangerous is their ability to hijack credibility and trust. When a well-known business leader appears to speak favourably about a finance app, it lends both visibility and legitimacy, creating an illusion of trust. A consumer is far more likely to believe in the efficacy or safety of a product when someone respected and successful seems to be backing it. However, these endorsements are often entirely fabricated. No permissions are sought. No contracts signed. And yet, the fake endorsement is powerful enough to drive clicks, downloads, and sometimes financial loss or physical harm.
Besides being misleading, such ads may also breach laws relating to impersonation, defamation and cyber fraud.
Three major forces are driving the deepfakes trend:
- Low barriers to creation: Free or low-cost deepfake tools allow even small-scale operators to generate convincing impersonations.
- Algorithmic reach: Once created, these ads can be cheaply amplified across platforms, reaching unsuspecting users through sponsored posts, chat groups, and reels. Given the popular personalities, these ads could typically be shared, or liked.
- Delayed detection: Often, the impersonated individual only becomes aware of the ad after it has been viewed thousands of times.
Advertisers, agencies, and platforms would do well to follow these rules:
- Consent is non-negotiable: If you are using someone’s likeness — even an AI-generated version — written permission is required. The ASCI code explicitly requires this. There are good examples of this. For instance, Cadbury’s Diwali campaign featuring actor Shah Rukh Khan citing individual businesses. This was a positive example of personalised advertising created using modern technology and through ethical means. There are international examples of it too. Footballer David Beckham appeared in a campaign for Malaria No More in 2019, speaking in nine different languages — AI replicated his voice and synchronised his lip movements, turning him into a multilingual advocate.
- Platform responsibility: Social media and content-sharing platforms must invest in detection tools and act promptly on flagged content.
Incidentally, there are numerous examples of AI being used to create fully digital influencers that are then used in brand campaigns. When there is complete transparency about this and consumers are left in no doubt that it is a digital creation they are engaging with. This, too, is an ethical approach.
Deepfake technology, like all tools, can be used ethically to enhance storytelling or personalise experiences. There’s a clear line between innovation and deception. When advertising crosses into impersonation, the line between creativity and deceit is crossed. Marketers can use AI to create meaningful brand engagement without fraudulent shock value.
It’s clear by now that AI will be a crucial part of brand storytelling going forward. The question is not whether but how marketers use it.
As India embraces the digital economy and AI-driven innovation, the advertising ecosystem must rise to the occasion, and ensure that innovations in AI are used to enhance, rather than destroy, consumer trust.",773,,True,False
