3977.0,Kothrud Man Duped of â‚¹39 Lakh in Deepfake Aviator Scam - Pune Mirror,Pune Mirror,"Fri, 11 Apr 2025 07:00:00 GMT",https://punemirror.com/city/crime/kothrud-man-duped-of-inr39-lakh-in-deepfake-aviator-scam/,success_decoder,success,Kothrud Man Duped of â‚¹39 Lakh in Deepfake Aviator Scam - Pune Times Mirror,PuneMirror Bureau,2025-04-11,"In a shocking case of cyber fraud, a 30-year-old man from Kothrud has been duped of a staggering Rs.39,77,297 in an online gaming scam, where deepfake videos featuring cricket legends Mahendra Singh Dhoni and Virat Kohli, along with entrepreneur Anant Ambani, were used to lure him into playing the ‘Aviator’ game.
This is the first case of Aviator game scam being registered in Pune. Pratik Ghuge (30), a resident of Kothrud, approached the Cyber Police after he was duped on the pretext of handsome returns by playing the online game. The complainant works in a private company.  
The scam unfolds
A notification pinged on Pratik’s WhatsApp number about a gaming platform on November 28, 2024. He also received an online link along with the message. Pratik clicked on the link and registered himself by creating a password. Initially, he invested Rs 10,000 in the Aviator game but didn’t receive any returns, prompting him to invest another Rs. 10,000. The complainant invested Rs.5,86,000 between November 28 and December 4, 2024, he invested and received Rs 1 lakh as returns.  Encouraged by the returns, he again invested
Rs. 8,30,000 between November 5 to December 11, 2024, and received returns of Rs 2 lakh. He continued investing money with the greed of returns and between December 12 to 19, December, he again invested Rs. 12,83, 298. Surprisingly, he received Rs.10 lakh in his account as returns on his investment. This made him more confident and he again invested
Rs. 12,13,198 between December 20, 2024 to January 15, 2025. But he did not  get any returns.
Surprisingly, the victim made around 319 transactions using his bank account and credit card, before realising that he was being scammed. The man tried contacting the gaming platform via WhatsApp but couldn’t get through. Finally, he went to Cyber Police station and lodged a complaint.
The Cyber Police have lodged the FIR under section 318 (4) and 319(2) of Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita and relevant sections of Information Technology Act. Deputy Commissioner of Police Sambhaji Kadam and his team are investigating the case.
What is Deepfake?
Advocate Rajas Pingle, a prominent cyber lawyer, said
“Deepfakes are fake videos or audio recordings created using Artificial Intelligence (AI) that look and sound real but are entirely fabricated. These videos can make it appear as if someone is saying or doing something they never actually did. This technology is often used to mimic the faces and voices of real people, including celebrities and public figures, in a very convincing way. Cybercriminals are now using deepfakes to trick people by impersonating trusted individuals or celebrities.”
Elaborating the modus of cyber crooks, advocate Pingle said, “The fraudsters create deepfake videos using AI that show celebrities promoting an app or a money making opportunity. These videos are circulated on social media platforms like Instagram, YouTube, or WhatsApp. Once the victim believes the video is genuine, they are directed to download the Aviator app and start putting in money with the hope of multiplying their investment. Eventually, the scammers vanish with the funds, and the victim has no legal recourse, especially since platforms like Aviator are not legal in India.”
What is Aviator game?
Aviator is an online betting game where a plane takes off, and players have to cash out before it flies away. The longer the plane stays in the air, the higher the multiplier on the bet. But if you don’t cash out before the plane disappears, you lose all your money. It’s designed to be addictive and is often promoted as a quick way to earn money. However, this game is banned in India, and its use is associated with online gambling scams.",611,,True,False
3978.0,LexisNexis Expands Epic MyChart Security with Deepfake and Fraud Defense - Digital Health News,Digital Health News,"Mon, 09 Mar 2026 11:36:47 GMT",https://www.digitalhealthnews.com/lexisnexis-expands-epic-mychart-security-with-deepfake-and-fraud-defense,success_decoder,success,LexisNexis Expands Epic MyChart Security with Deepfake and Fraud Defense,Dr Aishwarya Sarthe,2026-03-09,"LexisNexis Expands Epic MyChart Security with Deepfake and Fraud Defense
The initiative aims to protect sensitive personal health information (PHI) while maintaining a smooth user experience for patients.
LexisNexis Risk Solutions is strengthening security for Epic MyChart users through a broader suite of AI-driven identity verification tools, the company announced. U.S. hospitals using Epic can now deploy these solutions natively via the Epic Connection Hub.
The initiative aims to protect sensitive personal health information (PHI) while maintaining a smooth user experience for patients. LexisNexis’ expanded portfolio offers modular access to solutions such as ThreatMetrix, an AI-powered behavioral and device risk platform; Flex ID; Phone Finder; and One Time Password (OTP) authenticators.
The integration allows hospital IT teams to select the components that best match their risk tolerance. Passive behavioral analytics from ThreatMetrix monitors user behavior, device patterns, and transaction activity. Routine logins from recognized devices and locations proceed without friction, while suspicious access attempts are flagged for further verification.
For higher-risk scenarios, active verification tools such as Instant Verify, Phone Finder, and OTP authentication confirm the user’s identity and device possession.
LexisNexis plans to integrate its AI-powered IDVerse solution within the Epic framework. IDVerse leverages biometric and document verification to detect deepfakes, forgeries, and synthetic identities, offering a NIST IAL2-certified workflow for patient authentication.
Jonathan Shannon, AVP of healthcare strategy at LexisNexis Risk Solutions, said, “In an industry that remains a top target for cybercrime, striking a balance between user experience and security is critical. This integration is another step forward in instilling confidence and trust in healthcare organizations and patients alike.”
The combined platform positions hospitals to proactively defend against emerging cyber threats, ensuring that only verified patients can access their MyChart accounts while minimizing login friction.
