By - Newsmeter Network |Hyderabad: Hyderabad Cyber Crime Unit has busted a large-scale network using deepfake videos of celebrities and IPL-based predictions to lure unsuspecting users into illegal betting and fake investment schemes.
Deepfakes and Cricket Craze Exploited
Investigations revealed that fraudsters capitalised on the popularity of the Indian Premier League (IPL) by circulating match predictions, betting tips, and “sure-win” strategies across social media platforms.
To gain credibility, scammers deployed deepfake videos of well-known personalities, falsely portraying them as endorsing betting apps and investment platforms.
These manipulated videos, combined with cricket fever, created a powerful trap for users hoping to make easy profits during the IPL season.
Hundreds of fake profiles, Ads busted
Cybercrime officials identified 184 social media profiles on Facebook and Instagram that were actively promoting such content. These accounts collectively ran 801 paid advertisements designed to attract users with promises of high returns, bonuses, and referral commissions.
Authorities said the ads were strategically targeted at vulnerable users, particularly youngsters and first-time investors, increasing the risk of financial loss and data theft.
Swift action: Profiles taken down, FIRs filed
Acting swiftly, police reported and facilitated the takedown of all identified profiles to prevent further spread of misleading content. Six FIRs have been registered against those involved in promoting these illegal activities.
Officials added that ongoing cyber patrol operations have so far led to the identification and removal of 427 profiles and 1,903 advertisements linked to online betting and fraudulent investment schemes.
Financial trails under scanner
The investigation is now focused on tracing the individuals behind these operations and tracking the financial channels used to siphon money. Police said strict legal action will be taken against all those found involved.
Public urged to stay vigilant
Cyber Crime Police have issued a strong advisory warning citizens against falling prey to such scams:
Do not trust social media ads promising guaranteed profits or easy money.
Avoid clicking on betting or investment links shared via unknown profiles.
Be cautious of videos featuring celebrities endorsing financial platforms—these may be deepfakes.
Online betting is illegal in Telangana and can lead to legal trouble and financial loss.
Parents should guide youngsters about the risks of online gambling and scams.
Report, don’t ignore
Citizens are encouraged to report suspicious online activities to cybercrime authorities, call the helpline 1930, or lodge complaints through the official portal.
The Hyderabad Cyber Crime Unit reiterated that as scammers adopt advanced tools like deepfakes, public awareness and caution remain the strongest defence against digital fraud.",439,,True,False
