Jerome grew suspicious when the caller started aggressively pushing him to invest more. ""Jerome demanded a refund of his investment. The fraudster refused to refund the money and instead insisted that Jerome invest more to recover his initial investment. In an attempt to gain trust, the fraudster sent a passport copy claiming to be an employee of the stock market firm and provided details about the company. However, Jerome remained unconvinced and persisted in demanding the return of his money. His suspicions were confirmed when the caller adamantly refused to refund his investment,"" explained a police officer.In June, a 54-year-old Ayurveda doctor from Andheri (West) also fell prey to a similar share trading scam and lost Rs 7.09 lakhs. The scam involved an Instagram reel featuring a video clip of Mukesh Ambani praising a company called ""Rajiv Sharma Trade Group"" and encouraging viewers to join its BCF Investment Academy for high returns. The Oshiwara police registered an FIR against an unknown person. Dr KKH Patil was duped between May 28 and June 10 when she transferred Rs 7.09 lakhs on 16 occasions to different bank accounts after being persuaded to invest for substantial returns.
