4064.0,Retired Police Officer Loses ₹19 Lakh In Digital Arrest Scam After Fake NIA Officials Threaten Terror Link - Free Press Journal,Free Press Journal,"Sun, 23 Aug 2026 14:03:46 GMT",https://www.freepressjournal.in/mumbai/retired-police-officer-loses-19-lakh-in-digital-arrest-scam-after-fake-nia-officials-threaten-terror-link-probe,success_decoder,success,Retired Police Officer Loses ₹19 Lakh In Digital Arrest Scam After Fake NIA Officials Threaten Terror Link Probe,Somendra Sharma,2026-08-23,"Mumbai: A 61-year-old retired police sub-inspector has fallen prey to a digital arrest scam and ended up losing his hard earned money. The scammers who claimed to be National Investigation Agency (NIA) officials told the victim that his links with a terrorist organisation had been found during a probe and induced the victim to transfer money in different beneficiary bank accounts.
Fake officials create fear through video calls
According to the police, the complainant is a resident of Chhatrapati Sambhaji Nagar and had retired from the police force in June 2023. On August 04, the victim received a phone call from a person claiming to be from the Data Protection Board of India (DPBI) office in Mumbai. The caller told the victim that a sim card had been issued in Mumbai on the victim's name which had been used for criminal activities.
After some time, the victim received a video call from a person claiming to be from NIA's Mumbai office and told the victim that his links with a terrorist organisation had surfaced during a money laundering probe. Later, another person who claimed to be a police officer told the victim and threatened to arrest him. Interestingly, the scammers told the victim that they were aware of him being a retired policeman.
