In a shocking incident in Haryana’s Panchkula, a retired professor from Himachal Pradesh fell victim to ‘digital arrest’, resulting in the loss of her entire life savings, amounting to Rs 1.06 crore.
Haryana Police have apprehended one suspect from Ahmedabad while the search for other accomplices continues at the Panchkula Cyber Police Station.
Inspector Satyendra Narwal of the Cyber Police Station said that the victim, Suneela Malik, a resident of Sector-4, Panchkula, filed a complaint on September 17.
Recently retired as a professor from a university in Shimla, Malik received a WhatsApp video call on September 7. The caller’s profile displayed a photo of a senior Mumbai Police officer.
During the call, the perpetrators impersonated Mumbai Police Sub-Inspector Hemraj and falsely informed Malik of her alleged connection to a money laundering case involving an individual named Patel.
They claimed to have discovered documents bearing her name at the suspect’s residence. To further intimidate her, they sent a picture of her debit card. The scammers sent a fabricated arrest warrant and instructed her not to disconnect the call until the investigation concluded.
Malik endured 52 hours of ‘digital arrest’ under duress. The perpetrators demanded a payment of Rs 90 lakh to secure her release. Fearing for her safety, she transferred the funds to the provided account on September 7.
However, the criminals continued their coercion, forcing her to deposit an additional Rs 16 lakh on September 9, bringing the total amount stolen to Rs 1.06 crore. Following this ordeal, Malik reported the incident to the police.
