			This is an archive article published on October 15, 2024
		
	
														
In what has been called as a major wrap on a case that allegedly involved digital arrests of people with a view to con them, the Ahmedabad Cyber Crime Police on Monday said it has arrested four Taiwan nationals among 17 accused. While two of them were nabbed from Delhi, the other two were arrested from Bengaluru, said Assistant Commissioner of Police (ACP) Hardik Makadiya.
The alleged racket involved an elaborate scheme that saw the accused impersonating government officials from various departments, including the Telecom Regulatory Authority of India (TRAI), the Mumbai Crime Branch of Maharashtra Police, the Central Bureau of Investigation (CBI) as well as the Reserve Bank of India (RBI). The elaborate ruse also saw false claims made in the name of a Mumbai court, misuse of the Aadhaar card and bank account.
The gang had “digitally arrested” a senior citizen for 10 days, kept watch on him via video calls and got him to deposit Rs 79.34 lakh as “refundable” processing fees to sort out an “RBI issue”, said Joint Commissioner (Crime) Sharad Singhal. As per police, the senior citizen had registered a complaint that some persons calling themselves officials of TRAI, CBI and cyber crime branch had alleged that his account was being used for illegal transactions.
“After receiving a complaint last month, our teams raided places in Gujarat, Delhi, Rajasthan, Karnataka, Odisha and Maharashtra and nabbed 17people, including four Taiwan natives, for running this nationwide racket. We believe they may have targeted some 1,000 persons so far,” Singhal told reporters.
In a simultaneous operation by the Cyber Crime Police team, Taiwanese nationals Mu Chi Sung alias Mark and Chang Hao Yun alias Marco, were arrested from Hotel Taj Palace in Delhi and two others — Wang Chun Wei (26) and Shen Wei (35) — from Bengaluru after boarding a flight from Taiwan to Delhi.
The amount stolen from the complainant had been deposited in separate accounts. Based on this, raids were conducted in Surat, Vadodara, Ahmedabad, and Limbdi and eight people were arrested from Gujarat, Rajasthan, Delhi and Odisha. These people used to allegedly provide bank accounts on rent to Taiwanese accused and get commission from proceeds of crime.
A statement from the Ahmedabad Cyber Crime Police on Monday stated, “This crime has seen a new modus operandi where rooted mobiles were used — 20 such mobiles were found connected to routers, passing OTP links of various bank accounts to Taiwanese Gmail IDs. A technical analysis revealed that more than 120 mobiles were connected to it and such setups were operational in Delhi, Bengaluru and Mumbai.” Rooting a device disables built-in security features, said officials.
Story continues below this ad
Based on this, raids were conducted at centres in Mumbai and Delhi and one of the prime accused, Saif Haider, was arrested. He was in direct contact with a Taiwanese man named Mark.
Mark is said to have provided all these rooted mobiles and routers. He used to bring the necessary equipment from Taiwan to install this setup. He has traveled to Cambodia, Dubai, China as well as several cities in India, including Bengaluru, Delhi, and Mumbai.
During a recent visit to India on October 10, Mark and his partner Marco were allegedly planning to scale up their business. They allegedly collected rental accounts from their Indian agents and used them to fund illegal betting websites and cyber scams. They were arrested during this trip.
Further, during the raid at Bengaluru, two more Taiwanese citizens Wang Chun Wei alias Sumoka (26) and Shen Wei Haw aka Krish (35) were operating the same setup as the technical equipment found earlier and absconding accused Mark’s assistant Simon alias Sevanna and accused Saif Haider were arrested, said officials.
“The mobile app being used by the gang was developed by the Taiwanese accused. They have also integrated online wallets in their system. Money received from victims was transferred to other bank accounts as well as crypto accounts in Dubai using this app. They used to get a commission through hawala on the money they used to route through that app,” explained Singhal.
The racket was being run from call centres that were designed to resemble real offices of probe agencies and from which video calls were made, he added. Police have recovered Rs 12.75 lakh in cash, 761 SIM cards, 120 mobile phones, 96 cheque books, 92 debit and credit cards and 42 bank passbooks related to accounts taken on rent to make transactions, Singhal said.
