Surat Cyber Crime Police reached his home just in time and rescued him, as seen in a surfaced video showing officers confronting the cyber gang and saving his life savings.
Aadhaar card and bank account used in money laundering
- This incident began on Monday afternoon. Amit Desai, who retired in 2022, returned from Canada in May. He received a call from an unknown local number.
- The caller identified himself as a police and CBI officer and told the elderly man, Your Aadhaar card and bank account have been used in money laundering.
- In the Red Corner Notice issued against notorious accused Naresh Goyal, your documents have been used in a 45 crore fraud.
Barrage of bogus documents and warrants launched to scare Even before Amit Desai could react or understand anything regarding this, a barrage of bogus documents and warrants, written on the letterheads of SBI and CBI, was launched on his mobile.
This was shocking for a retired and respected officer. Cyber fraudsters had laid such a skillful trap that Amitbhai became convinced he was truly in trouble.
Virtual court also set up to scare victim This drama, which started on Monday afternoon, continued for 72 consecutive hours. During this time, Amit Desai was also 'digitally arrested'. He was forced by cyber mafia to stay with the video call on.
To scare the elderly victim, an atmosphere of a virtual courtroom was created. On the video call, Amitbhai could see that a judge was sitting opposite, two lawyers were arguing, the bank manager was present, and CBI officers were also in the courtroom. All this was a drama, but it seemed so real that Amitbhai got scared.
To increase this fear, the cyber mafia sentenced another accused to 90 days in jail in a fake court and told the elderly person, 'If you don't want to go to jail, you will have to cooperate.' Seeing these scenes, Amit Desai was terrified.
Suspicion arose when asked to empty property and bank balance
The scam that began on Monday escalated on Wednesday when the cyber mafia ordered Amit Desai to liquidate his property and transfer Rs 46 lakh to an account falsely claimed to belong to the RBI.
Growing suspicious, Amit informed his brother, who quickly alerted the Surat Cyber Cell. Acting on instructions from DCP Bishakha Jain, a police team immediately reached Amit Desai’s home.
Police catch cyber mafia on live call
When the police entered the house, Amit Desai was on a video call with the cyber fraudster 'Vijay Khanna.' Officers quietly approached him. Sensing something unusual, the fraudster asked, 'Who is around you?' At that moment, the cyber cell officer grabbed Amitbhai’s phone and turned the camera toward himself, exposing the trap.
Victim told police about his 72-hour ordeal Feeling relieved by the police's action, Amit Desai narrated his ordeal. He said,
I used to sit with my phone on charge so that the mobile battery wouldn't run out. They asked me for 5 types of details, which included property and bank information. They told me that I would have to sell everything and transfer the money within a single day. It was this talk of selling property in just one day that made me suspicious, and I contacted my brother.
Cyber cell issues warning after rescue operation
- Surat Cyber Cell’s DCP Bishakha Jain, who led the operation, said the team immediately reached Amit Desai’s house after receiving the alert.
- At that time, he was fully trapped in a “digital arrest” and extremely frightened. She confirmed that he was safely rescued.
- Jain also stated that such cybercrime operations are often run from abroad, mainly from South-East Asian countries, and number-tracing has begun to reach the culprits.
- This quick action by Surat Police saved the senior citizen’s lifelong savings, and the cyber mafia’s interaction with the police highlights how alert people must remain in the digital world.",764,,True,False
4149.0,"Pledged jewellery, paid ₹22 lakh, still not released: Couple kept under ‘digital arrest’ for 96 days; fraud... - Bhaskar English",Bhaskar English,"Thu, 27 Aug 2026 00:54:23 GMT",https://www.bhaskarenglish.in/local/mp/bhopal/news/bhopal-digital-arrest-scam-couple-loses-lakhs-cyber-fraud-138849159.html,success_decoder,success,"Pledged jewellery, paid ₹22 lakh, still not released: Couple kept under ‘digital arrest’ for 96 days; fraudsters threatened property seizure - Bhopal News",,2026-08-27,"- English News
