Pledged jewellery, paid ₹22 lakh, still not released:Couple kept under ‘digital arrest’ for 96 days; fraudsters threatened property seizure
- Copy link
An elderly couple from Kolar in Bhopal was kept under “digital arrest” for three months by fraudsters posing as Delhi Police officials, a chief investigation officer and a Supreme Court judge.
The accused threatened to implicate the couple in a money-laundering case involving crores of rupees. Fearing arrest and legal action, the couple mortgaged their jewellery and transferred ₹22 lakh to the fraudsters.
The accused used seven different WhatsApp numbers and kept the couple under constant surveillance through video calls. After suspecting that they had fallen victim to a fraud, the couple approached the cyber cell and lodged a complaint.
Bhopal couple originally from Andhra Pradesh targeted by fake Delhi Police officer
According to police, Suvarna Lakshmi and Narasimha Rao, residents of Krishna Complex in Ganpati Enclave, are originally from Palnadu district in Andhra Pradesh. The couple had travelled to Andhra Pradesh with their family in February to celebrate Shivratri.
On May 19, Suvarna received a call from a man who identified himself as Khanna, an officer from Daryaganj police station in Delhi. He told her that arrest warrants had been issued against her and her husband.
The caller further claimed that the couple had been involved in crores of rupees worth of transactions through a Canara Bank account in Delhi and linked them to an alleged money-laundering case involving Naresh Goyal.
Fake judge threatened to seize their property
After this, questioning began over a video call. The couple was prevented from speaking to anyone or leaving their house. On May 21, a woman claiming to be the Chief Investigation Officer obtained details of their bank accounts.
The following day, they were made to speak to a fake judge over a video call. Showing them the details on their Aadhaar cards, he threatened to take action against them.
On June 9, the fake judge again announced an order to seize their property. After this, a woman named Nisha Patel asked the couple to return to Bhopal.
Couple pledges jewellery to pay ₹22 lakh, remains under digital surveillance
The fraudsters allegedly told the couple to sell or pledge their fixed deposits and jewellery to settle the purported case. Following their instructions, the couple left Andhra Pradesh on June 10 and reached Bhopal the next day. On June 12, they raised ₹22 lakh by pledging their jewellery.
The couple then transferred the money in five instalments to different bank accounts provided by the accused.
The fraudsters remained in contact with them through seven different WhatsApp numbers. Despite receiving the full ₹22 lakh, the accused continued to threaten and monitor the couple.
The couple remained under surveillance through video calls until August 23. They eventually became suspicious and independently checked the documents shared with them.
They then discovered that the purported Supreme Court order was fake. The alleged digital arrest lasted 96 days, from May 19 to August 23.
Cyber cell registers FIR after couple exposes digital arrest fraud
After realising that they had been duped, the couple approached the cyber cell and lodged a complaint. Police registered an FIR on August 27 and subsequently transferred the case diary to Kolar police station.
Investigators are examining all seven WhatsApp numbers used by the accused, the video calls and transactions involving the five bank accounts into which the money was transferred.",590,,True,False
4150.0,"CBI’s Operation Chakra-VI Targets Digital Arrest Scams in Telangana, AP - Deccan Chronicle",Deccan Chronicle,"Thu, 25 Jun 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://www.deccanchronicle.com/southern-states/telangana/cbis-operation-chakra-vi-targets-digital-arrest-scams-in-telangana-ap-1966105,success_decoder,success,"CBI’s Operation Chakra-VI Targets Digital Arrest Scams in Telangana, AP",M Srinivas,2026-06-25,"CBI’s Operation Chakra-VI Targets Digital Arrest Scams in Telangana, AP
The mule accounts were reportedly used for laundering approximately Rs. 2 crore of suspected proceeds of crime: CBI
Hyderabad: Telangana and Andhra Pradesh were among the 16 States where the Central Bureau of Investigation (CBI) has conducted coordinated searches under operation Chakra-VI on the cybercrime infrastructure facilitating digital arrest scams.
In a major crackdown on the cybercrime infrastructure facilitating digital arrest scams, the CBI, under Operation Chakra-VI, constituted 60 special teams and conducted coordinated searches at more than 80 locations across 16 States, namely Punjab, Gujarat, Delhi, Maharashtra, Haryana, Tamil Nadu, Telangana, Andhra Pradesh, Uttar Pradesh, Madhya Pradesh, Rajasthan, Assam, West Bengal, Manipur, Karnataka and Odisha.
The searches were carried out as part of an ongoing investigation aimed at dismantling a targeted operational network involved in over 200 cases of digital arrest scams and arrested two persons from Chennai and Kolkata for their alleged involvement in the incorporation of shell companies and the opening and operation of mule bank accounts.
These accounts were reportedly used for laundering approximately Rs. 2 crore of suspected proceeds of crime.
The CBI recently unearthed a fraudulent website bearing a URL deceptively similar to the official website of the Supreme Court. The fraudsters allegedly used this fraudulently registered domain to deceive victims under the guise of digital arrest. Based on a complaint received from the Registry of Supreme Court, the CBI registered an FIR and commenced investigation into the matter.
Leveraging advanced forensic tools and technical expertise, the CBI identified critical elements of the criminal infrastructure operating both within India and abroad. Investigation has revealed that the perpetrators uploaded forged and fabricated documents, including fake orders resembling those issued by courts and various law enforcement agencies, to lend credibility to their fraudulent activities.
During the searches, several incriminating documents, digital devices, mobile phones and records relating to bank transactions were seized. These materials are being subjected to detailed forensic examination and analysis.
The CBI has also found evidence indicating that, in addition to Indian citizens, nationals of several other countries may have been defrauded by the same network. The concerned law enforcement agencies in those jurisdictions are being alerted through appropriate channels.
Investigation in the case is continuing. The CBI remains committed to dismantling cybercrime infrastructure and ensuring justice for victims of cyber-enabled frauds, including digital arrest scams.",393,,True,False
