Gwalior State Cyber Police have busted a major network involved in fraud under the pretext of “digital arrest” and Telegram trading, arresting two accused. The accused used to impersonate officials from FedEx and the Narcotics Control Bureau to intimidate people and extort lakhs of rupees.
Complainant Harshit Dwivedi said the accused called him claiming drugs and illegal items were found in his parcel. The call was then transferred to a supposed narcotics officer. By creating fear of “digital arrest”, they got ₹30,25,719 transferred in the name of “police clearance”.
After the complaint, police arrested Saurabh Kumar (24), a resident of Lakhimpur Kheri, and Rajesh Kumar (27). Cyber police traced the accused using fake IP addresses, bank transactions and digital evidence. They were arrested after a raid in Lakhimpur Kheri, Uttar Pradesh.
Investigation revealed that the accused opened fake bank accounts to route the fraud money. Transactions worth crores from 16 states were found in these accounts. Around ₹10 lakh from the complainant was directly transferred to the accused’s account.
According to police, the accused first posed as courier company employees and then scared victims by claiming drugs were found in parcels. The call was then connected to a fake officer. Through video calls, victims were threatened with “digital arrest” and made to transfer money.
Network Spread Across the Country
The probe revealed the gang’s network was spread across Jammu and Kashmir, Punjab, Uttar Pradesh, Uttarakhand, Andhra Pradesh, Gujarat, Karnataka, Maharashtra, Rajasthan, Tamil Nadu and West Bengal. The accused kept changing locations to evade arrest.
Police-Government Agencies Don't Ask for Money on Video Calls
Police clarified that no government agency demands money over phone or video calls and there is no legal provision like “digital arrest”. In such cases, people should immediately report on cyber helpline 1930 and never share OTP or bank details with anyone.",350,,True,False
