Hyderabad: A nationwide cyber fraud network that allegedly misused Aadhaar biometrics to activate illegal SIM cards and power ‘digital arrest’ scams from abroad has been busted by Hyderabad City Police under Operation Octopus 3.0.
The total fraud being probed is estimated to be worth Rs 101.87 crore.
Fingerprints illegally used
The crackdown exposed how unsuspecting citizens’ fingerprints and Aadhaar-linked details were allegedly used to generate ‘ghost SIMs,’ which were later converted into e-SIMs and remotely accessed by cybercrime syndicates operating overseas.
Police said these Indian mobile numbers were extensively used in cyber frauds, including digital arrest scams, sextortion rackets, fake matrimonial profiles and impersonation crimes, with total fraud linked to the network estimated at Rs 101.87 crore.
66 arrested in raids across 13 states
The Hyderabad Cyber Crime Wing launched the operation after identifying 1,194 suspicious SIM cards allegedly linked to cyber offences.
Eighteen special police teams conducted week-long raids across Telangana, Andhra Pradesh, Karnataka, Maharashtra, Uttar Pradesh, West Bengal, Delhi, Haryana, Gujarat, Madhya Pradesh, Tamil Nadu, Odisha and Punjab.
Police arrested 66 accused, including:
- 44 users of ghost SIM cards
- 20 telecom Point of Sale (PoS) agents and promoters
- Two SIM suppliers
Officials also seized 544 SIM cards, including 432 sealed SIMs and 112 activated SIMs.
Investigators found the accused were allegedly connected to 76 cybercrime cases registered across the country.
How customers were tricked during SIM verification
Police said telecom agents exploited routine SIM issuance and mobile number portability (MNP) procedures to illegally activate additional SIM cards.
According to investigators, customers visiting telecom outlets were often told that:
The network was slow,
The server was down, or fingerprints had not matched properly.
Using these excuses, agents allegedly collected multiple biometric scans from customers and activated extra SIM cards without their knowledge.
The fraud was reportedly widespread in remote villages and tribal areas, where camps were organised to target illiterate and economically weaker people.
Police said many victims were lured with promises of free internet services while their Aadhaar-linked biometric details were secretly used to create illegal SIM connections.
Indian SIMs converted into e-SIMs abroad
Investigators revealed that the illegally activated SIM cards were quickly converted into e-SIMs using advanced technology and digitally transferred to cybercrime handlers based abroad.
This allowed foreign fraudsters to use Indian mobile numbers without physically transporting SIM cards out of the country.
“These Indian numbers helped cybercriminals gain the trust of victims more easily,” officials said.
The numbers were allegedly used to:
- create WhatsApp and Telegram accounts,
- operate fake profiles on matrimonial and dating platforms,
- run sextortion rackets involving nude video calls and carry out “digital arrest” scams by impersonating agencies such as the CBI and police.
Agents allegedly vanished after bulk activations
Police said the network operated systematically.
Once thousands of SIM cards were activated, local agents allegedly shut down their stores overnight and disappeared to evade law enforcement agencies.
Among the arrested PoS agents:
10 were linked to Vodafone-Idea,
seven to Airtel,
and three to Jio.
Hyderabad police to meet telecom firms
The Hyderabad Police Commissioner said the city police were taking the ghost SIM menace seriously and would soon hold meetings with senior officials of Bharti Airtel, Reliance Jio and Vodafone Idea.
Police will also approach the Department of Telecommunications and the Telecom Regulatory Authority of India to seek stricter regulations and tighter KYC norms
Real-time verification, blacklisting proposed
To curb ghost SIM fraud, police recommended:
- mandatory real-time verification during SIM issuance and MNP processes,
- immediate deactivation of SIM cards linked to cybercrime,
- blacklisting of PoS agents and distributors violating norms,
- improved coordination between telecom companies and law enforcement agencies.
Officials also suggested integrating telecom KYC systems with databases of suspected cyber offenders maintained by agencies such as I4C.
Citizens asked to check SIMs linked to their Aadhaar
Police urged citizens not to share Aadhaar details, OTPs or biometric information with anyone and advised people to buy SIM cards only from authorised dealers.
People were also asked to verify how many SIM cards are active in their name through the government’s Sanchar Saathi portal and report any unauthorised numbers immediately.
Victims of cyber fraud were advised to contact the national cybercrime helpline 1930 or file complaints through the cybercrime reporting portal without delay, as the first hour after a fraud is considered crucial for recovery efforts.",805,,True,False
4173.0,Digital arrest scam: Tirupati police arrest eight for cheating elderly citizen of ₹80 lakh - The Hindu,The Hindu,"Wed, 19 Nov 2025 08:00:00 GMT",https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/andhra-pradesh/digital-arrest-scam-tirupati-police-arrest-eight-for-cheating-elderly-citizen-of-80-lakh/article70299772.ece,success_decoder,success,Digital arrest scam: Tirupati police arrest eight for cheating elderly citizen of ₹80 lakh,A D Rangarajan,2025-11-19,"The Tirupati police arrested eight persons, believed to be members of an inter-State cyber criminal gang, for allegedly swindling senior citizens on the pretext of ‘digital arrest’.
