4186.0,"Fraudster held from Andhra for putting elderly woman under ‘digital arrest’ for 10 days, extorting Rs 30L - The Times of India",The Times of India,"Wed, 28 Jan 2026 08:00:00 GMT",https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/city/bhubaneswar/fraudster-held-from-andhra-for-putting-elderly-woman-under-digital-arrest-for-10-days-extorting-rs-30l/articleshow/127647766.cms,success_decoder,success,"Fraudster held from Andhra for putting elderly woman under âdigital arrestâ for 10 days, extorting Rs 30L | Bhubaneswar News - The Times of India",Debabrata Mohapatra,2026-01-28,"Bhubaneswar:
 City police arrested a 39-year-old man from Tirupati in Andhra Pradesh on Tuesday for allegedly subjecting an elderly woman to âdigital arrestâ for over 10 days in Nov last year and coercing her into paying Rs 30 lakh.
The accused, identified as Gulipagi Chiranjeevi, along with his associates, impersonated officers from the Telecom Regulatory Authority of India (Trai), Mumbai police and Enforcement Directorate (ED) to instil fear in the victim and extort money.
The crime unfolded on Nov 6, 2025, when the 70-year-old woman, who lives with her 77-year-old husband, received a call around 2:20 pm. The caller claimed to be from Trai and alleged that her SIM card was linked to illegal activities. Before she could respond, another man joined the call, introducing himself as an officer from Colaba police station in Mumbai. He sent fabricated documents to her instant messaging account, reinforcing the false claim of her involvement in unlawful activities.
Soon after, the woman received a video call from another fraudster posing as an ED officer. He instructed her not to leave her home, declaring that she was under âdigital arrestâ. 
Exploiting her fear further, he claimed that an arrest warrant had been issued against her for money laundering.
âThe caller insisted that her bank accounts required auditing as part of the investigation and demanded a security deposit of Rs 30 lakh, assuring her that the amount would be refunded after the audit,â a police officer said.
Terrified by the threats, the woman and her husband reportedly transferred Rs 10 lakh on Nov 7, Rs 3.5 lakh on Nov 10, Rs 9.5 lakh on Nov 14 and Rs 7 lakh on Nov 15.
âThey were put under digital arrest from Nov 6 to 15. The money was transferred to the accounts of Chiranjeevi and two others,â the officer said. Police said that efforts are underway to identify and arrest other members of the racket who played a role in the fraud.
After the scam left the couple shaken and financially drained, they lodged a complaint with the cyber police station in the city on Nov 18.
