4193.0,"WhatsApp crackdown on digital arrest scams: 9,400 accounts banned, Supreme Court told - Moneycontrol.com",Moneycontrol.com,"Tue, 28 Apr 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://www.moneycontrol.com/news/india/whatsapp-crackdown-on-digital-arrest-scams-9-400-accounts-banned-supreme-court-told-13902178.html,success_decoder,success,"WhatsApp crackdown on digital arrest scams: 9,400 accounts banned, Supreme Court told- Moneycontrol.com",Rewati Karan,2026-04-28,"A significant number of accounts targeting Indian users were traced to scam hubs in Southeast Asia, especially Cambodia. 
  April 28, 2026 / 19:34 IST  
         Meta-owned WhatsApp has told the Supreme Court that it disabled more than 9,400 accounts tied to so-called “digital arrest” frauds over a 12-week period starting January 2026.
The disclosure was placed before the Court through Attorney General R Venkataramani in ongoing suo motu proceedings examining the surge in scams involving impersonation of police and judicial authorities.
According to the submission, the action was part of a focused enforcement effort carried out alongside Indian agencies. The platform’s internal probe also shed light on how these rackets function.
A significant number of accounts targeting Indian users were traced to scam hubs in Southeast Asia, especially Cambodia. Fraudsters commonly adopted names such as “Delhi Police,” “Mumbai HQ,” “CBI,” or “ATS Department,” often pairing them with official-looking insignia to appear legitimate.
Explaining its approach, the company said, “WhatsApp does not treat signals from investigation agencies and Government of India (GoI) as isolated, one-off reports. Each signal is treated as a seed to map and disrupt the entire criminal network.”
While government inputs initially identified roughly 3,800 suspicious accounts, the platform’s own systems expanded that sweep considerably, resulting in thousands more being acted upon.
To counter these schemes, WhatsApp outlined several new safeguards, it said. These include a system that flags logo-based impersonation, logging of display names or “pushnames,” and the use of a large language model to detect changing fraud tactics. It has also compiled a repository of scam-linked assets to identify repeat behaviour across networks.
Enforcement was not limited to primary operators; it extended to accounts amplifying such scams in groups and channels, as well as those sharing overlapping technical markers.
The company is also introducing product-level interventions aimed at prevention. These include alerts for suspicious first-time messages, showing how old an unknown account is, limiting visibility of profile photos in risky interactions, and improved caller identification for unfamiliar numbers. It said these steps are expected to produce a “material and observable decline” in such fraud.
At the same time, WhatsApp acknowledged the limits of platform-level action, stating, ""Many scams involve off-platform payment, coercion, and criminal infrastructure. WhatsApp can disrupt the accounts and networks; dismantling the underlying criminal operations requires coordinated multi-jurisdictional law enforcement action.""
The Court’s scrutiny began after a complaint by a senior citizen couple who alleged they were cheated of Rs 1.5 crore by individuals posing as officials of the Central Bureau of Investigation (CBI) and others. Taking note of similar reports, the bench ordered a probe and sought coordinated national action.
Submissions before the Court also highlighted parallel developments. The Reserve Bank of India (RBI) has proposed revised rules on customer liability in digital fraud, while telecom authorities are working on faster SIM blocking timelines.
Investigators further noted that most mule accounts used in such frauds remain active for less than a day, underscoring the urgency of real-time intervention.
