During the hearing, Attorney General R Venkataramani, appearing for the Centre, submitted that an Inter-Departmental Committee (IDC) is in the process of finalising a comprehensive report to identify systemic gaps in tackling digital arrest cases. Solicitor General Tushar Mehta too said the government is working on a law to address deepfakes and digital arrests.
According to government sources, the proposed law would not only define deepfakes and digital arrest as standalone crimes but also provide for consequential punishments, including the freezing of assets and bank accounts linked to such offences. The Bill is expected to complement the Information Technology Rules, which were strengthened earlier this year to tighten obligations on online platforms to act against AI-generated harmful content.
The proposal follows a sharp rise in cyber frauds involving AI-generated videos and voice cloning. Deepfakes have increasingly been used to impersonate public figures, celebrities and ordinary citizens, while “digital arrest"" scams involve fraudsters posing as police, CBI, ED or other government officials, coercing victims into transferring money under the false claim that they are under investigation.
Although offences such as cheating, impersonation, forgery and identity theft are already punishable under the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita (BNS) and the Information Technology Act, investigators have argued that the absence of specific legal definitions for deepfakes and digital arrest has complicated investigations and prosecutions. The new legislation is expected to provide greater legal clarity and stronger deterrence against these evolving forms of cybercrime.
If introduced and passed, the law would place India among a growing number of jurisdictions moving towards dedicated legislation for AI-enabled deception and online impersonation as governments worldwide grapple with the rapid spread of generative AI technologies.
