4217.0,Lucknow doctor duped of nearly Rs 3 crore by scammers after 'digital arrest' - India Today,India Today,"Wed, 14 Aug 2024 07:00:00 GMT",https://www.indiatoday.in/cities/lucknow/story/lucknow-doctor-duped-of-rs-3-crore-by-scammers-after-digital-arrest-2582410-2024-08-14,success_decoder,success,Lucknow doctor duped of nearly Rs 3 crore by scammers after 'digital arrest',Abhishek Mishra,2024-08-14,"Lucknow doctor duped of nearly Rs 3 crore by scammers after 'digital arrest'
Dr Ruchika Tandon, an associate professor at SGPGIMS Lucknow, said she was duped of Rs 2.81 crore by scammers who posed as government officials.
A doctor in Lucknow was duped of Rs 2.81 crore after she was 'digitally arrested' by scammers who posed as government officials, the police said.
Dr Ruchika Tandon, an associate professor at SGPGIMS Lucknow, said she received a call from a man who introduced himself as an officer with Telecom Regulatory Authority of India (TRAI). He told her that her mobile number would be switched off as about 22 complaints had been received for harassing messages being sent to people.
She was then informed that a complaint had been filed against her, and a link would be sent to her through which she could speak to a CBI officer. After connecting through the link, she spoke with a person named Rahul Gupta, who identified himself as a CBI officer.Â
Gupta informed her that her name had come up in a money laundering case and also in a case involving the trafficking of women and children, for which she would be arrested.Â
However, the fraudsters also provided a way to avoid arrest, saying that she would be under 'digital arrest' through a video call and should not contact anyone. During this time, they would examine her accounts, and then she would be released.Â
Ruchika Tandon agreed to this, and the fraudsters, by misleading her, obtained information about five of her accounts and transferred approximately Rs 2.81 crore to their account.Â
""They asked me not to tell anyone about it since this was a matter of national importance,"" Tandon said.
After realising the fraud, Tandon filed a complaint with the police.
Speaking about the case, DCP East Shashank Singh said, ""On August 10, a written complaint was given by PGI doctor Ruchika Tandon in the cyber crime cell. In her complaint, she said that from August 1 to August 8, she was kept under digital arrest by cyber fraudsters. During this time, the fraudsters transferred Rs 2.81 crore from the doctor's account to their account.""Â
Ghulam, the Investigating Officer of the case, said, ""If we talk from the point of view of law, there is no such term as 'digital arrest', but the modus operandi of cyber criminals today... when the name of a big investigating agency is cited, people start panicking thinking they have been named in a case. They make the victims download Skype for video calling, in which they can be seen wearing police uniform but don't show their face and tell that they cannot reveal their identity. They first take all information from the victim and then tell them that they have been arrested, they cannot talk to anyone, not even on phone.""",472,,True,False
4219.0,'Digital arrest' scam: Delhi woman loses ₹13cr - NewsBytes,NewsBytes,"Fri, 03 Apr 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://www.newsbytesapp.com/news/science/digital-arrest-what-s-this-fraud-and-how-to-avoid-it/story,success_decoder,success,'Digital arrest' scam: Delhi woman loses ₹13cr,Akash Pandey,2026-04-03,"'Digital arrest' scam: Delhi woman loses ₹13cr
What's the story
A 77-year-old retired woman from Delhi lost over ₹13 crore in a 'digital arrest' scam. The con artists impersonated police officials and a judge via a WhatsApp video call. Such scams are on the rise across India, mainly targeting financially stable but less informed citizens. The victims are isolated from their friends and family and coerced into transferring money.
Legal clarification
There is no such thing as a 'digital arrest'
Sanjeev Jain, a former Principal District and Sessions Judge in Delhi, has clarified that there is no such thing as a 'digital arrest' under Indian law.
He emphasized that any arrest must follow due process, including proper identification and documentation.
Jain also warned citizens to stay alert and report such scams to the police or cyber authorities immediately.
Scam details
How the scam works
The victim of this scam was duped by fraudsters posing as police officials. They told her that she was involved in a complex money laundering case.
Over 16 days, she was traumatized and coerced into 'virtual court' proceedings.
The con artists even staged a fake police station act and scripted court sessions to make their threats seem real.
Safety tips
Here are some tips to protect yourself from scams
Here are some ways you can protect yourself from such scams:
Verify independently: If faced with a 'digital arrest' threat, don't panic and verify independently.
Avoid urgent money transfers: Law enforcement officials never ask for immediate payments.
Talk to family or friends: Fraudsters rely on isolating their victims, so always keep your loved ones in the loop if you're facing threats of 'digital arrest.'
Additional precautions
More tips to safeguard yourself from scams
Protect sensitive info: Safeguard confidential data such as OTPs, CVVs, ATM PINs, and internet banking credentials.
Be wary of staged setups: Fake uniforms, ID cards, flags, and courts are common tricks used by fraudsters.
Use banking safeguards: Set transaction alerts and daily limits on your accounts to prevent unauthorized transactions.",336,,True,False
4220.0,"Brain behind digital arrest scams worth Rs 1,071-cr arrested - Ahmedabad Mirror",Ahmedabad Mirror,"Wed, 19 Aug 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://www.ahmedabadmirror.com/brain-behind-digital-arrest-scams-worth-rs-1071-cr-arrested/81919900.html,success_decoder,success,"Brain behind digital arrest scams worth Rs 1,071-cr arrested",Ahmedabad Mirror Aug ; UPDATED Aug,2026-08-18,"Ahmedabad Cover Story
Brain behind digital arrest scams worth Rs 1,071-cr arrested
25-year-old moved fraud proceeds via 1,754 bank a/cs, converted them into crypto and transferred them to a Chinese cybercrime network
Accused Rafikul Alam Ali (seating); cops who arrested him (standing)
The Cyber Centre of Excellence (CCoE), Gandhinagar, has arrested a 25-year-old Assam man who allegedly served as a key financial handler for a cybercrime network running digital arrest scams and other online frauds. Investigators say he routed money through hundreds of bank accounts, converted the proceeds into cryptocurrency and transferred them to members of a Chinese cybercrime network. He is believed to have used around 1,754 bank accounts to move cyber fraud proceeds worth Rs 1,071 crore.
The accused has been identified as Rafikul Alam Ali, 25, of Barpeta in Assam.
About 10 senior citizens were rescued during a live digital arrest scam that was underway, cyber crime sleuths said, with the hunt for his other associates now spread across several states.
The breakthrough came after officers identified a senior citizen who was allegedly being subjected to a digital arrest using forged letters purportedly issued by the Maharashtra Police and the Supreme Court. During counselling and questioning, investigators examined the bank accounts linked to the victim's case and found a wider network behind it.
Police then discovered around 10 other victims across the country were simultaneously being subjected to digital arrest scams. Teams visited their homes, counselled them and stopped further payments to the fraudsters. Technical analysis and victim statements then led investigators to the alleged principal financial handler of the racket.
Three-day undercover surveillance operation in Assam
A CCoE team travelled to Barpeta in Assam and spent three days conducting undercover surveillance. Once the technical analysis and victim information were in hand, the team raided the suspect's residence and arrested him, recovering several electronic gadgets allegedly used in the operation.
Rafikul allegedly handled money generated through digital arrest scams and other cyber frauds across India, investigators said, moving it through multiple bank accounts and converting the proceeds into cryptocurrency before transferring them to members of a Chinese gang.
Large amounts were split into smaller transactions
Investigators found large sums were allegedly broken into smaller transactions to evade the attention of financial surveillance agencies. In one instance, funds were reportedly spread across 1,754 bank accounts nationwide within an hour.
The accused allegedly maintained direct communication with Chinese cybercriminals through WhatsApp and Telegram, exchanging financial information including payment scanners, user IDs, passwords, cryptocurrency wallet details, the modus operandi of crimes and other instructions.
23 crypto wallets, Rs 16 crore in transactions
Police reportedly recovered details of 23 cryptocurrency wallets from the accused's mobile phone during the investigation, with transactions worth approximately Rs 16 crore allegedly carried out through them. He also allegedly used a cryptocurrency trading application to buy and sell digital currency illegally.
Rafikul involved in 1,090 cyber crimes
Investigators claimed Rafikul Alam had a role in 1,090 cybercrime cases across India worth a total of Rs 1,071.55 crore. Police also found evidence of financial transactions through gaming accounts on his mobile phone.
10 senior citizens saved from ongoing digital arrests
Three separate FIRs have been registered as CCoE teams moved to prevent victims across India from transferring money while the scams were still underway.
-In Ahmedabad
A senior citizen was allegedly threatened with arrest in a child pornography case after fraudsters posed as police officials and demanded Rs 20 lakh. Officers visited the victim's home at night, counselled him and prevented him from transferring the money.
-In Surat
Another senior citizen was allegedly threatened with arrest in money-laundering and terrorism-related cases. The fraudsters reportedly demanded Rs 60 lakh, after which the victim agreed to pay Rs 40 lakh. He was allegedly preparing to sell his flat but, when that failed, sold his shop for Rs 20 lakh and kept the money ready to hand over. Police reached his home, counselled him and stopped the transaction.
-In Jamnagar
A senior citizen was allegedly lured with promises of 50-60 per cent returns by fraudsters posing as brokers of a company. The victim had already transferred Rs 9.50 lakh into different bank accounts and was preparing to send another Rs 10 lakh when the cybercrime team intervened and stopped the payment.
-In Chhattisgarh and Mumbai
Police also prevented five other victims, one each from Raipur in Chhattisgarh and Mumbai in Maharashtra, and three from Odisha, including two from Bhubaneswar and one from Sundargarh, from losing money after counselling them.
Police warning to citizens
The CCoE has urged citizens not to panic if someone calls or makes a video call claiming to be a police officer, CBI or ED official, Supreme Court representative or another government or law-enforcement official and threatens them with arrest in connection with money laundering, drugs, courier-related offences or other crimes. Police have advised people not to share OTPs, bank details or copies of Aadhaar cards, and not to transfer money based on such threats or forged documents. Anyone who suspects a cyber fraud should immediately contact the nearest police station or report the incident on the national cyber crime helpline 1930.",855,,True,False
