Assam grad plays key role in Rs 1k‑cr cyber scam
Quick money lured Rafikul towards digital arrest scams; he used special app to convert his earnings into USDT and other cryptos
Assam grad plays key role in Rs 1k‑cr cyber scam
A 25‑year‑old BCA graduate from Assam who once worked for a private firm in Haryana has been arrested as a key financial handler in a Rs 1,071‑crore cybercrime network linked to Chinese operators.
Rafikul Alam of Barpeta allegedly joined the racket after being lured by agents promising easy money. He began as a mule account provider before managing funds from “digital arrest” scams.
Direct contact with handlers
Investigators said Alam maintained direct contact with Chinese handlers he called “China Madam” and “China Boss,” communicating via Google Translate on Telegram and WhatsApp.
His alleged speciality was moving cheated money without raising suspicion. Investigators said he split the proceeds into smaller amounts and routed them through hundreds of bank accounts and around 3,000 gaming accounts, converting the money into cryptocurrency and USDT through a specific application before transferring it to crypto wallets controlled by his Chinese handlers. Initial calls to digital arrest victims used pre-activated SIM cards, before switching to Cambodian SIM cards.
SP Vivek Bheda told Mirror that Alam is suspected of being linked to other Chinese cybercriminals and at least three Indian associates based in Cambodia, who allegedly posed as enforcement officials and threatened victims with arrest in money-laundering and child pornography cases. “It has been two-and-a-half years since Rafikul started transferring money to different Chinese syndicates operating through Cambodia. He owned a car in a village where most people did not have one and carried several iPhones,” said SP Bheda.
Police believe Alam is linked to about 25 FIRs and suspect his network handled cybercrime proceeds amounting to nearly Rs 1,071 crore, using around 1,754 bank accounts. He reportedly had 23 crypto wallets containing Rs 16 crore.
The Cyber Centre of Excellence (CCoE), Gandhinagar, arrested Alam, describing him as a key financial handler for a cybercrime network involved in digital arrest scams and other online frauds.
“This is one of the biggest scamsters we have caught in the country, particularly in connection with digital arrest scams. His associates are being traced, and the leads provided by him could prove crucial in cracking the wider syndicate,” investigators said.",391,,True,False
