Mumbai: Two men were held by the North Region Cyber Police for duping a senior citizen of Rs 50.5 lakh in a digital arrest scam.The complainant is a retired bank official. Between Sept 11 to 24, he received audio and video calls over WhatsApp from an individual claiming to be a Nashik police officer. He accused the senior citizen of involvement in a money laundering case and said that his bank balance would be verified. He sent an official-looking letter with govt stamps (which later turned out to be fake) addressed to the complainant and containing his Aadhaar number. He also sent across a fake copy of an identity card of an NIA officer to scare him. The complainant and his wife were threatened with arrest and paid Rs 50.5 lakh. After the Cyber Police registered a case, they traced Rs 29.5 lakh to the bank account of an individual who lived in Ulhasnagar. The individual turned out to be a money mule who had created a bank account and given the bank kit to cyber fraudsters for receiving defrauded sums from unsuspecting citizens. Police arrested the money mule and another accused to whom he had given out his bank kit. 
