4307.0,Ahmedabad man living in Australia digitally 'arrested’: Cheated of ₹48 lakhs; fraudsters took control of mo... - Bhaskar English,Bhaskar English,"Wed, 04 Feb 2026 08:00:00 GMT",https://www.bhaskarenglish.in/local/gujarat/news/ahmedabad-youth-australia-cheated-48-lakh-digital-arrest-scam-137120657.html,success_decoder,success,Ahmedabad man living in Australia digitally 'arrested’: Cheated of ₹48 lakhs; fraudsters took control of mobile & laptop using IP address - Gujarat News,,2026-02-04,"- English News
- Local
- Gujarat
- Ahmedabad Youth In Australia Cheated Of ₹48 Lakh In Digital Arrest Scam
Ahmedabad man living in Australia digitally 'arrested’:Cheated of ₹48 lakhs; fraudsters took control of mobile & laptop using IP address
- Copy link
In recent months, there has been a rise in digital arrest scams in which victims are intimidated using various false threats. Cyber fraud gangs have now begun targeting NRIs living abroad as well.
In one such case, a youth from Ahmedabad residing in Australia was digitally “arrested” in the name of the Mumbai Crime Branch and cheated of ₹48 lakh, including amounts in Australian dollars. The victim’s father, who lives in Ahmedabad, has lodged a complaint with the cyber crime cell, and further investigation is currently underway.
WhatsApp call made to youth living in Sydney in name of Mumbai Crime Branch
An elderly resident of Mira Ambika Road in Naranpura, Ahmedabad, has two sons. The elder son lives in Adelaide, Australia, while the younger son resides in Sydney. On August 26, 2025, the son living in Sydney received a WhatsApp call from an Indian mobile number.
The caller introduced himself as an officer from the Mumbai Crime Branch and frightened the youth by claiming that suspicious transactions worth crores of rupees had been carried out using his mobile number and bank account. He further alleged that these transactions were linked to anti-national activities.
Fraudsters gain control of laptop & mobile after obtaining IP address
The cyber criminals kept the youth under constant surveillance through continuous video calls. He was instructed to remain in front of the camera at all times and warned not to meet or speak to anyone. The fraudsters threatened that if he failed to cooperate, he would be arrested by the Australian police and detained at the airport upon returning to India.
Under severe mental pressure and fear, the youth followed their instructions. He was forced to purchase a new mobile phone and SIM card in Sydney. The fraudsters then obtained the IP addresses of his mobile phone and laptop, gaining complete control over both devices.
In the name of investigation, they collected his bank details and transferred 25,000 Australian dollars—approximately ₹14 lakh—from his Australian bank account.
FD worth 34 lakhs was broken & transferred from bank in Ahmedabad Along with this, the fraudsters also broke the youth's fixed deposit of Rs 34 lakh at Axis Bank in Naroda, Ahmedabad and transferred it to their account. The cyber fraudsters assured the youth that all the money would be returned after the investigation was completed, but in reality this was a big fraud. After this 'digital arrest' that lasted for a month, the youth realized that he had been cheated.
Filed a complaint with the Cyber Security Department in Australia
He filed a complaint with the Cyber Security Department in Australia. When his father came to know about the money being transferred from the bank in Ahmedabad, upon checking with the bank it was revealed that Rs 34 lakh had been transferred. After that, a complaint was filed with Ahmedabad Cyber Crime Branch and police have started investigation.
First case of extorting money from overseas Indian through digital arrest It is noteworthy that officials are saying this is the first case of cyber mafia extorting money from an Indian citizen living abroad through digital arrest. This incident has also become a warning for Indians living abroad that cyber fraud now knows no boundaries.",578,,True,False
