AHMEDABAD: A woman doctor from Gandhinagar fell prey to an elaborate âdigital arrest' scam in which fraudsters extorted Rs 19.25 crore from her over three months, from March 15 to June 25. Acting on the complaint, the cybercrime cell of CID (crime) arrested Lalji Jayantibhai Baldaniya, 30, a resident Valthan in Surat. According to cops, Rs 1 crore was deposited in his account from the cheated amount.According to police, Baldaniya offered his company's account, âMurlidhar Manufacturing', to cybercrime gangs in return for commission. The woman's complaint, registered on July 16, led CID to trace the money flow across 35 different bank accounts, eventually identifying Baldaniya.The complainant stated she was first contacted on March 15 by a person claiming to be Jyoti Vishwanath from the department of telecommunications. Over the next few days, she was added to WhatsApp and Skype calls by others who introduced themselves as a police sub-inspector, public prosecutors, and a notary officer. They alleged her mobile number was being used for sending ""standing offensive SMS"" and that a money-laundering case was registered against her.
