""The accused generated and sent fake notices from the Directorate of Enforcement and claimed she was under probe for FEMA and PMLA violations,"" said an official from the cybercrime cell. ""They mentally pressured her, saying she could be arrested unless she cooperated fully and kept the case secret.""She was then asked to provide full details of her movable and immovable assets. They instructed her to stay under constant WhatsApp video surveillance and told her that plainclothes officers were keeping physical watch over her.Following their instructions, she broke her fixed deposits, sold her jewellery, pledged gold kept in lockers, and even sold a property. She transferred Rs 19.24 crore in total to various accounts, believing the money was being held temporarily by the govt.
To convince her further, the accused sent fake acknowledgment letters with stamps and govt letterheads titled ""Financial Supervisory Freezing Certificate."" No money was ever returned.""We are coordinating with national agencies and suspect the gang may have links to a foreign country,"" added a CID officer. ""Four teams are working to trace financial trails, technical networks, sim cards, and field-level operations,"" he added.Trapped with fake charges- On March 15, the victim received a call from someone posing as an official from the department of telecommunications- Later, through WhatsApp and Skype calls, more individuals joined, identifying themselves as police sub-inspector, public prosecutors, and notary officer- They told her her mobile number was used to send offensive messages, and she would be booked under FEMA and PMLA- She was shown fake letters and warnings with the emblem of the Enforcement Directorate- To avoid arrest, she was told to follow instructions and avoid discussing the case with anyone- The accused demanded video proofs of her home, gold, lockers, and cupboards- She was put under ""digital arrest"" via continuous WhatsApp video calls and told she was being monitored physically- She was manipulated into breaking her FDs, selling gold and shares, mortgaging ornaments, and selling property- Rs 19.24 crore was deposited in 35 accounts through 117 transactions- Fraudsters sent fake receipts claiming the funds would be returned after verification- One such account led to the arrest of Surat-based Baldaniya 
