- Copy link
In Lucknow, cyber fraudsters adopted five steps to swindle ₹70 lakh from an elderly couple. They first controlled their minds by instilling fear, exploiting weakness, and gaining trust. Digitally arresting them for a week, they made the couple perform tasks like getting up, sitting, eating, and drinking, all under their instructions, except for sleeping. During this time, the couple transferred all the money from their accounts into the fraudsters' accounts.
The case is from Deepak Deluxe Sahara Grace Apartment in Jankipuram. Here, Bholanath Prasad (around 70 years old), a retired director from the airport, lives with his wife Madhuri Bhagat. They have a son who works as an engineer in Indore. Their daughter lives in Bengaluru. The elderly couple lives alone in Lucknow. From 16 July to 22 July, cyber fraudsters kept them on continuous video calls and made them transfer ₹70 lakh into four accounts. The very first call they received stated that they had to come to Pune ATS to give a statement.
In this report, learn how fraudsters lured a senior citizen couple, how they instilled fear of supporting terrorists. They promised to return the full amount if no involvement was found, and demanded secrecy and safety reports every two hours during the process. Later, when the fraud was discovered, police arrested two youths from Lucknow after an FIR was filed.
Now read about digital arrest sequentially
Introduced as a retired director
Madhuri Bhagat, wife of a retired airport director, filed a complaint with the police on 25 July when she did not find the money returned to her account even after two days. The police started an investigation and on 29 July, arrested two fraudsters who were assisting Cambodian cyber fraudsters from Lucknow. According to Madhuri Bhagat's complaint, she received a call on her mobile on 16 July.
The caller identified himself as Senior Inspector Akash Sharma from Lucknow Police Headquarters. He said that an illegal SIM issued from Jammu and Kashmir in her husband's name has been used in terrorist activities and a complaint has been filed against them. The fraudsters told her and her husband to immediately come to Pune ATS.
When the husband identified himself as a retired airport director and both as senior citizens, the accused asked them to cooperate in the action by digitally arresting them. After this, other individuals, identifying themselves as IPS officer Vikram Singh and NIA Chief, maintained continuous contact through video calls and phone.
Took advantage of no children or anyone else being at home
The fraudsters first gathered information about the house, from which they learned that the son is an engineer in Jabalpur, while the daughter is married and lives in Bengaluru. After this, the fraudsters lured them under the guise of help. They promised to get them an online Non-Involvement Certificate.
The elderly couple fell for their words and started cooperating. The fraudsters kept them on video call at home all the time and gave instructions for every task when they went out or to the bank, keeping them on call. During this, they took information about all the FDs and, under the pretext of investigation, got the FDs broken one by one and transferred ₹70 lakh to different bank accounts through RTGS.
Kept giving safety reports every two hours
The fraudsters kept telling the family to keep the whole matter confidential and that there was a threat to their lives from a terrorist organisation if the information was leaked. The fraudsters kept saying that the terrorist organisation would pose a threat to them as well as their family. The couple got completely scared by their words and started doing everything the fraudsters told them to do. For this, the elderly couple was made to send secrecy and safety reports from their mobile every 2 hours. This made the couple feel that the fraudsters were helping them.
Asked to keep their mobile phone with themselves constantly
The fraudsters asked both of them to stay together at all times and remain on video calls. Staying inside the house, a fixed time was set for every task of both elderly people. They even started eating meals while sitting in front of the video call. They only got a little relief during sleep.
When they went to the bank in the afternoon to break the FD, the fraudsters remained on the call. The fraudsters would tell them what to do and how to do it from there. After the FD money came into the account, the fraudsters transferred it to different accounts. Both were assured that the money would be returned after investigation.
Two arrested in the case, ₹35 lakh frozen
ADCP Crime Kiran Yadav said that during the investigation, the police arrested two accused associated with the gang. They were identified as Praveen Srivastava alias Amit, a resident of Chandroday Nagar, Rajajipuram, and Sumit Kumar Rawat, a resident of Devpur.
Praveen is the gang leader, while Sumit used to provide fake SIM cards. By interrogating both, information about other gang members and the fraud network is being gathered. The team has also frozen ₹35 lakh of the fraud.",899,,True,False
