4368.0,"‘Cambodia link, Chinese nationals’: How gang of 10 carried out digital arrests, online investment fraud - The Indian Express",The Indian Express,"Fri, 19 Dec 2025 08:00:00 GMT",https://indianexpress.com/article/cities/delhi/cambodia-link-chinese-nationals-how-gang-of-10-carried-out-digital-arrests-online-investment-fraud-10427845/,success_decoder,success,"‘Cambodia link, Chinese nationals’: How gang of 10 carried out digital arrests, online investment fraud",Alok Singh; Pragynesh; Maneeshkarki,2025-12-19,"3 min readNew DelhiDec 19, 2025 05:15 AM IST 					
						First published on: Dec 19, 2025 at 05:15 AM IST 					
				
							 
			
				
Investigations into a cyber fraud syndicate, leading to the arrest of 10 people in the last one week, have revealed that the accused were in touch with Chinese nationals who instructed them to operate “digital arrest and investment frauds” from Cambodia, The Indian Express has learnt.
The matter came to light after police received a complaint from a businessman who was allegedly duped of Rs 1 lakh in a digital arrest scam in South Delhi’s Shaheen Bagh. Following this, acting on a tip-off, raids were conducted across Delhi, Mumbai, Kerala, Odisha, Punjab, Uttar Pradesh, Rajasthan and Haryana. Ten men, including Indian masterminds operating from Kerala, were arrested.
Police said the syndicate had been operating for years and was allegedly involved in multiple types of financial cyber fraud, resulting in losses of Rs 50 crore to the public. Officers said they have identified the syndicate’s links to 66 police complaints filed across the country, including eight cases of digital arrest fraud and 43 of investment fraud.
Investigators said the syndicate was working through three chains – fake callers, SIM card providers and bank account providers – all suspected to be linked with Cambodia.
Central probe agencies have identified Cambodia as a major hub of international cyber fraud, including digital arrest and investment scams.
According to investigators, it is suspected that Chinese fraudsters are running the operation with the help of their Indian counterparts. “The defrauded money is likely transferred via multiple bank accounts, converted into cryptocurrency, and finally routed to Cambodia via Dubai,” an investigator said.
Deputy Commissioner of Police (Southeast) Dr Hemant Tiwari said, “The arrested members were instrumental in committing various cyber frauds, including digital arrest and investment frauds. In the current case, the victim, a resident of Shaheen Bagh, was duped by these men after being threatened through WhatsApp video calls. The fraudsters posed as Karnataka Police officers and claimed that his Aadhaar number and mobile phone number were involved in heinous crimes.”
He added that the 10 accused included mule account holders from Rajasthan and Odisha, who were lured by the gang to open current bank accounts for routing the cheated money. “One chain of this syndicate was responsible for providing fake SIM cards and other for making fraudulent calls.”
An officer said that Muhammad Shahid T and Muhammad Bugari from Kerala were among the India-based masterminds who were in contact with their foreign counterparts. “They assigned operations, coordinated the procurement of mule accounts and managed cash withdrawals through Muhammad Ahteshamul Haq and Dharmendra Chauhan from Uttar Pradesh,” the officer added.",443,,True,False
4369.0,Six cyber fraudsters held from four states; accounts flagged in three dozen cases of over Rs 100 crore - The Indian Express,The Indian Express,"Thu, 13 Nov 2025 08:00:00 GMT",https://indianexpress.com/article/cities/pune/six-cyber-fraudsters-held-from-four-states-accounts-flagged-in-three-dozen-cases-of-over-rs-100-crore-10361927/,success_decoder,success,Six cyber fraudsters held from four states; accounts flagged in three dozen cases of over Rs 100 crore,Sushant Kulkarni; Saurajitmazumdar,2025-11-13,"3 min readPuneNov 13, 2025 04:17 PM IST 					
						First published on: Nov 13, 2025 at 12:03 AM IST 					
				
							 
			
				
CRACKING TWO major ‘digital arrest’ cases in which senior citizens were duped of Rs 2.8 crore earlier this year, the Pimpri Chinchwad Police have arrested six cybercriminals operating from Hyderabad, Mysuru, Thiruvananthapuram and Mumbai.
Investigators said these suspects are linked to at least three dozen cases registered across the country, and bank accounts operated by them have collectively seen fraudulent transactions close to Rs 100 crore. Probe has revealed the role of the six suspects in the Rs 58 crore ‘digital arrest’ scam registered in Mumbai, the biggest ever fraud of the type registered in the country so far.
Two separate FIRs were registered at the Cyber Crime police station of Pimpri Chinchwad police in which two senior citizens were duped of Rs one crore and Rs 1.8 crore. As the investigations teams from the Cyber Crime police station launched a probe into the cell phone numbers, bank accounts used in the cases, they got leads about the locations of the suspects who had acted as mule account holders as well as mule account handlers in these two cases. Different teams from the police station were dispatched to Karnataka, Telangana, Kerala and Mumbai.
Over the past three weeks, the teams arrested a total of six cybercriminals in a coordinated operation. The details of the same were shared with the media on Wednesday. All the six suspects are currently in police custody. Police have identified the suspects as Mohammed Amer Akil Mohammed Arif (35), a real estate dealer based out of Hyderabad, Jigar Jitesh Patel (23), who works at the office of a Chartered Accountant in Mumbai, Ajith Vijayan (36), who runs a travel agency at Thiruvananthapuram, Sachin P Prakash (26), a security guard from Karnataka, Mohammed Rihan Mohammed Tajmul (18), a BSc student from Mysore and Sayyed Owaiz Afnan Sayyed Shaukat (20), a fruit seller from Mysuru.
Investigations have revealed that the mule accounts registered in the name of some of these suspects and accounts managed by some others as mule handlers have seen fraudulent transactions worth over Rs 100 crore. These accounts have been flagged in at least 35 cases of cyber frauds registered across the country including those in Mumbai, Karnataka, Telangana and Kerala. “The accounts used and managed by Mohammed Amer Akil Mohammed Arif have been flagged in the Rs 58 crore ‘digital arrest’ scam registered in Mumbai, the biggest ever fraud of the type registered in the country so far,” an officer who is part of the probe said.
Officials have expressed concern that despite extensive awareness campaigns and sensitisation efforts by the government and law enforcement agencies, incidents of digital arrest, online extortion and blackmail continue to be reported. These scams are typically carried out by cyber criminals impersonating officials from law enforcement agencies such as the police, CBI, Narcotics Department, RBI, and Enforcement Directorate and judges.
Such frauds have been serious enough to be flagged at the highest levels, including by Prime Minister Narendra Modi in his ‘Mann Ki Baat’ address last year. As previously reported by Express, these scams are orchestrated by complex, multilayered syndicates operating across borders with their lowest layers of mule accounts and account handlers operating from within India. Victims’ money is often funnelled into mule accounts and then routed to international cybercrime networks through cryptocurrency channels.",565,,True,False
