4382.0,Digital Arrest Trap: How Scammers Stole ₹15 Cr from 81-YO in Karnataka - The420.in,The420.in,"Mon, 23 Mar 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://the420.in/digital-arrest-scam-karnataka-15-crore-elderly-businessman-fake-cbi-fraud/,success_decoder,success,Digital Arrest Trap: How Scammers Stole ₹15 Cr from 81-YO in Karnataka,The,2026-03-23,"In a shocking case of cyber fraud in Karnataka, an 81-year-old businessman was allegedly duped of over ₹15 crore after being subjected to nearly 40 days of sustained psychological manipulation under the pretext of a so-called “digital arrest.” Fraudsters posing as officials of a central investigative agency intimidated the victim with false allegations of money laundering, ultimately coercing him into parting with his entire savings.
How the Scam Began: Fake Money Laundering Allegations
According to the complaint, the ordeal began on February 5 at around 9:15 am, when the victim received a phone call from an individual claiming to be a senior official. The caller alleged that two SIM cards were active in the businessman’s name and were being used for illegal activities. He further claimed that the victim was in contact with Naresh Goyal, thereby linking him to a high-profile financial irregularity.
The fraudsters told the victim that ₹25 lakh had been laundered in his name and that he had allegedly received ₹5 lakh as commission. The accusation left the businessman deeply shaken. What followed was a prolonged phase of “digital arrest,” during which he was kept under constant surveillance through phone and video calls.
FCRF Launches Premier CISO Certification Amid Rising Demand for Cybersecurity Leadership
40-Day Digital Arrest: Asset Liquidation Under Duress
The accused instructed the victim not to contact anyone and to strictly follow their directions regarding financial transactions. He was told that the matter was part of a “confidential investigation” and that any non-cooperation could lead to immediate arrest. Under this fear, the victim began liquidating his assets.
Over a period of nearly six weeks, the businessman was forced to sell his stock portfolio and prematurely break his fixed deposits. After each transaction, he was directed to transfer funds to multiple bank accounts specified by the fraudsters. Throughout the process, the callers remained in continuous touch, assuring him that the money would be returned once the “investigation” was completed.
The fraud came to light on March 18, when the victim finally approached authorities and filed a complaint. Preliminary findings suggest that the scam was meticulously planned, with the perpetrators exercising sustained psychological control over the victim.
Expert Insights on Digital Arrest Cyber Scams
Cyber experts point out that “digital arrest” is an emerging tactic in which fraudsters impersonate law enforcement or investigative officials to instill fear and isolate victims. By restricting communication with others and maintaining constant pressure, they ensure that the victim remains compliant and does not seek external advice.
Renowned cyber crime expert and former IPS officer Prof. Triveni Singh said, “In digital arrest scams, criminals exploit fear and authority. By posing as officials, they psychologically isolate the victim and push them into blindly following instructions, often resulting in massive financial losses.”
He cautioned that no legitimate investigative agency conducts such operations via phone or video calls. In such situations, individuals should immediately disconnect the call and report the matter to cyber helplines or local authorities.
The case underscores a worrying trend in cybercrime, where fraud is no longer limited to technical deception but increasingly relies on psychological manipulation. Elderly individuals and those with significant financial assets are particularly vulnerable, making them prime targets for such sophisticated scams.
