Agra: Police arrested two men, including manager of a private bank, on Monday for allegedly posing as officials from CBI and other govt agencies to extort money through a fraudulent ""digital arrest"" scheme.
Arif Khan and Divyansh Gehlot were allegedly in contact with accomplices in Cambodia and Vietnam. They called victims while impersonating govt officers, accusing them of money laundering and drug smuggling to coerce them into transferring money. Gehlot, who worked as a manager in a private bank, allegedly assisted Khan in opening bank accounts to receive the extorted money.
DCP Suraj Rai said that on Jan 20, a victim filed a complaint at the cybercrime police station, claiming the accused used forged documents to falsely accuse him of financial crimes, deceiving him into transferring Rs 2.8 lakh. A case was registered under multiple sections of BNS and IT Act.
After analysing mobile records, bank details, and digital footprints, police traced the accused to Kota, Rajasthan, where they were arrested. During the probe, Rs 80,000 of defrauded amount was frozen and returned to the victim as per court orders. Both accused were presented before the court and sent to jail.
