4430.0,Samay Raina goes from accused to ambassador. Maharashtra Cyber’s new anti-scam star - ThePrint,ThePrint,"Thu, 27 Aug 2026 05:08:12 GMT",https://theprint.in/feature/samay-raina-maharashtra-cyber-fadnavis-indias-got-latent/3026163/?amp,success_decoder,success,Samay Raina goes from accused to ambassador. Maharashtra Cyber's new anti-scam star,Kasturi Walimbe,2026-08-27,"Mumbai: A year after being summoned by the Maharashtra Cyber for questioning over his controversial YouTube show India’s Got Latent, comedian Samay Raina reunited with the state agency on 27 August. This time, however, Raina was a guest at a cyber-awareness campaign, where he was felicitated by Chief Minister Devendra Fadnavis.
The agency launched a cyber-awareness film on digital arrest scams and the “1930 Maharashtra Cyber Anthem” at Mumbai’s Sahyadri Guest House.
The ceremony was also attended by actors Pankaj Tripathi, Ashish Vidyarthi, Sharad Kelkar, Sonali Bendre, Javed Jaffrey and Mangesh Desai, singer Sunidhi Chauhan and Raina, all of whom contributed to the campaign and were felicitated by the CM, reported ANI.
The news agency referred to the 42-minute film as “Digital Arrest”, while Fadnavis, in a post on X, described it as “Operation 1930”. The Cyber department’s campaign will also use shorter videos and reels featuring the celebrities to spread cyber-safety messages across social media.
“Creating awareness is one of the most effective ways to prevent cyber fraud,” Fadnavis posted, stressing that “there is no such thing as a ‘Digital Arrest’.”
He urged people to immediately contact 1930, the national cyber-fraud helpline, if they fall victim to online fraud, adding that the first few hours are crucial for blocking transactions and recovering money.
The CM also warned about emerging threats such as AI-enabled voice cloning, deepfakes and manipulated audio and video. Maharashtra Cyber chief Yashasvi Yadav, meanwhile, presented upcoming initiatives including the Maharashtra Cyber Safe app and the “Asha Call Bot.”
Raina, on the other hand, joked about his previous encounters with the agency.
“Mera aur Maharashtra Cyber ka rishta bahut purana hai (My relationship with Maharashtra Cyber goes back a long way),” he said in a video from the event on Instagram.
“1930 is really a great number. I have actually saved it because I often get calls from there,” he added.
Raina then joked about how active the agency was and its ability to trace people wherever they might be, before quipping that this was the first time Maharashtra Cyber officials had come to a comedian’s house and taken him along to felicitate him.
The remarks were a reference to the India’s Got Latent controversy in February 2025.
The Maharashtra Cyber had registered a case over alleged obscene content on Raina’s show after podcaster Ranveer Allahbadia’s remarks triggered widespread criticism.
Raina, who was abroad at the time, was issued multiple summons. His request to initially record his statement through videoconferencing was rejected, and he eventually appeared before Maharashtra Cyber in Mumbai in March 2025 to record his statement.
Seventeen months later, his return was under markedly different circumstances — this time as part of the state’s effort to get people to remember one number: 1930.
(Edited by Insha Jalil Waziri)",463,,True,False
4431.0,"CBI raids over 80 locations in 16 states in digital arrest scam case, arrests two - Telangana Today",Telangana Today,"Thu, 25 Jun 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://telanganatoday.com/cbi-raids-over-80-locations-in-16-states-in-digital-arrest-scam-case-arrests-two,success_decoder,success,"CBI raids over 80 locations in 16 states in digital arrest scam case, arrests two",Telangana Today; IANS,2026-06-25,"CBI raids over 80 locations in 16 states in digital arrest scam case, arrests two
                Under Operation Chakra-VI, the CBI deployed 60 teams across 16 states to dismantle a massive ""digital arrest"" scam network. Two suspects were arrested for laundering Rs 2 crore through mule accounts, using a fake Supreme Court website to defraud victims.
                
                                        
                    Published Date - 25 June 2026, 04:37 PM 
                 
                                    
                                                
                
                            
New Delhi: In a major crackdown on cybercrime networks facilitating “digital arrest” scams, the Central Bureau of Investigation (CBI), under Operation Chakra-VI, constituted 60 special teams and conducted coordinated searches at more than 80 locations across 16 states.
The searches were carried out in Punjab, Gujarat, Delhi, Maharashtra, Haryana, Tamil Nadu, Telangana, Andhra Pradesh, Uttar Pradesh, Madhya Pradesh, Rajasthan, Assam, West Bengal, Manipur, Karnataka and Odisha as part of an ongoing investigation aimed at dismantling an organised network involved in more than 200 cases of digital arrest fraud.
                            
                                            
                
                
    
During the operation, the CBI arrested two individuals from Chennai and Kolkata for their alleged involvement in the creation of shell companies and the opening and operation of mule bank accounts. These accounts were reportedly used to launder around Rs 2 crore of suspected proceeds of crime.
According to officials on Thursday, the agency recently uncovered a fraudulent website with a URL deceptively similar to that of the official website of the Supreme Court of India. Fraudsters allegedly used the fake domain to deceive victims by creating a false impression of legal authority under the guise of digital arrest threats.
Based on a complaint received from the Registry of the Supreme Court, the CBI registered an FIR and initiated a detailed investigation into the matter.
Using advanced forensic tools and technical analysis, investigators identified key components of the cybercriminal infrastructure, which was found to be operating both within India and abroad. The probe revealed that the accused were uploading forged and fabricated documents, including fake court orders and documents resembling those issued by law enforcement agencies, in order to lend legitimacy to their fraudulent activities.
During the searches, officials recovered several incriminating materials, including digital devices, mobile phones and bank transaction records. These have been seized and are currently being subjected to forensic examination.
The investigation has also indicated that, in addition to Indian citizens, individuals from several other countries may have been targeted and defrauded by the same network. Relevant information is being shared with international law enforcement agencies through appropriate channels.
Officials said the probe is ongoing, and further arrests cannot be ruled out as more links in the network are being uncovered.",426,,True,False
