Nagpur: A 58-year-old homemaker from Raghujinagar was allegedly duped of ₹52.50 lakh by cybercriminals in a ‘digital arrest’ scam. The accused reportedly posed as senior police officers and told the woman that her Aadhaar details had been linked to money laundering and terrorism-related cases. The fraud took place between August 9 and 14 after the woman received a WhatsApp call from an unknown number.
Woman Threatened With Police Action
According to the complaint, the caller introduced himself as a senior police officer and claimed that her Aadhaar card had been misused.
The woman was allegedly told that her identity had appeared in an investigation linked to money laundering and terrorism. The fraudsters then sent documents through WhatsApp that appeared to be from government and legal authorities.
They reportedly told her that she was under ‘digital arrest’ and that her activities were being monitored while the investigation was underway.
Rs 52.50 Lakh Transferred to Bank Accounts
After allegedly creating fear of arrest and legal action, the fraudsters instructed the woman to transfer money to several bank accounts.
She reportedly followed their instructions and transferred a total of Rs 52.50 lakh.
Later, the woman realised that she had been cheated and approached the police.
Cybercrime Case Registered
Police have registered a case based on the complaint filed by Pushpa Kamalkar Borikar.
The investigation is underway to identify the people behind the calls and trace the bank accounts used to receive the money.
