4448.0,"Centre flags Cambodia role in digital arrest scams, WhatsApp bans 9400 accounts - Telegraph India",Telegraph India,"Wed, 29 Apr 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://www.telegraphindia.com/india/cambodia-link-in-digital-arrest-scams-centre-tells-supreme-court-of-india-whatsapp-bans-accounts-prnt/cid/2158253,success_decoder,success,"Centre flags Cambodia role in digital arrest scams, WhatsApp bans 9400 accounts",Our Bureau,2026-04-29,"The Centre on Tuesday informed the Supreme Court that most online frauds, such as digital arrests and cyber-bullying, were being carried out from locations in Southeast Asia, predominantly Cambodia, even as WhatsApp stated that it had banned 9,400 accounts for suspicious transactions.
The Centre shared the information in the form of an additional status report submitted by attorney-general R. Venkataramani, whose assistance was earlier sought by a bench of Chief Justice Surya Kant while taking suo motu cognisance of the increasing cases of digital arrests and other online scams.
“The overwhelming majority of accounts linked to digital arrest scams targeting Indian users are operated from scam centres located in Southeast Asia, predominantly Cambodia. This finding has been incorporated directly into our proactive detection systems to accelerate identification and enforcement,” the report stated while referring to a probe conducted by WhatsApp’s dedicated investigation wing.
According to the report, “the most prevalent display names used by impersonators include ‘Delhi Police’, ‘Mumbai HQ’, ‘ATS Department’, ‘CBI’, and specific fabricated officer names”.
The report stated that a coordinated drive by government agencies such as the CBI, the RBI, units of the home ministry and WhatsApp’s investigation wing had resulted in a “significant and measurable disruption to digital arrest scam operations on WhatsApp. The 9,400 accounts banned, the suspension of scam-linked groups, and the banning of network administrators, executed in a focused 12-week period, should be expected to produce a material and observable decline in digital arrest incidents originating from WhatsApp”.
Another status report filed on behalf of the Indian Cyber Crime Coordination Centre (I4C), operating under the home ministry, told the court that it had decided to adopt retrospective biometric verification of SIM connections. “It was observed that retrospective verification of such connections would assist in identifying SIM cards being misused in cyber frauds,” it said.
Representatives of telecom service providers told the court that new SIM connections were being issued through Aadhaar-based e-KYC, and the earlier SIMs were being progressively verified.",328,,True,False
4449.0,Retired lecturer loses Rs 45 lakh to 'Digital arrest' scam: WhatsApp call; fake Supreme Court receipts; G - The Times of India,The Times of India,"Fri, 22 Nov 2024 08:00:00 GMT",https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/technology/tech-news/retired-lecturer-loses-rs-45-lakh-to-digital-arrest-scam-whatsapp-call-fake-supreme-court-receipts-google-pay-paytm-payments-and-more/articleshow/115523377.cms,success_decoder,success,"Retired lecturer loses Rs 45 lakh to 'Digital arrest' scam: WhatsApp call; fake Supreme Court receipts; Google Pay, Paytm payments and more - Times of India",TOI Tech Desk,2024-11-21,"A retired lecturer in Hyderabad fell victim to a ""digital arrest"" scam, losing Rs 45 lakh. Posing as a CBI officer, the scammer claimed Sharma's Aadhaar card was linked to illegal activities. Through video calls and threats of arrest, the fraudster manipulated Sharma into transferring funds to various accounts, using fake receipts to maintain the facade.
Hereâs what happened
The victim, 73-year-old Ch Purshotham Sharma received a call from someone who claimed to have intercepted a parcel containing banned drugs linked to Sharma's Aadhaar card number, implicating him in a money laundering case. To prevent his alleged ""arrest,"" the caller provided Sharma with a contact number for a purported CBI officer in Ahmedabad and instructed him to cooperate with the investigation.
The scammer then conducted a video call with Sharma while dressed in a police uniform. The scammer then pressured Sharma into sharing his bank account details and tricked him into making multiple payments through Google Pay, Paytm, net banking, and RTGS (Real-Time Gross Settlement) to various accounts. The transactions, made over multiple days, totalled Rs 45.5 lakh.
To further convince Sharma, the fraudsters even sent him fake receipts, supposedly from the Supreme Court and signed by the registrar, as confirmation of his payments. 
Sharma eventually realised he had been deceived and approached the Rachakonda Cyber Crime Police to file a complaint. A case was registered under relevant sections of the Indian Penal Code (IPC) and the Information Technology Act, including charges of identity theft and cheating by personation.
This incident highlights the increasing sophistication of cyber scams targeting unsuspecting individuals. 'Digital arrest' scams, where fraudsters impersonate law enforcement officials and exploit the fear of legal repercussions, are becoming a significant threat.",282,,True,False
