Six men from Rajasthan were arrested for a digital scam where they defrauded a Kandivali resident of Rs 32 lakh by posing as government officials. They used multiple bank accounts and converted the money into cryptocurrency sent overseas. Police recovered multiple cell phones, SIM cards, and cash.
MUMBAI: Six men have been arrested by the Cyber Police in connection with a 'digital arrest' scam in which a Kandivali resident was duped of Rs 32 lakh last month. Police said the six accused would recruit impoverished individuals to open current accounts at various banks, take control of account operations, and then withdraw defrauded sums received into those accounts. 
They would further convert the defrauded sums into cryptocurrency and send them overseas to the masterminds of the scam.
All six accused are natives of Rajasthan and were apprehended from a South Mumbai hotel. The bank accounts they were operating were found to have been used in 10 cyber offenses across India.
The Kandivali resident who was duped last month had received a phone call from an unknown individual posing as a TRAI employee. The caller claimed that a case had been registered against the complainant with the Delhi Police for money laundering. The call was transferred to a ""DCP Sharma,"" who instructed the complainant to join a WhatsApp video call. The scammers convinced the complainant to share information about his assets, investments, and personal details, including his workplace. He was asked to provide information about his whereabouts every hour.
The next day, a ""CBI officer named Simi"" contacted him and provided him with a link to verify the charges he was facing. He was assigned a case number, and when he entered it on the website, an arrest warrant, a bank account freeze warrant, and an RBI notice appeared. 
Upon reviewing the documents on the link, the complainant was convinced that he was in legal trouble. The scammers forbade him from seeking advice from anyone.
After receiving a second notice with the ""Supreme Court of India"" letterhead, the complainant was spooked and, as instructed by the scammers, transferred Rs 32 lakh to them. After returning home and confiding in his family, he realized he had been scammed.
Police said the sum he had sent to the scammers was split and transferred to 15 other bank accounts. Police traced details of the account holders and found that they had handed over account operations to the accused.
A team supervised by DCP Datta Nalawade and senior inspector Suverna Shinde, and comprising inspector Kiran Aher and sub-inspector Sandeep Pachangne, recovered 10 cellphones, 17 SIM cards, Rs 3.5 lakh in cash, 15 debit cards, and 16 cheque books from the accused.
The accused have been identified as Gangavishan Maanju, 32; Vikas Bishnoi, 21; Premsukh Bishnoi, 19; Ramvilas Bishnoi, 32; Sunil Bishnoi, 24; and Ajay Kumar Bishnoi, 21.
