4454.0,"‘Cops’ put Mumbai doctor in digital custody, cheat her of Rs 7 crore - The Times of India",The Times of India,"Thu, 31 Oct 2024 07:00:00 GMT",https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/city/mumbai/mumbai-doctor-falls-victim-to-rs-7-crore-cyber-fraud-in-digital-custody-scam/articleshow/114784598.cms,success_decoder,success,Mumbai Doctor Falls Victim to Rs 7 Crore Cyber Fraud in 'Digital Custody' Scam | Mumbai News - Times of India,Mateen Hafeez,2024-10-30,"MUMBAI: A 57-year-old doctor from a government hospital lost nearly Rs 7 crore to cyber fraudsters in a âdigital arrest' scam. The complainant woman was told that a SIM card in her name was linked to a bank account found during investigation of a money laundering case. 
To avoid physical arrest, she was put in âdigital custody'. Besides this, she was asked to report whenever she stepped out of her home. Following In digital arrest scams, fraudsters impersonate law enforcement officials to cheat people.
The doctor told the police that on Jul 24 she was at the hospital when she received a call from a person who told her her number was involved in âsuspicious' activity and her SIM card would be deactivated. Next, a fraudster who identified himself as police inspector Vijay Khanna from Colaba police station told her that her number was linked to a bank account which was being investigated in a money laundering case and asked her to come to Colaba police station, but soon told her not to. He asked her if she knew Naresh Goyal, the doctor has stated in her complaint.
Later, the accused video-called her from a fraudulent Skype ID created by CBI officer Rahul Gupta. 
His camera was switched off so she couldn't see him. He sent her a fake âarrest order' purportedly issued by Niraj Kumar, assistant director, Enforcement Directorate. A letter for âsurveillance rule,consent to terms of digital custody' with an FIR number on a fake letterhead of CBI was also sent to her. It bore a stamp impression too. 
Gupta threatened her that she would be under digital arrest and in case she did not cooperate, he would send police personnel to her home to arrest her physically. 
She has said in her police complaint that Gupta added another fake skype ID with the name Justice Dhananjay. During the call Gupta told Dhananjay that a SIM card was issued in her name and the card holder was in touch with Naresh Goyal. Gupta said that Rs 54 million was deposited in an account in the complainant's name. Next, Gupta's assistant Nisha Patel was added to the call. She collected information about the complainant's bank accounts, mutual funds, shares etc. Patel told her she would have to keep Rs 86.5 lakh as security deposit and gave her a bank account number. Sandeep Rao, a fake vigilance officer, kept her under watch through the Skype video call.
Threatening arrest, the accused asked her to send money to them. In one and a half month, she transferred Rs 6.93 crore to 13 accounts, unitl she realised it was a scam. An FIR was registered on Oct 17. A team supervised by DCP Datta Nalawade, senior inspector Mousami Patil and sub-inspector Nilesh Hembade is investigating.",463,,True,False
