4476.0,"Retired Army Colonel Duped of ₹15.5 Lakh in Digital Arrest Scam, Four Held by Delhi Police - Shop SSBCrack",Shop SSBCrack,"Sat, 22 Aug 2026 14:00:34 GMT",https://shop.ssbcrack.com/blogs/blog/retired-army-colonel-duped-of-15-5-lakh-in-digital-arrest-scam-four-held-by-delhi-police,success_decoder,success,"Retired Army Colonel Duped of ₹15.5 Lakh in Digital Arrest Scam, Four Held by Delhi Police",SSB Crack,2026-08-22,"Retired Army Colonel Duped of ₹15.5 Lakh in Digital Arrest Scam, Four Held by Delhi Police
A retired Indian Army Colonel living in Munirka Enclave was cheated out of ₹15.50 lakh in a “digital arrest” cyber fraud after scammers impersonating police and enforcement officials kept him and his wife under pressure for two days and forced him to transfer money on the false claim that he was complying with Reserve Bank of India (RBI) directions. Delhi Police have arrested four men from Punjab and Rajasthan who are accused of helping overseas cyber-fraud networks by supplying mule and current bank accounts, with links traced to operators based in Cambodia and the UAE, including Dubai.
The case is another example of how digital arrest scams are becoming more elaborate and more targeted, particularly against senior citizens, retired professionals and defence personnel. In this instance, the fraudsters combined fake legal paperwork, threats of arrest and constant monitoring to make the victim believe he was trapped in an official investigation.
Threat Call, Fake Warrants and Two Days of Pressure
According to the complaint, the retired Colonel first received a call on April 15, 2026, in which he was told that his telephone connection would be disconnected on the directions of Maharashtra Police. He was then redirected to another number, where a fraudster posing as a police officer alleged that the Colonel’s personal details had been misused to open an illegal bank account in Mumbai.
The fake account was said to be connected to money laundering, investment fraud and terrorist financing. To make the claim appear genuine, the scammers sent forged Enforcement Directorate arrest warrants, fabricated court papers, fake anticipatory bail documents and messages that were purportedly issued by the Bombay High Court and the RBI.
The fraudsters also sent photographs of landmarks near the victim’s locality, apparently taken from online sources, so that he would believe they knew his exact location and were keeping him under watch.
The Colonel and his wife, a school teacher, were then placed under what the fraudsters described as “digital arrest”. Over the next two days, they were kept under continuous psychological pressure, told to remain isolated and monitored closely. During this period, the Colonel disclosed details of his assets and transferred ₹15.50 lakh to a designated account, believing he was following RBI instructions to verify or clear the alleged funds.
There is no legal basis for “digital arrest” in Indian law. No police force, the Enforcement Directorate, the CBI or any other agency is authorised to keep a citizen under custody by phone or video call, or to demand money transfers in this manner.
Complaint Registered, Money Trail Traced
The victim approached authorities through the National Cyber Crime Reporting Portal (NCRP). An e-FIR, No. 165/26, was registered on April 19, 2026, at the Cyber Police Station, South-West District, under Sections 308, 318(4), 319 and 340 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita (BNS).
The investigation was handled by a team consisting of Sub-Inspector Sombir and Head Constables Jitender, Puneet, Mayurpal and Sandeep, under Inspector Pravesh Kaushik, Station House Officer of Cyber PS South-West, and under the overall guidance of ACP/OPS Sandeep Ghai. The team examined the money trail, beneficiary accounts, digital footprints and other technical evidence.
Investigators traced the defrauded amount to a current bank account in Ludhiana belonging to one of the accused. That account, police said, was already linked to 17 complaints on the NCRP from several states and Union Territories, involving fraudulent transactions worth crores of rupees.
Four Arrested in Facilitation Network
Police arrested four men who are alleged to have been part of an inter-state network that procured and supplied mule and current accounts on commission for cyber-fraud syndicates operating from outside India.
The arrested men have been identified as Sarbjit, 38, of Ludhiana; Suraj, 30, of Ludhiana; Sandeep, 32, of Ludhiana; and Naresh alias Lucky of Sikar, Rajasthan.
According to police, Sarbjit was the holder of the current account into which the ₹15.50 lakh was transferred and had allegedly handed over the account for use by the fraud network. Suraj is accused of acting as an intermediary who procured accounts and passed them along the chain. Sandeep, a bank employee, allegedly used his position and contacts to facilitate the procurement of current accounts. Naresh alias Lucky is accused of procuring accounts from Suraj through Telegram and supplying them to cyber-fraud operators based in Cambodia and the UAE, including Dubai. Police described him as the key domestic link to the overseas syndicates.
Investigators said Sarbjit, Suraj and Sandeep had travelled to Jaipur to hand over the account kit to Naresh. Four smartphones and six SIM cards used in the offences were recovered during the investigation. Deputy Commissioner of Police (South-West) Amit Goel and Additional DCP Kumar Abhishek have confirmed the arrests and said the probe is continuing to identify the wider network and the eventual beneficiaries of the fraud.
Part of a Wider Digital Arrest Pattern
Police said the case fits into a broader pattern in which low-level facilitators in India provide bank accounts and SIM cards to organised cyber-fraud operations run from overseas. The masterminds, often based in Cambodia, the UAE or other locations, impersonate police, ED, CBI, court or RBI officials and use forged notices, threats of arrest on serious charges such as money laundering or terror financing, and continuous audio or video monitoring to extract money from victims.
Retired defence personnel and their families are often targeted because they are seen as financially secure and more likely to respond seriously to official-sounding threats. Even for disciplined and experienced individuals, the combination of isolation, fear of legal trouble and the threat of reputational damage can create immense pressure.
The South-West District Cyber Police have recently dismantled several such facilitation networks, and officers said the recovery of devices and the mapping of the account-supply chain in this case should help investigators identify higher-level handlers and freeze more accounts.
Authorities have also reiterated that genuine law-enforcement agencies never ask citizens to transfer money over phone or video calls, never place them under “digital arrest” and never tell them to keep such matters secret from family members or remain confined at home. Anyone receiving such a call is advised to disconnect immediately, avoid sharing personal or financial information and report the matter through the National Cyber Crime Helpline 1930 or on cybercrime.gov.in. Officials say prompt reporting can improve the chances of freezing the funds and tracing the money trail.",1072,,True,False
4477.0,"Delhi Police busts inter-state 'digital arrest' scam network, 4 held - Asianet Newsable",Asianet Newsable,"Fri, 21 Aug 2026 04:30:34 GMT",https://newsable.asianetnews.com/india/delhi-police-busts-inter-state-digital-arrest-scam-network-4-held-articleshow-rkg0cqh,success_decoder,success,"Delhi Police busts inter-state 'digital arrest' scam network, 4 held",Asianet News Central,2026-08-21,"Delhi Police's Cyber Cell has busted an inter-state network facilitating 'digital arrest' scams. Four operatives were arrested for supplying bank accounts to overseas fraudsters who extorted a retired Army Colonel of Rs 15.5 lakh in Munirka.
The Cyber Police Station of Delhi's South-West District has dismantled an inter-state cyber-fraud facilitation network linked to ""digital arrest"" scams, arresting four key operatives across Punjab and Rajasthan, officials said on Friday.
The syndicate allegedly specialised in procuring and supplying current bank accounts to overseas-linked fraud operators who targeted high-profile victims under the guise of law enforcement inquiries.
Retired Colonel Extorted in 'Digital Arrest' Scam
The crackdown follows a complaint lodged on the National Cyber Crime Reporting Portal (NCRP) by a retired Indian Army Colonel residing in Munirka Enclave, who was extorted with Rs 15.5 lakh On April 15, 2026, the victim received an automated alert claiming his phone line was being terminated on Maharashtra Police orders.
He was then connected to impersonators claiming to be police personnel, who alleged his identity was tied to an illegal Mumbai bank account involved in money laundering and terror financing.
The fraudsters sent forged Enforcement Directorate warrants, Bombay High Court papers, and fake RBI directives via messaging apps.
The victim and his wife were subjected to continuous video surveillance and psychological intimidation before being coerced into transferring Rs 15.50 lakh as ""verification deposits.""
Investigation Exposes Multi-Layered Racket
Following an investigation registered under relevant sections of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita (BNS) at PS Cyber, South-West, officers traced the initial money trail to a commercial bank account in Ludhiana.
The Procurement Chain
A series of targeted raids in Ludhiana and Sikar exposed a multi-layered procurement chain operating on a commission model: Account holder Sarbjit (38) handed his current account over to Suraj (30) with the assistance of Sandeep (32), an employee at the bank who facilitated the internal clearance.
Intermediary Logistics: The trio travelled to Jaipur to deliver the physical banking kits to Naresh alias Lucky (31).
Naresh processed the acquired bank accounts through Telegram network channels and handed them directly to cyber-fraud rings operating out of Cambodia and Dubai/UAE.
Wider Network and Ongoing Probe
During interrogation, police recovered four smartphones and six SIM cards used to execute the handoffs.
Analysis of the primary beneficiary account on the NCRP database revealed a wider footprint across multiple jurisdictions.
Linked to active cyber-fraud cases across several States and Union Territories. Multiple fraudulent transactions running into crores of rupees were routed through the same network before being siphoned out of domestic banking channels.
Delhi Police confirmed that all four suspects--Sarbjit, Suraj, Sandeep, and Naresh--are in custody. Further investigative efforts are underway to map the extended international money trail and apprehend additional domestic facilitators. (ANI)
(Except for the headline, this story has not been edited by Asianet Newsable English staff and is published from a syndicated feed.)",474,,True,False
4478.0,Restaurant chain Khan Chacha founder’s son arrested in Rs 54 lakh cyber crime scam - Punjab Kesari English,Punjab Kesari English,"Wed, 08 Apr 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://english.punjabkesari.com/india/khan-chacha-restaurant-chain-founders-son-arrested-in-explosive-54-lakh-cyber-crime-scam/,success_decoder,success,Restaurant chain Khan Chacha founder's son arrested in Rs 54 lakh cyber crime scam - english.punjabkesari.com,IANS,2026-04-08,"New Delhi, April 8 (IANS) The Delhi Police on Wednesday arrested the son of the founder of iconic restaurant chain, Khan Chacha, in a cyber crime case that has sent shockwaves across the national capital, officials said.
The accused, Mohammad Javed (49), was taken into custody for allegedly facilitating cyber fraud by allowing fraudsters to use his bank account to channel proceeds of crime.
Alongside him, Khan Chacha Director Mohammad Salim (49) was “bound down” for allegedly permitting cyber fraudsters to exploit the account as a mule account to process money extorted from victims of the digital arrest fraud.
In a dramatic raid, police recovered two mobile phones and two SIM cards from the accused.
During questioning, Javed and Salim admitted they had originally opened the bank account for their legitimate restaurant and catering business.
But facing financial distress, they agreed to let outsiders use it in exchange for a 2 to 3 per cent commission.
The accused revealed a murky network: they were first approached by Harvinder Kohli, who introduced them to Naseem and later to Sandeep Dwivedi.
By sharing their bank account details, they enabled the transfer of massive amounts of money.
Around Rs 54 lakh, siphoned from multiple fraud cases, was credited into the bank account and then funnelled into other accounts.
Authorities say suspicious chats — now deleted — point to an elaborate conspiracy.
The seized devices and account details have been handed over to the Indian Cyber Crime Coordination Centre (I4C) for nationwide cross-verification.
Police are now hunting for Kohli, Naseem, and Dwivedi, whose roles are under intense probe.
Deputy Commissioner of Police (New Delhi District), Sachin Sharma, issued a stern warning to citizens, saying: “Do not share your bank account details, ATM cards, OTPs or internet banking credentials with any unknown person in lieu of commission or financial gain. Such accounts are often used by cyber criminals for routing cheated money, making the account holder equally liable under law.”
The scandal has cast a dark shadow over Khan Chacha, a restaurant chain founded in Delhi in 1972, and now spread across Haryana, Punjab, and Uttar Pradesh.
Once celebrated for its kebabs and culinary legacy, the brand is now entangled in a high-profile cyber crime case that has jolted both the food industry and the public.
–IANS
