Global digital arrest scam network busted:Fraudsters using 22-country numbers; satellite systems helping them evade detection
- Copy link
Cybercriminals have adopted a new and more psychological method to cheat people. They are now making IVR calls from numbers across 22 countries, creating fear of a so-called ‘digital arrest’. To hide their identity, they are using satellite and foreign networks.
According to cyber experts, these automated IVR calls come from foreign numbers and play a recorded voice. In such calls, fraudsters pretend to be officials from the police, TRAI, FedEx, or the Department of Telecommunications.
They tell the victim that a parcel sent using their mobile number or name is linked to illegal activity. After this, they create panic by warning of arrest and demand money to settle the matter immediately.
In many cases, they carry out fraud by staging a fake digital arrest. They trick victims into sharing bank details and OTPs or make them transfer money directly.
Do not answer calls from numbers with these international codes
- Pakistan (+92)
- Bangladesh (+880)
- Nepal (+977)
- Afghanistan (+93)
- South Korea (+82)
- Iran (+98)
- Cambodia (+855)
- Saudi Arabia (+966)
- UAE (+971)
- Laos (+858)
- East Timor (+670)
- United Kingdom (+44)
- Australia (+61)
- Poland (+48)
- Bosnia (+387)
- Vanuatu (+678)
- Ivory Coast (+225)
- Botswana (+267)
- Tanzania (+255)
- New Caledonia (+687)
- Global Satellite (+881)
- Thuraya (+882)
Inmarsat (+870) satellite country code numbers are used the most.
TRAI's Warning
The Department of Telecommunications and TRAI have clarified that they never conduct personal investigations against any individual. TRAI does not ask anyone for Aadhaar card or bank details.
Do not give personal or financial information
Be cautious of calls coming from unknown international numbers. If you receive such suspicious calls, disconnect them immediately. Report them on the Sanchar Saathi portal or the cyber helpline number. Do not give personal or financial information to unknown individuals. If you become a victim of fraud, file a complaint at the cyber or police station. You can also report to the cyber helpdesk numbers 9256001930/9257510100. For online complaints, use https://cybercrime.gov.in.-Cyber Expert- Vinay Chaudhary, DSP",384,,True,False
