- Jaipur Bank Cashier Foils Cyber Fraud; Woman Rescued From Hostage Scam
Bank saves Jaipur woman from ₹25 lakh cyber fraud:Cashier grows suspicious after noticing her panic; victim says she is being held hostage
- Copy link
The manager and cashier of the Canara Bank branch located on Arvind Marg, MI Road, thwarted an attempt to commit cyber fraud of ₹25 lakh by showing the fear of digital arrest through their vigilance.
A woman resident of Brahmpuri had come on Monday to make an RTGS of ₹25 lakh to an ICICI Bank account in the name of Mahavir Traders, PB Road, Haveri (Karnataka).
When bank officials asked the reason for the large payment, the woman could not give a satisfactory answer and appeared nervous. She informed the bank staff about digital arrest. Bank employees explained to her that this is a fraud.
On suspicion, the cashier-manager spoke to the woman
Actually, the woman was asking the bank staff to transfer money quickly. Since the amount was large, the staff spoke to the manager. Upon suspicion, the employees made the woman sit separately and counseled her.
Then she revealed that she had been held hostage since Sunday. People claiming to be officers from CBI, ED, Police, RBI, and other government agencies told her that her bank account and mobile number were involved in money laundering.
After this, they were continuously pressuring her to transfer money by showing the fear of digital arrest.
Bank immediately stopped RTGS
After this, the bank officials immediately stopped the RTGS and informed the woman about cyber fraud. Due to timely action being taken, ₹25 lakh was saved from going into the cyber fraudsters' account.
Cyber thugs make video calls and claim to be government agency officials. They deceive by claiming that Aadhaar, bank account, or mobile number has been used in crime, money laundering, or parcel cases, and threaten arrest and action.
Cyber crime is continuously increasing
In the past few years, cases of 'Digital Arrest' scam have been continuously increasing in the country. According to the 'National Cyber Crime Reporting Portal' (NCRP), more than 1.23 lakh cases of digital arrest and related cyber crimes were registered in the year 2024.
In these, fraud of approximately ₹1,935.51 crore occurred. Meanwhile, in just the first two months of 2025, 17,718 cases came to light, in which fraud of ₹210.21 crore occurred. The surprising thing is that even educated people are falling victim to this scam.
Things to always remember
- Government offices or police never make arrests over WhatsApp/video calls.
- If someone threatens or intimidates you on a call, don't panic. Real police summon you at home with proper documents.
- Never share OTP, bank password, UPI PIN with anyone, no matter who they claim to be.
- If someone says to send money immediately or don't tell anyone, understand that they are the fraudster.
- Never download remote control apps like AnyDesk; this is the biggest trap.
- If you have any doubt, immediately disconnect the call and inform family/friends.
- If fraud occurs or you suspect it, immediately call 1930 or file a complaint on cybercrime.gov.in.
- Remember that legal processes take time; things like immediate arrest are just tricks of fraudsters.
- Teach children not to trust unknown links/calls/messages.
- Make sure to tell elderly people that real government officials never ask for money.",570,,True,False
