4499.0,Septuagenarian Retired Officer Loses ₹16.16 Lakh In Digital Arrest Fraud - Deccan Chronicle,Deccan Chronicle,"Wed, 12 Aug 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://www.deccanchronicle.com/southern-states/telangana/septuagenarian-retired-officer-loses-1616-lakh-in-digital-arrest-fraud-1978693,success_decoder,success,Septuagenarian Retired Officer Loses ₹16.16 Lakh In Digital Arrest Fraud,DC Correspondent,2026-08-12,"Septuagenarian Retired Officer Loses ₹16.16 Lakh In Digital Arrest Fraud
A 76-year-old retired Central government employee from Serilingampally lost ₹16.16 lakh after fraudsters posing as police officials subjected her to a digital arrest.
A 76-year-old retired Central government employee from Serilingampally lost ₹16.16 lakh after fraudsters subjected her to a digital arrest. According to the complaint, the fraud began on August 2 when the victim received a call from a person claiming to be from the Mumbai Police headquarters.
The caller said a complaint had been filed against her by the ATS in Lucknow in connection with a money-laundering case involving terrorist groups. When she said she could not travel to Mumbai due to her age, the suspects shifted the communication to WhatsApp video calls.
The fraudsters claimed that a bank account in her name was being used for terror-related transactions and declared her a suspect. They verified her details to gain her confidence and claimed that the RBI would examine her financial transactions. They instructed her to keep the matter confidential and not contact anyone. The suspects kept her on video calls for two days, instructed her to install Signal and share her mobile screen, and monitored her movements.
Posing as police officials, they pressured her to consolidate her savings and pension funds. On August 4, she was instructed to transfer ₹16.16 lakh to an account linked to a business in Haryana through RTGS. The suspects also sent her a forged document purportedly issued by the RBI as an acknowledgement.
When the fraudsters continued asking her to encash mutual funds and transfer savings, she contacted her daughter using a neighbour's phone. She then realised that she had been digitally arrested and defrauded. Police registered a case and are investigating the transactions in an effort to identify the suspects and recover the money during the investigation.",306,,True,False
4500.0,Hyderabad Digital Arrest scam: Retired LIC employee loses Rs 4 lakh - Hyderabad Mail,Hyderabad Mail,"Mon, 17 Aug 2026 05:05:20 GMT",https://hyderabadmail.com/hyderabad-digital-arrest-scam-retired-lic-employee-loses-rs-4-lakh/,success_decoder,success,Hyderabad Digital Arrest scam: Retired LIC employee loses Rs 4 lakh,Durga Prasad Sunku,2026-08-17,"Hyderabad Digital Arrest scam: Retired LIC employee loses Rs 4 lakh
HYDERABAD: A 67-year-old retired LIC employee lost Rs 4 lakh to cybercriminals who pretended to be officials from the Data Protection Board of India, Mumbai Police, and senior Indian Police Service (IPS) officers. Police said this was done through a complex ‘digital arrest’ scam.
The victim, a resident of Krishna Nagar Colony in Secunderabad, filed a complaint. He said that he got a phone call from someone who said he was Aman Kumar Sharma, a Senior Executive Officer at the Data Protection Board of India, Mumbai. The caller told him that someone had gotten a SIM card in his name using his Aadhaar card. Furthermore, the caller said the mobile number was being used for illegal activities.
Next, the caller connected him through a WhatsApp video call to a person pretending to be an officer from Colaba Police Station. During the call, this person said his Aadhaar card was being used for illegal activities and that a Canara Bank account had been opened in his name in Colaba, possibly for money laundering.
After that, the cybercriminals sent him fake First Information Report (FIR) copies, fake documents, a fake Supreme Court order, and fake Canara Bank statements. They also sent photographs and asked him about people he might know.
Over the next few days, the cybercriminals called him from different phone numbers. Additionally, they claimed to be senior police officials, including a person pretending to be IPS officer Vishwas Nangare Patil. They forced him to write a statement promising not to talk about the case, send photographs, and report to them every two hours.
Cybercriminals pose as senior IPS officers, threaten victim
On August 10, 2026, they asked him about his personal and bank details. Then, they told him to transfer Rs 4,00,000 from his Axis Bank account for checking. Thinking they were real police officers, he sent the money by Real Time Gross Settlement (RTGS) to the bank account they gave him.
When the cybercriminals kept pressuring him to cash out his mutual fund investments, he became suspicious and blocked all their numbers. Nevertheless, he still received calls from five other mobile numbers. He tried to call the National Cybercrime Helpline (1930), but the call did not go through. Eventually, he filed a complaint through the National Cyber Crime Reporting Portal and went to the local police station.
The cybercriminals used fake documents, such as fake Mumbai Police ID cards, fake Reserve Bank of India papers, and fake Securities and Exchange Board of India (SEBI) documents to gain his trust. Police have registered a case and are investigating.
The public has been warned to watch out for ‘digital arrest’ scams. In these scams, cybercriminals pretend to be government officials and create fear to get people to send them money. People should never share personal or financial details on phone calls. Always check if a caller is really from law enforcement or a government agency by using official contact information. If anything seems suspicious, call the National Cybercrime Helpline (1930) to report it.",513,,True,False
4501.0,Man arrested for defrauding victims of ₹24 lakh in digital arrest scam - The Hindu,The Hindu,"Fri, 17 Apr 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://www.thehindu.com/news/cities/Hyderabad/man-arrested-for-defrauding-victims-of-24-lakh-in-digital-arrest-scam/article70873745.ece,success_decoder,success,Man arrested for defrauding victims of ₹24 lakh in digital arrest scam,The Hindu Bureau,2026-04-17,"A 26-year-old man was arrested by the cyber crime wing of the Cyberabad Police in connection with an international “digital arrest” scam that defrauded victims of around ₹24 lakh.
The accused, identified as Gajarla Avinash from Jagtial district, had travelled to Cambodia on the pretext of a data entry job but was subsequently involved in organised cyber fraud operations targeting Indian citizens.
Investigations revealed that the accused impersonated law enforcement officials and made threatening calls to victims, falsely accusing them of involvement in criminal cases and coercing them into transferring money. The funds were routed through mule bank accounts in India and later withdrawn and shared among the network. Between January and February 2026, ₹24 lakh was fraudulently transacted and subsequently withdrawn.
During the operation, police seized one mobile phone, an Indian passport, and ₹12 lakh in cash from the accused.
Officials said the accused has confessed to his role, and further investigation is underway to trace additional victims, follow the money trail, and identify other members of the international cyber crime network.
Police have also issued an advisory urging the public to remain vigilant against scams involving fake calls, suspicious links, screen-sharing apps, and fraudulent investment or job offers.
Published - April 17, 2026 06:46 pm IST",208,,True,False
4502.0,Cyberabad Police recover additional ₹3.48 lakh in ‘digital arrest’ scam probe - The Hindu,The Hindu,"Tue, 21 Apr 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://www.thehindu.com/news/cities/Hyderabad/cyberabad-police-recover-additional-348-lakh-in-digital-arrest-scam-probe/article70889077.ece,success_decoder,success,Cyberabad Police recover additional ₹3.48 lakh in ‘digital arrest’ scam probe,The Hindu Bureau,2026-04-21,"The cybercrimes wing of the Cyberabad Police recovered an additional ₹3.48 lakh during the custody of a 26-year-old accused in a ‘digital arrest’ scam case, taking forward the investigation into an international cyber fraud network.
The accused, Gajarla Avinash from Jagtial district, was earlier arrested in connection with the case. Police had previously seized ₹12 lakh in cash, along with a mobile phone and passport. During interrogation, he disclosed that he had concealed part of the proceeds of crime, leading to the fresh recovery of ₹3,48,500 and another mobile phone.
Police said around ₹24 lakh was fraudulently transacted during January and February this year, with funds routed through mule bank accounts in India before being withdrawn and shared among members of the network.
Police issued an advisory urging the public to remain cautious of fraudulent calls, suspicious links and fake investment offers, and warned against sharing sensitive banking details or allowing bank accounts to be misused for illegal transactions.
