HYDERABAD: A 68-year-old retired Group-A officer of Indian Railways was allegedly cheated of ₹80,50,280 in a “digital arrest” cyber fraud after scammers posed as officials from the Data Protection Board and Mumbai police, according to a complaint lodged within Uppal police station limits.
The complainant, resident of Uppal, stated that he received a call on March 7, 2026, from unknown mobile number. The caller allegedly introduced himself as a Data Protection Board officer and claimed misuse of the victim’s Aadhaar details.
The call was then escalated to persons impersonating police officials from Colaba police station. They allegedly intimidated the complainant using fabricated FIR details and conducted video calls to reinforce the claim of an ongoing investigation.
Fake documents used to gain trust
According to the complaint, the fraudsters sent forged documents, including fake bank statements, ATM card details and a fabricated Supreme Court directive. They allegedly coerced the victim into sharing personal and banking information, claiming it was required for a national-level investigation.
The accused then threatened arrest and instructed the complainant to transfer money into “suspect accounts” for verification. The victim transferred ₹44,50,280, ₹21,00,000 and ₹15,00,000 from his accounts.
Gold pledged, family funds arranged
The fraudsters allegedly forced the complainant to break fixed deposits, pledge gold ornaments and arrange additional funds. His son also transferred ₹20,00,000 as instructed by the callers.
The accused continued sending fake warrants, fabricated Reserve Bank of India letters and maintained persistent communication through phone calls and messaging platforms. The complainant later realised that all communications were fraudulent and that he had been deceived.
He reported losing a total of ₹80,50,280 and sought necessary action from police.
Police Precautionary Guidelines
Telangana police emphasize “digital arrest” is not legal, no real agency detains via video or demands money transfers.
Key tips:
- Never share OTPs, PINs, CVV, or bank details.
- Verify suspicious claims directly with official sources.
- Ignore threats from unknown calls claiming CBI, RBI, or police links.
- Cover webcams during video interactions.
- Report immediately within the “golden hour” via helpline number 1930 or www.cybercrime.gov.in to freeze funds.
Hyderabad Cyber Crime Unit urges vigilance against spoofed IDs, fake documents, and panic tactics in these organized scams.",377,,True,False
4515.0,"Hyderabad Digital Arrest Scam: ₹42 Lakh Fraud Busted, One Arrested - Hyderabad Mail",Hyderabad Mail,"Sat, 18 Apr 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://hyderabadmail.com/hyderabad-cybercrime-arrest-digital-arrest-scam-42-lakh-fraud/,success_decoder,success,"Hyderabad Digital Arrest Scam: ₹42 Lakh Fraud Busted, One Arrested",Subhransu Satpathy,2026-04-18,"Hyderabad Digital Arrest Scam: ₹42 Lakh Fraud Busted, One Arrested
HYDERABAD: Cyberabad police arrested a 26-year-old engineering dropout from Jagtial on Friday for cheating a 66-year-old retired employee from Balanagar of ₹42 lakh in a digital arrest scam.
Officials identified the accused as G. Avinash and arrested him from Katlakunta village after he returned to Jagtial on March 26. Subsequently, police recovered ₹12 lakh from him.
Fake officer call used for intimidation
According to Cybercrime DCP T. Sai Manohar, Avinash travelled to Cambodia last year for data entry work. However, he was allegedly forced into cybercrime operations there. Later, he joined a syndicate that targeted Indian citizens.
On January 27, Avinash called the victim while posing as a law enforcement officer. During the call, he threatened arrest in a fake human trafficking case. Thereafter, he and his associates pressured the victim to transfer ₹42 lakh between January 28 and February 4.
Funds routed through multiple accounts
Inspector G. Vijay Kumar said the victim lodged a complaint on February 16. Following this, police registered a case and began investigation.
During the probe, officials found that ₹24 lakh was transferred to a bank account in Visakhapatnam. However, the account holder Krishna remains absconding.
Earlier, police arrested Krishna’s associates Dhanunjay Reddy from Visakhapatnam and Shashi Kumar from Jagtial. They later admitted that they provided Krishna’s account to Avinash.
Of the total amount, investigators confirmed that Avinash received ₹12 lakh. Meanwhile, Krishna got ₹10 lakh, while the two intermediaries received ₹1 lakh each.
Arrest after tracking movements
Meanwhile, police tracked Avinash’s movements after he returned to Jagtial on March 26. Eventually, they arrested him after confirming his location.
At present, investigation is underway to trace other linked bank accounts and recover the remaining money.
Police advisory
DCP Sai Manohar clarified that there is no legal concept called “digital arrest.” Furthermore, he warned citizens against such scams.
“If anyone calls claiming to be police and threatens digital arrest, inform the police immediately,” he said. In addition, he added that calling the 1930 helpline within one hour significantly increases chances of recovery.",346,,True,False
4516.0,Police Arrest 32 Bank Officials For Multistate Cybercrime Racket - Deccan Chronicle,Deccan Chronicle,"Mon, 20 Apr 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://www.deccanchronicle.com/southern-states/telangana/police-arrest-32-bank-officials-for-multistate-cybercrime-racket-1951572,success_decoder,success,Police Arrest 32 Bank Officials For Multistate Cybercrime Racket,DC Correspondent,2026-04-20,"Police Arrest 32 Bank Officials For Multistate Cybercrime Racket
The city cybercrime police arrested 52 people, including 32 bank officials, for allegedly facilitating a pan-India cyber fraud network through mule accounts to siphon off victims’ money across Telangana, Andhra Pradesh, Karnataka, Maharashtra, West Bengal, Bihar, Delhi, Rajasthan, and Gujarat.
Hyderabad: The city cybercrime police arrested 52 people, including 32 bank officials, for allegedly facilitating a pan-India cyber fraud network through mule accounts to siphon off victims’ money across Telangana, Andhra Pradesh, Karnataka, Maharashtra, West Bengal, Bihar, Delhi, Rajasthan, and Gujarat.
Cases have been registered against the accused for their alleged role in opening and operating fraudulent bank accounts used in cybercrimes. The arrests were made under “Operation Octopus 2.0”, a coordinated crackdown targeting insider involvement in financial fraud networks.
According to police commissioner V.C. Sajjanar, Operation Octopus 2.0 was launched to dismantle organised cyber fraud networks involving bank officials. “There has been a significant rise in investment scams, trading frauds, and “digital arrest” scams, wherein fraudsters exploit victims through manipulation, intimidation, and emotional coercion, leading to substantial financial losses,” he said.
The operation was carried out across nine states by 16 special teams over seven days. The accused include bank employees such as relationship managers, KYC approvers, probationary officers, clerks and field officers from multiple banks, who allegedly colluded with fraudsters to enable the creation of mule accounts, police said.
The arrested officials are from several banks located across the states.Investigators said 15 account holders knowingly allowed their accounts to be used for laundering illicit funds, while five middlemen allegedly procured such accounts and routed money to masterminds behind the frauds.
Through an intensive investigation, the commissioner said the cybercrime police station (CCPS), Hyderabad, uncovered a nexus of 350 bank accounts used to siphon off victims’ funds linked to 850 cases across India, involving transactions amounting to nearly Rs 150 crore.
The frauds include investment scams, trading frauds and “digital arrest” scams, where victims were manipulated and coerced into transferring money. During the operation, police seized 26 mobile phones, 14 cheque books, storage devices, laptops and other materials used in the offences.
Cybercrimes DCP V. Aravind Babu said ACP R.G. Siva Maruthi and staff performed with tactical precision and professional competence.
According to officers, the arrests point to insider complicity within banks, particularly lapses in KYC verification that enabled the misuse of accounts for cyber fraud. Police said further action will be taken against the accused as per law, and efforts are ongoing to identify higher-level operatives in the network.
( Source : Deccan Chronicle )
