4751.0,"₹34 lakh lost in cyber scams in Pune: Bitcoin trap, fake trading app and ‘digital arrest’ fraud exposed - Pune Mirror",Pune Mirror,"Mon, 09 Mar 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://punemirror.com/news/pune-cyber-fraud-bitcoin-trading-digital-arrest-scam/,success_decoder,success,"₹34 lakh lost in cyber scams in Pune: Bitcoin trap, fake trading app and ‘digital arrest’ fraud exposed",Pune Times Mirror Digital Desk,2026-03-09,"Citizens lose Rs 34L in crypto, trading app and ‘digital arrest’ scams
Cyber fraud cases continue to rise across Pune, with three separate incidents exposing how criminals use cryptocurrency investment traps, fake trading platforms and intimidation tactics to cheat people. In the three cases, the victims lost over Rs 34 lakh, sparking a fresh police alert to citizens on how to protect themselves from conmen’s traps.
Bitcoin scam: Hadapsar man Rs 12.11 lakh
A 54-year-old resident of Hadapsar was cheated of Rs 12.11 lakh after fraudsters lured him into investing in cryptocurrency through a fraudulent online platform.
According to police, the accused contacted the victim through online channels and convinced him to invest in Bitcoin. The fraudsters initially asked the victim to complete registration through a link and later provided a website where he was instructed to purchase Bitcoin as part of “investment tasks.”
Believing the opportunity was genuine, the victim followed the instructions and transferred money online between October 12 and October 14, 2024. However, once the payments were made, the promised returns never materialised and the accused became unreachable. Realising he had been cheated, the victim approached police.
A case has been registered under sections 319 (2) of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita and Section 66D of the Information Technology Act for online cheating.
A senior officer from Hadapsar police station said such scams are becoming increasingly common. “Fraudsters often lure victims with the bait of high returns on cryptocurrency investments. They ask victims to register on fake websites or apps and then gradually extract money through multiple transactions,” the officer said.
Police have begun tracing the bank accounts and digital trail used by the accused.
Share trading: Woman duped of Rs 10.86 lakh
A 36-year-old woman from Katraj lost Rs 10.86 lakh after cybercriminals convinced her to invest in the share market through a mobile application.
According to police, the accused contacted the victim and persuaded her to download an app called “VSLNHW.” The fraudsters claimed the application offered profitable stock market investment opportunities. After installing the app, the victim was guided through what appeared to be trading activities and shown fabricated profits on the platform.
Gradually, the accused convinced her to deposit money into different accounts as part of the investment process. Trusting the information displayed on the app, she continued transferring funds between November 10 and December 31, 2025. When she attempted to withdraw her earnings, the accused stopped responding and blocked access to the platform.
Police later discovered that the application was a fraudulent platform designed to display fake trading data and mislead investors.
A case has been registered under Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita sections 319(2), 318(4), 3(5) and Section 66D of the IT Act.
Police said that investors should only use verified trading platforms and consult authorised brokers
‘Digital arrest’: Elderly woman loses Rs 12 lakh
In a separate incident reported earlier in the city, a 74-year-old woman from Wanawadi lost around Rs 12 lakh after fraudsters trapped her in a “digital arrest” scam, a tactic increasingly used by cybercriminals. The victim has retired from the postal services.
According to police, the accused contacted the victim while posing as law enforcement officials from the CBI and falsely claimed she was involved in a financial crime investigation. The fraudsters threatened her with arrest and legal consequences and instructed her to remain on continuous calls while they conducted what they described as a “verification process.”
“In this case, the victim was kept under constant pressure and made to transfer money into three different bank accounts on the pretext of clearing an investigation. Fraudsters often use intimidation and the fear of legal action to manipulate victims. Citizens must remember that police never ask for money during any investigation,” said Inspector Jadhav of Wanowrie Police Station.
Cybercrime officials say such scams rely heavily on psychological pressure and fear. Authorities have repeatedly urged citizens to immediately disconnect such calls and verify claims directly with police authorities before taking any action.
Police warning
Cybercrime investigators say the three cases highlight how fraudsters are exploiting emerging technologies and financial trends.
From cryptocurrency investments and online trading platforms to impersonation scams, criminals are constantly changing their tactics to target unsuspecting victims.
Follow basic precautions
- Avoid investing through unknown websites or apps
- Do not trust unsolicited messages promising high returns
- Never transfer money to unknown accounts for ‘verification’ or ‘investment tasks.’
- Report suspicious activity immediately to the National Cybercrime Helpline 1930 or through www.cybercrime.gov.in
Officials say early reporting can significantly improve the chances of freezing funds before they are withdrawn or transferred further. As cyber fraud evolves, awareness remains the most effective defence against online financial crimes.",777,,True,False
4817.0,"Kerala loan app scam: Students, homemakers among worst hit - Mathrubhumi English",Mathrubhumi English,"Tue, 21 Apr 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://english.mathrubhumi.com/news/crime/kerala-loan-app-scam-students-homemakers-among-worst-hit-mpth8hc2,success_decoder,success,"Kerala loan app scam: Students, homemakers among worst hit",M Basheer,2026-04-21,"Over the past three years, loan app operators have defrauded people of nearly ₹70 crore.
Thiruvananthapuram: A growing number of victims of illegal loan apps in Kerala are homemakers and students, with quick disbursal of loans within hours, without guarantors, collateral or documentation, drawing them in.
Unlike legitimate platforms available on app stores, these loan apps are often installed through links shared on social media. Once installed, they gain access to users’ photo galleries and contact lists without their knowledge, tightening their grip on victims. Only a small fraction of those affected come forward to file complaints.
Also read | Fake trading apps and deepfake videos trap investors
Exorbitant interest rates and processing fees
People who have no other access to formal credit often turn to these loan apps. In most cases, the maximum loan amount available is around ₹50,000. However, many apps charge interest rates exceeding 40 per cent.
In addition, processing fees of around 10 per cent are deducted upfront before the loan is disbursed. The loan amount is often credited through digital payment platforms like Google Pay, reducing the actual amount received by the borrower.
Also read | RBI proposes 1-hour lag for high-value money transfers, 'Trusted Person' authentication for seniors
Students take loans without family’s knowledge
 Police officials said that over the past three years, loan app operators have defrauded people of nearly ₹70 crore. Among registered cases, a majority of complainants are employees of private companies and homemakers, while around five per cent are students. Most students reportedly take these loans without informing their families.
Threats if repayment is delayed
 Victims face severe harassment if they fail to repay on time. Even after repeated threats, if payments are not made, the fraudsters begin contacting the victim’s friends and relatives using details accessed from their phones. In some cases, photos from the victim’s phone are morphed into obscene images and used for blackmail. If the victim still does not pay, these images are circulated to others as a form of intimidation.
Fearing such consequences, many victims take out new loans to repay earlier ones, trapping themselves in a vicious cycle of debt. Despite ongoing awareness campaigns by the police, the number of people falling prey to such scams remains high.
