4904.0,ED swoops down on Nexa Evergreen scam masterminds: Real estate Ponzi used Dholera Smart City hype to trap 7... - Bhaskar English,Bhaskar English,"Thu, 12 Jun 2025 07:00:00 GMT",https://www.bhaskarenglish.in/local/rajasthan/jodhpur/news/dholera-smart-city-scam-busted-by-ed-raids-army-ex-servicemen-ran-land-fraud-fake-projects-duped-lakhs-company-135231962.html,success_decoder,success,"ED swoops down on Nexa Evergreen scam masterminds: Real estate Ponzi used Dholera Smart City hype to trap 70,000 investors and loot ₹2700 crore nationwide - Jodhpur News",,2025-06-14,"- English News
- Local
- Rajasthan
- Jodhpur
- ED Busts ₹2700 Cr Nexa Evergreen Scam In Dholera Project
ED swoops down on Nexa Evergreen scam masterminds:Real estate Ponzi used Dholera Smart City hype to trap 70,000 investors and loot ₹2700 crore nationwide
- Copy link
The Enforcement Directorate (ED) has launched a major crackdown on the Rs 2,700 crore Nexa Evergreen Ponzi scheme. On June 12, ED conducted simultaneous raids at 24 locations across Rajasthan, Gujarat, and Delhi. In Rajasthan, raids were carried out in Sikar, Jhunjhunu, Jaipur, and Jodhpur. These actions have once again brought the multi-crore fraud case into public focus.
Brothers From Sikar: The masterminds
The entire scam was orchestrated by two brothers from Sikar, Subhash Bijarania, a retired army man, and Ranveer Bijarania. They formed the Nexa Evergreen company with the pretext of investing in real estate projects in Dholera, Gujarat, where the government is developing India’s first greenfield smart city. The duo falsely promised huge returns on land and flat investments, attracting over 70,000 people across multiple states.
Luring investors with smart city hype
Dholera Smart City, part of the Delhi-Mumbai Industrial Corridor, was used as bait. Covering 920 sq km and 22 villages, it’s one of the most ambitious urban projects in India. Promising world-class infrastructure, AI-enabled services, and an international airport, the project lent credibility to Nexa Evergreen’s fake investment schemes. Victims believed they were buying into government-endorsed real estate.
How the scam was run
Registered on April 17, 2021, Nexa Evergreen collected Rs 2,700 crore from investors through an aggressive agent network. The company purchased 1,300 bighas of land in and around Dholera, initially for Rs 50,000 per bigha, and eventually up to Rs 8 lakh per bigha. They floated various investment plans and paid Rs 1,500 crore in commissions to agents to lure in more investors. In January 2023, after a year and a half of operations, the company suddenly shut down, and the founders fled.
Fraud unearthed in Jodhpur
In January 2023, an Rs 80 crore fraud case linked to Nexa Evergreen surfaced at Karwad police station in Jodhpur. Victims filed FIRs against agents Megh Singh, Shakti Singh, and Surendra Singh, who had even arranged fake land registries. When ED raided the company’s office in BJS Nagar on June 12, it was found deserted. The accused agents are currently in Jodhpur Central Jail, although no interrogation has been conducted yet by any agency.
Big names on company board
The company's board included several key associates: Salim Khan, Sameer, Datar Singh, Rakshpal, Ompal, and Sanwarmal. They first began operations in Sikar, targeting investors with promises of high returns. The company had connections across the state and lured many, including ex-servicemen and police officials. The core leadership used 27 shell companies to transfer and launder funds.
Massive purchases from scam money
The ED believes that the Bijarania brothers used scam proceeds to purchase:
- A Rs 25 crore resort in Goa
- Granite and marble mines in Rajsamand
- Hotels in Jaipur
- Luxury flats and vehicles in Ahmedabad
- Multiple bungalows and properties in villages like Kudan
A significant portion, Rs 250 crore in cash and Rs 15 crore in bank accounts, has been tracked, but full recovery remains uncertain.
Company origins and expansion
The fraud began in 2014, when Ranveer Bijarania partnered with local property dealer Rajal Jangid to buy land near Dholera. Later, Subhash invested his ₹30 lakh army retirement money to buy 10 bighas. After losing money in another chain scheme, they decided to launch their own, roping in investors from across India through local agents and offering fake land deals with big returns.
Will victims get their money back?
The scam falls under the Banning of Unregulated Deposit Schemes (BUDS) Act, 2019, which allows investor compensation from the seized assets of such fraudulent companies. ED sources suggest recovery will depend on the tracing and liquidation of all properties and movable assets linked to the scam.
