4908.0,Two arrested in Thoothukudi in two separate cyber fraud cases - The Hindu,The Hindu,"Thu, 20 Nov 2025 08:00:00 GMT",https://www.thehindu.com/news/cities/chennai/two-arrested-in-thoothukudi-in-two-separate-cyber-fraud-cases/article70302625.ece,success_decoder,success,Two arrested in Thoothukudi in two separate cyber fraud cases,The Hindu Bureau,2025-11-20,"Two suspected mule account holders, who were allegedly part of ‘digital arrest’ and ‘investment’ scams in Tamil Nadu, were arrested in Thoothukudi for allowing their bank accounts to be used by scamsters to receive and transfer funds, for which they received a commission.
A senior citizen, S. Srivatsan, 73, of Mylapore, was reportedly cheated of ₹4.15 crore by a cyber fraud gang that contacted him on WhatsApp last September. The gang impersonated officials of the Crime Department, Mumbai, and claimed that a SIM card issued in his name had been used for illegal activities. They intimidated him by showing forged documents allegedly issued by the CBI, Enforcement Directorate, Interpol, and the Supreme Court. Under the guise of an ‘RBI verification, they coerced him into transferring large sums of money to different bank accounts totalling ₹4.15 crore.
Technical analyses of the WhatsApp number used by the fraudsters revealed that it was registered in the name of Manish Kumar, 23, of Jhansi, Uttar Pradesh. The Cyber Crime Police arrested Manish Kumar last month. It was found that he had impersonated government officials and cheated several victims using social media and fake online job offers. Further investigation showed that ₹10 lakh of the money had been transferred to the Tamil Nadu Mercantile Bank (TMB) account of Selvakumar, 35, from Kayalpattinam in Thoothukudi district. From this account, ₹9,99,000 had been withdrawn in cash.
A CCB Cyber Crime team went to Thoothukudi and arrested Selvakumar on Tuesday. A mobile phone, two cheque books, and the bank passbook used for the fraudulent transactions were seized.
Another held in online trading scam
In a separate case, Krishnakumar, 32, of Anna Nagar, Chennai, came across an online investment advertisement on social media and joined a WhatsApp group linked to it. Fraudsters lured him with promises of high returns, asked him to download an investment app, and over various dates, he transferred ₹1,02,10,000 into multiple bank accounts. The app was rigged to falsely show high profits to make the investment seem genuine. When Krishnakumar attempted to withdraw his money, the fraudsters demanded more payments under various pretexts.
During investigation, the police found that ₹9,59,000 had been withdrawn from a Punjab National Bank (PNB) account belonging to Nishanth, 37, from Chidambar Nagar, Thoothukudi district. Nishanth had used multiple bank accounts, including those of his associates, to withdraw illegal commission amounts from the proceeds of the fraud.
A Cyber Crime team arrested Nishanth. The police seized three mobile phones, four cheque books, four debit cards, two QR code scanners, and four bank passbooks used for fraudulent withdrawals.
