4982.0,‘It’s practically a prison city’: Inside Sihanoukville’s largest scam compound - The Korea Times,The Korea Times,"Fri, 17 Oct 2025 07:00:00 GMT",https://www.koreatimes.co.kr/southkorea/law-crime/20251017/its-practically-a-prison-city-inside-sihanoukvilles-largest-scam-compound,success_decoder,success,‘It’s practically a prison city’: Inside Sihanoukville’s largest scam compound - The Korea Times,Hankookilbo,2025-10-17,"‘It’s practically a prison city’: Inside Sihanoukville’s largest scam compound
SIHANOUKVILLE, Cambodia — Rows of gray buildings with thick iron bars line the streets of Sihanoukville, about 250 kilometers southwest of Phnom Penh. Near the city’s Chinatown stands Cambodia’s largest so-called “yuanqu” — a slang term in Chinese criminal circles describing a “scam compound.”
Inside these complexes, online crimes such as voice phishing and romance scams are part of daily life. Young people from Korea and other countries are kidnapped or lured by Chinese-run syndicates, then stripped of their passports and phones and forced to commit fraud. Iron bars sealed the lower floors, apparently to stop captives from jumping off balconies in an attempt to escape.
Surrounded by 3- to 4-meter-high concrete walls topped with barbed wire and even shards of broken glass, the compound looked more like a high-security prison than an apartment block. Closed-circuit cameras were installed on nearly every corner.
Armed guards kept watch over the area. When this reporter stepped out of the car to take pictures, one glared in warning. There was only one entry gate, where each visitor and vehicle was thoroughly checked.
Local residents said most of the operations were led by Chinese nationals, though some Koreans served as middlemen. According to Amnesty International, at least 53 compounds of this kind are scattered across Cambodia.
A Korean restaurant owner nearby said, “Many of these compounds even run their own internal canteens. Sometimes we get delivery orders for gimbap or kimchi from casinos or buildings like that. When we deliver, a Chinese man usually comes out to pick up the food.”
The crimes are not limited to these gray, prison-like compounds. Many of the city’s luxury hotels and casinos also conceal scam operations.
Two Korean men the reporter met at the Sihanoukville provincial police office said they had been locked on the 13th floor of a city hotel and forced to conduct voice phishing calls before being rescued. In that 15-story building, the first 10 floors were regular guest rooms, while the upper five floors served as the criminal group’s base.
A local Korean resident pointed to the reporter’s own hotel and said, “That place used to be a yuanqu compound too, before it was converted into a hotel.”
Oh Chang-soo, head of the Sihanoukville Korean community and a missionary who helps rescue Koreans held captive, described the situation starkly: “The entire city of Sihanoukville is practically a prison.”
He added that on the previous night, a Korean man in his 20s who was trapped in a hotel casino called him for help and tried to meet near a small opening in the wall, but gave up. “He said the security surveillance was too intense,” Oh said.
This article from the Hankook Ilbo, the sister publication of The Korea Times, is translated by a generative AI system and edited by The Korea Times.",479,,True,False
4985.0,Google Sues Outsider Enterprise Over Massive AI-Powered Scam Network - Techgenyz,Techgenyz,"Sat, 13 Jun 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://techgenyz.com/google-outsider-enterprise-lawsuit-ai-scam-network/,success_decoder,success,Google Sues Outsider Enterprise Over Massive AI-Powered Scam Network - Techgenyz,Tanisha Bhowmik,2026-06-13,"On June 12, 2026, Google launched a major lawsuit to shut down a massive international cybercrime group called the Outsider Enterprise.
Operating mostly out of China via Telegram, this group uses Artificial Intelligence (AI) to send out highly realistic scams.
In just two weeks this past May, Android users reported 55,000 spam texts (more than 2 a minute).
The group sent 2.5 million fake messages directing people to over 9,000 scam websites, stealing millions of dollars from everyday people. Google is teaming up with the FBI and major cell carriers (AT&T, T-Mobile, Verizon) to block them.
But can a corporate-led legal dragnet actually outrun an adversary that mutates in real time?
The Pennies-to-Millions Pipeline: How Cheap AI Powered a $200M Scam Wave
It has become incredibly cheap (costing fractions of a cent) for criminals to use AI to blast out fake text messages and cloned voices.
- The Damage:
In early 2025 alone, North Americans lost $200 million (about ₹1,670 crore) to AI scams.
- The Future:
AI fraud is expected to cost the financial sector $11.5 billion, roughly ₹960,000,000,000 based on current exchange rates, over the next four years because scams now sound exactly like real company bosses or loved ones.
Laser-Targeted Lies: Are You on the AI Hit List?
The Cyber Shield Dilemma: Is Fraud Protection Worth Loss of Privacy?
To catch these rapid AI scams, Google and phone companies have to intercept and analyze billions of messages every month. While this protects people, it creates new risks:
The Good
Instant Protection:
Scams are stopped automatically. If a scam is blocked on one phone network, it is instantly blocked across all of them.
The Bad
The Surveillance Trap:
To catch clever AI scams, defensive systems have to read the actual content of your messages. Even if it’s done by computers, scanning billions of texts every day pushes society closer to constant, mass surveillance.
A Target for Hackers:
To train defensive AI, companies have to store massive amounts of flagged texts and data in one central place. Should hackers ever gain access to this “data vault,” the privacy loss would be catastrophic.
Accidental Blocking (False Positives):
As safety AI gets stricter, it might mistake normal messages for scams. This means your important texts or business emails could get accidentally blocked or deleted without you knowing.
The Hidden Carbon Cost of Fighting Cybercrime
Fighting AI with AI requires massive computing power, resulting in a heavy physical toll on the planet:
High Energy:
Running round-the-clock defense systems requires massive data centers that burn through electricity.
Water Waste:
These giant computer centers require millions of gallons of water every day just to cool down the overheating servers.
The Ultimate Choice: Convenience vs. Absolute Security
Google’s aggressive multi-front litigation and legislative push represent a necessary evolution in corporate accountability. Yet, technology alone cannot solve a crisis of weaponized trust. True safety forces a cultural shift toward systemic skepticism—verifying every high-stakes request through a secondary, offline channel.
Take Action Against AI Scams, don’t let yourself or your business become the next statistic in the multi-billion dollar fraud epidemic. Protect your digital identity today by enabling multi-factor authentication and verifying all financial requests through offline channels.",531,,True,False
4986.0,Family says scammers used AI to impersonate South Philly woman's granddaughter - NBC10 Philadelphia,NBC10 Philadelphia,"Thu, 14 Aug 2025 07:00:00 GMT",https://www.nbcphiladelphia.com/investigators/consumer/philadelphia-grandparent-scam-ai-theft-impersonation-elder-fraud/4254278/,success_decoder,success,Family says scammers used AI to impersonate South Philly woman's granddaughter,Kate Reilly; Tracy Davidson,2025-08-14,"It came out of the blue. Christine, an 86-year-old grandmother from South Philadelphia, told NBC10 she received a phone call in mid-July from someone whom she believed was her granddaughter, claiming she had been in an accident.
“She’s crying and I’m saying, ‘What’s the matter? What’s the matter, hon?’” Christine said. “She said that the airbag came back and hit her in the face.”
Stream Philadelphia News for free, 24/7, wherever you are with NBC10.
Christine’s “granddaughter” told her she was being detained by police and had been given a lawyer. As Christine tried to process everything, a man who claimed he was a lawyer named “John” got on the phone. He then told her he needed money to get her “granddaughter” out of jail.
Christine told NBC10 they wanted $10,400 from her. When she told them she didn’t have it, the lawyer said they’d take whatever she had in order to help her “granddaughter.”
“Six thousand,” Christine said. “I was a nervous wreck because I had to count everything.”
Christine told the lawyer that she had the money in cash. The lawyer told her they’d send someone to pick the money up and he kept her on the phone until that person showed up at Christine’s home.
Ring camera from Christine’s neighbor captured a red car park in front of her home. A person then got out of the vehicle and walked up to her stoop.
NBC10 Responds
Have a consumer complaint? Call 215-201-5310
“So, I opened my door, you know, to see if I see somebody,” Christine said. “And I happen to look out and I see this red car. And I’m saying to myself, ‘I wonder if this is the person.’”
The man told her he was there to pick up the money. Christine told him to write a receipt for the cash which he gave her. A few minutes after he left, Christine learned the truth. Her grandson, who lives next door, came over and told her that her granddaughter was at work and was never involved in a crash.
“I actually broke down because you understand it was just so much emotion and I started crying,” Christine said. “He comes, ‘It’s all right Gram. It’s all right.’”
Christine was the victim of a grandparent scam, which has been around for years. The scam involves criminals who call elderly victims for help while pretending to be their grandchildren.
Elder fraud -- which includes the grandparent scam – impacted more than 147,000 victims in 2024 with losses totaling nearly $4.9 billion, according to the FBI. The FBI also warns that criminals are getting even more sophisticated by using artificial intelligence (AI).
“I’m thinking, another person’s voice, I would have picked up on,” Christine said. “But I know this from a baby so I know her voice.”
In Christine’s case, the caller likely sounded similar to her real granddaughter. According to experts, scammers are using AI to make the voices sound real.
“It’s not hard to find something of my voice,” Christine’s granddaughter told NBC10. “I’m on social media.”
Christine and her family reported the scam to police though no arrests have been made.
Eric Wong, an assistant professor at Penn Engineering who studies AI, showed NBC10 how easy and fast voice cloning can be done.
“So, scams using AI are really just an evolution of scams from the past, from phone scams to mail scams. The AI really just makes things more scalable and more realistic,” Wong said. “If you can make the scam sound like a personal relative or family, then it becomes much more convincing.”
NBC10 Responds reporter Tracy Davidson gave Wong permission to use her voice from one of her recent reports to demonstrate how the AI works. Using the text-to-speech model, TTS, as well as hardware commonly found in desktop computers, Wong duplicated Tracy’s voice to make it sound like she said something that she never said. It took him only 30 seconds to duplicate a minute-long recording and turn it into a fake soundbite.
So how do you protect yourself from an AI-generated voice scam? Ask the “person” who’s speaking with you a question that only a family member would know the answer to. Also, as a family, decide on a security word that you all know to use during an emergency.",720,,True,False
4999.0,"ChatGPT, Button Cameras, Earbuds: How Fireman Ran High-Tech Cheating Racket - NDTV",NDTV,"Fri, 07 Aug 2026 07:00:00 GMT",https://www.ndtv.com/india-news/delhi-exam-cheating-scam-chatgpt-button-cameras-earbuds-how-fireman-ran-high-tech-cheating-racket-11879589,success_decoder,success,"ChatGPT, Button Cameras, Earbuds: How Fireman Ran High-Tech Cheating Racket",Mukesh Singh Sengar,2026-08-07,"A routine government recruitment exam in Delhi blew the lid off an elaborate cheating racket, and the man accused of running it was himself a government employee.
Delhi Police have busted a network accused of helping candidates cheat in recruitment examinations, arresting eight people in connection with the case. Police said the alleged mastermind, Sonu Kumar, has been working as a fireman in Chandigarh since 2022.
How The Racket Came To Light
The case came to light on May 26 after two candidates were caught cheating during a recruitment examination conducted by the Central Sanskrit University in Delhi.
Around 256 candidates had appeared for the examination to fill six Lower Division Clerk (LDC) posts. After the two candidates were caught, police registered a case and launched an investigation, eventually uncovering what they describe as a larger organised network.
Those arrested have been identified as Hemant Sharma, Ramesh Kumar, Rinku Sharma, Vedprakash, Akshay, Sunny, Deepak and the alleged mastermind, Sonu Kumar. Police said some of the accused appeared as candidates, while others acted as agents who arranged the cheating. One of those arrested was reportedly scheduled to appear for a job interview.
Candidates Charged Rs 8-12 Lakh
During questioning, investigators found that the gang allegedly tracked recruitment drives across various government departments and institutions before approaching job seekers directly.
Police said candidates were charged between Rs 8 lakh and Rs 12 lakh in exchange for guaranteed success in recruitment examinations.
According to investigators, the gang primarily targeted recruitment for Group D posts, where candidates have to clear both written examinations and skill tests.
Police suspect the network may have helped candidates secure government jobs in several states using the same modus operandi. So far, investigators have identified 10 to 12 candidates who allegedly used the service, though they believe the number could rise.
Hidden Cameras, Nano Earbuds
Police said the gang supplied candidates with concealed electronic devices, including hidden cameras, GSM devices, nano earbuds and specially configured mobile phones.
Investigators said miniature cameras were fitted near shirt buttons to avoid detection. During the examination, images of the question paper were allegedly transmitted to an accomplice outside the examination centre, who solved the paper using ChatGPT and relayed the answers back through the nano earbuds.
Police also recovered mobile phones fitted with applications capable of capturing images and remotely operating devices even when the screen was switched off.
During the investigation, officers seized seven mobile phones, four GSM devices, several electronic gadgets, a tablet and a Tata Nexon allegedly used by the accused. The devices are being examined by forensic experts.
Police are now trying to establish how many recruitment examinations may have been compromised and how many candidates benefited from the alleged racket.
Further arrests are likely, police said.
