- Engineer, B.Ed Graduates & REET Aspirants Ran ₹6.25 Cr Digital scam: Rajasthan Cyber Cell Busts Network Spanning 6 States; Accused Posed As CBI Officers, Judges
Sunday Big StoryPoliceman’s son turns cyber fraudster to fund Australia dream:Impersonated CBI inspector, judge to dupe Rs 6.25 cr; Rajasthan Cyber Cell busts network spanning 6 states
- Copy link
A gang posing as top law enforcement officers, ranging from Mumbai Police officials to CBI inspectors and even judges, has been busted by Rajasthan’s SOG Cyber Police Station for running an elaborate scam worth over ₹6.25 crore across multiple cities.
The accused, including the son of a policeman, an engineer, and exam aspirants, trapped elderly victims by falsely implicating them in money laundering cases and subjecting them to digital arrests. Read the full story of how this gang used to scam people in the Sunday Big Story..
Modus operandi of the gang
The gang executed scams in a highly professional manner, dividing themselves into three teams. The first team searched for potential victims, mainly targeting elderly retired government officials living in big cities like Jaipur, Delhi, Chandigarh, Ahmedabad, and Patna. They would stay in hotels under fake identities while carrying out operations.
The second team handled bank accounts rented specifically to transfer the scammed money, while the third team was tasked with processing transactions from those accounts via net banking using hotel Wi-Fi.
DSP Neeraj Kumar Mewani of SOG’s cyber police station confirmed that the gang included Omprakash alias Nitesh, Vanshul alias Aryan alias Praveen, and Bhupesh Fagedia—all arrested from Sonipat on June 18. Earlier, on May 30, the police had also arrested Suresh Kumar Jat alias Surendra from Delhi.
WhatsApp notices, video calls & fake courts
The scam typically began with a WhatsApp notice sent to the victim, modeled on genuine ED or CBI summons. Soon after, a person posing as a Mumbai police officer would make a video call, accusing the victim of involvement in a money laundering case. They would then threaten arrest and conduct a fake 'digital arrest' to isolate and pressure the target.
In one case, a 75-year-old retired central government employee from Jaipur was digitally arrested for three days before being presented in an “online court” with a fake judge. Under stress and confusion, he transferred ₹23.56 lakh to various bank accounts, believing he was cooperating with a legitimate investigation.
₹6.25 crore cheated in just one month
In the span of a single month, the gang executed scams across several states, siphoning off over ₹6.25 crore. Victims were selected based on their vulnerability, elderly, financially secure, and unfamiliar with digital threats. All communications were staged using doctored video calls, forged notices, and false judicial proceedings.
The money would be immediately transferred using net banking to multiple bank accounts, often operated under fake names and accessed via hotel Wi-Fi.
Chandigarh architect loses ₹2.5 crore
On May 3, the gang contacted a 70-year-old retired chief architect in Chandigarh. Pretending to be a female TRAI inspector, they told her that her phone number was involved in fraud. She was digitally arrested and threatened with imminent arrest by Mumbai Police.
After a fake video hearing, the gang convinced her to break several FDs and transfer ₹2.5 crore from her pension fund into their accounts.
Retired doctor couple from Patna duped of ₹1.95 crore
In Patna, the gang targeted Dr. Radhemohan Prasad and his wife Dr. Chhavi Prasad. On May 21, they were told to replace their SIM card and appear at Colaba Police Station. After resisting, they were digitally arrested for 12 days, from May 23 to June 3.
On May 22, they were made to appear before a fake online judge. Believing the threat of arrest was real, the couple transferred ₹1.95 crore through RTGS in six installments to accounts in Indore, Chennai, Delhi, and Saharanpur.
Sonipat woman duped of ₹56 lakh
Renu Taneja of Sunshine County, Sonipat, received a fake notice alleging money laundering through a number linked to her Aadhaar. She was shown arrest warrants via WhatsApp and was even given a mock bail hearing. Within a week, she lost ₹56 lakh. Realizing the fraud on May 27, she immediately called 1930 to report the crime.
Retired employee loses ₹23.56 lakh in Jaipur
On May 23, the gang reached out to Santosh Kumar Bhargava (75), a retired central government officer living in Jaipur. He received a video call from someone dressed as a policeman, followed by calls from fake CBI officials and a judge.
He was accused of laundering ₹2.8 crore and told to transfer funds or face arrest. On May 26, he transferred ₹23.56 lakh via RTGS. His wife and he live alone, and with both daughters away, one abroad, he panicked. Luckily, a bank manager, sensing something wrong, advised him to report it to cyber police.
Cop’s son turned scammer, wanted to go to Australia
Among those arrested was Vanshul, the son of a serving constable in Bikaner Police. Dreaming of moving to Australia for higher studies, Vanshul had come to Jaipur for IELTS coaching. In pursuit of quick wealth, he joined the gang and began operating under fake IDs.
He booked hotel rooms for the gang in Jaipur’s Jawahar Circle under a fake name and arranged a bank account through his cousin Bhupesh.
This account, registered under the name of Suresh Kumar alias Surendra from Jhunjhunu, was where the ₹23.56 lakh from the Jaipur scam was deposited. When arrested, police discovered that Suresh’s account had seen a transaction of ₹3 crore that same day, suggesting use by other scam gangs as well.
Search on for two more accused
DSP Mewani stated that five accused have been arrested so far. Two more suspects, both residents of Bihar, are being traced. One is a qualified engineer from Bengaluru. Multiple teams are currently conducting raids to nab them.
The cyber crime unit continues its investigation into how this tech-savvy gang coordinated scams across multiple cities and targeted vulnerable elderly victims with such chilling precision.",1002,,True,False
