Uttarakhand has seen a sharp rise in 'digital arrest' cases, with 15 incidents until September 2023, involving a total fraud amount of Rs 12.70 crore. Major cases were reported in Garhwal division. Police are intensifying efforts to apprehend the perpetrators.
One of the accused in a digital arrest case, nabbed from Durg by STF on Tuesday.
Senior superintendent of police (SSP), special task force (STF), and state cyber police, Navneet Singh, informed that of the total 15 cases registered this year, 12 were from Garhwal division while the remaining three were from the Kumaon division.
""Of the total 15 cases, the biggest fraud was of Rs 3cr while the smallest was of Rs 14 lakh. Other major cases involved fraud of Rs 2.3cr and Rs 1.7cr respectively, all three big cases in Garhwal division. In Kumaon, the biggest fraud was of Rs 45 lakh. Overall, of the total fraud of Rs 12.7cr, around Rs 11.9cr was in Garhwal while the remaining Rs 82.7 lakh was in Kumaon,"" he added.
The SSP added that police are putting all-out efforts into nabbing accused involved in aforementioned cases.
""In the ongoing probe of the cases, police nabbed one from Durg, Chhattisgarh on Sunday evening. He was involved in one of the cases in which the complainant was duped of Rs 1.3cr. A probe is also ongoing in a recent case in which a retired professor of a college in Dehradun was held in digital arrest at his home for seven days from Sept 11 and duped of Rs 2.3cr,"" said Singh. Speaking on the precautions that need to be taken to avoid becoming a victim, deputy superintendent of police of state cyber police, Ankush Mishra, said that one should not transfer money by believing what any unidentified person tells them over the phone or any other medium.
""Also, on finding anything suspicious or falling victim to cybercrime, people should immediately inform the nearest police station to lodge their complaint on the cybercrime helpline- 1930. Police will help them in all their capacity,"" said Mishra.
Notably, digital arrest is a new cybercrime in which the perpetrators pose as officers of law enforcement agencies to trick their targets into paying money. They claim that the targeted person's bank account or confidential personal details have been involved in money laundering and drug trafficking. After trapping the person, perpetrators make them turn on a video calling app under 'online detention' as per false legal provisions, hence, the term digital arrest.
End of Article
