On Dec 1, the callers told Ray that RBI was verifying his bank accounts. âHe was told that his money would remain âprotectedâ if he revealed all his savings. Any attempt at concealing information would result in the seizure of all his money. Unable to bear the intense mental stress any longer, Ray disclosed all his finances â two fixed deposits and a savings bank account with a nationalised bank, totalling over Rs 1 crore,â the officer said.
The next day, as directed by the fraudsters on a video call, Ray went to his bank, collected RTGS forms, prematurely closed his fixed deposits and transferred Rs 99 lakh to a current account mentioned by the gang.
âHe believed that the entire amount would be credited back to his account within three days. When the money did not return and repeated calls to the numbers used by the imposters went unanswered, Ray realised he had been cheated,â the officer said.
