âWe are trying to trace the money trail, identify the account holders and operators of the accounts and examine digital evidence, including call records, WhatsApp communications and forged documents used to deceive the victim. Requests for transaction details and freezing of the account (where the money had been credited) have been initiated,â the officer said.",487,,True,False
6231.0,"Elderly man in Hyderabad duped of ₹26 lakh in cybercrime involving fake ATS, NIA officials - The Hindu",The Hindu,"Tue, 23 Sep 2025 07:00:00 GMT",https://www.thehindu.com/news/cities/Hyderabad/elderly-man-in-hyderabad-duped-of-26-lakh-in-cybercrime-involving-fake-ats-nia-officials/article70083815.ece,success_decoder,success,"Elderly man in Hyderabad duped of ₹26 lakh in cybercrime involving fake ATS, NIA officials",The Hindu Bureau,2025-09-23,"A 68-year-old resident of Humayun Nagar in Hyderabad was cheated of ₹26.06 lakh by criminals impersonating senior law enforcement officials. The scammers claimed to be officers from the Anti-Terrorist Squad (ATS), the National Investigation Agency (NIA), and even the Director General of Police’s office, linking the victim to money laundering and the Pahalgam attack in Kashmir.
The victim first received a phone call in which he was accused of involvement in terrorism-related activities. During the WhatsApp video calls that followed, the fraudsters presented forged arrest warrants and fabricated documents to intimidate him into compliance. Under duress, the victim prematurely closed his fixed deposits and transferred ₹6,06,000 from his accounts at Kotak Mahindra Bank and RBL Bank. Subsequently, he also transferred ₹20 lakh from his wife’s account, all into a bank account held in the name of ‘Hentry Jones’. The scammers even produced counterfeit letters purportedly from the Reserve Bank of India to lend credibility to their threats.
The victim — who was warned not to disclose the matter — informed his family only after they grew suspicious. He then reported the case to the National Cyber Crime Helpline- 1930.
The cyber crime wing of the Hyderabad police urged the public to exercise extreme caution: there is no such thing as a ‘digital arrest’. Police and other law enforcement agencies never demand money to clear names, nor do they issue certificates in exchange for payments.
Tools used by cyber fraudsters
Officials noted that fraudsters are increasingly using caller ID spoofing, WhatsApp video calls, and forged documents to intimidate citizens with false allegations of terrorism or financial crime. Victims are often pressured to keep the matter secret while being coerced into transferring money under the guise of verification or obtaining a “No Involvement Certificate.”
Numbers to lodge complaints
Police have advised citizens to remain calm, avoid sharing personal or financial information, and immediately disconnect such calls. Any suspicious claims should be verified directly with local police or official government helplines. Individuals who have fallen victim to financial fraud are urged to contact the Cyber Crime Helpline at 1930 or report incidents online at www.cybercrime.gov.in. In emergencies, citizens may also call or WhatsApp 87126 65171.
