6232.0,"Woman duped of ₹84 lakh, fraudsters execute two-month illusion of justice - The Hindu",The Hindu,"Thu, 08 May 2025 07:00:00 GMT",https://www.thehindu.com/news/cities/Hyderabad/woman-duped-of-84-lakh-fraudsters-execute-two-month-illusion-of-justice/article69552955.ece,success_decoder,success,"Woman duped of ₹84 lakh, fraudsters execute two-month illusion of justice",The Hindu Bureau,2025-05-08,"A 57-year-old government employee was duped of ₹84 lakh in ‘digital arrest’ and money laundering scam.
Impersonating officials from TRAI, Mumbai Cyber Crime, and Supreme Court, fraudsters coerced the victim via video calls to transfer funds.
The scam began on March 4, 2025, when the victim received a phone call from a man claiming to be from the Telephone Department, identifying himself with an employee ID. He alleged that a complaint had been registered against her for using a mobile number in illegal activities and directed her to contact the Telecom Regulatory Authority of India (TRAI).
Subsequently, the victim was connected to an ‘officer’ from the ‘Mumbai Cyber Crime Department’ via a video call, who informed her that she was implicated in a money laundering case involving Naresh Goel. The caller claimed the matter was highly sensitive and involved national security, instructing her to maintain complete confidentiality, including from her own family.
Over the following weeks, the scammers maintained regular contact, building psychological pressure and fabricating an elaborate web of lies.
On April 8, the victim received a WhatsApp video call from another fraudster claiming to be the ‘Chief of Investigation’. She was told that the legality of her bank funds would be verified by the Reserve Bank of India and warned of potential threats from sleeper cells. She was further informed that her phone was under surveillance and that her safety was being monitored.
Three days later, on April 11, she received another WhatsApp video call, this time from a person claiming to be from the ‘surveillance office’.
The victim was introduced to an alleged defence lawyer and informed that she was being virtually presented before the Supreme Court. She was told she had been granted an exemption from physical appearance due to her husband’s ill health. The scammers then sent her a temporary bail order and a surety bond worth ₹30 lakh via WhatsApp.
Under continuous pressure and convinced of the official nature of the proceedings, the woman liquidated her fixed deposits, took out gold and personal loans, and transferred a total of ₹84 lakh to bank accounts provided by the scammers. On May 6, she was told the case would soon be closed and that her money would be refunded. However, all communication ceased the next day, prompting her to panic and file an online complaint.
A case was booked by the cybercrime wing of the Hyderabad police and investigation is underway.
Cyber fraud victims are advised to immediately call the helpline number 1930 or register a complaint at cybercrime.gov.in. In emergencies, the victims can contact through WhatsApp on: 8712665171.
