6270.0,"India’s biggest digital arrest scam unfolds in Gandhinagar: Woman doctor held in 'mental captivity', defrau... - Bhaskar English",Bhaskar English,"Mon, 28 Jul 2025 07:00:00 GMT",https://www.bhaskarenglish.in/local/gujarat/news/india-biggest-digital-arrest-scam-unfolds-in-gandhinagar-woman-doctor-held-in-mental-captivity-defrauded-of-1924-cr-investigation-reveals-cambodia-link-135549602.html,success_decoder,success,"India’s biggest digital arrest scam unfolds in Gandhinagar: Woman doctor held in 'mental captivity', defrauded of ₹19.24 cr; probe reveals Cambodia link - Gujarat News",Vyas Dirghayu Balkrushna,2025-07-28,"- English News
- Local
- Gujarat
- India Biggest Digital Arrest Scam Unfolds In Gandhinagar Woman Doctor Held In Mental Captivity, Defrauded Of 19.24 Cr Investigation Reveals Cambodia Link
Bhaskar InvestigationIndia’s biggest digital arrest scam unfolds in Gandhinagar:Woman doctor held in 'mental captivity', defrauded of ₹19.24 cr; probe reveals Cambodia link
- Copy link
A woman doctor from Gandhinagar became a victim of a cyber mega-scam, losing Rs 19.24 crore over three months starting in March. Ironically, while cyber fraud awareness is being promoted from Gujarat’s capital itself, a resident of Gandhinagar fell prey to a ‘digital arrest’ fraud.
In India, the longest digital arrest lasted 40 hours with a maximum loss of Rs 12 lakh. But this case, where a woman was digitally arrested for three months and defrauded of Rs 19.24 crore, is now the biggest digital arrest fraud in both Gujarat and India.
In this case, the active police have arrested a person named Laljibhai Jayantibhai Baldaniya from Surat. In the woman doctor's case, one crore was transferred to Laljibhai's account.
Threatened to file case under FEMA and PMLA Act]
- A senior citizen woman doctor from Gandhinagar was kept under digital arrest for 3 months by an international and Indian cyber gang. Threatened with fake letters under FEMA and PMLA, and claims of FIRs over offensive posts from her phone, she was gradually forced to sell her home gold, break FDs, sell shares, and take loans.
- The gang extorted Rs 19.24 crore through multiple bank accounts. Gujarat CID Crime registered a case against 35 people on July 16.
- This is India's longest and biggest digital arrest fraud, exposing lapses in cyber awareness despite Gandhinagar being the state capital. The victim's two daughters live abroad.
- The woman doctor, living with her family in Gandhinagar, received a call on March 15, 2025, from someone claiming to be Jyoti Vishwanath from the Department of Telecommunications.
This was followed by calls from individuals posing as a Police Inspector and a Public Prosecutor, marking the beginning of the 3-month-long cyber fraud.
In the call, the woman was told that,
All your mobile phones will be shut down because offensive messages are repeatedly being posted to many people from your phone. For this, an FIR will have to be filed against you. A money laundering offense has been registered in this case and for that, charges will be filed under FEMA (Foreign Exchange Management) Act and PMLA (Prevention of Money Laundering) Act, Government of India sections
Doctor duped in digital arrest
- A woman doctor from Gandhinagar was trapped in a digital arrest scam starting March 15, 2025. Fraudsters posed as officials from the Department of Telecommunications, police, and public prosecutors, sending fake legal letters and threatening arrest under FEMA and PMLA.
- They claimed plainclothes officers were monitoring her and demanded details of all her assets.
- Over time, they made her sell home gold, deposit household cash, break FDs, take loans on locker gold, and sell shares and property.
- Using the excuse of depositing money in 'government accounts,' they transferred Rs 19.24 crore into 35 bank accounts. Later, they claimed the money was frozen and stopped all contact.
- After discussing with family, the woman realised it was a cyber scam. A case has been filed with the Gandhinagar Cyber Crime against five accused and 35 account holders.
Daily attendance through video calls
- The Gandhinagar woman doctor was under such intense pressure during the digital arrest that she had to make video calls and share her live location wherever she went.
- Even during her nephew’s three-day wedding in Surat, she secretly made daily video calls, showing scenes from the venue to prove her presence. She was psychologically controlled to this extent.
India's longest and largest amount first case
CID Crime believes this is India’s first case where over Rs 19 crore was extorted during a 3-month digital arrest. Though the woman moved freely and met people, previous cases involved amounts up to Rs 7 crore or less.
Cambodia connection revealed
In the initial probe into the Gandhinagar doctor fraud case, it has been found that a gang from Cambodia and another from an Indian state are involved. Gandhinagar Cyber Cell is conducting a detailed investigation.
Details collected before fraud
The international cyber gang, in collaboration with Indian fraudsters, first collected full details of the doctor’s assets, gold, FDs, shares, and cash. Using this information, they laid multiple traps and extorted a large sum without her knowledge.
35 bank accounts involved, CID deploys four teams
This digital arrest case involves 35 bank accounts. CID has deployed four teams, one to trace bank accounts, one for social media, another for ground investigations, and the fourth to track mobile numbers. Being India’s biggest digital arrest case, it's now a major test for Gujarat Police and the government.
Police awareness campaign failed to prevent fraud
Despite awareness videos by the Home Department and police officers on digital arrest scams, the woman was still tricked by fraudsters using fake identities under FEMA and PMLA, showing the campaign's limited impact.
Over 5,000 call centers in Cambodia
Though Cambodian gangs have duped Indians before, Gujarat Police has yet to nab a major gang. A returnee from Cambodia revealed over 5,000 call centers operate there specifically to scam Indians, yet strict action is still lacking.
Cambodia: Epicenter of cyber fraud
Cambodia and neighboring countries have become hubs for scamming Indians. Despite active travel agents linked to these frauds, little action has been taken. Police now consider Cambodia the main center for call centers targeting Indians.",934,,True,False
