6297.0,Kharghar’s senior couple duped of Rs 44.9 Lakh in ‘Digital Arrest’ Scam - Newsband,Newsband,"Sat, 08 Nov 2025 08:00:00 GMT",https://www.newsband.in/article_detail/kharghars-senior-couple-duped-of-rs-449-lakh-in-digital-arrest-scam,success_decoder,success,Kharghar’s senior couple duped of Rs 44.9 Lakh in ‘Digital Arrest’ Scam,Vikram Gaikwad,2025-11-08,"Kharghar’s senior couple duped of Rs 44.9 Lakh in ‘Digital Arrest’ Scam
- Newsband
- 08 Nov, 2025
Kharghar: A 78-year-old retired couple from Kharghar has been defrauded of Rs 44.91 lakh by a cybercrime gang that trapped them in a fake ‘Digital Arrest’ scam. The fraudsters impersonated top government officials, including IPS officer Vishwas Nangare Patil, and terrorized the victims by invoking the names of the Enforcement Directorate (ED) and the police. The Kharghar police have registered a case under relevant sections of cheating and the IT Act and launched a probe.
The incident began on October 15 when the elderly man received a call from someone identifying himself as “Mr. Sharma” from the Data Protection Board. The caller falsely claimed that complaints had been filed against the victim for extorting money in Nashik, allegedly by a complainant named “Rodrix.” Soon after, another member of the gang, posing as “police officer Hemraj Kohli,” threatened the senior citizen with immediate arrest if he failed to cooperate.
Fear of 'Digital Arrest' and seven years imprisonment
The scammers warned the couple that non-cooperation would lead to a “digital arrest” and imprisonment for seven years or a fine of Rs 5 crore. Terrified, the couple complied with every instruction. To appear credible, the accused even conducted a fake video conference call featuring a fraudster impersonating IPS officer Vishwas Nangare Patil. Gaining the victims’ complete trust, they provided multiple bank account numbers and instructed them to transfer money for “verification.”
Rs 44.91 lakh transferred through RTGS
Over several transactions, the couple transferred Rs 44.91 lakh via RTGS from their Saraswat Bank and Shamrao Vithal Co-op Bank accounts. The fraudsters later sent a fake receipt on WhatsApp, claiming it was an acknowledgement from the ED.
Death threat issued after 'Digital Arrest'
To further intimidate the victims, the gang declared that the man was now under “Digital Arrest” and threatened to kill him if he informed anyone about the case. It was only after the transfers were completed that the senior citizen realized he had fallen prey to an elaborate online scam and approached the Kharghar Police Station to lodge a complaint.
Police urge caution
Police have urged citizens to remain alert against such cyber fraud. They emphasized that no government agency or officer will ever demand money via phone or video calls. “Digital Arrest” is a deceptive tactic used by scammers, and citizens are advised to report such calls immediately to the nearest police station.",410,,True,False
6308.0,‘Your case linked to Pahalgam terrorist’: Pune businessman loses Rs 1.44 crore to fraudster posing as NIA chief - The Indian Express,The Indian Express,"Sat, 18 Oct 2025 07:00:00 GMT",https://indianexpress.com/article/cities/pune/case-linked-pahalgam-terrorist-pune-businessman-loses-1-44-crore-fraudster-posing-as-nia-chief-10314828/,success_decoder,success,‘Your case linked to Pahalgam terrorist’: Pune businessman loses Rs 1.44 crore to fraudster posing as NIA chief,Chandan Haygunde; Sushant Kulkarni; Anupamayadav,2025-10-18,"Cyber fraudsters posing as National Investigation Agency (NIA) chief and top Lucknow officers of the Anti Terrorist Squad (ATS) duped a 70-year-old businessman from Pune of Rs 1.44 crore in a “digital arrest” scam.
The victim, who is a resident of Mahatma Society in Kothrud, lodged the first information report (FIR) in this case at the Cyber police station of Pune city police on Friday.
The complainant, in the FIR, stated he was residing with his wife and a fraudster, posing as police inspector Rajesh Kumar Singh from Mumbai, called him on WhatsApp around 4 pm on September 23.
The fraudster said that the ATS Lucknow had arrested a terrorist who used bank accounts in the complainant’s name for terror activities. The fraudster also claimed that the complainant had been given “commission” for using his bank accounts for supplying money to terrorists. Then, a fraudster claiming to be an officer from ATS Lucknow spoke to the complainant over a WhatsApp call for an “investigation”.
Later, the complainant received a video call on WhatsApp from a fraudster wearing a police uniform, who claimed that this case was linked to the Pahalgam terrorists. The fraudster said they will record the complainant’s statement and that the NIA chief will conduct his inquiry.
Then, a fraudster posing as the NIA chief spoke to the complainant over video call. The fraudster assured the complainant’s name will be removed from their list of suspects by giving him a “Non Involvement Certificate (NIC)”, after checking his financial transactions and conducting a forensic audit of his bank accounts. The fraudster also asked the complaint to keep the video call on throughout the day. They also asked him to “report” on the video every two hours.
The fraudsters went on to ask the complainant to transfer money from his bank accounts to other bank accounts, the details of which would be shared with him. Accordingly, between September 26 and October 7, the complainant transferred Rs 1.44 crore into various bank accounts as per the instructions of the fraudsters.
Then, on October 8, the fraudsters asked him to deposit Rs 3 lakh in cash in a bank account. This time, the complainant found it suspicious and realised he had been cheated by online scammers.
He then submitted a complaint application to the police. After verification, the police filed an FIR against the online fraudsters under Bharatiya Nyay Sanhita (BNS) sections 204,3(5),318(4),319(2) and sections of the Information Technology Act. Police have launched a probe into the bank accounts and phone numbers used by the fraudsters for committing the crime. 
Cyber investigators have expressed concern that despite extensive awareness campaigns and sensitisation efforts by the government and law enforcement agencies, incidents of digital arrest, online extortion and blackmail continue to be reported.
In an advisory issued in May last year, the Ministry of Home Affairs had noted a significant rise in complaints related to intimidation, blackmail, extortion, and so-called “digital arrests.”
These scams are typically carried out by cyber criminals impersonating officials from law enforcement agencies such as the police, CBI, Narcotics Department, RBI, and Enforcement Directorate and judges.
Such frauds have been serious enough to be flagged at the highest levels, including by Prime Minister Narendra Modi in his ‘Mann Ki Baat’ address last year.
As previously reported by the Indian Express, these scams are orchestrated by complex, multilayered syndicates operating across borders. Victims’ money is often funneled into mule accounts and then routed to international cybercrime networks through cryptocurrency.",579,,True,False
6309.0,Delhi: Priest among eight nabbed in digital arrest fraud - The New Indian Express,The New Indian Express,"Sun, 25 Jan 2026 08:00:00 GMT",https://www.newindianexpress.com/cities/delhi/2026/Jan/25/delhi-priest-among-eight-nabbed-in-digital-arrest-fraud,success_decoder,success,Delhi: Priest among eight nabbed in digital arrest fraud,Express News Service,2026-01-25,"Delhi
Delhi: Priest among eight nabbed in digital arrest fraud
Victims kept under video surveillance, threatened with arrest & death, & forced to transfer cash to ‘fake RBI accounts’
NEW DELHI: Eight people, including a priest, have been arrested for allegedly duping an elderly NRI couple of over Rs 14 crore through a “digital arrest” scam, police said. Of the total Rs 14.84 crore siphoned off, Rs 2.08 crore has been frozen so far, police added.
The accused acted as facilitators by procuring and operating mule bank accounts and layering the defrauded money through multiple accounts. The funds were routed at the behest of an international cybercrime syndicate operating from Cambodia and Nepal, police said.
The case came to light after a 77-year-old woman residing in Greater Kailash reported that she had been cheated of Rs 14.84 crore. Police said the victim received a call in December last year claiming that a SIM card issued in her name was linked to a money-laundering case.
She was subsequently placed on video calls, during which fraudsters impersonating officials of agencies such as the CBI and police intimidated her by showing a forged arrest warrant, a police official said.
“The fraudsters contacted her repeatedly from various unknown mobile numbers. They conducted fake court proceedings through video calls and kept both the complainant and her husband continuously on video calls. They were instructed not to contact anyone else and were threatened that if they disclosed the matter, both of them would be killed,” a senior police officer said.
Under fear and coercion, the couple was persuaded to transfer funds, including fixed deposits and share investments, into so-called ‘RBI-mandated accounts’ for verification, with false assurances that the money would be returned.
Believing these claims, the complainant transferred Rs 14.84 crore through eight transactions. The accused were arrested from Gujarat, Uttar Pradesh and Odisha during a multi-state operation. “Police arrested Patel Divyang, Shitole Krutik and Ankit Mishra from Vadodara; Mahavir Sharma from Bhubaneswar; Arun Kumar Tiwari and Pradyuman Tiwari from Varanasi; and Bhupendra Mishra and Adesh Yadav from Lucknow,” DCP (IFSO) Vinit Kumar said.
